window.HEARTBEAT_SEARCH = [{"t":"Heartbeat Documentation","u":"index.html","h":[{"t":"By audience","id":"by-audience"},{"t":"By topic","id":"by-topic"},{"t":"Glossary","id":"glossary"}],"x":"Heartbeat Documentation Start with the page for your role. Every topic has one home; other pages link to it instead of repeating it. These pages are also published as an HTML site at https://gdpais.github.io/Heartbeat/, rebuilt from master on every push. The Markdown is the source; make docs-site builds the site locally into docs/site/, which is not committed. By audience New to Heartbeat (clients, stakeholders, new team members) Product overview: what Heartbeat is, who it is for, MVP scope, current status Roadmap: phases and what is done How we work: phase outcomes, who decides what, scope, reversals and the measures the scorecard watches Architecture overview: diagrams of the current runtime and the target platform Developers Local development: how to run the app (one command sequence), the SQL Server sandbox, tests, image builds, troubleshooting Walkthrough (optional): a guided first run of the same commands, from installing the tools to breaking the database on purpose Live demo (optional): every implemented feature on a running stack, stage by stage, from the collector's SQL to Grafana and Alertmanager, with what to judge Kubernetes delivery: the Helm chart, values, images and the kind cluster Data model: PostgreSQL schema and ownership rules Probe review checklist: before adding a SQL Server probe or approving a query_template override Architecture decisions: why things are the way they are Contributing: branches, commits, checks, pull requests and releases; changelog Service internals: DB collector Test application: PatientZero, the synthetic OutSystems application Heartbeat is tested against, and its fault scenarios and run journal TODO.md: implementation task list Operators and SREs Installing a release: install, upgrade and remove the published chart from GHCR, without a source checkout Onboarding SQL Server targets Configuration reference: integrations.yaml and environment variables Metrics and endpoints reference: ports, health endpoints, metric names, known gaps By topic Folder Contents product/ Overview, roadmap, how we work architecture/ Overview, data model, database observability, application observability, operator workflows, decisions guides/ Task-oriented how-tos reference/ Lookup tables for configuration, metrics and endpoints archive/ Superseded planning documents, kept for history Dated working documents (docs/plans/, docs/reviews/) are for decision review and are not tracked in git. Their lasting outcomes are recorded as architecture decisions. Glossary Term Meaning Collector A configured unit of collection in integrations.yaml (collectors[]), identified by a stable id, with a kind (sqlserver), scrape interval, probes and targets. The DB collector service runs one poller per enabled collector. Target One monitored database endpoint (host, port, database, credential reference) inside a collector. Becomes the target metric label. Probe A read-only SQL query from the built-in catalog (waits, blocking, …) whose result rows become Prometheus metrics. Credential reference (credential_ref) A pointer such as env/finance-prod that is resolved to a secret at runtime. Secret values are never stored in config or PostgreSQL. Config version Hash of the active integrations.yaml, exposed by health and admin endpoints. Environment The top ownership boundary (production, staging, …). Everything monitored belongs to exactly one environment. Application / component A business system operators care about, and its parts (modules, screens, workers). Application-owned records derive their environment from the application. Telemetry source A configured feed of logs or metrics for one application (for example an OutSystems log export). Investigation A request to correlate evidence for a subject (user, IP, session, request) in an application over a time window. Evidence link A URL into Grafana, Loki or Prometheus that supports an investigation result; raw evidence is never copied. Adaptive baseline A computed, versioned threshold (for example rolling median + MAD) rendered into Prometheus rules. OTel gateway A Heartbeat normalization service that never got a caller, removed by ADR 0009. Not the stock OpenTelemetry Collector, which does OTLP ingest and routing. Plane One of Heartbeat's four areas (telemetry, analysis jobs, control plane, presentation); see core systems. Control plane The planned Go API plus PostgreSQL, Redis and YAML config, which manage inventory, policies, schedules and investigations. ADR Architecture decision record, under architecture/decisions/. PatientZero The synthetic OutSystems 11 application Heartbeat is tested and demonstrated against (ADR 0010)."},{"t":"Application Observability Architecture","u":"architecture/application-observability.html","h":[{"t":"Scope","id":"scope"},{"t":"Export throughput","id":"export-throughput"},{"t":"OutSystems fields","id":"outsystems-fields"},{"t":"Derived metrics","id":"derived-metrics"},{"t":"SRE: requests, latency and errors","id":"sre-requests-latency-and-errors"},{"t":"Infrastructure","id":"infrastructure"},{"t":"Business","id":"business"},{"t":"Not built, on purpose","id":"not-built-on-purpose"},{"t":"SLOs and alerts","id":"slos-and-alerts"},{"t":"Dashboards","id":"dashboards"},{"t":"Request traces","id":"request-traces"},{"t":"The gap","id":"the-gap"},{"t":"Step A: request timeline from logs (with phase 2)","id":"step-a-request-timeline-from-logs-with-phase-2"},{"t":"Step B: synthesized OTLP traces (after step A)","id":"step-b-synthesized-otlp-traces-after-step-a"},{"t":"Open questions","id":"open-questions"}],"x":"Application Observability Architecture How Heartbeat observes OutSystems applications: where their telemetry comes from, which fields matter, the metrics derived from it and how requests are correlated across logs. The decision behind the ingest path is ADR 0008; the operator flow is in workflows. Status: partly built. Extraction to the OTel Collector and the derived metrics (data path steps 1, 2, 4 and 5) run as the db-collector's outsystems_logs kind, switched off by default (configuration); the chart ships their recording rules and SLO alerts. Loki indexes outsystems.log_type as a label (outsystems_log_type in LogQL). Request traces are designed, not built. Field names and log types follow OutSystems 11; they are confirmed against the SRE team's queries and the logging database DDL before the queries are reviewed. Scope Deployment Ingest path Status OutSystems 11 self-managed (Traditional and Reactive) The db-collector's outsystems_logs kind reads the oslog_* tables of the logging database and sends OTLP logs to the OTel Collector First, phase 2 OutSystems 11 on OutSystems Cloud Log streaming add-on sends OTLP straight to the OTel Collector When needed ODC App Analytics Stream sends OTLP straight to the OTel Collector When needed Data path for self-managed OutSystems 11: The collector runs each enabled log-type query (one .sql file per log type, see the query contract) for each logging-database target, from its watermark to now minus a lag, in batches of at most batch_limit rows and about 8 MiB of row data. Each row becomes an OTLP log record: Instant is the timestamp, Message the body, every other column an attribute under its OutSystems name. The collector adds error.fingerprint to error records. Values longer than 16 KiB, mostly Stack and EnvironmentInformation, are cut and the record is marked outsystems.truncated. Records go to the OTel Collector, then to Loki. Loki indexes only service.name, deployment.environment.name and outsystems.log_type (service.namespace too, which the collector does not set). The records hold personal data, so Loki deletes them after 30 days (loki.loki.limits_config.retention_period; the compactor's delete_request_store must name the store that holds the chunks: filesystem on kind, s3 in production). Compliance confirms the period before the first production deploy (ADR 0012). From the same rows the collector updates the derived metrics, which Prometheus scrapes from its /metrics. The watermark moves only after the batch is delivered: accepted by the OTel Collector, whose logs exporter sends batches of at most 4 MiB to Loki one at a time, in order, and retries a throttled one until Loki takes it (a push above Loki's 6 MB burst is never taken). Loki rejects for good a row more than an hour behind the newest row of its stream, so batches sent out of order lose rows. The logs pipeline has no batch processor, which would accept rows before they are queued. The exporter's queue holds one request, on disk (the otel-collector-queue volume), and the receiver waits for room in it. A larger queue does not keep its order across a Collector restart: it sends the requests it was sending when it stopped after the rest, and with 64 MiB a restart during a three-hour backlog lost 1,297 of 6,000 rows in the kind test. With one request nothing queues behind the request being sent, and the disk keeps that request across the restart: the kind test restarts the Collector during backlogs of 45 minutes and three hours, with Loki down, and Loki ends with every row of both, once. So the Collector takes the next batch only once Loki has the one before, and while Loki is down it takes none. The collector waits for it without treating it as a failure: the export runs in the background, outside the probe deadline, and is retried until the Collector accepts it; meanwhile the log type reads nothing more, and heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked is 1 (OutSystemsLogExportBlocked fires after 2 minutes). The target stays up and does not back off, so it resumes as soon as the Collector accepts the batch. The rows were read and the query closed before the export started, so nothing is held on the logging database while it waits, and the batches held in memory are bounded (64 MiB of row data). A backlog drains at about the rate Loki takes it (throughput). Export throughput Measured on kind with one log type of 12,000 error rows of about 6 KB (72 MB) spanning three hours, polled every 10 s: Queue Backlog with Loki up Backlog after a Loki outage One request (this chart) 85 s, 87 s, 102 s, 105 s 61 s after Loki was back; the target stayed up while it waited 64 MiB (earlier charts) 108 s, 177 s The queue filled, the target failed and backed off, and 15 minutes after Loki was back it held 12,339 lines for the 12,000 rows: batches sent again after 503s Loki's ingestion rate limit sets the pace either way: the Collector pushes 4 MiB at a time and waits at least 5 s after a throttled push. The 64 MiB queue only moved the wait from the collector into the Collector. Other OTLP sources of logs, none yet, wait the same way; one that gives up sooner (an OpenTelemetry SDK retries for about a minute) drops its batch, where a larger queue would have held it. OutSystems fields The extractor and the request correlation look these columns up by name. Other columns pass through unchanged. Column Meaning In Heartbeat Instant When the screen request or timer started (screen, cyclic_job), when the integration or mobile request completed (integration, mobile_request), or when the row was logged (error, general, extension, request_event), in the platform servers' time zone Record timestamp; watermark. Start-stamped types need their own lag Application_Name, Espace_Name Owning application and module service.name (application); Espace_Name attribute and metric label Module_Name (error and general logs) The free text the row was logged with; SLOWSQL marks the platform's slow-query messages Attribute; selects slow-SQL rows Request_Key One request handled by a front-end server; shared by the rows that request logs, across log types and modules Request correlation key Session_Id, User_Id, Username Platform session and user (Login_Id on mobile requests is returned as Session_Id) Investigation filters; distinct counts for active users Client_IP Caller address (Source on mobile requests and exposed integrations is returned as Client_IP) Investigation filter Tenant_Id Multi-tenant apps Attribute; optional business breakdown Server Front-end server that handled the request (Executed_By on most views is returned as Server) front_end metric label Duration Elapsed time of screens, server calls, integrations, extensions, timers and slow queries, in milliseconds (the queries convert the log types the platform logs in seconds and parse the SLOWSQL message) Latency metrics; span duration Screen, Action_Name The entry point and the operation that ran; the queries map each view's own column (Entrypoint_Name, Endpoint, Action, Cyclic_Job_Name, the event name) to these two operation metric label (capped); span name Error_Id Links a row to its error log entry Outcome error Message, Stack Error text and stack Body; error.fingerprint (from the whole values); cut at 16 KiB Session_Bytes, Viewstate_Bytes Traditional web screen state sizes Size histograms Endpoint, Type, Is_Expose Integration URL, protocol and direction Attributes; dependency breakdown Should_Have_Run_At Timer schedule Timer delay The mapping from each view's own columns to these names is the standard columns table of the queries README. Identifiers are never Loki labels or metric labels. They stay structured metadata, queryable with | User_Id=\"…\". They are kept raw, with access to Loki and Grafana restricted, and production needs compliance sign-off first (ADR 0012); the extractor can hash them before export without changing anything else. Derived metrics The collector computes these from every row it delivers to the OTel Collector and exposes them on its /metrics (code). They are counted when the row is extracted, so they trail real time by the lag plus the poll interval (about a minute by default), and a batch delivered twice (a crash between delivery and the watermark save) is counted twice. Every metric has the labels collector, environment and application (the record's service.name), plus those shown. Labels are capped so a name the platform invents cannot grow the series without bound: the first 100 values of module per application and of operation per application and log type keep their own series, later ones are counted as other; front_end is capped at 50 and integration type at 20 per collector, application at 1,000. The kept values are the first seen since the collector started, so a restart can keep different ones. The caps multiply, so each collector also holds at most max_derived_series series of these metrics; past it a row that needs a new series is left out of that metric and counted, and OutSystemsDerivedSeriesOverflow fires (series budget). User, session, request and IP never become labels. Entry points are the rows that stand for one request or timer run: screen (Traditional and Reactive screens; operation is the screen), mobile_request (Reactive server calls; the endpoint), cyclic_job (the timer) and integration rows of exposed APIs (the API action). SRE: requests, latency and errors Metric Type Labels What it answers heartbeat_outsystems_operation_duration_seconds histogram module, log_type, outcome Latency percentiles and SLOs per module for entry points heartbeat_outsystems_operations_total counter module, log_type, operation, outcome Throughput and error rate per screen, server call, exposed API or timer heartbeat_outsystems_operation_seconds_total counter module, log_type, operation Mean latency per operation (with the counter above); finds the slow screen inside a slow module heartbeat_outsystems_integration_duration_seconds histogram direction (consume/expose), type (REST/SOAP), outcome External dependency latency; a slow partner shows here first heartbeat_outsystems_errors_total counter module Error rate by module, from the error log heartbeat_outsystems_slow_events_total counter module, kind (sql/extension) Slow SQL (SLOWSQL) and slow extension (SLOWEXTENSION) rows the platform writes to the general log heartbeat_outsystems_timer_delay_seconds histogram operation How late timers start against Should_Have_Run_At: scheduler saturation heartbeat_outsystems_screen_state_bytes histogram module, kind (session/viewstate) Traditional web pages whose state size makes them slow or loads front-end memory outcome is error when the row has an Error_Id or a severity of ERROR or above, otherwise success; both series exist from an entry point's first row, so an error ratio reads 0 rather than nothing. Rows without a usable Duration (NULL or negative) are counted but not timed. Latency buckets include the SLO thresholds (0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 30, 60 s). Infrastructure Metric or source What it answers heartbeat_outsystems_front_end_operations_total{front_end, outcome} Load balance and error skew per front-end server: one bad node heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total{log_type}: rows the recount found committed after the extraction had read past their Instant Whether the platform's log service falls behind by more than the lag; those rows are not in Loki and the metrics undercount (ADR 0008) heartbeat_outsystems_log_delay_seconds{log_type}: now minus the newest Instant read, while rows arrive How fresh extraction is: about the lag plus the poll interval. It cannot show the log service falling behind, since a row is read only once it is older than the lag; the late-row counter does heartbeat_outsystems_extract_lag_seconds, heartbeat_outsystems_extract_rows_total, heartbeat_outsystems_export_failures_total, heartbeat_outsystems_duplicate_rows_total, heartbeat_outsystems_recount_failures_total, plus the collector's probe duration, target and freshness metrics Health of Heartbeat's own extraction The sqlserver collector kind on the logging and platform databases Waits, blocking, storage growth and I/O of the databases OutSystems depends on; the log database's growth and the platform database's blocking are common OutSystems incidents Front-end Windows/IIS hosts Not collected: it needs an agent on the platform servers (for example windows_exporter). Optional, the operator's decision Business Source What it answers Distinct User_Id and Session_Id per application over 5m and 1h, from Loki How many people use each application now. Daily and monthly actives come from Loki in reports (phase 7) heartbeat_outsystems_operations_total by operation Feature and screen usage: what people actually use heartbeat_outsystems_integration_duration_seconds_count by direction and type; per endpoint from Loki Business transaction volume with partner systems Tenant_Id (Loki, reports) Usage per tenant, where apps are multi-tenant; not a metric label unless the tenant count is small Not built, on purpose Active users and sessions as gauges. A distinct count needs every identifier of the window in the collector's memory; it resets on a restart and would double when collectors run as replicas (phase 8). Loki counts them correctly from the structured metadata already sent, so dashboards query Loki instead. A LogQL distinct count holds one series per ID, so the chart raises Loki's max_query_series from 500 to 20000. New error fingerprints. \"Never seen before\" needs a persisted, bounded set of fingerprints, or every restart reports all errors as new and looks like a bad deployment. It waits until a dashboard or alert needs it; until then error.fingerprint is on every error record in Loki. SLOs and alerts Recording rules in heartbeat.rules.yml turn the metrics into SLIs per environment and application, over user-facing requests (screens, Reactive server calls and exposed APIs; timers are left out): Availability: the share of requests with outcome=\"success\" (heartbeat:outsystems_request_errors:ratio_rate\u003cwindow\u003e). Latency: the share of requests answered within 2 s, from the histogram's le=\"2\" bucket (heartbeat:outsystems_request_slow:ratio_rate\u003cwindow\u003e). Alerts use multiwindow burn rates against static default objectives, 99.9% available (a 0.1% error budget) and 99% within 2 s over 30 days: fast burn (14.4× over 1h and 5m, an error ratio above 1.44%) is critical, slow burn (6× over 6h and 30m, above 0.6%) is warning. Low-traffic applications are left out so a handful of failures cannot page: the availability alerts need five requests a minute over the long window, so a fast burn takes at least 5 failed requests in the hour; the latency alerts need one. Because the SLIs go quiet, not red, when extraction stops, OutSystemsLogExtractionBehind fires when a log type's extraction lag passes 30 minutes, and OutSystemsLogExportBlocked sooner, 2 minutes after the OTel Collector stops taking batches, as it does while Loki is down. These are guardrails; per-application objectives and adaptive baselines come in phase 7. Dashboards Four dashboards in Grafana's Heartbeat folder read these metrics and the log records (files/grafana/dashboards/). Each one filters by environment and application. A header link opens the others with the same filters and time range, and another opens the SQL Server Overview for the same time. Dashboard What it answers From OutSystems Overview (outsystems-overview) Are users served well right now: requests, failed and slow shares against the objectives, p95, error-budget burn, active users and sessions, extraction lag; then the busiest operations, front-end skew, integrations, slow SQL, timers and Heartbeat's own extraction Metrics; users and sessions from Loki OutSystems Errors (outsystems-errors) What fails, where and for whom: errors by module and front-end, failed operations, the top fingerprints, users affected, and the error log Metrics and Loki OutSystems Latency (outsystems-latency) Where time goes: percentiles and the duration distribution, slowest operations by mean, p95 by module, integrations, screen state size, and the requests over 2 s Metrics and Loki OutSystems Request Timeline (outsystems-request) Everything one request logged, by Request_Key; the requests of a user or session; how many rows of each log type carry a Request_Key (step A) Loki Drill-down goes from the overview's top operations to a module's latency or errors, then from any Request_Key in a table to the request timeline. A fingerprint in the top errors filters the error log to that error. The metric panels trail real time by the extraction delay (about a minute); the Loki panels show rows as soon as they are in Loki. A Go test checks every dashboard's datasources, variables and links (tests/dashboards_test.go); the metric-reference test covers the metric names. Request traces The gap APMs such as Dynatrace instrument the .NET processes behind OutSystems: they see IIS requests, ASP.NET handlers and SQL statements, but not which OutSystems application, module, screen or action they belong to, and OutSystems 11 logs carry no trace ID to join them by. The result is traces that are hard to map to the platform's own concepts. OutSystems already ties its own logs together: every row a request writes carries the same Request_Key, across log types and modules. Heartbeat can build request timelines from that key, in OutSystems terms, without any agent. Step A: request timeline from logs (with phase 2) Request_Key is kept as structured metadata on every record. A Grafana dashboard shows one request across log types: entry point (screen, server call, timer or exposed API) with its duration, the integrations and extensions it called, slow SQL, and errors, ordered by time. Investigations filter by user, session or IP, then open the requests they find. This needs no new storage and proves whether Request_Key is reliably present in each log type. The OutSystems Request Timeline dashboard does both (dashboards); its Rows carrying a Request_Key panel is the measurement, which needs a real installation's logs to mean anything (PatientZero writes the key on every row). scripts/outsystems-log-queries.sql holds the SQL the SRE team runs against the logging database for the same timelines. Step B: synthesized OTLP traces (after step A) The extractor turns rows into spans as well as log records: Span From Trace ID A hash of Request_Key, so the logs and the trace link both ways without storing a mapping Root span The entry-point row: screen, server call, timer or exposed API, with its duration Child spans Integration, extension and slow-SQL rows with the same Request_Key, timed from Instant and Duration (Instant is the end for integrations and mobile requests, the start for screens and timers) Span events Error rows Attributes Application, module, operation, front-end, outcome; identifiers as on the logs Span links Calls from one OutSystems app to another one's exposed API, matched by endpoint and time; marked as inferred The OTel Collector's spanmetrics connector can then produce the same RED metrics from spans, and Tempo, the store Heartbeat's own traces already use (ADR 0018), shows waterfalls with links to the logs and the SQL Server dashboards for the same time. Step B is not part of phase 2's exit. Limits, stated in the product: spans exist only where OutSystems logs something. Fast SQL, internal actions and client-side time do not appear, and timings are as precise as the platform's millisecond durations. That covers what SREs triage first (which request, which module, which dependency) without replacing a code-level profiler. Not recommended now: OTel .NET auto-instrumentation on the front-end servers. It needs an agent on the platform servers and produces the same unlabelled .NET spans an APM does. Open questions Answered from the OutSystems 11 documentation (Log data reference, The log tables and views): Is Instant the start or the end of the logged operation? Per log type: the start for screen and cyclic_job, the completion for integration and mobile_request, the time of logging for error, general and extension, the time of the event for request_event. The start-stamped rows are written when the operation ends, hence the per-log- type lag (ADR 0008). Which time zone? The platform servers' own. OutSystems Cloud runs UTC; a self-managed installation uses whatever its front-ends and database share. log_timezone is set per collector. Which log types carry Request_Key? All eight current ones: general, error (Request_key), screen, integration, extension, mobile request, cyclic job and request event (REQUESTKEY). Only the deprecated web reference, web service and SMS logs and the detail tables lack it. Retention. Ten weekly cycle tables per log type; the oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e view is the current week, oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e_Previous the week before, switched every Friday at 23:45 database time. The retention period is set in the Configuration Tool (Log tab, Advanced settings); OutSystems Cloud keeps 9 weeks, 7 with log separation. initial_lookback can reach back one week at most, since the queries read two weeks' views. scripts/outsystems-log-survey.sql (read-only) ran against one installation's logging database on 2026-10-19. Its native tables were empty and its data a flattened one-week extract, so it answered some questions and left others open: Schema: answered. The test fixtures are its DDL: varchar text, datetime2 instants, Error_Id as text in every log (empty for none), the error log's EnvironmentInformation, and cycle tables clustered on Id or not clustered at all, so rows that share an Instant come back in no particular order. The oslog_Error view also joins oslog_Error_Detail for full stack traces. Rows per minute at peak: about 3,800 across screen, integration, extension and slow-SQL rows at the busiest minute of the week, about 1,000 on average; no single log type passed 2,200. The default batch_limit of 5000 covers it. Duration unit: milliseconds for screen, integration and extension (the reference says seconds for extension); maxima sit at timeouts in milliseconds. Still open for mobile request and cyclic job: the survey had none of their rows. Still open: whether every request writes an entry-point row (screen or mobile request), and the installation's time zone (from an Instant next to the database's SYSDATETIMEOFFSET(); a stale copy cannot tell). The hourly curve peaks at 11:00 and bottoms at 04:00, which reads as local time."},{"t":"Data Model","u":"architecture/data-model.html","h":[{"t":"What PostgreSQL does not store","id":"what-postgresql-does-not-store"},{"t":"Conventions","id":"conventions"},{"t":"Entity-relationship diagram","id":"entity-relationship-diagram"},{"t":"Domains","id":"domains"},{"t":"Identity and access","id":"identity-and-access"},{"t":"Audit outbox","id":"audit-outbox"},{"t":"Environment and inventory","id":"environment-and-inventory"},{"t":"Database observability","id":"database-observability"},{"t":"Investigations and evidence","id":"investigations-and-evidence"},{"t":"Alerting","id":"alerting"},{"t":"Reporting","id":"reporting"},{"t":"Relationship and delete rules","id":"relationship-and-delete-rules"},{"t":"Deferred topology","id":"deferred-topology"}],"x":"Data Model PostgreSQL is Heartbeat's durable system of record for product metadata: inventory, monitored-target metadata, investigation requests and results, alert and report metadata. It is not a telemetry store. The schema is defined by the migrations in db/migrations (0001_foundations, 0002_audit_outbox, 0003_login_roles, 0004_probe_definitions, then 0005_database_targets), which are authoritative. If this page and the migrations disagree, the migrations win; please fix this page. Status: the schema is migrated and covered by tests. The API (apps/api) writes users, user_roles and audit_outbox so far, through api users create-admin, logins (users.last_login_at, upgraded password hashes) and the users API, the inventory, environments, applications and application_components, and telemetry_sources, through /api/v1 (inventory, telemetry sources); the other tables wait for its later endpoints. The DB collector takes its targets from YAML, not these tables (ADR 0002). What PostgreSQL does not store See ADR 0001 for the reasoning. Data Where it lives instead Raw or derived metrics, live alert evaluation Prometheus Raw or normalized logs, session evidence Loki Alert grouping, deduplication, silencing, delivery Alertmanager Queues, locks, retries, idempotency keys, short-lived caches, login sessions Redis (ADR 0013) Integration endpoints, Grafana URL templates, credential references, collector desired state config/integrations.yaml / Kubernetes ConfigMaps Audit records Loki, as OTLP log records; written first to a transient outbox in the change's transaction (ADR 0016) Report binaries Object storage or filesystem (planned); PostgreSQL keeps only artifact_uri Tables deliberately not created in the MVP: Table Why not outsystems_sources OutSystems is a source type, not a domain object parallel to applications. Its settings live in telemetry_sources.config. Promote to a table only if the settings become large, queryable or constraint-heavy. session_identity_mappings Sessions are high-volume, potentially sensitive and telemetry-derived; they are reconstructed from Loki/Prometheus at investigation time. integration_connections, grafana_links Deployment-owned YAML and URL templates. Add only if operators need UI-editable mappings. audit_events Audit records are OTLP log records in Loki; the API's audit_outbox only holds them until the OTel Collector accepts them (ADR 0016). Add only for relational audit search or compliance exports. collector_instances, collector_assignments Would conflict with YAML/Kubernetes as the collector's desired state. Runtime state is exposed via /metrics, /readyz and logs. assets, asset_relationships Deferred until SQL Server/host topology is in scope (see deferred topology). Conventions Primary keys are UUIDs (gen_random_uuid(), pgcrypto). Every mutable table has created_at and updated_at. Status and type columns use CHECK constraints where values are stable (for example status in ('active','disabled')). jsonb is used only for flexible provider or rule configuration and labels, never for core relational data, and is not over-constrained early. Secrets are never stored. Columns such as credential_ref and target_ref hold references resolved at runtime. Prefer disabling (status, enabled) over deleting. Most foreign keys are ON DELETE RESTRICT. There is one ownership path per record. Application-owned rows derive their environment through applications.environment_id and do not duplicate it. Entity-relationship diagram Domains Identity and access users, roles, user_roles. Intentionally minimal for the MVP: a shared SRE/admin-style user is acceptable, with no permission tables, SSO tables or environment-scoped RBAC yet. Disable users (status = 'disabled') rather than deleting them; foreign keys restrict deletion. users.password_hash is the argon2id hash of a local login's password, in the PHC string format with its parameters; null for a user without one (ADR 0013). Emails are stored in lower case, and users_email_lower_uq (unique (lower(email))) keeps one user per address in any case, whoever writes the row. Migration 0002 adds the admin role, which api users create-admin grants, and 0003 adds operator and viewer. They rank viewer \u003c operator \u003c admin, and each API operation declares the least one it needs (what each role may do). Admins manage users and their roles through the API; users.updated_at moves with every change to a user, their roles included. A login sets users.last_login_at, only while the user is active and still has the hash it checked: a password change or a disabling locks the row, ends the user's sessions and wins over a login racing it. Sessions are not here: they live in Redis. Audit outbox audit_outbox is a queue, not a store (ADR 0016): each change the API makes inserts its audit record (record, valid against audit_record.schema.json) in the change's transaction, and the API's drainer deletes it once the OTel Collector has accepted it, normally within seconds. seq orders delivery, id is the record's id, and created_at gives the age of the oldest row. Nothing reads a row after delivery; audit records are searched in Loki. Environment and inventory Table Meaning Examples environments Top ownership boundary; everything observable belongs to exactly one. prod, staging applications Business system operators care about. Belongs to one environment. Claims Portal (platform = outsystems_traditional, criticality = high) application_components A part of an application: what part of the app it is, not where it runs. OutSystems module ClaimsWeb, screen flow Login, worker auth-worker telemetry_sources A configured feed of telemetry for one application: which logs and metrics are its own. No environment_id: it is derived through the application. For OutSystems 11, the extraction collector itself stays in YAML (ADR 0017). source_type = outsystems, ingest_mode = agentless, config = {\"application_name\":\"Claims\"} normalization_rules Versioned mappings from source-specific fields to Heartbeat's normalized fields. Scoped by source type and version, not environment. outsystems / default-log-v1: {\"session_field\":\"SessionId\",\"user_field\":\"UserId\"} OutSystems Traditional and Reactive are represented through applications.platform, application_components.component_type and, where necessary, telemetry_sources.config. Map default OutSystems log fields faithfully first so existing SRE queries keep working. What the API keeps true of these rows [4.3]: applications.environment_id and application_components.application_id are set at creation and never updated: an application in another environment is another application, so its history (investigations, alerts, reports) stays with the environment it happened in. A component's environment is its application's, read through the join. environments.slug is the collectors' environment and the telemetry's deployment.environment.name (ADR 0017 §2): lower-case letters, digits and hyphens, 1 to 63, starting and ending with a letter or a digit, and fixed once created. platform and component_type are identifiers (outsystems_traditional, module): lower-case letters, digits and underscores. labels are a JSON object of strings, at most 64. telemetry_sources.application_id is fixed like the others, and a source's config is valid against its source_type's JSON Schema in packages/config-schema/src/telemetry-sources [4.4]: closed, with credential references only. Within an environment, a config.application_name belongs to one application; every source change takes the environment's row lock (for no key update, which applications being added do not wait for) before it checks, so two changes cannot both find a name free. No migration: the rule spans two tables. Deletes follow the foreign keys: refused while any row refers to the environment or application. Components have no status and nothing refers to them, so they are deleted; environments and applications are disabled. The one cascade: deleting an application deletes its adaptive_baselines, which are computed from its telemetry. Database observability database_targets, probe_definitions, probe_assignments describe monitored databases and versioned probe policies for the future control-plane workflow. Targets belong to an environment; credential_ref is a reference only. Probe definitions are versioned and disabled rather than deleted (probe_assignments → probe_definitions is RESTRICT). Each row is one version, numbered from 1 per engine and name, and never changes except enabled, which the API sets on every version of a definition at once; a change to what runs is a new row, so an assignment pins the version it was approved with. Built-in rows (builtin) are the db-collector's SQL Server catalog, seeded by 0004 with no query_template, and read-only; a test fails when they and the catalog differ. A custom row's name starts with custom_, which no built-in's does, so a later seed never meets one; it names the built-in it overrides, the same in every version, takes its category, and carries a query_template checked as one read-only SELECT (a check constraint keeps the two kinds apart). timeout_ms is null for the collector's default. Managed through /api/v1/probe-definitions. A target's name, the target label of its metrics, is unique in its environment (0005). Managed through /api/v1/database-targets, which refuses to delete a target that has assignments: the foreign key would cascade to them. Every probe must be reviewed for non-blocking behavior before being assigned to a production target. There is deliberately no direct application → database_target relation. Today the collector ignores these tables and reads targets and probes from config/integrations.yaml; see database observability. Investigations and evidence investigations stores the question: application, derived environment, subject (user, ip, session, request), time window and filters. investigation_jobs tracks asynchronous execution; a partial unique index allows only one queued or running job per investigation. investigation_results stores bounded summaries, and evidence_links points to Grafana, Loki or Prometheus views. Raw evidence is never copied into PostgreSQL. Jobs, results and links cascade when an investigation is removed by a retention job. Alerting Alerting is application-owned. alert_policies stores policy intent, rendered into Prometheus rules (rendered_rule_ref). adaptive_baselines stores computed threshold parameters. notification_routes stores route intent and references to Alertmanager routes. alert_events records alert lifecycle and fingerprints from Alertmanager for history, reporting and investigation context; alert_policy_id is nullable because external or unmapped alerts may arrive. PostgreSQL never evaluates alerts. Reporting Reporting is application-owned. report_templates defines structure, report_schedules defines cadence, timezone and recipients, and report_runs records executions. report_schedule_id is null for on-demand runs, and requested_by is null for scheduled runs. artifact_uri is a pointer only. Environment-level reports are a later aggregation across applications. Relationship and delete rules Child → parent On delete Notes user_roles → users, roles restrict Composite primary key (user_id, role_id) applications → environments restrict Unique (environment_id, name) application_components → applications restrict Unique (application_id, name, component_type) telemetry_sources → applications restrict Index (application_id, source_type, status) normalization_rules → users (created_by) set null Unique (source_type, version); system rules have no creator database_targets → environments restrict Unique (environment_id, engine, host, port, lower(coalesce(database_name,''))) and (environment_id, name) probe_assignments → database_targets cascade Unique (database_target_id, probe_definition_id) probe_assignments → probe_definitions restrict Definitions unique by (engine, name, version); an assignment pins a version investigations → applications, environments, users restrict time_end \u003e= time_start investigation_jobs, investigation_results, evidence_links → investigations cascade One active job per investigation alert_policies, notification_routes → applications restrict Unique (application_id, name) adaptive_baselines → applications cascade Unique (application_id, signal_key, window, method) alert_events → applications restrict Unique (application_id, fingerprint, started_at) alert_events → alert_policies set null  report_schedules → applications, report_templates restrict Unique (application_id, report_template_id, cron_expression, timezone) report_runs → applications restrict  report_runs → report_schedules, users set null  Deferred topology Added only when SQL Server or host topology work needs explicit inventory. Assets describe where things run; keep them minimal so they don't turn into a CMDB. Deferred topology: environments contain assets, assets optionally host database targets, and asset_relationships link two assets Planned hooks when enabled: database_targets.asset_id (nullable, set null), applications.primary_asset_id, application_components.asset_id, unique assets(environment_id, name) and asset_relationships(source_asset_id, target_asset_id, relationship_type). Examples: prd-iis-01 (server, windows), sql-prod-01 (database_server, sqlserver), public-lb (load_balancer, nginx)."},{"t":"Database Observability Architecture","u":"architecture/database-observability.html","h":[{"t":"Scope","id":"scope"},{"t":"SQL Server signal coverage","id":"sql-server-signal-coverage"},{"t":"Safety rules","id":"safety-rules"},{"t":"Probe review checklist","id":"probe-review-checklist"},{"t":"Collector recovery and high availability (planned)","id":"collector-recovery-and-high-availability-planned"},{"t":"Custom collector and Alloy comparison (late roadmap)","id":"custom-collector-and-alloy-comparison-late-roadmap"},{"t":"Metadata model","id":"metadata-model"}],"x":"Database Observability Architecture Scope SQL Server is the first DB engine in scope. Oracle is the intended future monitored engine; PostgreSQL is Heartbeat's metadata store, not a monitoring target in the current scope. PostgreSQL can store target metadata, probe definitions, and probe assignments for future control-plane workflows. Active collector runtime desired state is not read from PostgreSQL for MVP. SQL Server signal coverage Target signal set for the MVP, compared with what the built-in probe catalog emits today. Metric names and labels are listed in the metrics reference. Signal Status Current probe / metric Wait statistics Collected waits → heartbeat_sqlserver_wait_seconds_total (counter) Blocking and locks Collected (blocked requests) blocking → heartbeat_sqlserver_blocked_requests Sessions and connections Collected sessions → heartbeat_sqlserver_sessions Memory pressure Collected memory_pressure → heartbeat_sqlserver_total_server_memory_bytes Database size / file size Collected storage → heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_size_bytes Throughput counters (batch requests, transactions) Collected throughput → heartbeat_sqlserver_batch_requests_total, heartbeat_sqlserver_transactions_total (counters) Instance availability Partial heartbeat_collector_target_up (collector reachability) CPU utilisation Collected (one-minute granularity; other processes on Windows only) cpu → heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_sql_process_ratio, heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_other_process_ratio Buffer/cache hit ratio, page life expectancy Collected buffer_cache → heartbeat_sqlserver_buffer_cache_hit_ratio, heartbeat_sqlserver_page_life_expectancy_seconds Physical reads/writes, I/O, IOPS, I/O latency Collected per database file file_io → reads, writes, bytes and I/O stall seconds per file, e.g. heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_reads_total (counters) Logical reads (page lookups) Planned — Query latency Planned — Rollbacks, user transactions Planned — Free space and growth trends Planned — TempDB pressure Planned — Error events Planned — Output expectations: stable metric names; labels for environment, target, database, instance and, where safe, application; dashboards for overview, waits/locks, sessions, storage and regressions. Only infra/helm/heartbeat/files/grafana/dashboards/sqlserver-overview.json exists so far. Safety rules Credentials are referenced by credential_ref only. The collector logs in with only VIEW SERVER STATE and VIEW ANY DEFINITION and warns when its login is sysadmin-equivalent (sysadmin or CONTROL SERVER) (login permissions). Production probes must be non-blocking: every probe passes the probe review checklist, and every collector batch runs with LOCK_TIMEOUT 1s and DEADLOCK_PRIORITY LOW (what the collector runs). Probe definitions are versioned and can be disabled instead of deleted. The built-in catalog is versioned with the code, and the API holds one read-only definition per catalog probe; custom definitions, which replace a built-in's query, are managed through /api/v1/probe-definitions by admins: each change is a new immutable version, and disabling one disables every version. Until probe assignments are rendered [4.5], a collector still runs what its probes list in integrations.yaml names. Runtime collector grouping, target activation, probe activation, and scaling live in config/integrations.yaml and Kubernetes delivery. Probe review checklist Apply it to every new or changed probe in the built-in catalog (catalog.go), to every query_template override in integrations.yaml, and to every version of a custom probe definition created through POST /api/v1/probe-definitions (or added with POST /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id}/versions), before it reaches a shared or production target. A reviewer other than the author signs it off; an override also needs the target's DBAs. A probe added to the built-in catalog also needs a migration that seeds its definition: a test fails until one does. Read-only and least privilege One read-only query: SELECT, optionally with CTEs. No DML, DDL, EXEC, DBCC, temporary tables, BEGIN TRAN or USE. A bare stored procedure name does not work either: every batch starts with the session settings, so it is no longer sent as a procedure call. Loading the configuration rejects a query_template that is not one read-only SELECT (rules), the API refuses to store a custom definition whose query fails the same check, and the built-in queries pass it in their tests; the check is a safety net, the read-only login is the control. Returns a bounded number of rows: well under the probe's max_rows (default 10000) on the largest target, or the probe fails every cycle. Needs no grant beyond VIEW SERVER STATE and VIEW ANY DEFINITION. make test-sqlserver runs every catalog probe as such a login; a view the login cannot see often returns no rows instead of an error, so check the probe returns data. Does not change session settings: no SET LOCK_TIMEOUT, SET DEADLOCK_PRIORITY, SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL, and no locking hints (HOLDLOCK, UPDLOCK, TABLOCK, XLOCK, NOLOCK). Non-blocking Reads server-level DMVs (sys.dm_os_*, sys.dm_exec_*) or server-scoped catalog views (sys.master_files, sys.databases), never user tables. DMVs read in-memory state and take no data locks; catalog views can briefly wait on metadata locks during DDL, which the 1-second LOCK_TIMEOUT bounds. No per-database iteration (sp_MSforeachdb, cursors, dynamic SQL), no cross-database metadata functions per row (OBJECT_NAME(id, db_id), OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME), and nothing that scans data (sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats, sys.dm_db_database_page_allocations). Joins to sys.dm_exec_sql_text or sys.dm_exec_query_plan are bounded (TOP) and justified; they are expensive on busy servers. Measured on a production-sized non-production server (SET STATISTICS TIME, IO ON): it finishes far below the probe timeout (half the scrape interval, at most 10s) and well below the 1s lock timeout when nothing blocks it. Bounded output Returns one row per bounded entity (wait type, counter, database file, status), never per session, request, query text, login or host, and at most one row per label set (the live test fails on duplicate series). Label columns come from a small, stable set and are trimmed (RTRIM on nchar columns such as counter_name). Metrics and tests Metric names follow Prometheus conventions with the unit in the name; cumulative values are exported as counters (TODO §2.4). Metric descriptors, the metrics reference, dashboards and rules are updated together (a Go test fails on references to metrics the catalog does not emit). make test and make test-sqlserver pass. Collector recovery and high availability (planned) Status: items 1-3 are implemented for a single collector replica (failure isolation, a hard deadline per probe so a query the driver cannot cancel never stalls collection, backoff, freshness metrics, per-probe error counters and durations, stale-series cleanup, readiness, reload rollback); see the DB collector README. Items 4-5 remain planned. The remaining items are planned improvements, not guarantees of the current runtime. The current SQL path is remote queries -\u003e custom collector's in-memory metrics -\u003e Prometheus scrape/storage -\u003e Grafana. There is no separate custom loader or durable collector-side sample queue. PostgreSQL and Redis do not buffer this metric stream. Implement recovery before adding replicas: Isolate probe and target failures. A failed query must not permanently stop unrelated collection. Retry transient failures with bounded exponential backoff and jitter, enforce query/concurrency budgets, and surface persistent credential/configuration failures without creating retry storms. Expose per-target/probe success, failures, last successful collection time, and freshness. Remove or expire stale series, including series belonging to removed targets. An HTTP endpoint being reachable is not proof that SQL collection is healthy. Make readiness reflect expected collector state. Define degraded behavior separately from process liveness: an unavailable database should not cause endless container restarts. Add deployment probes and recovery policies for actual process failures, and test reload failure/rollback behavior before claiming that the previous working runtime is preserved. Design target ownership and takeover across replicas. Evaluate active/standby ownership or sharding with reassignment; use leases plus fencing, or an equivalent mechanism, to limit duplicate SQL polling during partitions and takeover. Increasing replica count alone is not an HA design. Account for rolling updates, node placement, takeover delay, and aggregate database load. Define downstream resilience independently. Agree recovery-time and acceptable data-gap objectives, then choose storage availability and any durable buffering/replay mechanism needed to meet them. Specify retention, disk limits, backpressure, replay ordering, and duplicate handling. Buffering can preserve already-collected samples; it cannot reconstruct observations missed while collection was stopped. Validate with non-production target outages, slow queries, collector/process and node loss, network partitions, configuration reloads during failure, and Prometheus/downstream outages. Record recovery time, data gaps, stale or duplicate series, effects on healthy targets, and database load. Keep these reliability improvements in the core hardening phase; they do not depend on the later Alloy evaluation. Custom collector and Alloy comparison (late roadmap) The agreed plan is a parallel evaluation, not a decision to replace the custom collector. It is the last planned roadmap phase, after the core workflows, reliability work and Oracle integration are validated. Perform the experiment in a dedicated comparison branch created at that time; do not mix it with ordinary collector fixes or introduce Alloy into production as part of planning. Oracle work starts with a short, time-boxed check of what Alloy and established Oracle exporters cover. It is a desk check, not this comparison. It decides whether to build a custom Oracle collector, adopt Alloy for Oracle, or run this comparison before the Oracle phase, so that a custom collector is not built only to be replaced. Run the custom collector and Grafana Alloy against equivalent non-production SQL Server workloads. Isolate their metric identities or storage destinations so dashboards, recording rules, and alerts cannot accidentally double-count the same database. Budget the combined polling load; use controlled sequential runs as well when concurrent collection would distort performance measurements. Use the same workload, intervals, permissions, and comparable queries wherever possible. Compare: Signal coverage across waits, blocking, sessions, memory, storage, and throughput; units, counter/gauge semantics, labels, and cardinality. Custom query support, structured investigation evidence, and integration with Heartbeat's target/probe configuration and operator workflows. Database overhead, collector CPU/memory, connection limits, query timeouts, least privilege, credentials, and TLS behavior. Reload behavior, error isolation, retries, target ownership/takeover, freshness, and behavior during collector, node, network, and downstream outages. Buffering/replay limits, recovery time, gaps, duplicates, deployment complexity, upgrades, diagnostics, and ongoing maintenance cost. SQL Server and Oracle fit, assessed separately rather than assuming equivalent engine support. Record versions/configurations, reproducible tests, measurements, coverage gaps, and operational tradeoffs in a decision record on the comparison branch. Verify Alloy's capabilities at evaluation time. The outcome may be to retain the custom collector, use Alloy for suitable workloads, or keep both available for distinct needs. Supporting both requires a shared telemetry contract and explicit per-target/backend selection, ownership, and duplicate-prevention rules; it does not imply routinely polling every production target twice. Review the evidence before merging any selected integration or planning production adoption. Metadata model The planned control-plane tables (database_targets, probe_definitions, probe_assignments) and the deferred asset/topology extension are described in the data model."},{"t":"0001. PostgreSQL stores durable product metadata only","u":"architecture/decisions/0001-postgresql-metadata-only.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0001. PostgreSQL stores durable product metadata only Status: Accepted Date: 2026-06-04 (recorded 2026-09-29) Context Heartbeat handles high-volume telemetry (metrics, logs, session evidence) and a comparatively small amount of product state: inventory, policies, schedules, investigation requests and results. Putting both in one relational database would make PostgreSQL a bottleneck, duplicate data that Prometheus and Loki already store and query better, and spread \"alert truth\" across systems. Decision PostgreSQL is the system of record for product metadata only: environments, applications, components, telemetry sources, database targets and probe policies, users and roles, investigation requests and results, alert policy intent and alert history, report templates, schedules and runs. It does not store raw or derived metrics, logs, sessions or session identity mappings, integration config, audit streams, collector runtime state, or report binaries. It never evaluates alerts. Executable rules are rendered to Prometheus and routes to Alertmanager. Application-owned records derive their environment through applications.environment_id; there are no duplicate ownership paths. Consequences Telemetry scales independently in Prometheus/Loki; PostgreSQL stays small. Investigations recompute correlation from Loki/Prometheus at query time and store only bounded summaries and links. Some features need two systems (policy intent in PostgreSQL, evaluation in Prometheus), so rendering and reconciliation must be designed carefully. Tables for audit, integrations, collector fleets or assets can be added later if a real UI or compliance workflow needs them. See the data model."},{"t":"0002. Runtime integration and collector config come from YAML/Kubernetes","u":"architecture/decisions/0002-runtime-config-in-yaml.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0002. Runtime integration and collector config come from YAML/Kubernetes Status: Accepted Date: 2026-06-04 (recorded 2026-09-29) Context Collectors must be added, changed and removed without redeploying the platform. An earlier design stored collector instances and assignments in PostgreSQL, which conflicted with Kubernetes as the deployment's desired-state source and required the API to exist before any collection could run. Decision config/integrations.yaml (delivered as a Kubernetes ConfigMap in clusters) is the desired state for integration endpoints, Grafana URL templates, notification channels, credential references and collectors (targets and probes). Secrets are referenced, never embedded. Services load it through the shared config manager in internal/config: parse into typed structs and validate before applying anything; compute a config_version hash; publish immutable snapshots; never mutate active config in place; diff collectors by stable ID and start, update, drain or stop only what changed; fail closed: an invalid candidate keeps the previous config, and a failed apply is rolled back. Reload triggers: explicit SIGHUP and authenticated POST /admin/config/reload (deterministic, preferred in production), and optional polling for file changes (HEARTBEAT_CONFIG_WATCH_INTERVAL). ConfigMap volumes update through symlink swaps, so watchers must watch the parent directory and debounce. Runtime state is exposed through /metrics, /healthz, /readyz and logs, not stored in PostgreSQL. Consequences Collection works without the control plane, and config changes are reviewable in git. The database_targets, probe_definitions and probe_assignments tables exist for a future UI workflow but are not read by the collector. Reintroduce API-driven assignments only once that workflow exists, and keep desired state and observed state separate. How they will reach the collector: ADR 0014. Onboarding a database is a config change today; see the database targets guide. Credential rotation needed a restart while credentials came from environment variables. Since [2.5] the chart mounts the credentials Secret as files that the collector reads on every connection, so a new or rotated credential needs none (credential resolution)."},{"t":"0003. Deliver the platform with Helm on kind and production Kubernetes","u":"architecture/decisions/0003-helm-on-kind-and-production.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Proposed design (as planned; see Implementation for what changed)","id":"proposed-design-as-planned-see-implementation-for-what-changed"},{"t":"Consequences and risks","id":"consequences-and-risks"},{"t":"Acceptance criteria","id":"acceptance-criteria"},{"t":"Implementation (2026-10-03)","id":"implementation-2026-10-03"},{"t":"Open decisions","id":"open-decisions"}],"x":"0003. Deliver the platform with Helm on kind and production Kubernetes Status: Accepted. Implemented for kind and CI on 2026-10-03; production delivery follows ADR 0005. Date: 2026-09-29 Context Local development uses Docker Compose (infra/docker-compose.yml, 9 services). A small Kustomize bundle (infra/k8s/local) deploys only the DB collector, the gateway and PostgreSQL. Production is intended to run on cloud Kubernetes. Keeping Compose for development and a separate production definition means two deployment systems that drift, and Compose never exercises Kubernetes behavior such as pod replacement, readiness routing, projected ConfigMaps and rollouts. The collector hardening work depends on exactly that behavior. Decision Package Heartbeat with Helm and use the same versioned chart locally, in CI and in production, with environment-specific values. Use kind for the local and integration-CI cluster. Keep SQL Server in Docker as an external test target, outside the cluster. Adapt Make targets and CI as part of the migration, then retire the platform Compose definition once equivalent workflows pass. Do not complete the Kustomize bundle first and translate it again later. Principle: what Heartbeat deploys goes through Helm; what Heartbeat tests against (the SQL Server target, the disposable PostgreSQL schema fixture) stays as Docker fixtures. Keep the collector a singleton until explicit target ownership across replicas exists; never scale it by raising the replica count. Proposed design (as planned; see Implementation for what changed) One umbrella chart heartbeat owns Heartbeat workloads and content (collector, gateway, integrations config, Prometheus rules, Grafana dashboards). Third-party engines are pinned, condition-gated dependencies, using namespaced charts only (prometheus-community/prometheus with Alertmanager, grafana/grafana, Loki single binary, OpenTelemetry Collector), with no cluster-scoped CRDs. Production can disable any engine and point at shared services. The collector runs as a StatefulSet with one replica, which gives at-most-one semantics during node partitions. A headless Service with publishNotReadyAddresses lets Prometheus scrape it via dns_sd_configs even when it is unready. Values files embed complete integrations.yaml documents per environment, validated by the same schema and Go loader. Two local profiles: minimal (collector, Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana) and full (adds Loki, OTel Collector, gateway). PostgreSQL and migrations stay out of the deployed stack until the API exists. Grafana is stateless; dashboards come from source. Access uses kind extraPortMappings on the existing Compose ports instead of kubectl port-forward. The Prometheus alerting block and a Watchdog rule (external dead-man's switch) close the current Prometheus → Alertmanager gap. Consequences and risks Ranked by effect on the goal: Monitoring failure domain. If Heartbeat and its alert path run on the infrastructure being investigated, a failure there removes the evidence too. Keep an external availability check or notification path; a separate namespace is not a separate failure domain. Collector ownership and database load. Kubernetes can overlap or replace pods, but it does not know who owns a SQL target. Control update overlap and test query budgets. Durability. PVCs are not backups. Metadata and telemetry need separate retention and restore policies, tested independently of pod startup. Environment fidelity. kind validates objects and application behavior, not cloud storage, networking or identity. Test production values, ingress, TLS and SQL connectivity in the target environment. Release boundaries. A Helm rollback does not reverse schema changes or restore telemetry. Pin dependencies; keep app, schema and stateful upgrades separate. Development cost. A local cluster adds CPU/RAM and concepts. Keep unit tests fast, automate the loop, and measure the cost. Acceptance criteria The migration is done when these pass with retained evidence: A fresh cluster loads both images on the node's actual CPU architecture and every workload becomes ready. The same chart renders for kind, CI and production values without separately maintained production templates. A pod reaches the Docker SQL target (DNS, TCP, authentication, TLS) without widening database exposure. A code change produces a new running image; a stale :local tag cannot mask it. With one SQL target configured, heartbeat_collector_target_up and probe metrics are fresh in Prometheus and Grafana shows the target. A valid ConfigMap update changes the active config version. An invalid one is rejected visibly while collection continues. A partial reload failure rolls back or reports divergence and unreadiness. An unavailable SQL target shows as failed, healthy targets continue, and the collector does not restart just because a database is down. A singleton collector update drains the old pod before the new one polls; the collection gap and SQL-side concurrency are measured. A stateful restart keeps data designated as persistent; concurrent CI runs are isolated and cannot delete developer resources. The daily development loop meets an agreed time and resource budget. Implementation (2026-10-03) The chart is infra/helm/heartbeat, values are in infra/helm/values, and the workflow is make kind-* (guide). The proposed design was followed, with these differences: Object names are fixed (db-collector, prometheus, alertmanager, loki, otel-collector, ...) and one release runs per namespace, so in-cluster addresses match the Compose-era names that integrations.yaml and the engine configs already used. Grafana and Loki come from the grafana-community chart repository, where Grafana moved those charts. Every engine chart pins the same engine version Compose ran. The integrations schema is embedded in the chart's values.schema.json, so Helm rejects invalid config before applying anything; a Go test keeps the copy in sync and loads each rendered profile with the Go loader. Loki has no host port: its chart cannot set a NodePort. It is reached through Grafana or kubectl port-forward. PostgreSQL, Redis and the platform Compose stack were removed. Compose remains only as the disposable PostgreSQL test fixture. Helm 4 upgrades with server-side apply, so a hand edit to a chart-owned field makes the next upgrade conflict until the chart's version is re-applied. Acceptance criteria and how they are checked (make chart-check and make kind-e2e, both in CI). Measurements are from one kind run on an AMD64 host; the e2e script prints them on every run. Criterion Check Result Fresh cluster loads both images on the node's architecture; all workloads ready e2e; kind.sh refuses an architecture mismatch All 7 workloads ready; full deploy about 50s after images load One chart renders for kind, CI and production values chart-check over 5 profiles, Helm 4 and Helm 3 Pass A pod reaches the Docker SQL target (DNS, TCP, auth, TLS) without widening exposure e2e; SQL Server on the kind network, no host port in CI, loopback only locally Target up A code change produces a new running image Content-derived tags, imagePullPolicy: Never; e2e compares the running image to the built tag Pass Target metrics fresh in Prometheus; Grafana shows the target e2e queries Prometheus and Grafana's datasource proxy Pass Valid ConfigMap update changes the config version e2e, same pod About 65s (kubelet sync dominates) Invalid ConfigMap update rejected visibly while collection continues e2e checks last_reload_err, version and freshness Reported after about 90s; collection uninterrupted Partial reload failure rolls back or reports divergence Unit tests in the collector (not repeated on kind) Pass Unavailable target shows failed; others continue; no restart e2e stops the SQL container; a second, unresolvable target stays failed Down after about 20s, recovered about 60s after restart, restartCount 0 Singleton update drains the old pod first; gap and SQL concurrency measured e2e samples running pods and SQL Server sessions every second during a rollout Never more than 1 collector or 2 sessions; collection gap 12s and 31s in two runs at a 20s scrape interval Stateful restart keeps persistent data e2e replaces the Prometheus and Alertmanager pods TSDB and silences kept Concurrent CI runs isolated, cannot delete developer resources e2e uses its own cluster name, kubeconfig and container names, and deletes only those By construction Daily loop meets an agreed budget Budget agreed 2026-10-28: the full profile holds at most 2.5 GiB of RAM in the kind node, the SQL Server sandbox and PatientZero not included. CI's kind end-to-end job times out after 25 minutes Full profile with the API, PostgreSQL and Valkey 1.85 GiB at its peak under make patientzero-load LOAD=steady (2026-11-02, method below); earlier, before them, about 2.0 GiB RAM in the kind node, measured idle, and 1.8 GiB after 12 hours up (SQL Server sandbox +0.9 GiB at first, 2.2 GiB after 12 hours: its 2 GiB cap limits SQL Server's own memory, not the container's); kind end-to-end 10–12 minutes in CI on 21–22 October, up to 18.6 on 28 October; redeploy under a minute; minimal profile for smaller machines Update 2026-10-28: the local loop's budget is agreed (last row above). Memory is the peak docker stats usage of the kind node container (heartbeat-control-plane for the default KIND_CLUSTER) over at least 30 minutes of make patientzero-load after a deploy, less the patientzero namespace: PatientZero and its k6 Job (up to 512 MiB) run in the same node but are not part of the profile, and docker exec heartbeat-control-plane crictl stats --label io.kubernetes.pod.namespace=patientzero shows their share. Load grows Prometheus's head block and Loki's ingesters until they flush, so an idle reading understates it; the 2.0 GiB above was measured idle. Yet every run by this method, each on a restarted node, has come in below it (1.44 and 1.79 GiB for the telemetry change, 1.71 and 1.85 GiB below); the idle reading's conditions were not recorded, so it stays as history, not as a baseline. The 4.1 chart change that adds the API, PostgreSQL and Valkey was measured this way on 2026-11-02 (chart at 8dd7efe): the full profile with and without them, one after the other on the same node, restarted before each run, with docker stats every 10 seconds under LOAD=steady. With them the peak over the 30-minute run was 1,892 MiB (1.85 GiB), 0.65 GiB under the budget. Without them the sampler stopped after 25 minutes, short of the method's 30 (the host paused: k6's 30-minute run took 40), so the two are compared over their first 25 minutes: 1,867 against 1,756 MiB at the peak, 111 MiB more, and 1,757 against 1,670 MiB on average, 87 MiB more. Their own containers held 47 MiB at the peak (API 17, PostgreSQL 18, Valkey 12 MiB, from crictl stats); the rest is outside their containers and was not broken down. The budget gives up a second local cluster. A sandbox that has run for hours holds about 2.2 GiB, so the budget plus the sandbox is 4.7 GiB, which leaves the rest of this project's Docker host (7.6 GiB in docker info) for image builds. A second cluster with its own fresh SQL Server, such as make kind-e2e, adds about 3.4 GiB at the budget: 8.1 GiB in all, more than the host has. Today's profile, 1.85 GiB at its peak, still fits, with about 0.8 GiB to spare (two profiles, the sandbox's 2.2 GiB and the second cluster's fresh SQL Server's 0.9 GiB). Run the second cluster's checks in CI, or docker restart heartbeat-sqlserver-dev first, which frees the sandbox's memory and keeps its databases. On 2026-10-22, before SQL Server's memory was capped, a load flood and a second cluster each got it killed. Time is bounded by CI, not by this budget: the kind end-to-end job fails after 25 minutes. It grew with its checks, not its pods, so when it nears the timeout the levers are the checks: shorter wait_for windows, waits run in parallel, or the job split in two. A change that would exceed the budget is trimmed first: smaller requests, or the component off in the kind profile. Otherwise it asks for a new budget instead of growing past this one. Not covered by kind (risk 4): cloud storage classes, ingress and TLS, IAM and External Secrets, ECR pulls, and SQL connectivity from the EKS VPC. Open decisions Production Kubernetes distribution and cloud; image and chart registry; deploy mechanism (GitOps or CI); where production values live; dead-man's-switch service; secrets backend; Grafana SSO provider. These are tracked in TODO.md. Update 2026-09-30: decided in ADR 0005 (AWS EKS, Argo CD, ECR, AWS Secrets Manager, alert channels, Grafana access)."},{"t":"0004. Core technology choices","u":"architecture/decisions/0004-core-technology-choices.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0004. Core technology choices Status: Accepted Date: 2026-05-30 (recorded 2026-09-29) Context Heartbeat is built for use inside a large company. The constraints: few runtimes and moving parts, free for enterprise use, Grafana-native, and compatible with OpenTelemetry. It complements existing APMs (Dynatrace, Datadog) rather than replacing them. Decision Concern Choice Why Alternatives deferred API and collectors Go One backend language: static binaries, strong concurrency for polling, good telemetry libraries NestJS (more runtime layers) Web UI TypeScript + React Practical for an internal operator UI; no SSR unless needed — Metadata PostgreSQL Reliable, free, well understood (ADR 0001) — Metrics Prometheus Smallest operational surface; Grafana-native Mimir only if retention, HA, multi-tenancy or cross-region scale outgrow Prometheus; VictoriaMetrics, Thanos Logs Loki Grafana-native, far fewer moving parts OpenSearch/Elastic only if a validated requirement needs document search Loki cannot serve without harmful cardinality Dashboards Grafana, provisioned as code Existing operator familiarity; no custom dashboard engine — Alert routing Alertmanager Grouping, dedupe, silencing, delivery — Telemetry ingress OpenTelemetry Collector Standard OTLP; configuration before custom code — Async work Redis Alerting, reporting and investigations are asynchronous enough to justify a queue/cache — Report artifacts S3-compatible object storage, only if persistence is required Otherwise generate on demand — Deployment Kubernetes with Helm (ADR 0003) Reproducible cloud delivery — Auth Local RBAC first Minimal MVP OIDC/SSO when corporate requirements demand it Collection runs from a separate monitoring environment. No agent or collector is installed on monitored database hosts. Consequences MVP stack: Grafana + Prometheus + Loki + PostgreSQL + Redis. Scale-out (Mimir) and richer search (OpenSearch) are explicit later decisions, not defaults. These engines are the bundled platform, not a dependency of Heartbeat's services: ADR 0011 keeps the platform replaceable, and ADR 0019 keeps the Grafana stack after the review against the organization's ELK stack. Redis runs as Valkey, and how PostgreSQL and Redis are deployed is ADR 0015. A late-roadmap evaluation compares the custom SQL Server collector with Grafana Alloy; see database observability."},{"t":"0005. Production delivery and operations defaults","u":"architecture/decisions/0005-production-delivery-and-operations-defaults.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0005. Production delivery and operations defaults Status: Accepted. Chart side implemented 2026-10-03 (exact version pins, chart rules below, Watchdog route, production.example.yaml); the production infrastructure, config repository and Argo CD are not built yet. The kind profile has no Discord receiver yet. Date: 2026-09-30 Context ADR 0003 chose Helm on kind and production Kubernetes but left production decisions open. Production will run on AWS. There is no production cluster yet and no GitOps tooling. The notification requirements and the constraints around them: Production notifications must reach Microsoft Teams, Slack and WhatsApp. Microsoft disabled the Office 365 connector webhooks for Teams in May 2026; Power Automate Workflows webhooks replace them. Alertmanager has no native WhatsApp receiver. WhatsApp Business allows business-initiated messages only through pre-approved templates. Amazon EKS supports Kubernetes up to 1.36 (upstream latest is 1.37). Decision Concern Choice Why Platform Amazon EKS Production on AWS. Kubernetes version Newest minor EKS supports (1.36 today). kind pins the same minor (kindest/node:v1.36.4@sha256:099e0493…cbfaed) and moves only when EKS does Local and CI test the API version production runs. Deploy mechanism Argo CD (GitOps) from the first production deploy; no interim CI push pipeline Production does not exist yet, so a push pipeline would be thrown away. GitOps keeps cluster credentials out of CI, makes every change a reviewed commit, reverts manual drift, and rolls back via git revert. Desired state A separate config repository (heartbeat-deploy) with Argo CD Applications and per-environment values; this repo keeps the chart, defaults and production.example.yaml Argo CD best practice: clean audit trail, separate write access for production, no CI commit loops. Registry Amazon ECR for images (multi-arch, referenced by digest) and the chart (OCI). GitHub Actions pushes through GitHub OIDC → IAM role; no long-lived AWS keys EKS nodes pull with IAM (no pull secrets), in-region, with image scanning. Secrets AWS Secrets Manager, synced into Kubernetes Secrets by External Secrets Operator using EKS Pod Identity. ESO also refreshes Argo CD's short-lived ECR credentials Managed, IAM-scoped, audited in CloudTrail, supports rotation. The chart only consumes existingSecret names, so the backend stays swappable. Versions Latest stable that production supports, pinned exactly (tools, CI runner image, node image by digest, Chart.lock, image digests in production); Renovate proposes updates, kind CI must pass Current without floating tags; upgrades are reviewed, one change at a time. Alert notifications Discord now; Slack (slack_configs) and Teams (msteamsv2_configs) in production; webhook URLs from mounted Secrets Native Alertmanager receivers; the legacy msteams_configs depends on the retired connectors. WhatsApp Production, critical severity only: Alertmanager sns_configs → Amazon SNS topic → small Lambda → AWS End User Messaging Social SendWhatsAppMessage with an approved utility template No custom code in the cluster; AWS-native IAM and audit; SNS can fan out to more channels later. Critical-only because messages cost money, reach personal phones and are rate-limited by Meta. Dead-man's switch Alertmanager Watchdog → healthchecks.io, which notifies the chat channels Chat receivers cannot detect silence; the watcher must be outside Heartbeat's failure domain (ADR 0003, risk 1), and outside AWS too. Grafana access Admin login. kind: admin/admin, loopback only. Production: generated admin password from Secrets Manager, anonymous access off, TLS at ingress Matches ADR 0004's local-auth-first choice; SSO is a later values change. Vault was considered for secrets and not chosen. It is a system to operate (HA storage, unsealing, upgrades) or a paid HCP service, and its license changed in 2023 (OpenBao is the open fork). It earns its cost for multi-cloud or dynamic secrets. Heartbeat is AWS-only, and the monitored databases belong to other teams, so dynamic SQL Server logins are unlikely to be allowed. Chart rules that follow from Argo CD (it renders with helm template and applies the result itself): Deterministic rendering: no randAlphaNum, genCA or lookup. Secrets are created outside the chart. No reliance on Helm release state (.Release.Revision, IsInstall, IsUpgrade), and no Helm hooks unless Argo's hook mapping is acceptable. The chart does not create its namespace. It must render with Helm 3 as well as Helm 4. Consequences helm list shows nothing in production; history and rollback live in Git and Argo CD. The team has to learn Argo CD. An optional local rehearsal (Argo CD in kind) precedes the first production deploy. AWS-specific pieces (ECR, Secrets Manager, SNS, ingress, storage) live in production values and the config repo. kind keeps local equivalents (loaded images, generated Secrets, Discord only), so the local loop needs no AWS account. Production defaults, adjustable during the production track: EKS Auto Mode (AWS manages nodes, EBS CSI, load balancing and VPC CNI with NetworkPolicy); infrastructure as code with OpenTofu and the community EKS module; internal ALB + ACM certificate for Grafana; gp3 EBS for Prometheus; S3 for Loki; RDS for PostgreSQL once the API needs it. WhatsApp needs, before it can go live: a Meta WhatsApp Business Account with a verified business and phone number, linked in AWS End User Messaging Social; an approved alert template; recipient opt-in. Phone numbers are personal data. WhatsApp gives no acknowledgement or escalation. If on-call escalation becomes a requirement, that calls for a paging tool, which would be a separate decision. Still open: where the monitored SQL Servers live relative to the EKS VPC (on-premises via VPN/Direct Connect, EC2 or RDS). It determines network design and is needed before the first production deploy."},{"t":"0006. Trunk-based development and tagged releases","u":"architecture/decisions/0006-trunk-based-development-and-tagged-releases.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0006. Trunk-based development and tagged releases Status: Accepted. In effect from 2026-10-05, after phase 1 merged to master. Release workflows built; v0.1.0 was tagged and published by hand on 2026-10-12, and the release app and tag-creation ruleset follow at the end of phase 2 (TODO 0.5). The required checks are replaced by ci-ok (ADR 0007). Tag protection covers all tags, not only v*, so tags with other names cannot be moved either, and is split into two rulesets so the release app's bypass covers only tag creation. Date: 2026-10-05 Context Phase 1 (DB collector) was delivered on a long-lived phase branch, db-collectors. Each TODO item had its own feature/* branch and pull request into that branch, often worked by agents in parallel, and one pull request merged the whole phase into master (#16). That worked for one phase at a time but does not scale: Several phases running at once on separate phase branches drift apart. Shared files (TODO.md, the roadmap, the Helm chart, CI, contracts) conflict at the end, and work built on a stale master has to be redone. The phase branch fell behind master and had to be caught up by hand. Once master feeds production, merging a whole phase means one large deploy and a rollback that undoes the whole phase. Nothing was versioned. The chart said 0.1.0, images were tagged with a content hash for kind, and production (ADR 0005) will run images pinned by digest, but no rule said which commit becomes a release or where its images live. The repository builds two deployable images today, db-collector and otel-gateway (later also the API, web UI and workers), plus one Helm chart that deploys them together. Third-party engines (Prometheus, Grafana, Loki, Alertmanager, OpenTelemetry Collector) come from their upstream registries, pinned by the chart. Decision Concern Choice Why Branching Trunk-based development. Short-lived feature/* branches from master, one per TODO item, merged back by pull request. No phase or integration branches Work is integrated continuously, not at the end of a phase; parallel work across phases is limited only by real dependencies. Merging Merge commits only; head branches are deleted on merge; master requires a pull request and passing CI (test, Helm chart, kind end-to-end) Merge commits keep each branch's commits intact, which the changelog reads. Unfinished work Merged but not reachable: not wired in yet, or behind a configuration flag. Schema changes follow expand, then contract master stays releasable at every commit; ADR 0003 (risk 5) notes that a rollback does not reverse schema changes. Versions One Semantic Versioning version for the repository. A release versions every artifact together: both images and the chart are 0.2.0 in release v0.2.0, even if only one changed. 0.y.z until the first production release Heartbeat ships as one chart that deploys all its images, so one number answers \"what is running\". Per-component versions suit libraries released independently, and would need a version matrix between chart and images. Releases A vX.Y.Z tag on master creates a release. release.yml checks that the tag is on master, that the chart version matches, and that the required CI checks passed on that commit (waiting for them if they are still running), then builds and publishes A release is an explicit point on master that passed the same checks as every pull request; tags themselves cannot require status checks. Changelog CHANGELOG.md, generated from Conventional Commits by Google's release-please, with Keep a Changelog section names (Added, Changed, Fixed). release-please keeps an open release pull request with the next version, CHANGELOG.md, version.txt and the chart version; merging it creates the tag and the GitHub release No hand-edited section for parallel branches to conflict on; tags come from a reviewed pull request, which fits a protected master. Release identity release-please runs with a GitHub App token (RELEASE_APP_CLIENT_ID, RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY) Pull requests and tags created with the default GITHUB_TOKEN start no workflows: CI would never run on the release pull request, and the tag would not start the release. An app, unlike a personal token, is not tied to one person's account. Artifacts Multi-arch (linux/amd64, linux/arm64) images heartbeat/\u003cservice\u003e:\u003cversion\u003e with SBOM and provenance attestations, and the chart as an OCI artifact charts/heartbeat. A published version is never rebuilt or overwritten A version names exactly one build; deployments pin the digest anyway (ADR 0005). Registry GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io/gdpais/...) is the release registry. Production pulls from Amazon ECR (ADR 0005), which receives the same images when production delivery [18] is built The repository and its artifacts are public; GHCR sits next to the code and the releases, needs no cloud account, and is pullable from kind, which makes the Argo CD rehearsal on kind [18] realistic. Copying an image keeps its digest, so both registries serve the identical build. Pre-releases Every 0.y.z release is marked Pre-release on GitHub (release-please prerelease), so none is shown as Latest. The setting is removed for 1.0.0, the first production release Heartbeat is a prototype until then; releasing early still tests the release path and gives real artifacts for the Argo CD rehearsal. Moving tags None: no latest or stable Git tags or image tags. GitHub marks the newest release as Latest by itself Moving tags make \"what is deployed\" ambiguous, which digest pinning (ADR 0005) exists to prevent. Promotion between environments is what says a release is stable. Tag protection Two tag rulesets on all tags: one forbids updates and deletions with no bypass; the other forbids creation, with the release app as its only bypass A release cannot be moved or removed; a bad release is fixed by the next patch release. A bypass covers a whole ruleset, so a single ruleset would also let the app move and delete tags. Promotion Argo CD deploys from heartbeat-deploy (ADR 0005). Promoting a release is a commit there that sets the image digests for an environment: dev, then staging, then production Every deploy is a reviewed commit that names an exact build; rollback is git revert. Process Contribution rules live in CONTRIBUTING.md One home for how to branch, commit, test, open pull requests and release, for people and agents. The first release, v0.1.0, is the phase 1 merge commit (64ac1b6): the manifest starts at 0.1.0, release-please counts commits after it, and the tag is created once by hand with the release workflow run manually. Considered and not chosen: Phase branches for every phase. Kept master at \"last finished phase\", but a finished phase is now marked by a release instead, without the drift. GitFlow (develop, release/*, hotfix/*). Built for shipping several supported versions; Heartbeat runs one deployment. A hand-written changelog. Every parallel branch would edit the same section and conflict. Squash merging. Rewrites each branch into one new commit, which breaks any long-lived branch that is merged again and hides the commits the changelog reads. Publishing nothing until ECR exists. Releases would carry no artifacts to rehearse with, and the release workflow would go untested until production. Status checks on tags. A tag has no pull request to check; the release workflow checks the tagged commit's CI instead. Consequences Every commit on master must pass CI and be safe to release. Large features land in several small pull requests, the unfinished parts switched off. Commit messages are release input: a feat commit bumps the minor version, fix the patch, and ! or a BREAKING CHANGE: footer marks a breaking change, which bumps the major version (the minor while 0.y.z, through bump-minor-pre-major). Other types (docs, test, ci, …) alone do not produce a release, and commits that are not Conventional Commits do not appear in the changelog. The chart's version and appVersion follow the release; the chart is no longer versioned on its own. The release app is a credential to look after: its private key is a repository secret, and the app is installed on this repository only. GHCR packages can start private when first published; each is made public once, in its package settings, so kind and Argo CD can pull without credentials. ECR publishing in production delivery [18] adds a registry to the same workflow (GitHub OIDC to AWS); it is not a second build pipeline. Documentation still publishes from master on every push, so docs can be ahead of the latest release. The db-collectors branch, its ruleset and its CI trigger are retired."},{"t":"0007. Change-based CI with one required check","u":"architecture/decisions/0007-change-based-ci-with-one-required-check.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0007. Change-based CI with one required check Status: Accepted. In effect from 2026-10-10: master requires ci-ok. Date: 2026-10-10. Amended 2026-10-26: the release pull request also runs every PatientZero scenario (scenarios.yml, about two and a half hours), chosen by its branch rather than its paths, and ci-ok includes it; the same workflow runs nightly on master and by hand. Ordinary pull requests keep their change-based jobs. Amended 2026-11-01: test runs the Go tests once, with the race detector, not once with and once without it. Context CI ran every job on every pull request: Go tests and checks (about 3 minutes, 80 seconds of it the SQL Server probe tests), the chart checks (40 seconds) and the acceptance checks on a temporary kind cluster (about 9 minutes). A pull request that changed only docs or TODO.md waited about 10 minutes for checks its change could not affect. The next phases add services (OTel gateway, API, web UI), each with its own slower tests, so running everything on every change gets more expensive. ADR 0006 made test, Helm chart and kind end-to-end required checks on master. Skipping jobs interacts badly with required checks: A workflow filtered out by paths: never starts, so its checks are never reported, and a pull request that requires them waits forever. GitHub counts a skipped job as a passed check. A required job skipped by mistake, by a wrong condition or because a job it depends on failed, lets an untested change merge. Decision Concern Choice Why Workflow One workflow, .github/workflows/ci.yml (was db-collector-ci.yml), started by every pull request, every push to master and by hand. No paths: filters on its triggers Every check is always reported; the old name no longer matched what it tests. What always runs Go tests with the race detector and vet, Prometheus rules, the docs site, the PostgreSQL integration tests (test, about 2 minutes) They are cheap and catch breakage in shared code that a path rule might miss. What runs on change A changes job lists the paths the pull request changes and scripts/ci-changes.sh turns them into flags: SQL Server probe tests (db_collector), chart checks (chart), kind acceptance checks (kind_e2e). Each slow job runs only when its flag is true The slow jobs are where the time goes. Unknown paths Any path the script does not list (go.mod, internal/, the config schema, the Makefile, workflows, new directories) runs every job A missing rule costs minutes, not an untested merge. Full runs Pushes to master and manual runs (Actions → ci → Run workflow) run every job Every commit a release can tag passed the full suite; a rule that skipped too much is caught before a release. A manual run on a branch is the way to force a full run on its pull request. Required check ci-ok only. It always runs and scripts/ci-gate.sh fails it unless every job did what the flags asked: the always-run jobs succeeded, each flagged job succeeded, and each unflagged job was skipped. It also fails if a job is missing from its list One check that cannot pass by being skipped, and that need not change when jobs are added. Release verification scripts/release-verify.sh waits for ci-ok on the tagged commit; commits from before ci.yml keep the three checks they ran The check name changed; old tags such as v0.1.0 stay releasable. Detection tool A shell script over git diff --name-only --no-renames of the pull request's merge commit, tested by tests/ci_scripts_test.go No third-party action to pin and trust; testable locally; --no-renames counts a file moved out of a component as a change to it. Considered and not chosen: A workflow per component with paths: filters. The usual first attempt, and the one that leaves required checks waiting forever. Requiring every job. A skipped job reports success, so it adds nothing over ci-ok and has to be edited for every new job. dorny/paths-filter. Works well, but it is a third-party action with repository access, and the rules would live in YAML with no test. Precise detection from go list -deps. More exact for Go, but the services share most code today, so it would rarely skip more. Consequences A docs-only or TODO.md-only pull request takes about 2 minutes instead of about 10. A change to internal/, go.mod or the config schema still runs everything, which is most of phase 1's history. Adding a job: give it needs: changes and an if: on a flag, add the flag to scripts/ci-changes.sh and its test, and add the job to ci-ok's needs and arguments. The gate fails until all three agree. When a new directory appears it runs everything until a rule is added for it. A new push to a pull request cancels its older run; runs on master are never cancelled. The master ruleset must list ci-ok as its required check, a manual change in the repository settings."},{"t":"0008. Extract OutSystems 11 logs from the logging database","u":"architecture/decisions/0008-outsystems-11-log-extraction.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0008. Extract OutSystems 11 logs from the logging database Status: Accepted. Extraction, the eight log-type queries, the watermark volume, the Loki path, derived metrics and dashboards are built. The survey of a real logging database ran on 2026-10-19: the fixtures hold its DDL; the review of each query against it is pending (TODO 3.3). Date: 2026-10-10. Amended 2026-10-26: the recount (below) shows the rows committed past the lag, which the log delay metric could not. Context The first OutSystems deployment Heartbeat must observe is OutSystems 11 self-managed (Traditional and Reactive apps). Unlike OutSystems 11 on OutSystems Cloud (log streaming add-on) and ODC (App Analytics Stream), it has no native telemetry export: the platform writes its logs to the oslog_* tables of its logging database, a SQL Server database. The SRE team already has queries that read those tables well. The phase 2 plan assumed a push path: something sends JSON events to the OTel gateway, which normalizes them. Nothing in a self-managed installation sends those events, so that path does not apply. Heartbeat needs to poll the logging database, under the same rules as the SQL Server collector: no agents on database hosts, a least-privilege login, bounded queries that never block the platform. The db-collector already has everything such a poller needs except the log handling itself: SQL Server connections with pooling, credential references, login checks and session settings (LOCK_TIMEOUT, DEADLOCK_PRIORITY LOW), a hard deadline per query, per-target failure isolation and backoff, hot reload, readiness and self-metrics. Decision Concern Choice Why Source The oslog_* tables of the OutSystems 11 logging database, read with a login that has SELECT on them and nothing else The only complete log source in a self-managed installation; no agent on the platform servers. Where the extractor runs A new collector kind, outsystems_logs, in the db-collector Reuses the SQL Server connector, safety settings, reload, backoff and readiness instead of copying them into another service. The logging database is a database; the collector already owns how Heartbeat reads databases. Where the queries live One .sql file per log type in services/db-collector/internal/probes/outsystems/queries/, embedded in the binary. The file name is the log type (error.sql → error) Queries are code: reviewed with the probe review checklist, tested in CI against SQL Server and versioned with the release. Not in Prometheus or OTel Collector config, which never talk to SQL Server. Choosing log types probes: in the collector's YAML lists the log types to extract, as for the sqlserver kind. A query_template override per environment is allowed under the same contract and review Same configuration model and hot reload as the SQL Server probes (ADR 0002). Query contract Parameters @from (exclusive), @to (inclusive) and @limit; at most @limit rows ordered by an Instant column. Every other column becomes a log attribute under its OutSystems name, or under one standard name where the views disagree (entry point, operation, server, session, caller address, duration unit); Message becomes the log body; an optional Severity column sets the severity. Details in the queries README OutSystems field names stay queryable where they are consistent, so SRE habits carry over; one name per concept lets the derived metrics, the request timeline and Loki searches work across log types. The query author, who knows the tables, controls joins, filters and severity. Incremental reads A watermark per target and log type: read Instant in (watermark, now − lag], advance only after the batch is delivered. When a batch is full, rows sharing its last Instant are left for the next cycle. The watermark is stored in a file on the collector's volume; without one, start at now − initial_lookback No gaps from rows the log service commits late (lag), none at batch boundaries, and a restart resumes where it stopped. Weekly rotation Each query reads the current week's view (oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e) and the previous week's (oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e_Previous) with UNION ALL. The platform keeps ten weekly cycle tables per log type and switches the views every Friday at 23:45 Rows logged just before the switch are committed into the old table after it; reading only the current view would lose them every Friday, and could never look back past the switch. Lag per log type lag is set per collector and can be overridden per log type (probes[].lag) A screen or cyclic_job row carries the start of the operation as Instant but is written when it ends, so its lag must cover the longest screen request or timer; the other log types keep the short default. Rows committed past the lag (2026-10-26) Once a minute, each log type counts again, per minute of Instant, the rows its own query returns in the recount_window (default 10 minutes) behind its watermark, and compares them with the rows it delivered there. More rows in the database are late rows, counted in heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total with an alert; fewer are rows read twice, heartbeat_outsystems_duplicate_rows_total. Each minute's difference is counted once. Nothing is read again or sent The extractor never reads behind its watermark, so a late row was lost silently: only the derived request rate dipped. Counting by minute finds each late row without a row identity, which the log tables do not have; wrapping the log type's own query counts exactly what extraction reads, overrides included, and SQL Server answers it from the Instant index (a few hundred logical reads for 100,000 rows). Sending the late rows too would need Loki to take rows out of order and a way to tell them from rows already sent; counting first shows whether it is needed. Delivery OTLP/HTTP logs to the OTel Collector (opentelemetry.endpoint), then Loki. At least once: a failed export is retried from the same watermark, so a retried batch can appear twice in Loki Keeps Loki behind the Collector like every other log source; the Collector stays the place for routing and processing. Loki labels Resource attributes service.name (the application), deployment.environment.name and outsystems.log_type. User, session and request IDs, client IP, module, screen and the rest stay structured metadata Few streams per application; high-cardinality fields stay filterable without becoming labels. Derived metrics Computed by the extractor from the rows it reads and exposed on the collector's /metrics, scraped by Prometheus. The catalog is in application observability Exact counts and durations from every row, testable with fixtures, and the same Prometheus-scraped model as the SQL Server metrics. Traces None in this decision. Synthesizing request traces from Request_Key is designed in application observability and needs its own decision (storage) First prove the identifiers are reliable in real logs. Considered and not chosen: The OTel Collector's sqlquery receiver. No custom code, but it tracks progress by one column only, so it either loses rows that share an Instant at a batch boundary or rereads them; it has no lag window for late commits, no hard deadline or LOCK_TIMEOUT guarantee, and none of the collector's readiness and backoff. The OTel gateway. It would need its own copy of the SQL Server connector, safety settings, reload and readiness. A separate extractor service. The same copy, plus another image and deployment, for no isolation the per-collector failure isolation does not already give. Queries in integrations.yaml only. Editable without a release, but unreviewed SQL against a production database, with no CI test. The per-environment query_template override covers the exceptions. Consequences The db-collector is no longer stateless for this kind: it keeps a watermark per target and log type, so its StatefulSet gets a small persistent volume. The sqlserver kind stays stateless. The db-collector now pushes logs over OTLP as well as serving metrics, so it needs network access to the OTel Collector (NetworkPolicy egress). The OTel gateway takes no part in OutSystems 11 ingest. Its JSON event endpoint and alert sink are superseded; the service was then removed (ADR 0009). CI needs a SQL Server fixture per log type with the cycle tables and the two views the query reads, built from the real DDL, and rows copied from the logging database on both sides of a rotation. OutSystems 11 on OutSystems Cloud and ODC, when needed, send OTLP straight to the OTel Collector; the attribute names they use are mapped there, not in the extractor. Instant is in the platform servers' own time zone: OutSystems Cloud runs UTC, a self-managed installation whatever its front-ends and database share (the platform requires them to agree). The time zone is a per-collector setting (default UTC), confirmed per installation against the database. In a zone with daylight saving time, rows logged in the hour that repeats when clocks go back can be missed; UTC avoids it. Rows the platform's log service commits later than the lag after their Instant are missed, and the recount counts them (OutSystemsLateLogRows fires) while they are within its window; rows committed later still go uncounted. For screen and cyclic_job the lag also delays every row by the longest operation it has to cover. The log delay metric (heartbeat_outsystems_log_delay_seconds) measures the newest row read, which is never newer than the lag: it shows how fresh extraction is, not how far behind the log service is, so it cannot warn of late commits; the late-row counter does. The recount's delivered counts are in memory. After a restart, or a reload that changes recount_window or the query, it compares only the minutes after the watermark it starts again at. A query_template must be a query a derived table holds (no WITH, OPTION or FOR XML, and an ORDER BY only with TOP or OFFSET), or the collector sets recount_window: 0s. The duplicate-row counter sees rows the extraction read twice, or rows deleted after they were sent. It does not see a batch sent again after the Collector had taken it, which delivery at least once allows."},{"t":"0009. Remove the OTel gateway","u":"architecture/decisions/0009-remove-the-otel-gateway.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0009. Remove the OTel gateway Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-18 Context The OTel gateway (services/otel-gateway) was planned as a thin Go service that parses what the stock OpenTelemetry Collector cannot, starting with OutSystems. It never got a caller: POST /v1/heartbeat/events normalized one JSON event into the application event contract and returned it to the caller. Nothing was exported to the OTel Collector, and nothing sends those events. POST /v1/heartbeat/alerts was Alertmanager's default receiver. It counted each delivery and discarded it, so every alert except Watchdog reached a sink that looked like delivery and was not. ADR 0008 moved OutSystems 11 ingest to the db-collector and left the gateway's future open until push sources (TODO 3.4) start. The push sources in view already speak OTLP: OutSystems 11 Cloud log streaming and ODC's App Analytics stream point straight at the OTel Collector's OTLP receiver, and their field mapping fits the Collector's transform processor (OTTL). Keeping the gateway still costs something on every change: a multi-arch image built and published for each release, a Deployment, a scrape job and an up alert in the chart, a kind NodePort, kind end-to-end runs on its changes, and docs describing a component that does nothing. Decision Concern Choice Why The service Remove services/otel-gateway: code, Dockerfile, chart template and values, scrape job, kind port, release image, CI path rule It has no caller and no planned one that OTLP plus OTTL does not cover. Alertmanager's default receiver A receiver with no integrations (a null receiver) in the chart's defaults; environments configure real receivers, as the production example already does (ADR 0005) Alerts stay visible in Alertmanager and Grafana without pretending to be delivered. Alert history for investigations (phase 7) goes through the API into PostgreSQL when that work starts. Future push sources OTLP into the OTel Collector, mapped with OTTL. A source that needs parsing the Collector cannot express gets its own decision then Collector configuration first, as the guardrails already say. Application event contract Kept in packages/telemetry-contracts It describes the normalized event the API and analysis jobs will read; it has no producer today. opentelemetry.endpoint in the runtime config Kept The db-collector's outsystems_logs kind exports to it. Considered and not chosen: Keep the gateway as the bridge for push sources. It would need forwarding, auth, body limits, batching and config reload before carrying any traffic, all of which the OTel Collector already has. Keep only the alert endpoint for phase 7. A no-op sink until then, and phase 7 needs persisted alert events with ownership and audit, which belong in the API. Drop both endpoints and keep an empty service. All the release and chart cost, for no behavior. Consequences A release publishes the db-collector image and the chart. The heartbeat/otel-gateway package on GHCR only serves releases up to v0.1.0. Until an environment configures receivers, alerts other than Watchdog notify no one. This was already true in practice; the default no longer hides it. The minimal kind profile no longer has to override the default receiver. Adding a custom ingest service later is a new decision, with forwarding to the OTel Collector from the start."},{"t":"0010. PatientZero, a synthetic OutSystems application to test against","u":"architecture/decisions/0010-patientzero-test-application.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0010. PatientZero, a synthetic OutSystems application to test against Status: Accepted; first slice being built (service, databases, three entry points, k6 load). Updated 2026-10-19: the survey ran and the replica is gone (see Context); its DDL and aggregates are what PatientZero uses. Updated 2026-10-23: the bad-deploy and slow-sql rows below describe those scenarios as built. Date: 2026-10-18 Context Heartbeat only reads. The SQL Server collector reads DMVs, the outsystems_logs collector reads the oslog_* tables of an OutSystems 11 logging database, and nothing in the stack makes either of them do anything. Today the tests get their input three ways: The OutSystems log fixtures were written from the OutSystems 11 Log data reference, not from a logging database (ADR 0008). make kind-e2e and the live demo create load by hand: an orders table, a blocking chain started from the shell. No test has an application whose database load and application logs come from the same requests, although that link is what Heartbeat sells: \"what happened to this user between 10:00 and 10:15\", across logs, metrics and database evidence. The exits of phases 2 to 7 need input that is realistic, repeatable and comes with a known answer: dashboards and derived metrics in phase 2, onboarding through the API and UI in phases 3 and 4, investigations, alerts and reports in phase 7. A replica of a real OutSystems 11 logging database was available to the team on 127.0.0.1:1433 (an SSH tunnel), with the real DDL, units and traffic shape, and real users' data. The survey (scripts/outsystems-log-survey.sql) ran against it on 2026-10-19. Its native schema was complete, ten cycle tables per log type and their views, but every cycle table was empty: the data was one flattened week extracted by a performance-analysis tool. So the survey gave the DDL and an aggregate traffic profile (rows per hour, peak minute, integration-to-screen ratio, millisecond durations), but no native rows, no time zone and no entry-point coverage. The replica was then deleted: real users' data does not stay on a laptop for numbers already taken. Decision Concern Choice Why What PatientZero: a small Go web application that behaves like an OutSystems 11 application, for tests and demos only. The name leaves room for more complex test applications later A target Heartbeat can monitor end to end, under the test's control. Behaviour HTTP endpoints modelled on OutSystems entry points: Traditional web screens, Reactive server actions, an exposed REST API, timers, extension calls and a consumed REST partner (a fake partner inside PatientZero with injectable latency and errors). Each request runs real SQL against PatientZero's business database and writes the log rows the platform would write for it: one Request_Key across log types and modules, with the caller's session, user and client IP Database load and application logs share a cause. Blocking in the business database really slows the screens and fills the error log, so correlation can be tested, not assumed. Where it writes Two databases on the sandbox SQL Server (make sqlserver-dev-up): patientzero (business tables) and patientzero_log (the ten cycle tables per log type, oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e and oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e_Previous views), created from the surveyed DDL. PatientZero logs in with its own login, with write rights on those two databases only The collector reads PatientZero exactly as it reads a real installation, with its unchanged least-privilege login. The survey A real logging database is only read, never written to: the logging-database survey runs against it read-only. Its DDL becomes PatientZero's logging schema and the CI fixtures' schema; its aggregates become PatientZero's traffic profile (load/traffic.js). Synthetic data is PatientZero's alone, written to its own databases A real database holds real users' data and may itself be read-only; synthetic rows mixed into real ones would break every exact expectation. Reading it is how PatientZero stays faithful. Load k6 scripts drive virtual users with stable identities (user, session, client IP) through the endpoints. Profiles: smoke, steady, peak, soak, and day (the surveyed day's hourly curve, compressed) Realistic concurrency and a known population, so distinct-user counts and per-user timelines can be checked. Scenarios Named fault scenarios, each a file under PatientZero: what it injects, for how long, and the signals it must produce (a PromQL expression, a rule or alert, a LogQL query). Started through PatientZero's admin endpoint; make kind-e2e runs them and checks the signals. First set in the table below One scenario is one test with an exact expectation; the same files drive demos. Known answers Every run writes a JSONL journal: users, sessions and requests, and each fault with its start and end The expected result for investigations (phase 7) and reports, written before Heartbeat looks. Time PatientZero never changes the collector's clock. It compresses the calendar instead: weekly rotation on demand (the view switch the platform does on Fridays at 23:45), backfill of past days within Loki's 168h reject_old_samples_max_age, commits held back past the lag, long-running screens and timers for the start-stamped log types, and Instant written in a configured time zone, daylight saving switches included Exercises rotation, lag, watermarks, late rows and time zones in minutes, without a time-warp switch in production code. Where it lives tools/patientzero/, its own Go module (like tools/docsite), with its Dockerfile, k6 scripts, scenarios and a small Helm chart installed into a patientzero namespace on kind by make patientzero-up Test code stays out of the product's module, chart, images and releases. First scenarios: Scenario Injects Must show blocking A long transaction holding locks other requests need; a deadlock variant Blocked requests, lock waits; slower screens and timeout errors for the same requests bad-deploy A broken publish of one module: its requests fail with a new exception until the previous version is published again The module's error rate, the exception in Loki, and the error-budget burn-rate alerts in Alertmanager slow-partner Latency and errors from the consumed REST partner heartbeat_outsystems_integration_duration_seconds, slower parent screens slow-sql A slow query: the business statement runs longer in SQL Server (a slow extension call comes with extensions, phase 7) Running sessions on SQL Server, heartbeat_outsystems_slow_events_total from SLOWSQL rows, and the latency-budget burn-rate alerts in Alertmanager timer-backlog Timers that start late against their schedule heartbeat_outsystems_timer_delay_seconds bad-front-end Errors and latency on one simulated front-end server Skew in heartbeat_outsystems_front_end_operations_total log-growth Fast growth of patientzero_log The SQL Server storage probes on the logging database log-backlog Rows committed late, then within the lag again heartbeat_outsystems_log_delay_seconds; no gaps in Loki rotation The Friday view switch while traffic runs No rows lost or duplicated across the switch Considered and not chosen: A real OutSystems 11 installation. The most faithful, but it needs Windows Server, IIS and a licence, and faults cannot be injected on demand. Personal Environments run on OutSystems Cloud, with no access to the logging database. A survey of a real logging database gives the realism this would give. Replaying rows from the replica. Realistic logs, but no database load behind them, no controlled faults, and real users' data in the test path. A row generator without an application. Cheaper, but logs and database load would be unrelated, which is the one thing that must be tested. OpenTelemetry Demo, Online Boutique and similar. They emit OTLP, which OutSystems 11 self-managed does not. For the OutSystems Cloud and ODC streams and OTLP traces, PatientZero gains an OTLP mode instead. Its own repository, a dependency of this one. It would keep Heartbeat's repository smaller, but the logging schema and the scenarios' expected signals (Heartbeat's metric names and queries) change together with the product: every such change would become two coordinated pull requests and a version bump. Revisit when PatientZero has users outside Heartbeat, its own release cadence, or grows into a general simulator; its own module, Dockerfile and chart, with no imports from Heartbeat's module, keep that move cheap. Outside git (a local tool). It produces the known answers the phase exit checks compare against, and CI must build it from the same commit, so it needs review and history like any test. HammerDB or WideWorldImporters as the main target. SQL Server load only, with no application logs and no exact expectations. HammerDB stays an option for probe-overhead soak tests. Consequences Nothing from a real database reaches git but DDL and aggregates. The repository is public. The fixtures' rows are synthetic; the traffic profile holds aggregates only. What the survey could not answer stays open: the installation's time zone (log_timezone remains a setting), the mobile request Duration unit, and how many requests lack an entry-point row. A survey of a live logging database answers them. Derived metrics are computed when rows are extracted, so backfilled rows land in Loki at their own times but in Prometheus all at once. Tests of baselines and weekly reports over days need elapsed time (soak runs) or a Prometheus TSDB backfill; phase 7 decides which. PatientZero follows the OutSystems schema the survey showed. Its DDL feeds both PatientZero and the collector's fixtures, so a schema change is made once: PatientZero builds its ten cycle tables and two views per log type from the fixtures' DDL at startup, so the fixtures are the only copy. PatientZero is the application onboarded in the end-to-end tests of phases 3 and 4, and the source of known answers in phase 7. Later modes, each when its phase needs it: OTLP logs and traces (OutSystems Cloud, ODC, the trace store), a MuleSoft-like source, an Oracle-backed variant."},{"t":"0011. Keep the observability platform replaceable; bundle the Grafana stack","u":"architecture/decisions/0011-platform-agnostic-telemetry-boundary.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0011. Keep the observability platform replaceable; bundle the Grafana stack Status: Accepted; the platform review before production is closed by ADR 0019 Date: 2026-10-19 Context ADR 0004 chose Prometheus, Loki, Grafana and Alertmanager, and the chart bundles them. The organization standardizes new observability work on the ELK stack in Elastic Cloud, so the platform Heartbeat's data lands in may change. The project has no budget for one yet: it runs on kind, with about $100 of AWS credit for later. What was found on 2026-10-18 and 2026-10-19 (list prices; check them again before deciding): Elastic Cloud has no free plan, only a 14-day trial. Serverless Observability bills per GB ingested (from $0.07–0.09) and retained (from $0.017–0.019 per month); Hosted bills provisioned capacity (about $99 a month for the smallest Standard deployment). Kibana on Elastic Cloud only comes with an Elastic Cloud deployment and cannot front a self-managed cluster. Self-managed Elasticsearch and Kibana are free under the Basic license, but Basic cannot notify (index and server-log actions only; email, Slack, webhook and PagerDuty are paid) and has no SLO burn-rate rules. They need about 4 GiB of RAM, twice the bundled Grafana stack. Elastic Cloud accepts OTLP directly (the managed OTLP endpoint, generally available on Serverless and on Hosted from 9.0), like most platforms. Heartbeat's code was already close to neutral: the db-collector exposes Prometheus metrics and sends logs as OTLP to the OpenTelemetry Collector, and only the Collector and the chart name a backend. The coupling was the integrations config, which required grafana, loki and alertmanager blocks (nothing read them), push endpoints for Loki and Alertmanager that nothing used, and chart names that assumed Prometheus. Decision Keep the Grafana stack as the bundled platform, and keep everything else independent of it: Heartbeat's services emit only open formats. OTLP to the OpenTelemetry Collector, and Prometheus exposition on /metrics. They never write to a platform's own API. The Collector and the chart are the only places that name a platform. Another platform is a values file: turn the bundled engines off, let the Collector scrape /metrics and send logs and metrics as OTLP (external-platform.example.yaml). Exporters are named for their role (otlp_http/logs), so such a file repoints them. The integrations config is neutral. platform (grafana, or generic for a platform Heartbeat has nothing for) with dashboards, logs and alerting, each the platform's UI or query API. No push endpoints. dbCollector.networkPolicy.prometheus becomes scrapers. Content is per platform. Dashboards, recording and alert rules, label or index policy, and deep-link templates are written for one platform. The catalog of what to measure and alert on stays neutral (derived metrics); Heartbeat ships content for Grafana only, until another platform is chosen. Future readers go through adapters. The API's deep links, the session analyzer and reports will query logs and metrics through an interface, chosen by platform, with Grafana (Loki and Prometheus) the first implementation. The platform is reconsidered before production (roadmap phase 5): the organization's ELK against the Grafana stack, on the same data, with the Collector sending to both. Consequences Moving to Elastic, or to any platform that takes OTLP, needs no service code: a values file, the platform's content, and its query adapter once the API exists. Phase 2 builds Grafana dashboards and Prometheus rules, as planned. They are the part a platform change rewrites; the derived metrics, extraction and queries are not. The integrations config is incompatible with earlier releases: rename grafana, loki and alertmanager to dashboards, logs and alerting, and drop loki.endpoint and alertmanager.endpoint. The loader rejects an unknown platform and the push endpoints. Traces follow the same rule when they arrive TODO 3.6: the Collector's traces pipeline exports through a role-named exporter, and the example sends them as OTLP too. Not covered: validating the Collector's config with the real binary in CI; both the default config and the example were checked with otelcol-contrib validate when this was written."},{"t":"0012. Keep user IDs, session IDs and client IPs raw in Loki; restrict who reaches them","u":"architecture/decisions/0012-personal-data-in-telemetry.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0012. Keep user IDs, session IDs and client IPs raw in Loki; restrict who reaches them Status: Accepted; compliance sign-off required before production Date: 2026-10-21 Context OutSystems 11 log rows carry user IDs, session IDs, request keys and client IPs. The extractor sends them to the OTel Collector, and Loki keeps them as structured metadata, never as index labels (application observability). Whether to keep them raw, replace them with a keyed hash (HMAC) or truncate them (for example, the last octet of an IPv4 address) was left open in the product overview, and the 2026-10-19 security review flagged that any pod in the namespace can reach Loki. What each option costs: Raw. Investigations search for the user or session that reported a problem, by the value support gives them, and correlate it with the application's own screens and tables. Nothing to manage. The data is personal data wherever Loki or a copy of it goes. Keyed hash. Still correlates within Heartbeat, and the key can be rotated. But support has the raw value, so every search needs the key to hash it first, and Grafana's log views show only hashes. The key becomes a secret every environment must hold and protect, and anyone with the key and the data can reverse a known value anyway. Truncate. Loses the per-user and per-session answers the dashboards and investigations need (active users and sessions come from Loki [10.4]). Only fits client IPs, and only when nobody needs the exact address. Decision Keep user IDs, session IDs, request keys and client IPs raw in Loki's structured metadata. They stay out of index labels and metric labels, as before. Restrict who can reach them. NetworkPolicies allow only the components that use Loki to reach it [9.6]. Grafana, the only human path to the logs, needs a login outside kind (anonymous viewing is allowed only in the kind values, behind loopback ports). Later Heartbeat features that copy identifiers out of Loki (investigation evidence, reports) keep them under the same access rules and retention. The extractor stays able to hash before export. If compliance asks for a keyed hash or truncation, it is added in the extractor behind a setting, without changing the pipeline, the labels or the queries' shape. Production needs compliance sign-off first. Before the first production deploy [18], someone accountable for data protection confirms raw storage, Loki's retention and who has Grafana access, or asks for option 3. Until then raw identifiers live only on kind and in test environments with synthetic (PatientZero) or approved data. Consequences Investigations and dashboards keep working on the values support and the SRE team already use. Access is restricted in the chart: Loki admits only the OTel Collector, Grafana and Prometheus, and the other bundled engines only their own clients [9.6]. Operators reach Loki through Grafana or kubectl\nport-forward (network access). Loki is a store of personal data: its retention, backups and access are part of the production review, and any application that receives the logs as OTLP (ADR 0019) must carry the same access rules [18]. docs/architecture/workflows.md's \"prefer hashed identifiers\" rule now reads as \"keep identifiers out of labels; raw values only behind access control\" and points here. Switching to a keyed hash later re-keys every stored value: old and new logs don't correlate across the switch unless the old ones age out first."},{"t":"0013. API design: stdlib HTTP, spec-first OpenAPI, sqlc, Redis sessions and job streams; frozen boundaries","u":"architecture/decisions/0013-api-design-and-subsystem-boundaries.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Boundaries and ownership (frozen)","id":"boundaries-and-ownership-frozen"},{"t":"HTTP and contract","id":"http-and-contract"},{"t":"Data","id":"data"},{"t":"Identity","id":"identity"},{"t":"Jobs","id":"jobs"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0013. API design: stdlib HTTP, spec-first OpenAPI, sqlc, Redis sessions and job streams; frozen boundaries Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-21 Context Phase 3 builds the API (apps/api), the first service that reads and writes PostgreSQL. Today apps/api/cmd/api and apps/api/internal are empty directories. What already constrains it: One backend language, few runtimes and dependencies, free for enterprise use; local RBAC first, OIDC later (ADR 0004). The module is on Go 1.27. The schema is db/migrations/0001_foundations.up.sql with a matching .down.sql. Nothing records which migrations a database has: the integration tests apply the files with psql. users has no password column. PostgreSQL holds product metadata only (ADR 0001); collector desired state comes from YAML, never from the API's tables (ADR 0002); services emit only OTLP and Prometheus exposition and read telemetry through platform adapters (ADR 0011). Durable job state already has tables (investigation_jobs, report_runs), and the architecture guardrails say Redis is never the system of record and workers are idempotent. The Web UI (phase 4) needs a typed client, and production deploys through Argo CD, which renders the chart with helm template: no Helm hooks unless Argo's hook mapping fits (ADR 0005). The subsystem boundaries and ownership rules have been open in TODO 0.1 since phase 0. Their content is already in ADRs 0001, 0002 and 0011; the API is where they become interfaces, so this record freezes them. Decision Boundaries and ownership (frozen) Who owns what. Subsystem Owns Talks to others only through API (apps/api) Every PostgreSQL table except job results (below); users, sessions, approvals; rendering approved probe assignments (ADR 0014) Its OpenAPI contract (UI, scripts); job envelopes on Redis; telemetry adapters (ADR 0011); the collector's admin API, read-only DB collector Reading monitored databases; collector runtime state integrations.yaml, /metrics, /healthz, /readyz, /admin/config, OTLP out Workers (session analyzer, reporting) Running jobs; the rows of the jobs they claimed, and their results (investigation_results, evidence_links, report_runs status) Job envelopes on Redis; PostgreSQL; telemetry adapters Web UI Nothing durable The API only, same origin OTel Collector and the observability platform Telemetry: metrics, logs, rules, alert routing OTLP and Prometheus scrapes (ADR 0011) The API never queries a monitored database and never writes cluster state. Ownership rules. Application-owned records derive their environment through applications.environment_id; there is one ownership path (ADR 0001). PostgreSQL stores control-plane metadata only (ADR 0001). YAML/Kubernetes stores integration and collector desired runtime config (ADR 0002). Approvals in PostgreSQL are requests; the config file in git is the desired state (ADR 0014). The observability platform (Prometheus and Loki when bundled) stores telemetry (ADR 0011). Redis stores transient state only: job delivery, locks, idempotency keys, rate limits, short-lived caches and login sessions. Losing it logs users out and delays jobs; it loses no record. HTTP and contract Routing: the standard library. net/http's ServeMux with method and wildcard patterns (GET /api/v1/applications/{id}), middleware as func(http.Handler) http.Handler. No web framework. The resource API is under /api/v1; /healthz, /readyz and /metrics stay outside it. Errors are application/problem+json (RFC 9457). Spec-first OpenAPI 3.1, server types generated with oapi-codegen. The contract is apps/api/openapi.yaml, written by hand and reviewed like code. oapi-codegen generates the models and a strict, typed server interface for the standard library's ServeMux (its std-http-server target); handlers implement that interface. Generated code is committed, and CI regenerates it and fails on a difference. v2.8.0 added what its release notes call \"initial OpenAPI 3.1 support\" (it needs Go 1.25 or later), so the spec keeps to plain constructs (type: [T, \"null\"], oneOf enums); one that doesn't generate is written another way, not a reason to change tools. The UI's client is generated from the same file (phase 4). Within v1, changes are additive; a breaking change is v2. Data pgx with sqlc. pgx v5 (pgxpool) directly, not database/sql. Queries are .sql files under apps/api/internal/store/queries/; sqlc reads the schema from db/migrations (it parses .up.sql and skips .down.sql) and generates typed Go. No ORM: SQL stays reviewable, and a query that no longer matches the schema fails at generation, not at run time. Generated code is committed and checked in CI like the OpenAPI code. A request that writes several rows, and its audit record [4.10], runs in one transaction. Migrations stay in db/migrations, applied by golang-migrate. The existing NNNN_name.up.sql / .down.sql pairs are the format golang-migrate reads, so nothing is renamed, and zero-padded numbers keep its numeric order and sqlc's lexical order the same. A small Go package in db/migrations embeds the files; the API image runs api migrate up as an init container before the API starts. golang-migrate records the version in schema_migrations and takes a PostgreSQL advisory lock, so replicas starting together apply each migration once. No Helm or Argo hook. Changes are expand, then contract (CONTRIBUTING); .down.sql files are for tests and local use, never run in production. Identity Local users, argon2id. A migration adds a password hash to users. Hashes are argon2id (golang.org/x/crypto/argon2) in the PHC string format, which carries its parameters, so they can be raised later and old hashes upgraded at the next login. Start with RFC 9106's second recommended setting (t=3, p=4, 64 MiB), and cap concurrent hashing so a burst of logins can't run the pod out of memory. Failed logins are rate-limited per account and per client address in Redis. There is no default password: the first admin is created with api users create-admin, which reads the password from standard input. Cookie sessions held in Redis. A random 256-bit session ID in a __Host- cookie (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, Path=/); Redis keeps the ID's SHA-256 hash with the user, created and last-seen times, with an idle and an absolute expiry (configurable; proposed 8 hours and 7 days). Unsafe methods also need an Origin header matching the configured site. Logout deletes the session; disabling a user or changing their password deletes all of theirs. Roles come from roles / user_roles (MVP: admin, operator, viewer for wallboards [4.2]); each operation declares the least role it needs, and the middleware checks it. Jobs Versioned job envelopes on Redis Streams, with consumer groups. The PostgreSQL row (investigation_jobs, report_runs) is the job; the stream message only delivers it. One stream per job type (heartbeat:jobs:\u003ctype\u003e), one consumer group per worker service, trimmed by length. The envelope carries type, version, job_id, enqueued_at and a typed payload, defined as JSON Schema beside the existing payload contracts in packages/telemetry-contracts. A breaking payload change bumps version; a consumer that doesn't know a version dead-letters the message instead of guessing. Workers are idempotent by job_id: a claim is a conditional update (queued → running), and a message is acknowledged (XACK) only after the result is committed. Messages idle too long are reclaimed (XAUTOCLAIM); after a set number of deliveries they go to a dead-letter stream and the job is marked failed. The API re-enqueues jobs that stay queued past a timeout, which covers a lost message or a Redis restart. A duplicate is harmless. Contracts are versioned before the first producer or consumer exists, and each workflow is integration-tested when its worker lands [4]. Considered and not chosen: A router or framework (chi, Echo, Gin). Go's ServeMux has method and wildcard routing since 1.22, and oapi-codegen generates for it, so a dependency would add little. ogen for the OpenAPI code. Fuller OpenAPI 3.1 support, but it brings its own router and runtime instead of the standard library's. Code-first specs (annotations that generate the spec). The spec drifts from what reviewers read, and the UI client would depend on Go comments. An ORM (GORM, ent). Hides the SQL that reviewers and the probe review culture expect to see, for a schema that is small and stable. Sessions in PostgreSQL. Survives a Redis restart and keeps Redis off the request path, but writes a row on every request to refresh last-seen, and needs a cleanup job. Redis expires keys itself, and a Redis outage already stops jobs. PostgreSQL as the queue (FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED). One store fewer and no re-enqueue sweep, and enough for the volumes expected. Not chosen because Redis is already in the stack (ADR 0004) for sessions and caches, consumer groups give per-service delivery, pending tracking and reclaiming without writing them, and polling stays off the small production database. It remains the fallback if Redis Streams prove more work than they save. OIDC from the start. ADR 0004 defers it until corporate requirements demand it [17]. Consequences The API's dependencies are few and conventional: pgx, a Redis client, golang-migrate, golang.org/x/crypto, and the oapi-codegen runtime. Code generation needs two pinned tools (oapi-codegen, sqlc); CI checks that their output is current. Every API request needs both PostgreSQL and Redis: if Redis is down, nobody can log in or stay logged in. Readiness reports both. schema_migrations appears in the database; the integration tests keep applying the files with psql and gain a test that golang-migrate applies them too. Job delivery is at least once; correctness rests on idempotent workers and conditional state changes, tested per workflow. The 0.1 freeze items are closed by this record; changing a boundary above now needs a new ADR. How assignments reach the collector is ADR 0014; how PostgreSQL and Redis are deployed is ADR 0015; how the audit record is written in the transaction and delivered is ADR 0016."},{"t":"0014. Approved probe assignments reach the collector as a pull request to the config repository","u":"architecture/decisions/0014-probe-assignments-to-runtime-config.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0014. Approved probe assignments reach the collector as a pull request to the config repository Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-21. Amended 2026-11-04, when the API side was built [4.5]: a target runs at one interval, so it renders to one collector (§2); the none output renders in the API's pod for make kind-assignments, beside the endpoint (§5); approval, changes and the applied check are spelled out (§4). Context Phase 3 lets operators register database targets and assign probes to them in the API [4.5]. The DB collector must then run those probes. Constraints: The collector reads only integrations.yaml, mounted from the heartbeat-integrations ConfigMap, and hot-reloads it: file polling every 5 s after the kubelet updates the volume (about a minute; 65 s measured on kind), or POST /admin/config/reload. An invalid file is rejected and the previous config keeps running (ADR 0002, configuration reference). The chart renders that ConfigMap from the integrations values. Values files are layered and lists replace, not merge: a second file that sets integrations.collectors replaces the whole list. Production deploys through Argo CD from the heartbeat-deploy config repository (ADR 0005), which doesn't exist yet. Argo CD reverts changes made to the cluster outside git, so an API that writes the ConfigMap would be undone at the next sync, or would need Argo told to ignore it, and git would no longer be the desired state. On kind, make kind-deploy runs helm upgrade with kind.yaml plus the files in EXTRA_VALUES. The collector's model and the database's differ. A collector has one scrape_interval, a probe list and a target list; probe_assignments has an interval per assignment, probe_definitions carries a versioned query_template, and neither table has an approval state. Credentials are references (env/…, kv/…, secret/…). The collector resolves them from HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_* variables loaded from one Secret, so a new reference needs a new Secret key and a collector restart. A missing one fails only that target's connection. Decision The API never writes cluster state. It has no Kubernetes credentials and runs no Helm. Its output is a values file. One generated values file, owned by the API. The API renders every approved, enabled assignment into one file holding a new chart key, managedCollectors, a list of collectors. The chart appends it to integrations.collectors when it renders the ConfigMap, so hand-written collectors stay in the environment's own values and the API never edits them. Rendering rules: one sqlserver collector per target and interval, with the ID api-\u003ctarget id\u003e-\u003cinterval\u003es; changing an interval replaces that collector, as any reload does when an ID changes. Hand-written collector IDs may not start with api- (schema check). Amended 2026-11-04: a target runs at one interval, so it has one collector. The collector refuses a target that two enabled collectors scrape (validateUniqueSQLServerTargets): its series carry only the environment and target labels, so two collectors would write the same ones. An approval is therefore refused while the target's other approved, enabled assignments run at another interval; to change it, change each assignment and approve them again; targets carry name, host, port, database, environment name and credential_ref; probes are listed by name, and a custom probe_definitions.query_template is written out in full, so its SQL is reviewed in the pull request under the probe review checklist; output is deterministic (sorted, no timestamps): the same approvals render the same bytes, so nothing changes when nothing changed; the file is validated with the integrations schema and the Go loader before it leaves the API, and starts with a header saying it is generated. Approval opens a pull request; merging applies it. In production the API pushes the rendered file to one branch it owns on heartbeat-deploy (for example heartbeat-api/assignments) and opens, or updates, one pull request against the environment's path. Pending approvals share that one pull request, so they never conflict with each other. The description lists each assignment, who approved it, and any credential reference new to the file. The config repository's own CI (chart render, schema, loader) and review rules apply; Argo CD syncs the merge and the collector reloads. Approval in the API records intent; the merged commit is the change, with its review trail. States are workflow metadata, separate from desired and observed state. An expand migration adds to each assignment a state (draft, approved, proposed, applied, rejected, failed), the pull request URL and the approver. applied is set only when the collector reports the collector ID with the rendered targets and probes in its redacted GET /admin/config; the API holds the admin token as a Secret reference and reads nothing else from the collector. PostgreSQL holds the request, git the desired state, the collector the observed state (ADR 0002). Amended 2026-11-04: operators create and change assignments; admins approve and reject them, as admins own probe definitions. A draft or a rejected assignment can be approved. A change (interval, timeout, row limit, enabled, or another version of the same definition), or to its target's host, port, database or credential reference, puts an assignment back to draft, so the next render leaves it out until it is approved again: what runs is always what was approved. An approval renders the whole file in its transaction and checks it with the schema and the loader, so one that would make an invalid file is refused. The applied check runs every 30 s and moves approved to applied only; an assignment the collector stops running stays applied, and the collector's reload metrics and readiness say why (§7). Its audit records name the API itself (actor type system) as the actor. kind renders the same file. The API's output target is configurable: github (above) or none, which only serves the rendered file at an admin endpoint. On kind, a make target (for example make kind-assignments) downloads it to an ignored path and runs make kind-deploy with it in EXTRA_VALUES. Same renderer, same chart key, no pull request, and the API still needs no access to the host. Amended 2026-11-04: make kind-assignments runs api probe-assignments render in the API's pod, which reads PostgreSQL only, rather than downloading from the endpoint, so kind needs no login. The file stays in the cluster's ignored state directory, and later make kind-deploy runs keep it, as they keep the SQL Server sandbox. The endpoint, GET /api/v1/probe-assignments/rendered, serves the same bytes to operators. none is the only output until the config repository exists, so there is no setting yet. Secrets are references only. The API validates the reference format and renders references; it never sees or stores a credential. The value reaches the cluster on its own path: External Secrets from AWS Secrets Manager in production, a kubectl-created Secret on kind (database targets guide). Until the collector reads credentials from mounted files (the ADR 0002 follow-up), a new reference also needs a collector restart, so the pull request flags it. A target whose credential is missing fails on its own, visibly in GET /admin/config; other targets keep running. Drift and reconciliation. Cluster against git: Argo CD's self-heal. Git against PostgreSQL: on every approval and on a schedule, the API renders the file and compares it with the default branch. A difference it didn't cause (a hand edit to the generated file) is reported and overwritten by its next pull request. Collector against git: the collector's last_reload_err, runtime_diverged and readiness, and the API's applied check. When it doesn't go through. Pull request closed without merging: its assignments become rejected with the link, and later renders leave them out. To retry, edit and approve again. The config repository's checks fail: the assignments become failed with the check's link; the pull request stays open for the next render to fix. Merged, but the collector rejects or fails to apply the file: it keeps the previous config, the assignments become failed, and the fix is a revert or a new pull request. GitHub unreachable: the assignments stay approved and the API retries with backoff; nothing is lost. Considered and not chosen: The API writes the ConfigMap or patches the collector. Argo CD reverts it, or must ignore it; the API would need cluster write access; no review trail; git stops being the desired state. The API commits straight to the config repository's default branch. Simpler, but it bypasses the repository's review and checks, and \"approved\" means one click. The collector reads assignments from the API or PostgreSQL. Reverses ADR 0002: collection would depend on the control plane being up. An Argo CD plugin or ApplicationSet generator that asks the API. The desired state would live in a database, not in git. The API rewrites integrations.collectors directly. Lists replace, so it would have to own every collector, hand-written ones too. Consequences Assigning a probe takes a review and a sync, minutes rather than seconds. That is the point for production; on kind the make target keeps it to one command. The chart gains managedCollectors (schema, render test, the api- prefix rule). The integrations file format itself doesn't change. To build the GitHub path, the API needs write access to heartbeat-deploy: a GitHub App installed on that repository only, with contents and pull requests read and write, its private key in AWS Secrets Manager through External Secrets [18]. Not set up now; until then the output target is none. The config repository needs CI that renders the chart with each environment's values and loads the result with the Go loader, and branch protection, before the API opens pull requests against it [18]. The API needs a network path to the collector's admin port, allowed in the collector's NetworkPolicy. The same pattern (render to a file in the config repository, review, sync) fits rendered Prometheus rules and Alertmanager routes later [4.7]; that is a separate decision. outsystems_logs collectors are not rendered: an OutSystems source maps an application to its logs, and the extraction collector stays in YAML (ADR 0017)."},{"t":"0015. PostgreSQL and Redis: small chart templates for kind and CI, managed services in production; Redis runs as Valkey","u":"architecture/decisions/0015-postgresql-and-redis-delivery.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0015. PostgreSQL and Redis: small chart templates for kind and CI, managed services in production; Redis runs as Valkey Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-21 Context The API (phase 3) needs PostgreSQL and Redis on kind and in CI (TODO 4.1). Neither is deployed today: ADR 0003 removed them until a service uses them, and the only PostgreSQL is the disposable postgres:17 integration-test fixture in infra/docker-compose.test.yml. Constraints: What Heartbeat deploys goes through the chart; what it tests against stays a Docker fixture. Third-party engines are namespaced, with no CRDs, and each can be turned off (ADR 0003). Argo CD renders the chart: no randAlphaNum or lookup, and Secrets are created outside it. Production uses RDS for PostgreSQL, Secrets Manager through External Secrets, and the latest stable versions production supports, pinned exactly (ADR 0005). Free for enterprise use (ADR 0004). Nothing in Redis is a record: losing it logs users out and delays jobs, which PostgreSQL re-enqueues (ADR 0013). What was checked on 2026-10-21: Bitnami. Broadcom's announcement on bitnami/containers#83267: since 28 August 2025 no new Bitnami images or charts are published to Docker Hub. Existing images moved to docker.io/bitnamilegacy, which gets no updates. A small set of hardened images stays free for development on the latest tag only, and production users are pointed to the paid Bitnami Secure Images. The packaged charts at docker.io/bitnamicharts are no longer updated, and their images may not deploy without overrides. The chart source stays Apache 2.0. Redis licensing. Per Redis's licenses page, Redis 8 and later are under a choice of RSALv2, SSPLv1 or AGPLv3; 7.4 to 7.8 under RSALv2 or SSPLv1; 7.2.x and earlier under BSD-3-Clause. Valkey. The Linux Foundation fork of Redis 7.2, under BSD-3-Clause; it speaks the same protocol. The project publishes valkey/valkey on Docker Hub (tags up to 9.2 today). ElastiCache. Per the engine versions page, it supports Redis OSS up to 7.1, and Valkey from 7.2. A newer engine on ElastiCache is Valkey. PostgreSQL. The Docker Official Image postgres publishes 18.x as stable (19 is in release candidate). Decision Two small templates in the Heartbeat chart, postgresql and valkey, on the projects' own images (postgres, valkey/valkey). Each is a one-replica StatefulSet with a ClusterIP Service, a readiness probe (pg_isready, valkey-cli ping), resources, a non-root security context, and a NetworkPolicy that admits only the API and the workers. Turned on with postgresql.enabled and valkey.enabled, and only once the API uses them. Storage. PostgreSQL keeps its data on a PVC from the cluster's default StorageClass, so a pod restart keeps it. Valkey has no PVC by default (an emptyDir): nothing in it is a record, and a restart costs a login. A PVC can be turned on. Credentials from existing Secrets. The templates read the PostgreSQL password and the Valkey password from existingSecret names. On kind, scripts/kind.sh generates them, as it does the collector's admin token; in production they come from Secrets Manager. Production uses managed services. production.example.yaml sets both enabled: false and points the API at RDS for PostgreSQL and ElastiCache for Valkey, with TLS, by endpoint and Secret name. The templates are for kind and CI only: no replicas, no backups, no major-version upgrades (on kind, delete the PVC and migrate again). Redis runs as Valkey. ADR 0004's \"Redis\" is the role and the protocol; the server is Valkey: BSD-3-Clause, so free for enterprise use without a license review, and what ElastiCache runs for any version past Redis OSS 7.1, so kind tests what production runs. Docs keep saying Redis for the role; the API's client libraries don't change. One version per engine, everywhere. Each engine runs the newest major its AWS managed service supports, pinned to an exact tag. The integration test fixture moves to the same PostgreSQL major in the same change, so tests, kind and production agree. Renovate proposes updates, and the kind CI job must pass (ADR 0005). Considered and not chosen: Bitnami charts. No longer updated, and their default images are in an unmaintained legacy repository (above). An operator (CloudNativePG, a Redis operator). Adds cluster-scoped CRDs and a controller, which ADR 0003 rules out, for HA and backups that production gets from RDS anyway. Another community chart. Another upstream to track and audit for two StatefulSets of about a hundred lines each, which the chart's own tests cover. The redis image. Redis 8's licenses need a legal review in a large company, and ElastiCache wouldn't run the same server. PostgreSQL and Valkey as Docker containers beside kind, like the SQL Server sandbox. They are part of what Heartbeat deploys, not something it tests against (ADR 0003). Consequences The chart gains two templates, values, schema entries and render tests, and make kind-e2e checks that the API reaches both (build slice 2.2). The full kind profile grows by one PostgreSQL and one Valkey pod. With the API they must fit the local loop's 2.5 GiB budget (ADR 0003), measured under load when they land. They do: measured on 2026-11-02, figures in ADR 0003. Production needs RDS and ElastiCache in the infrastructure code and their Secrets in Secrets Manager [18]. If the integration fixture's PostgreSQL major moves, the migration tests run against the new major in the same pull request. Valkey is protocol-compatible with Redis 7.2; Redis-8-only commands or modules are not available, and the API must not depend on them. Update 2026-11-02: the templates landed with the API's chart pull request [4.1]. Amazon RDS has supported PostgreSQL 18 since 14 November 2025 (its release calendar lists 18.6 from 25 August 2026), but the integration fixture was still on 17.11, against point 6. The chart and the fixture moved to postgres:18.6 together, and the migration tests ran on it. Valkey runs 9.1.3, the version of the API's integration tests; tests/chart_engines_test.go fails when the chart and the tests disagree on either engine."},{"t":"0016. Audit records are written in the change's transaction and delivered as OTLP log records","u":"architecture/decisions/0016-audit-records-through-an-outbox.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0016. Audit records are written in the change's transaction and delivered as OTLP log records Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-27 Context Phase 3 builds the API, the first component that changes Heartbeat's own records, so it needs an audit trail [4.10]. What is already said about it: Operator workflows: every operator or admin change appends one JSONL line (time, actor, action, entity type and ID, before and after, request ID, config version) to a dated file such as audit/heartbeat-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl; files are rotated and shipped to Loki or a SIEM [15.3]. Secrets never enter it. ADR 0013: \"A request that writes several rows, and its audit record [4.10], runs in one transaction.\" ADR 0001 and the data model: PostgreSQL holds no audit streams; an audit_events table only if relational audit search or compliance exports are needed [17]. ADR 0011: services emit only OTLP and Prometheus exposition; only the OTel Collector names a platform. These don't fit together: A line appended to a file can't be part of a PostgreSQL transaction. If the file is written after the commit, a crash in between loses the record of a change that happened; if before, a rolled-back change leaves a record. The API is a Deployment that may run more than one replica. A file per pod needs a volume per pod (a Deployment can't give each replica its own ReadWriteOnce volume), or is lost with the pod on an emptyDir. Nothing in the stack ships container files or standard output: the platform receives OTLP from the services and scrapes Prometheus endpoints. Shipping files would mean a node log agent, which no other part of Heartbeat needs. Phase 2 built an ordered log path that loses nothing across restarts: OTLP to the OTel Collector, a persistent sending queue, Loki (application observability). Decision The record. One JSON object per change: id (a UUID, unique per record), time, request_id, actor (user ID and email), action, entity_type, entity_id, before, after, and config_version when the change concerns runtime config. Defined as a JSON Schema, audit_record.schema.json, in packages/telemetry-contracts. A read is not audited; a failed login is, without the password. Written in the same transaction. The API inserts the record into an audit_outbox table in the transaction that makes the change. A change that commits always has its record; one that rolls back has none. Delivered as OTLP log records. A drainer in each API replica claims the oldest rows (FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, in batches), sends them to the OTel Collector as OTLP/HTTP log records (the record as the body; service.name = heartbeat-api, event.name = heartbeat.audit and the record's id as attributes), and deletes them once the Collector accepts them. Delivery is at least once; the id identifies a duplicate. The Collector routes them to Loki through the same persistent queue as the OutSystems logs, and to a SIEM later by adding an exporter there. The outbox is a queue, not a store. Rows live until the Collector accepts them, seconds normally. PostgreSQL answers no audit queries and keeps no history, so ADR 0001 holds. While the Collector is unreachable, changes still commit and rows wait. The API exports the outbox's row count and the age of its oldest row, and an alert fires when delivery has stalled for a few minutes, as OutSystemsLogExportBlocked does for logs. Searched where it lands. Audit records are read in Grafana from Loki ({service_name=\"heartbeat-api\"} | event_name=\"heartbeat.audit\"). An audit search API and an audit_events table stay deferred [17]. No secrets, tested. Records hold credential references, never values; password hashes, session IDs and tokens never appear in before or after. A test changes each audited entity and fails if any of them shows up in a record. Retention is Loki's for now: 15 days by default, 30 days in the production example. How long audit records must be kept is part of the compliance sign-off before production (ADR 0012, [9.2]); if it is longer, the Collector exports them to a SIEM or object storage as well. Considered and not chosen: Dated JSONL files. Not atomic with the change, one file per replica needs a volume per pod, and shipping them needs a node agent. Send OTLP straight after the commit. No table, but a crash after the commit, or a Collector outage longer than the retry, loses the record of a change that happened. An audit_events table as the store. Atomic and searchable, but it makes PostgreSQL an audit store, which ADR 0001 keeps out until relational search or compliance exports are needed [17]. Standard output. Nothing collects it. Consequences One expand migration adds audit_outbox; the API gains a small OTLP log exporter. The db-collector's (internal/otlp) is the model; sharing it is a refactor for that slice to weigh. The OTel Collector's NetworkPolicy admits the API on its OTLP port. Changes don't depend on the Collector being up; audit delivery does, and is alerted on. ADR 0013's sentence on the audit record now holds as written. The workflows doc's file-based audit, and TODO 15.3's file rotation, are replaced by this record; shipping to a SIEM becomes a Collector exporter. Collector reload results and collector add, remove and change decisions stay where they are: the collector's own logs and its heartbeat_collector_config_* metrics."},{"t":"0017. An OutSystems 11 telemetry source maps an application to its log identity; the extraction collector stays in YAML","u":"architecture/decisions/0017-outsystems-sources-map-application-identity.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0017. An OutSystems 11 telemetry source maps an application to its log identity; the extraction collector stays in YAML Status: Accepted Date: 2026-10-27 Context Phase 3 registers telemetry sources through the API [4.4], and its exit registers PatientZero with its source. What exists: telemetry_sources rows belong to one application (application_id not\nnull), with source_type, ingest_mode (otlp, file, api or agentless) and a config JSON object; the environment is derived through the application (data model). OutSystems 11 self-managed keeps every application's logs in one logging database per installation. One outsystems_logs collector reads all of it, sets service.name from each row's Application_Name and deployment.environment.name from its own environment setting (ADR 0008; example in infra/helm/values/production.example.yaml). Its settings (log types, lag, batch_limit, time zone, the logging database and its credential) belong to the installation, not to an application. ADR 0014 renders approved probe assignments into sqlserver collectors. It says nothing about outsystems_logs. What the API and later workers need from a source is which telemetry belongs to an application: deep links [4.9], investigations [6], alert policies [13] and reports [14] all filter Loki and Prometheus by service.name and deployment.environment.name. So a source per application can't own an extraction collector: two applications on one installation would read the same database twice. Decision A source is an identity mapping. An OutSystems 11 source is source_type = outsystems, ingest_mode = agentless (Heartbeat reads the logging database; nothing is installed on the platform), with config holding application_name: the OutSystems Application_Name, which is the service.name of its logs and the application label of its derived metrics. The API validates config against a JSON Schema per source type in packages/config-schema. No migration. One path for the environment. The source has no environment of its own; it is its application's. The environment's slug must equal the extraction collector's environment, the logs' deployment.environment.name. Within an environment, an application_name maps to one application. The extraction collector stays in YAML. One outsystems_logs collector per installation, hand-written in the environment's values and reviewed there (ADR 0002). Installations change rarely; applications change often, and the API manages those. The API checks the mapping is observed. Using the same read-only access to the collector's redacted GET /admin/config as ADR 0014's applied check, the API shows whether an enabled outsystems_logs collector reports the source's environment. A source can be registered before extraction is set up; the check reports it, it doesn't block it. OTLP sources map the same way. OutSystems Cloud and ODC, and other OTLP sources [3.4], send service.name themselves; their source carries the same application_name, with ingest_mode = otlp. Considered and not chosen: The API renders the outsystems_logs collector too. The logging database would become a database target, the log types probe definitions assigned to it, and ADR 0014's renderer would emit an outsystems_logs collector for them. One onboarding path, but more renderer and state for something set up once per installation. Revisit, in a new record, if installations are onboarded from the UI [10.1]. One collector per source. Reads the same logging database once per application. Match applications by name, with no mapping. Display names and OutSystems Application_Name differ, and renaming either breaks the link. Consequences Registering an OutSystems application is metadata only: no pull request, no reload. Adding an installation is a values change, as today. The data model's example for telemetry_sources changes from ingest_mode\n= api with log_labels to agentless with application_name. Deep links, and later investigations, alerts and reports, filter by the source's application_name and the environment's slug. An application_name that matches no logs shows nothing; the observed check covers the collector, not the name. Checking the name against Loki would need the API to query telemetry, which waits for the adapters investigations need [6]."},{"t":"0018. Heartbeat's own telemetry","u":"architecture/decisions/0018-heartbeats-own-telemetry.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0018. Heartbeat's own telemetry Status: Accepted Date: 2026-11-02. Amended 2026-11-02: every trace is kept by default, not 10%, after the volume was measured (decision 4) Context Heartbeat is built to watch other systems, and little of that was turned on itself. Before the API and Web work continued, it was assessed on 2026-11-01: The db-collector and the API expose useful Prometheus metrics, but nothing shows connection pools or OTLP export. Cycle time is a last-value gauge, which hides the tail, and no metric says which build is running. The services log JSON to stderr, so their logs are reachable only with kubectl logs and are gone with the pod. The OutSystems applications' logs, by contrast, are in Loki. There are no traces, and nothing to store them in (TODO 3.6 waited for a first source). The only dashboard for Heartbeat itself has one placeholder panel, and it has no objectives of its own. ADR 0011 already bounds the answer: services emit only OTLP and Prometheus exposition. Decision Metrics stay on Prometheus /metrics. An OpenTelemetry metrics SDK would duplicate a path that works and that every rule and dashboard reads. Logs go over OTLP in-process, and stderr stays. A slog handler (internal/telemetry) writes each record to stderr as before and hands it to a bounded queue: at most 1,024 records and 8 MiB. A full queue drops the record and counts it. Logging never blocks a service and never depends on the Collector. A failing export warns on stderr only, at most once a minute, so it cannot feed itself. A filelog agent was the alternative: it would also catch crashes, but it needs a DaemonSet with host access in a chart hardened against exactly that. Output from before the exporter starts, and panics, stay in kubectl logs; restarts have an alert. Heartbeat's logs have their own Collector receiver and pipeline. They go to port 4319 (otlp/self → logs/self → otlp_http/self-logs), never to 4318. The OutSystems logs pipeline holds one request on disk and makes the receiver wait for it, which is what keeps a backlog in order (application observability). Sharing it, Heartbeat's logs would stall during the backlog they should explain, and take places in its drain. A routing connector could split them on the same port, but it would put new code in the middle of that path, and a second receiver leaves the path as it is. telemetry therefore reads only OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT, not the general OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, so that a service cannot join the delivery path by accident. Traces use the official OpenTelemetry Go SDK and OTLP/HTTP exporter, configured by the standard OTEL_* variables. In a scratch build this grows the collector's binary from 21.1 MB to 26.8 MB and brings gRPC into the build, which the OTLP logs client had kept out. That is accepted: a hand-written span exporter is more code to own for a binary size that costs nothing at run time. Sampling is parent-based by ratio, set by telemetry.traces.sampleRatio: everything by default. A trace is one collection cycle, a few a minute; on kind's full load Tempo stored about 80 MB a day (what the telemetry costs), and the production example makes about half as many spans. A 10% default, decided first, would have lost nine in ten of the failing cycles a trace is opened for, to save storage that costs cents a month on S3. An environment lowers the ratio in its values once the API's request traffic [4.1] makes storage matter. Tempo stores traces, in single-binary mode, in the full profile and off in minimal. Heartbeat is the first trace source, so TODO 3.6 comes before the rest of 4.1. The Collector's exporter is named for its role, like the others (ADR 0011). Identity follows OpenTelemetry: service.namespace=heartbeat, service.name=db-collector|api, service.version, service.instance.id, deployment.environment.name (telemetry.environment in the chart). The heartbeat_service_info gauge carries version, revision and environment. It cannot be a scrape label: the collector's series already use environment for the monitored target, and Prometheus would rename theirs. pprof is opt-in, on a loopback listener (HEARTBEAT_PPROF_ADDR), reached with kubectl port-forward. It needs no token and no NetworkPolicy rule, and an address that is not loopback is refused. Continuous profiling is left out. Restarts come from changes(process_start_time_seconds[1h]). kube-state-metrics stays off; the full profile has about 0.5 GiB of headroom (ADR 0003), and Tempo needs it more. Heartbeat's dashboards and rules are its own, separate from the OutSystems ones, sharing the multiwindow burn-rate pattern but no recording rules. Consequences Heartbeat's logs are in Loki under service_namespace=\"heartbeat\", with Loki's retention and access. They can name hosts, ports and logins, as the raw driver errors in GET /admin/config do, and never passwords (ADR 0012 covers access). Logs can be lost: a full queue, a Collector restart (the logs/self queue is in memory) or a Loki outage longer than the retry time. stderr still has them, and heartbeat_telemetry_log_records_total counts what the service dropped. That is the difference from OutSystems rows, which must not be lost. Records in Loki are cut at 4 KiB per value; stderr keeps the full text. The OTLP client moved from the db-collector to internal/otlp, since the API's audit outbox (ADR 0016) needs it too. A platform other than the bundled one repoints otlp_http/self-logs as it does otlp_http/logs (external-platform.example.yaml). The API has this wiring but is not deployed yet. The chart's API pull request [4.1] sets its environment variables and adds its scrape job. Update 2026-11-02: the chart deploys the API with the db-collector's variables, the api scrape job, and admission to the Collector's 4319 for its logs and 4318 for its spans, which take the OTLP/HTTP receiver like the collector's (point 4); make kind-e2e finds its logs in Loki, its metrics in Prometheus and a request's trace in Tempo."},{"t":"0019. Keep the Grafana stack; share telemetry as OTLP from the Collector","u":"architecture/decisions/0019-keep-grafana-and-share-telemetry-as-otlp.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"}],"x":"0019. Keep the Grafana stack; share telemetry as OTLP from the Collector Status: Accepted Date: 2026-11-02 Context ADR 0011 kept the Grafana stack bundled and left a review of the organization's ELK stack (Elastic Cloud) for before production [TODO 18]. Every Grafana dashboard and Prometheus rule built until then would be rewritten if ELK won. The owner decided on 2026-11-02 to keep the Grafana stack. Other applications in the organization that consume OpenTelemetry must still be able to receive Heartbeat's metrics, logs and traces. Two places could translate Heartbeat's data to OpenTelemetry for them: The API (ADR 0013). It is the control plane: inventory, policies, investigations and audit. It does not carry telemetry. Routing telemetry through it would tie the data path's volume and availability to the backoffice, and rebuild the batching, retry and on-disk queue the Collector already has. OTLP is also a push protocol with no query side, so an API endpoint could not serve \"OTLP\" to a caller that asks. The OpenTelemetry Collector. It already receives every OTLP signal Heartbeat emits, has a pipeline per signal (metrics, logs, logs/self; traces comes with Tempo [TODO 3.6]) with role-named log exporters, and is the only place besides the chart that names a platform (ADR 0011). In the bundled stack Prometheus scrapes the db-collector's /metrics directly, and the Collector's own prometheus exporter (otel-collector:8889), so the db-collector's series do not pass through the Collector today; the external-platform example already has the Collector scrape them instead. Decision The Grafana stack is Heartbeat's observability platform. Prometheus, Loki, Grafana and Alertmanager stay bundled, Tempo joins them (ADR 0018, [TODO 3.6]), and dashboards, rules and query adapters are written for them only. The ELK review in TODO 18 is closed; ADR 0011's boundary stays, so another platform is still a values file away. The Collector is the transformation layer. A consumer that wants Heartbeat's telemetry gets it pushed as OTLP from the Collector: an optional, role-named exporter per signal (otlp_http/share-metrics, /share-logs, /share-traces) added next to the bundled exporters, set in the chart's values with the endpoint and credentials in a Secret. When metrics are shared, the Collector scrapes the db-collector's /metrics with the same job as Prometheus, so both see the same series, in a pipeline of its own that exports only to the shared exporter: in the metrics pipeline they would also reach the Collector's prometheus exporter, and Prometheus would store them twice. The API serves Heartbeat's own resources, not raw telemetry. Inventory, investigations, evidence links and, later, query results go through the API as JSON, and its reads of logs and metrics go through the ADR 0011 adapters, with the Grafana stack's (Loki, Prometheus, and Tempo once it is bundled) the only implementation. Consequences Grafana dashboards and Prometheus rules are no longer at risk of a platform change before production. Sharing with a consumer is configuration plus tests, not service code [TODO 9.5]. The shared OTLP carries the raw series and log records; Prometheus recording rules and alert state stay in Prometheus, and a consumer that needs them reads Prometheus' API. A shared exporter sends personal data in logs (user, session and request IDs, client IPs; ADR 0012) to another system: each consumer needs the same compliance sign-off as Loki, and the exporter needs TLS and authentication [TODO 18]. The Collector carries more traffic when sharing is on: its export queue and memory limits are sized for the extra exporters."},{"t":"0020. Web UI: a Vite single-page app served by the API","u":"architecture/decisions/0020-web-ui-served-by-the-api.html","h":[{"t":"Context","id":"context"},{"t":"Decision","id":"decision"},{"t":"Consequences","id":"consequences"},{"t":"Alternatives considered","id":"alternatives-considered"}],"x":"0020. Web UI: a Vite single-page app served by the API Status: Accepted Date: 2026-11-04 Context Phase 4's outcome is that an operator onboards an application, a SQL Server target and its probes in a browser, with every change audited (roadmap). The API already does all of it [TODO 4.1–4.5]. What is fixed already: TypeScript and React, no server-side rendering (ADR 0004). The UI owns nothing durable and talks only to the API, from the same origin (ADR 0013 §1). The session is a __Host- cookie (Secure, SameSite=Lax, Path=/), and every unsafe request needs an Origin equal to HEARTBEAT_API_SITE_ORIGIN (§8). One chart for kind, CI and production, rendered by Argo CD (ADR 0003, ADR 0005); one version for every image (ADR 0006); CI jobs run when a change can affect them, behind one required check (ADR 0007). The UI's client is generated from apps/api/openapi.yaml (ADR 0013 §4). Open: how the UI is built, where it is served from, which libraries it uses and how it is tested. Decision The API serves the UI. The built app is embedded in the API binary (embed.FS) and served at /. /api/*, /metrics and the health endpoints are unchanged; any other GET that accepts HTML gets index.html, so client routes survive a reload. Hashed assets are cached for a year as immutable, index.html is no-cache, and every UI response carries a strict Content-Security-Policy with no inline script. One image and one origin by construction: no new Deployment, Service, NetworkPolicy or ingress rule, and kind keeps working through the existing port-forward. Build with Vite, TypeScript strict, in apps/web. Node is pinned to the active LTS in apps/web/.nvmrc and checked by make tools-check; packages come from npm with a committed lockfile (npm ci), at exact versions, kept current by Renovate. Typed client generated from openapi.yaml with openapi-typescript (types only) and openapi-fetch (a small typed fetch). make generate writes it and make generate-check fails when it is stale, as it does for oapi-codegen and sqlc. TanStack Query for server state, React Router for routes, no global store. The UI reads the user's roles from GET /api/v1/session to show or hide actions; the API stays the authority, and the UI handles a 401 or 403 rather than trying to prevent it. Mantine for components (forms, tables, modals, notifications), with a light theme of our own and no Tailwind toolchain. Tests. A web CI job, run when apps/web or openapi.yaml changes and counted by ci-ok, runs ESLint, tsc --noEmit, Vitest with Testing Library, the build and npm audit. Playwright (Chromium only) drives the UI against the real API in kind-e2e: log in, onboard PatientZero, approve a probe assignment, follow a deep link. Development loop. npm run dev serves the app with Vite and proxies /api to the API port-forwarded from kind; kind's api.siteOrigin is the Vite origin, http://localhost:5173. Browsers treat localhost as a secure context, so the __Host- cookie works over plain HTTP there. The API image builds the UI. A Node stage on the build platform runs npm ci and npm run build; the Go stage embeds apps/web/dist. A Go build without a bundle embeds a placeholder page, so go test and go build never need Node. Consequences The UI and the API ship together, at one version, in one image; a UI-only change rebuilds the API image. That fits ADR 0006 and costs about 1 MB of assets in the image. The API grows a small static handler, its cache and CSP headers and the index.html fallback, all tested in Go. The UI is not a pod, so the API's NetworkPolicy gains no peer for it and the kind memory budget [TODO 0.4] is unchanged. npm brings a dependency tree into the repository: exact versions, a lockfile, npm audit in CI and Renovate keep it reviewable. kind-e2e gains a browser run; it must stay within the job's 25-minute limit, measured when Playwright is added. Investigation, alert and report screens are built in phase 7 with their APIs and workers, not as shells now. Alternatives considered A separate static container (nginx or distroless) behind an ingress that splits / and /api. A second pod, image and release artefact, and kind would need an ingress just to get one origin. Worth revisiting only if the UI and the API must release on different schedules. Next.js or another SSR framework. ADR 0004 rules out SSR, and an operator console needs none. pnpm. Faster installs, but one more tool to pin; npm ships with Node. orval or openapi-generator for the client. More generated code to review than types plus a 6 kB fetch wrapper. Radix primitives with our own CSS, or MUI. Radix gives more control but is slower to the first screen; MUI is heavier. Mantine covers the forms and tables the onboarding screens need."},{"t":"Architecture Decisions","u":"architecture/decisions/index.html","h":null,"x":"Architecture Decisions Short records of decisions that shape Heartbeat: the context, what was decided, and the consequences. Add a new record instead of rewriting an old one; mark the old record Superseded by ADR NNNN. ADR Decision Status 0001 PostgreSQL stores durable product metadata only, never telemetry Accepted 0002 Integrations and collector desired state come from YAML/Kubernetes, with hot reload Accepted 0003 Deliver the platform with Helm on kind (local, CI) and production Kubernetes Accepted; implemented for kind and CI 0004 Core stack: Go, React, Prometheus, Loki, Grafana, Alertmanager, OTel Collector, PostgreSQL, Redis Accepted 0005 Production on AWS EKS via Argo CD GitOps; ECR; AWS Secrets Manager + ESO; pinned latest-stable versions; Discord → Slack/Teams/WhatsApp alerts; healthchecks.io dead-man's switch; Grafana admin auth Accepted; chart side implemented (version pins, Watchdog route, production example values); production not built 0006 Trunk-based development on master; one SemVer version per release; vX.Y.Z tags from release-please publish multi-arch images and the chart to GHCR; changelog from Conventional Commits; no moving tags Accepted; in effect from 2026-10-05; workflows built, release app and first release pending; required checks replaced by ADR 0007 0007 One CI workflow; slow jobs run only when a change can affect them, everything runs on master; ci-ok is the only required check and fails unless each job ran or was skipped as intended Accepted; in effect from 2026-10-10 0008 OutSystems 11 self-managed logs are extracted from the logging database by a new db-collector kind, outsystems_logs: one reviewed .sql file per log type, watermarked reads, OTLP to the OTel Collector and Loki, derived metrics on the collector's /metrics Accepted; extraction built, derived metrics pending 0009 Remove the OTel gateway service; Alertmanager's default receiver has no integrations until an environment configures one; push sources go to the OTel Collector over OTLP, mapped with OTTL Accepted 0010 Heartbeat is tested against PatientZero, a synthetic OutSystems 11 application on the sandbox SQL Server: real SQL and OutSystems log rows from the same requests, k6 load, named fault scenarios with expected signals, a run journal; a replica of a real logging database is only read, for its schema and traffic profile Accepted; first slice being built 0011 Heartbeat's services emit only OTLP and Prometheus exposition; only the OTel Collector and the chart name a platform; the integrations config is neutral (platform, dashboards, logs, alerting); content is per platform. The Grafana stack stays bundled Accepted; the ELK review is closed by ADR 0019 0012 User IDs, session IDs, request keys and client IPs stay raw in Loki's structured metadata, never labels; only the components that use Loki reach it and Grafana needs a login; the extractor can hash them later behind a setting; production needs compliance sign-off first Accepted; compliance sign-off before production 0013 API: stdlib net/http routing; spec-first OpenAPI 3.1 with oapi-codegen; pgx + sqlc; db/migrations applied by golang-migrate from an init container; local users with argon2id and cookie sessions in Redis; versioned job envelopes on Redis Streams, with the PostgreSQL row as the job. Freezes subsystem boundaries and ownership rules Accepted; routing, the OpenAPI spec with generated server types, pgx + sqlc and golang-migrate (api migrate up) built; the init container and the rest pending 0014 The API never writes cluster state: approved probe assignments render to one generated values file (managedCollectors), proposed as a pull request to heartbeat-deploy and synced by Argo CD; on kind the same file goes into make kind-deploy Accepted, amended 2026-11-04 (one interval per target; kind renders in the API's pod); the none output is built, the pull request output waits for heartbeat-deploy and the API's GitHub App 0015 PostgreSQL and Redis: small chart templates on the projects' images for kind and CI, off in production, which uses RDS and ElastiCache; Redis runs as Valkey; no Bitnami charts Accepted 0016 Audit records are written to an audit_outbox in the change's transaction and drained as OTLP log records through the OTel Collector to Loki; no JSONL files; the outbox is a queue, not a store Accepted 0017 An OutSystems 11 telemetry source maps an application to its service.name (application_name); the outsystems_logs collector per installation stays in YAML, not rendered by the API Accepted 0018 Heartbeat's own telemetry: metrics stay on /metrics; logs go over OTLP as well as stderr, on their own Collector receiver and pipeline, never the OutSystems delivery path; traces use the OTel SDK and Tempo; opt-in loopback pprof Accepted 0019 The Grafana stack is the platform; the ELK review is closed. Other applications receive Heartbeat's metrics, logs and traces as OTLP from optional Collector exporters; the API serves Heartbeat's own resources, not raw telemetry Accepted; sharing not built [TODO 9.5] 0020 Web UI: a Vite + React + TypeScript single-page app embedded in the API binary and served at /, same origin; client generated from openapi.yaml (openapi-typescript, openapi-fetch); TanStack Query, React Router, Mantine; a web CI job and Playwright in kind-e2e Accepted; not built [TODO 5] Template: Working documents such as dated plans (docs/plans/) and reviews (docs/reviews/) stay out of git. When one of them leads to a decision, record the outcome here."},{"t":"Heartbeat Architecture Overview","u":"architecture/overview.html","h":[{"t":"How to read the diagrams","id":"how-to-read-the-diagrams"},{"t":"Current runtime","id":"current-runtime"},{"t":"Component details","id":"component-details"},{"t":"Target platform","id":"target-platform"},{"t":"Open questions","id":"open-questions"},{"t":"Data ownership and boundaries","id":"data-ownership-and-boundaries"},{"t":"Implementation references","id":"implementation-references"},{"t":"Core systems","id":"core-systems"},{"t":"Architectural guardrails","id":"architectural-guardrails"}],"x":"Heartbeat Architecture Overview Heartbeat collects telemetry from a separate monitoring environment. SQL Server hosts run no Heartbeat agents. Metrics and logs belong in Prometheus and Loki; PostgreSQL holds the durable metadata used by the planned control plane. How to read the diagrams Arrows follow the direction data moves. Labels say whether it is pulled (scraped/queried by the receiver) or pushed by the sender. Colour encodes status only: blue implemented, amber partial, dashed gray planned or idle. Solid gray boxes are external systems, configuration and people. Dashed outlines group components by environment or plane. Solid arrows are implemented or configured connections, not proof of a successful live deployment. Dashed arrows are planned; an amber arrow, where present, marks a known gap in otherwise-implemented components. The diagrams are SVG files in diagrams/. Edit them together with this page when the architecture changes. Current runtime This diagram reflects the checked-in services and the Helm chart (infra/helm/heartbeat), as deployed on kind and, with production values, on EKS. Current runtime: SQL Server targets and OTLP clients feed the DB collector and OTel Collector on Kubernetes; Prometheus, Loki and Alertmanager with its Watchdog dead-man's switch; Grafana and the operator The default integration file has no SQL Server targets. Configure a target using the database onboarding runbook, or use the local development sandbox, to collect database metrics. The SQL metric path has no PostgreSQL dependency, Redis queue, or durable collector-side sample buffer. Self-scrapes (Prometheus, Loki, Alertmanager) are omitted. OutSystems ingestion is still pending. Component details Component Endpoints Notes DB collector :8082 — /metrics, /healthz, /readyz, /admin/config, POST /admin/config/reload Reload also on SIGHUP, and on file change when HEARTBEAT_CONFIG_WATCH_INTERVAL is set. Credentials resolved from HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_*. API :8081 — /metrics, /healthz, /readyz The control plane's bootstrap (ADR 0013); an init container applies db/migrations first, and readiness waits for PostgreSQL at the newest of them and for Redis. Its own logs and spans go to the OTel Collector like the collector's. PostgreSQL / Valkey :5432 / :6379 The API's metadata store and Redis, as small one-replica StatefulSets for kind and CI; production uses Amazon RDS and ElastiCache (ADR 0015). OTel Collector OTLP :4317 gRPC / :4318 HTTP; Heartbeat's own logs :4319 HTTP; Prometheus export :8889; health :13133 Exports logs to Loki's OTLP endpoint. Loki indexes only service.name, service.namespace, deployment.environment.name and outsystems.log_type as labels; every other attribute, including user, session and request IDs and client IP, is kept as structured metadata. Prometheus :9090 15s scrape. Rule files heartbeat.rules.yml and generated/ (currently empty). Sends alerts to Alertmanager. Alertmanager :9093 default and deadmans-switch receivers with no integrations on kind (ADR 0009); the always-firing Watchdog alert routes to deadmans-switch. The production example adds chat receivers and healthchecks.io. Loki / Grafana :3100 / :3000 Grafana provisioned with Prometheus (default) and Loki data sources. Inside the cluster each component accepts connections only from the components that use it, on these ports, through ingress NetworkPolicies: the DB collector from Prometheus (:8082); the API from Prometheus (:8081); PostgreSQL (:5432) and Valkey (:6379) from the API; the OTel Collector from the DB collector and the API (:4318, :4319) and Prometheus (:8889); Prometheus from Grafana (:9090); Alertmanager from Prometheus (:9093); Loki from the OTel Collector, Grafana and Prometheus (:3100). Grafana is open to whatever serves its UI and relies on its login. Kubelet probes and kubectl port-forward are not filtered. Loki holds raw user and session IDs and client IPs, so this is part of their protection (ADR 0012); the full table and the values are in the chart guide. The API, PostgreSQL and Valkey are deployed but not drawn yet: the API has no operator workflow until login and inventory [4.2, 4.3]. Integration tests use disposable PostgreSQL and Valkey containers of the same versions (ADR 0015). Target platform The target adds operator workflows around the telemetry path. The implemented collectors are collapsed into one node and Prometheus and Loki into one Stores group; see Current runtime for their internals. The dashed groups are the planes described in Core systems. An existing store does not imply that its planned consumers are implemented. Target platform: telemetry (collectors, Prometheus and Loki stores, Alertmanager) across the top; presentation, analysis jobs and the control plane below, with the operator underneath Session analysis owns correlation and baselines; reporting owns report generation. Their queue protocol, scheduling details, artifact backend, and provisioning interfaces still need to be defined. Baseline metadata lives in PostgreSQL; live rule evaluation belongs in Prometheus. Audit records (ADR 0016) and optional DB evidence snapshots are omitted from this overview; the collector's current evidence sink does not retain them. Open questions How do alerts become persisted investigation events (phase 7): an Alertmanager webhook into the API, or the API reading Alertmanager? Who renders the chart's files/prometheus/rules/generated/ — the API (as drawn) or a build step? Data ownership and boundaries System Owns Boundary PostgreSQL Inventory, policy intent, investigation summaries, evidence links, baseline versions, report-run metadata No raw telemetry, raw sessions, audit streams, or report binaries YAML / Kubernetes Integration endpoints, dashboard URL templates, collector desired runtime state, credential references Active collector targets/probes come from config, not PostgreSQL Prometheus Metric time series and executable recording/alert rules Metrics are scraped from collectors; no SQL metric buffering in Redis/PostgreSQL Loki Logs and queryable operational evidence High-cardinality investigation identifiers are kept as structured metadata, never index labels Redis (Valkey) Transient job delivery, retries, locks, short-lived caches, login sessions Not the durable source of investigation or report state (ADR 0013) Artifact storage (planned) Generated report files PostgreSQL retains the artifact URI only; backend is undecided Application-owned workflows derive environment from applications.environment_id. Remote SQL probes must respect query budgets and least-privilege access. Collector recovery, replica ownership, fencing, and downstream buffering remain planned reliability work. Oracle is a future target; Grafana Alloy is a later comparison, not a selected replacement in the current architecture. Implementation references The current runtime diagram was checked against the chart and a kind deployment on 2026-10-03 (make kind-e2e). Chart values: Prometheus scrape jobs and alerting, OTel pipelines, Alertmanager routes, Grafana data sources; Heartbeat templates. DB collector wiring, and probe runner and evidence sink. Integration configuration (the chart's integrations values), operator workflows, data model, and implementation checklist. Core systems Heartbeat is organized into four planes, the same four groups as the target platform diagram. Plane Components Status Details Telemetry Collection (DB collector, OpenTelemetry Collector), storage and evaluation (Prometheus, Loki), alert routing (Alertmanager) Implemented, including OutSystems derived metrics and SLO alerts Current runtime, database observability, metrics and endpoints Analysis jobs Session analyzer (correlation, adaptive baselines), reporting, report storage Planned Operator workflows Control plane Go API, PostgreSQL (durable metadata), Redis (queues, locks, retries), YAML/Kubernetes config Schema and config manager implemented; API bootstrapped (health, metrics, OpenAPI spec), not yet connected to PostgreSQL or deployed Data model, configuration, decisions Presentation Grafana dashboards, React web UI with Grafana deep links Grafana implemented; UI planned Operator workflows Architectural guardrails Rules every subsystem follows. The reasoning is recorded in the architecture decisions. Services emit only OTLP (to the OpenTelemetry Collector) and Prometheus exposition (/metrics), never a platform's own write API. Only the Collector config and the chart name the observability platform; dashboards, rules and link templates are per-platform content (ADR 0011). The API never queries SQL Server directly; all database-specific collection stays in db-collector. Redis is never the system of record. Durable job and request state lives in PostgreSQL (investigation_jobs, report_runs); workers must be idempotent. Loki labels stay low-cardinality. User, session, request and IP identifiers go in structured metadata or the log body, never in labels. Adaptive alert math is not computed in Grafana dashboards. Services compute and version baselines; executable rules are rendered to Prometheus. Custom parsers never bypass the shared telemetry contract in packages/telemetry-contracts. There is one ownership path per record: application-owned rows derive their environment through applications.environment_id. Collector desired state comes from YAML/Kubernetes, not PostgreSQL. Runtime state is exposed through metrics, health endpoints and logs. Stock OpenTelemetry Collector configuration first: push sources arrive over OTLP and are mapped with OTTL. Custom ingest code needs its own decision (ADR 0009). The repository layout is described in the README."},{"t":"Operator Workflows","u":"architecture/workflows.html","h":[{"t":"Services and responsibilities","id":"services-and-responsibilities"},{"t":"Application telemetry ingestion (OutSystems first)","id":"application-telemetry-ingestion-outsystems-first"},{"t":"Session investigation","id":"session-investigation"},{"t":"Alerting","id":"alerting"},{"t":"Reporting","id":"reporting"},{"t":"Audit","id":"audit"}],"x":"Operator Workflows How the planned services turn telemetry into operator workflows: application telemetry ingestion, session investigation, alerting and reporting, all driven through the control-plane API and web UI. Status: mostly planned. Implemented today: the stock OpenTelemetry Collector pipeline, OutSystems 11 log extraction by the DB collector, Prometheus rules, Alertmanager routing, and the PostgreSQL schema. The API, web UI, session analyzer and reporting service are empty scaffolds. See the current runtime and the roadmap. All four workflows follow the same pattern: PostgreSQL stores intent and durable metadata (what was asked, which policy, which schedule, what the result was). See the data model. Prometheus, Loki and Alertmanager do the runtime work (store, query, evaluate, route). Redis dispatches asynchronous jobs; durable job state stays in PostgreSQL. Everything is application-owned: the environment is derived through applications.environment_id. Services and responsibilities Service Owns Status API / control plane (apps/api) Inventory CRUD, telemetry source and database target onboarding, alert policy and report schedule CRUD, users/roles, investigation APIs, Grafana deep links from YAML templates, audit records. Uses PostgreSQL for durable data and Redis for queues, idempotency keys and cached summaries. Bootstrap built: health, readiness, metrics and the OpenAPI spec; audit records through the outbox, and api users create-admin; login, logout and cookie sessions with roles, and users and their roles managed by admins [4.2]; environments, applications and components [4.3]; telemetry sources and their observation [4.4] Web UI (apps/web) Operator screens: environments/applications, telemetry sources, DB target onboarding, investigations, alert policies, reports, integrations. Investigation is app-first; drill-downs open Grafana/Loki. Planned Session analyzer (services/session-analyzer) Investigation jobs: correlation across Prometheus, Loki, collector evidence and metadata; anomaly windows; adaptive baseline computation. Planned Reporting (services/reporting) Scheduled and on-demand report jobs, rendering, delivery, run metadata. Planned Application telemetry ingestion (OutSystems first) OutSystems 11 self-managed: the DB collector's outsystems_logs kind reads the logging database and pushes the rows as OTLP logs to the OpenTelemetry Collector (ADR 0008). Sources that stream OTLP themselves (OutSystems Cloud, ODC) point straight at the Collector, which maps their fields with OTTL (ADR 0009). The OpenTelemetry Collector batches and routes with stock processors. Logs land in Loki through its OTLP endpoint. Loki's otlp_config limits index labels to three resource attributes (see the OTel Collector notes). Everything else, including user, session and request IDs and client IP, is kept as structured metadata, so investigations can filter and correlate on it. Derived metrics (latency, throughput, errors, availability, saturation) land in Prometheus. Grafana shows application health and links to Loki log views. Normalization targets: environment; application/module; component/action/screen/API operation; tenant where available; user, session and request identifiers; client IP; severity; error category and stack fingerprint; latency; host/node/runtime metadata; deployment/version context. Rules Support Traditional and Reactive OutSystems from the start. Map default OutSystems log fields faithfully first, so existing SRE queries keep working. Confirm improvements with the SRE team before changing field semantics. Keep identifiers out of labels; store raw user, session and client IP values only where access is restricted, and no more of them than investigations need (ADR 0012). MuleSoft and generic JSON/plaintext sources come after the OutSystems path is proven. Session investigation Input: application, plus user, IP, session or request identifier, plus a time range. Output: timeline, anomaly windows, likely bottlenecks, affected components, and evidence links into Grafana/Loki. The operator submits the query in the UI. The API derives the environment from the application and writes an investigations row. The API enqueues a job in Redis and records it in investigation_jobs. The session analyzer picks up the job and queries Prometheus, Loki, DB collector evidence and the metadata database. The analyzer correlates by application, environment, user, IP, request/session IDs, component, host and time window, then stores a bounded summary in investigation_results plus evidence_links. Short-lived results are cached in Redis. The UI renders the timeline and links. Non-goals in the MVP: no session table, no session identity mapping table, no raw evidence blobs in PostgreSQL. Session correlation is recomputed from Loki/Prometheus at investigation time. Risk: correlation quality depends on stable identifiers in source logs. Validate which OutSystems fields are reliably present early. Alerting Static rules cover hard limits and always remain in place as guardrails. Adaptive baselines cover noisy signals: rolling median + MAD or EWMA bands, computed per metric, application and period class. Baseline parameters and versions are stored in adaptive_baselines, and thresholds are rendered into Prometheus-compatible rules. Baselines must be explainable, because opaque adaptive alerting erodes trust. Evaluation happens in Prometheus. Routing, grouping, deduplication, silencing and delivery happen in Alertmanager (email, webhook, later PagerDuty/Slack). PostgreSQL stores policy intent (alert_policies), route intent (notification_routes) and alert history (alert_events). It never evaluates alerts. Do not build a second alert engine. Grafana links in alert evidence are generated from YAML URL templates, not database tables. Planned rendering path: API → infra/helm/heartbeat/files/prometheus/rules/generated/ (loaded by Prometheus) and API → Alertmanager routes. Who renders generated/ (the API or a build step) is an open question. Current state: infra/helm/heartbeat/files/prometheus/rules/heartbeat.rules.yml holds hand-written rules; make rules-check validates them and runs their unit tests. Prometheus sends alerts to Alertmanager. Alertmanager routes the always-firing Watchdog alert to a dead-man's switch and everything else to a default receiver with no integrations until an environment configures one; see known gaps. Adaptive telemetry (later): escalation profiles that raise collection detail during anomalies or incidents, without ever amplifying database load unsafely. Reporting A schedule (report_schedules) or a user triggers a report. The API or reporting service enqueues a job in Redis and records a report_runs row. The reporting service gathers metrics, logs and investigation summaries. The output is rendered as HTML, CSV or PDF and delivered by email. The artifact is stored only if retention requires it, and PostgreSQL keeps artifact_uri. The UI/API exposes run status and downloads. MVP report content: application/service health summary, top regressions, recurring wait/lock hotspots, alert noise summary, SLA/SLO deltas where available. The first target is a weekly summary delivered by email. Audit Every operator or admin change writes one audit record (time, actor, action, entity type and ID, before and after, request ID, config version) in the same transaction as the change, to an outbox the API drains as OTLP log records through the OTel Collector to Loki (ADR 0016). A change that rolls back leaves no record; while the Collector is down, changes still commit and records wait. Secrets never enter audit records. Records are searched in Grafana; a SIEM is added as a Collector exporter. Collector reload results and collector add, remove and change decisions stay in the collector's logs and its heartbeat_collector_config_* metrics."},{"t":"Heartbeat Monitoring Platform Implementation Plan","u":"archive/2026-05-30-mvp-implementation-plan.html","a":true,"h":[{"t":"1. Goal and product scope","id":"1-goal-and-product-scope"},{"t":"MVP outcomes","id":"mvp-outcomes"},{"t":"Explicit non-goals for MVP","id":"explicit-non-goals-for-mvp"},{"t":"2. Current context / assumptions","id":"2-current-context--assumptions"},{"t":"3. Proposed high-level architecture","id":"3-proposed-high-level-architecture"},{"t":"3.1 Core subsystems","id":"31-core-subsystems"},{"t":"3.2 Recommended MVP topology","id":"32-recommended-mvp-topology"},{"t":"3.3 Integration posture","id":"33-integration-posture"},{"t":"3.4 Core system decisions for MVP","id":"34-core-system-decisions-for-mvp"},{"t":"Programming languages","id":"programming-languages"},{"t":"Databases / storage systems","id":"databases--storage-systems"},{"t":"Core platform components","id":"core-platform-components"},{"t":"Why these revised choices","id":"why-these-revised-choices"},{"t":"Important tradeoff","id":"important-tradeoff"},{"t":"3.5 Refined service architecture","id":"35-refined-service-architecture"},{"t":"A. API / control-plane service (apps/api)","id":"a-api--control-plane-service-appsapi"},{"t":"B. OTel gateway and application normalizer (services/otel-gateway)","id":"b-otel-gateway-and-application-normalizer-servicesotel-gateway"},{"t":"C. DB collector service (services/db-collector)","id":"c-db-collector-service-servicesdb-collector"},{"t":"D. Session analyzer (services/session-analyzer)","id":"d-session-analyzer-servicessession-analyzer"},{"t":"E. Reporting service (services/reporting)","id":"e-reporting-service-servicesreporting"},{"t":"F. Web UI (apps/web)","id":"f-web-ui-appsweb"},{"t":"3.6 Data-flow architecture","id":"36-data-flow-architecture"},{"t":"Flow 1: OutSystems application observability","id":"flow-1-outsystems-application-observability"},{"t":"Flow 2: Session investigation","id":"flow-2-session-investigation"},{"t":"Flow 3: Database observability","id":"flow-3-database-observability"},{"t":"Flow 4: Reporting","id":"flow-4-reporting"},{"t":"3.7 Refined PostgreSQL persistence model","id":"37-refined-postgresql-persistence-model"},{"t":"Schema conventions","id":"schema-conventions"},{"t":"Terminology and examples","id":"terminology-and-examples"},{"t":"Data ownership boundary","id":"data-ownership-boundary"},{"t":"Identity and access","id":"identity-and-access"},{"t":"Environment and inventory","id":"environment-and-inventory"},{"t":"Database observability / SQL Server second priority","id":"database-observability--sql-server-second-priority"},{"t":"Investigations and evidence","id":"investigations-and-evidence"},{"t":"Alerting","id":"alerting"},{"t":"Reporting","id":"reporting"},{"t":"Integrations and audit","id":"integrations-and-audit"},{"t":"Recommended indexes","id":"recommended-indexes"},{"t":"PostgreSQL ER diagram","id":"postgresql-er-diagram"},{"t":"Exact ER relationships","id":"exact-er-relationships"},{"t":"Identity and access","id":"identity-and-access-1"},{"t":"Environment and inventory","id":"environment-and-inventory-1"},{"t":"Telemetry source and OutSystems","id":"telemetry-source-and-outsystems"},{"t":"SQL Server collector","id":"sql-server-collector"},{"t":"Investigations and evidence","id":"investigations-and-evidence-1"},{"t":"Alerting","id":"alerting-1"},{"t":"Reporting","id":"reporting-1"},{"t":"Integrations, runtime collectors, and audit","id":"integrations-runtime-collectors-and-audit"},{"t":"PostgreSQL topic review","id":"postgresql-topic-review"},{"t":"Identity and access","id":"identity-and-access-2"},{"t":"Environment and inventory","id":"environment-and-inventory-2"},{"t":"Application observability / OutSystems-first","id":"application-observability--outsystems-first"},{"t":"Database observability / SQL Server second priority","id":"database-observability--sql-server-second-priority-1"},{"t":"Investigations and evidence","id":"investigations-and-evidence-2"},{"t":"Alerting","id":"alerting-2"},{"t":"Reporting","id":"reporting-2"},{"t":"Integrations, runtime collectors, and audit","id":"integrations-runtime-collectors-and-audit-1"},{"t":"Indexing strategy","id":"indexing-strategy"},{"t":"Redis data classes","id":"redis-data-classes"},{"t":"Observability backends","id":"observability-backends"},{"t":"3.8 Architectural guardrails","id":"38-architectural-guardrails"},{"t":"3.9 Pre-implementation review findings and refinements","id":"39-pre-implementation-review-findings-and-refinements"},{"t":"4. Recommended repo layout","id":"4-recommended-repo-layout"},{"t":"Go backend layout rule","id":"go-backend-layout-rule"},{"t":"5. Domain model to design early","id":"5-domain-model-to-design-early"},{"t":"Primary PostgreSQL entities","id":"primary-postgresql-entities"},{"t":"Non-PostgreSQL MVP config/state","id":"non-postgresql-mvp-configstate"},{"t":"Key relationships","id":"key-relationships"},{"t":"6. MVP feature breakdown","id":"6-mvp-feature-breakdown"},{"t":"6.1 Application observability / OutSystems-first","id":"61-application-observability--outsystems-first"},{"t":"6.2 Database full-coverage observability","id":"62-database-full-coverage-observability"},{"t":"6.3 End-to-end telemetry and logs","id":"63-end-to-end-telemetry-and-logs"},{"t":"6.4 Session analysis","id":"64-session-analysis"},{"t":"6.5 Grafana / Loki integration","id":"65-grafana--loki-integration"},{"t":"6.6 Alerting","id":"66-alerting"},{"t":"6.7 Reporting","id":"67-reporting"},{"t":"7. Phased implementation plan","id":"7-phased-implementation-plan"},{"t":"Phase 0: Requirements and architecture baseline","id":"phase-0-requirements-and-architecture-baseline"},{"t":"Task 0.1: Write product requirements document","id":"task-01-write-product-requirements-document"},{"t":"Task 0.2: Write architecture overview","id":"task-02-write-architecture-overview"},{"t":"Task 0.3: Select MVP platform choices","id":"task-03-select-mvp-platform-choices"},{"t":"Phase 1: Repository and platform bootstrap","id":"phase-1-repository-and-platform-bootstrap"},{"t":"Task 1.1: Create monorepo scaffolding","id":"task-11-create-monorepo-scaffolding"},{"t":"Task 1.2: Add local infrastructure stack","id":"task-12-add-local-infrastructure-stack"},{"t":"Task 1.3: Define shared schemas/contracts","id":"task-13-define-shared-schemascontracts"},{"t":"Phase 2: Application observability MVP — OutSystems first","id":"phase-2-application-observability-mvp--outsystems-first"},{"t":"Task 2.1: Implement OutSystems telemetry source management","id":"task-21-implement-outsystems-telemetry-source-management"},{"t":"Task 2.2: Implement OutSystems normalization contract","id":"task-22-implement-outsystems-normalization-contract"},{"t":"Task 2.3: Wire OutSystems logs/metrics into Loki and Prometheus","id":"task-23-wire-outsystems-logsmetrics-into-loki-and-prometheus"},{"t":"Task 2.4: Build OutSystems dashboards and investigation entry points","id":"task-24-build-outsystems-dashboards-and-investigation-entry-points"},{"t":"Phase 3: Database observability MVP — SQL Server second","id":"phase-3-database-observability-mvp--sql-server-second"},{"t":"Task 3.1: Implement SQL Server connection management","id":"task-31-implement-sql-server-connection-management"},{"t":"Task 3.2: Implement core SQL Server metric probes","id":"task-32-implement-core-sql-server-metric-probes"},{"t":"Task 3.3: Export normalized SQL Server metrics","id":"task-33-export-normalized-sql-server-metrics"},{"t":"Task 3.4: Build SQL Server dashboards","id":"task-34-build-sql-server-dashboards"},{"t":"Phase 4: Generic OTLP and additional log sources","id":"phase-4-generic-otlp-and-additional-log-sources"},{"t":"Task 4.1: Harden OTLP gateway","id":"task-41-harden-otlp-gateway"},{"t":"Task 4.2: Add next source normalization","id":"task-42-add-next-source-normalization"},{"t":"Task 4.3: Wire Loki log views/search","id":"task-43-wire-loki-log-viewssearch"},{"t":"Phase 5: Session analysis","id":"phase-5-session-analysis"},{"t":"Task 5.1: Define session correlation model","id":"task-51-define-session-correlation-model"},{"t":"Task 5.2: Build investigation API","id":"task-52-build-investigation-api"},{"t":"Task 5.3: Build session investigation UI","id":"task-53-build-session-investigation-ui"},{"t":"Phase 6: Alerting and adaptive telemetry","id":"phase-6-alerting-and-adaptive-telemetry"},{"t":"Task 6.1: Implement static alert policy provisioning","id":"task-61-implement-static-alert-policy-provisioning"},{"t":"Task 6.2: Implement adaptive baseline calculation v1","id":"task-62-implement-adaptive-baseline-calculation-v1"},{"t":"Task 6.3: Implement adaptive telemetry controls","id":"task-63-implement-adaptive-telemetry-controls"},{"t":"Phase 7: Reporting and integrations","id":"phase-7-reporting-and-integrations"},{"t":"Task 7.1: Implement report templates and schedules","id":"task-71-implement-report-templates-and-schedules"},{"t":"Task 7.2: Deliver reports and artifacts","id":"task-72-deliver-reports-and-artifacts"},{"t":"Task 7.3: Harden Grafana and Loki integrations","id":"task-73-harden-grafana-and-loki-integrations"},{"t":"Phase 8: Security, reliability, and operability","id":"phase-8-security-reliability-and-operability"},{"t":"Task 8.1: Add authn/authz and audit logging","id":"task-81-add-authnauthz-and-audit-logging"},{"t":"Task 8.2: Add rate limits and query budgets","id":"task-82-add-rate-limits-and-query-budgets"},{"t":"Task 8.3: Add service health and runbooks","id":"task-83-add-service-health-and-runbooks"},{"t":"8. Files likely to change in implementation","id":"8-files-likely-to-change-in-implementation"},{"t":"Product/docs","id":"productdocs"},{"t":"API/UI","id":"apiui"},{"t":"Services","id":"services"},{"t":"Shared packages","id":"shared-packages"},{"t":"Infra","id":"infra"},{"t":"Tests","id":"tests"},{"t":"9. Test and validation strategy","id":"9-test-and-validation-strategy"},{"t":"9.1 Unit tests","id":"91-unit-tests"},{"t":"9.2 Integration tests","id":"92-integration-tests"},{"t":"9.3 End-to-end tests","id":"93-end-to-end-tests"},{"t":"9.4 Performance / resilience tests","id":"94-performance--resilience-tests"},{"t":"9.5 MVP exit criteria","id":"95-mvp-exit-criteria"},{"t":"10. Risks, tradeoffs, open questions","id":"10-risks-tradeoffs-open-questions"},{"t":"Risks","id":"risks"},{"t":"Tradeoffs","id":"tradeoffs"},{"t":"Open questions","id":"open-questions"},{"t":"11. Recommended delivery order","id":"11-recommended-delivery-order"},{"t":"12. Suggested first milestone","id":"12-suggested-first-milestone"}],"x":"Heartbeat Monitoring Platform Implementation Plan Archived. Superseded planning document kept for history; see docs/archive/README.md for where its content lives now. For Hermes: Use subagent-driven-development skill to implement this plan task-by-task. Goal: Build Heartbeat, an SRE-focused monitoring platform that complements Dynatrace/Datadog by adding stronger database observability, end-to-end telemetry coverage, session-centric troubleshooting, adaptive alerting, and reporting. Architecture: Start with an application-observability-first ingestion plane focused on OutSystems, a queryable telemetry store, a session-analysis workflow, and an operator-facing API/UI. Add SQL Server deep observability as the second major capability. Keep autonomous RCA out of MVP but design clean event/context pipelines so it can be added later. Tech Stack: Go API/control plane and collectors, TypeScript/React web UI, PostgreSQL for product metadata, Prometheus for scraping/rules, Loki for logs, Grafana, Alertmanager, OpenTelemetry Collector, Redis for jobs/caching/ephemeral coordination, optional Mimir only if scale/retention require it, optional S3-compatible object storage for report artifacts, Kubernetes for deployment. 1. Goal and product scope Heartbeat should fill gaps left by commercial APMs: Deep database observability beyond generic APM plugins Wait events, locks, memory pressure, connection/session saturation, storage pressure Cross-system telemetry for app/platform logs and metrics Session-centric issue discovery by application, user/IP, and time range; environment is derived from the application Flexible integrations instead of lock-in MVP outcomes OutSystems-first application observability OTLP metrics/log ingestion path session investigation workflow by application, user/IP, and time range Grafana integration for dashboards Loki-backed log exploration/search through Grafana Alerting w/ static rules + adaptive thresholds Scheduled operational reporting SQL Server deep observability as the second delivery priority Explicit non-goals for MVP Full multi-database engine parity Full APM-style code-level tracing agent ecosystem Autonomous root cause analysis Custom dashboarding engine if Grafana already satisfies the need 2. Current context / assumptions Repository is currently mostly empty: README.md and Plan.md exist. There is no established app/runtime structure yet. Product must complement, not replace, existing APMs. Database collectors must be safe for production use and use least-privilege credentials. OTel compatibility is required for future-proofing and ecosystem interoperability. Grafana and Loki are the go-to observability stack for MVP and default future direction; OpenSearch/Elastic is not part of the core architecture unless a future hard search requirement proves Loki insufficient. Alert quality matters more than alert count -\u003e reduce false positives early. 3. Proposed high-level architecture 3.1 Core subsystems Control plane Manages environments, applications, telemetry sources, monitored database targets, rules, report schedules, and metadata Does not own runtime integration/collector desired state in MVP; that comes from versioned YAML delivered through Kubernetes Collection plane DB collectors for engine-specific probes OTLP ingestion for metrics/logs/traces where available Storage/query plane Time-series store for metrics Search/index store for logs and session evidence Relational metadata store for Heartbeat config/state Analysis plane Session correlation engine Adaptive threshold engine Report generation jobs Presentation plane API for UI/integrations UI for SRE workflows Grafana/Loki exports and deep links 3.2 Recommended MVP topology apps/api =\u003e REST API for control plane and investigation workflows apps/web =\u003e operator UI services/otel-gateway =\u003e OTLP gateway + OutSystems-first normalization pipeline services/session-analyzer =\u003e correlates logs/metrics/events for user/IP/time range services/reporting =\u003e scheduled report jobs and export generation services/db-collector =\u003e SQL Server metrics/waits/locks/session polling as second delivery priority infra/ =\u003e docker-compose/k8s, Grafana, Loki, Prometheus, Alertmanager, storage wiring 3.3 Integration posture Prefer standards and pluggable adapters OTLP in, Prometheus out, Loki log export/search Grafana dashboard provisioning as code Avoid building custom charting/reporting primitives unless required 3.4 Core system decisions for MVP These are the recommended default choices given the constraints: used inside a major company minimize dependencies and operational moving parts free to use in an enterprise environment Programming languages API / control plane: Go Reason: one backend language across product logic and collectors -\u003e fewer runtimes, fewer frameworks, simpler deploys, good performance, easy static binaries Web UI: TypeScript + React Reason: practical choice for internal operator UI; avoid unnecessary SSR complexity unless later required Collectors / telemetry-heavy workers: Go Reason: best fit for concurrent polling, long-running services, low memory footprint, and strong telemetry tooling Infra as code / packaging: Helm + Kubernetes manifests Databases / storage systems System-of-record metadata DB: PostgreSQL Stores environments, minimal app/inventory metadata, monitored targets, users, alert policies, report schedules, and investigation/report metadata Does not store integration config, audit logs, raw sessions, raw logs, or raw metrics in MVP Metrics backend (MVP): Prometheus Reason: free, proven, Grafana-native, smallest dependency footprint for a serious MVP Metrics backend (scale-up path): Mimir Introduce only if retention, HA, multi-tenancy, or cross-region scale outgrow Prometheus Logs backend (MVP): Loki Reason: free, Grafana-native, materially fewer moving parts than OpenSearch/Elastic Redis: required in MVP Used for background jobs, report generation queues, alert webhook/enrichment jobs, short-lived session-analysis caches, and lightweight distributed coordination Report artifact storage: S3-compatible object storage only if report persistence/export requires it in MVP Otherwise generate reports on demand and store metadata in PostgreSQL Core platform components Telemetry ingress: OpenTelemetry Collector Dashboarding: Grafana Alert routing: Alertmanager Deployment target: Kubernetes for staging/prod, Docker Compose for local dev Auth for MVP: local RBAC first, OIDC/SSO if corporate requirements demand it early Why these revised choices Go over NestJS for API: fewer runtime layers and dependencies; better fit for an internal enterprise platform Prometheus over VictoriaMetrics/Mimir for MVP: smallest operational surface while staying Grafana-native Mimir as later option, not day-1 dependency: better for enterprise scale, but heavier to operate Loki over OpenSearch for MVP: keeps the stack coherent and much simpler to run Redis kept in MVP: justified because alerting, reporting, and session workflows are asynchronous enough to benefit from a dedicated queue/cache layer PostgreSQL as the primary durable state store: reliable, free, well understood in enterprise environments Important tradeoff Loki is the core/default log backend for Heartbeat. OpenSearch/Elastic is not part of MVP or default future architecture. Reconsider OpenSearch/Elastic only if a validated future requirement needs rich document search that Loki cannot support without harmful label cardinality or query complexity. Clean recommendation: MVP and default path: Grafana + Prometheus + Loki + PostgreSQL + Redis Scale path: add Mimir only if Prometheus retention/HA/multi-tenancy requirements outgrow the MVP topology 3.5 Refined service architecture A. API / control-plane service (apps/api) Owns product-facing state and orchestration. Responsibilities environment, application, and component management database target and probe policy onboarding telemetry source registration alert policy CRUD report schedule CRUD user/role/RBAC management investigation query APIs Grafana deep-link generation from YAML URL templates append-only audit event logging to files Internal modules auth users environments applications database_targets telemetry_sources investigations alerts reports integrations audit Storage PostgreSQL for durable data Redis for queues, ephemeral state, idempotency keys, and cached investigation summaries B. OTel gateway and application normalizer (services/otel-gateway) Owns ingestion and normalization of application telemetry, with OutSystems as the first supported platform. Responsibilities receive OTLP metrics/logs ingest supported OutSystems logs/events/metrics parse source-specific payloads when required normalize labels and event fields forward logs to Loki expose or forward metrics to Prometheus-compatible ingestion path emit correlation hints for session analysis OutSystems-first normalization targets application name/module environment tenant, when available user/session/request identifiers request path/action/screen/API operation error category/message/stack fingerprint latency/duration host/node/runtime metadata Rule keep this service thin; do not rebuild the whole OTel Collector in app code use OpenTelemetry Collector config first, custom code second custom parsers must output the shared telemetry contract C. DB collector service (services/db-collector) Owns SQL Server probing and DB-specific normalization as the second delivery priority. Responsibilities secure connection handling to SQL Server targets scheduled execution of approved probe sets query timeout/budget enforcement metric normalization into Prometheus-friendly series session/blocking snapshots for investigation evidence collector self-observability hot-reload collector runtime config from config/integrations.yaml via the shared config manager read target/probe metadata from API/PostgreSQL when SQL Server observability is enabled; YAML controls collector runtime groups, integration endpoints, and credential refs, not the full target catalog Key subcomponents connector manager probe scheduler probe executors metric transformer evidence snapshot publisher health exporter Output Prometheus scrape endpoint optional event payloads to Redis for downstream investigation/report jobs D. Session analyzer (services/session-analyzer) Owns investigation-time correlation logic. Responsibilities accept investigation jobs from API fan out queries across PostgreSQL, Prometheus, Loki, and collector evidence correlate by application, derived environment, user, IP, service/component, host, and time range compute anomaly windows and likely bottleneck summaries cache short-lived results in Redis Outputs investigation timeline evidence bundle links to Grafana dashboards/log views summarized incident narrative seeds for reporting/RCA later E. Reporting service (services/reporting) Owns scheduled and on-demand report generation. Responsibilities consume schedules from API/DB enqueue and run report jobs via Redis gather metrics/log/investigation summaries render HTML/CSV/PDF outputs persist run metadata in PostgreSQL store artifacts in object storage when enabled F. Web UI (apps/web) Owns operator workflows. Primary screens environments/applications DB targets onboarding SQL Server overview and drill-down entry points investigations alert policies reports integrations/admin 3.6 Data-flow architecture Flow 1: OutSystems application observability OutSystems emits logs/events/metrics through supported export path or OTLP-compatible bridge OpenTelemetry Collector receives and routes data where possible OTel gateway normalizes OutSystems-specific fields into Heartbeat telemetry contract Logs land in Loki w/ controlled low-cardinality labels Metrics land in Prometheus-compatible path Session analyzer uses normalized identifiers for user/IP/session/time-range investigations Grafana visualizes application health and links to Loki log views Flow 2: Session investigation User submits application + user/IP/session + time range in UI API derives environment from the application and creates investigation record in PostgreSQL API enqueues analysis job in Redis Session analyzer pulls job Analyzer queries Prometheus, Loki, collector evidence, and metadata DB Analyzer stores short-lived summary/cache in Redis and durable result metadata in PostgreSQL UI retrieves result and renders timeline + evidence links Flow 3: Database observability API stores SQL Server target/probe metadata in PostgreSQL when SQL Server observability is enabled DB collector loads runtime collector config from YAML and target/probe metadata from the API/PostgreSQL Collector probes SQL Server on schedule Collector exposes normalized metrics Prometheus scrapes collector Grafana visualizes metrics Alertmanager routes alert events from Prometheus rules Flow 4: Reporting Scheduler or user triggers report request API/reporting service enqueues job in Redis Reporting service gathers required metrics/logs/investigation summaries Report output is generated and stored UI/API exposes downloadable report and run status 3.7 Refined PostgreSQL persistence model PostgreSQL is the durable system of record for product metadata. It stores environments, minimal app/inventory metadata, lifecycle state, investigation metadata, alert/report metadata, and monitored target metadata. It does not store raw metrics, raw logs, raw sessions, integration config, or audit log streams in MVP; those stay in Prometheus, Loki, YAML config, and append-only audit files as appropriate. Schema conventions Primary keys: UUIDs Timestamps: created_at, updated_at, deleted_at for soft-delete where relevant Status fields: explicit enums/check constraints where practical Config fields: jsonb only for flexible integration/provider config, not for core relational data Secrets: store secret references only, not raw passwords/tokens Auditability: every operator/admin mutation should append a JSONL audit event to the configured audit log path Terminology and examples assets Meaning: concrete infrastructure/runtime things Heartbeat can point at, monitor, or relate to other infrastructure. Fits here: VM, Windows server, Linux host, SQL Server instance host, IIS node, Kubernetes namespace, Redis instance, queue, gateway, load balancer. Why PostgreSQL: this is metadata, not telemetry. It gives stable IDs for ownership, topology, database target placement, collector assignment, and human-readable investigation context. MVP constraint: keep it minimal. If phase 1 only observes applications and not host/database topology, defer asset_relationships and optional app/component asset_id links. Examples: name = prd-iis-01, asset_type = server, platform = windows name = sql-prod-01, asset_type = database_server, platform = sqlserver name = public-lb, asset_type = load_balancer, platform = nginx applications Meaning: business/application-level system operators care about. Fits here: OutSystems app, customer portal, admin portal, payment service, batch processing app. Examples: name = Claims Portal, platform = outsystems_traditional, criticality = high name = Mobile API, platform = dotnet, criticality = medium application_components Meaning: pieces inside an application. A component may run on an asset, but it is not itself the infrastructure asset. Fits here: OutSystems module, Reactive screen group, Traditional eSpace, background job, API endpoint group, worker, frontend, backend service. Examples: application = Claims Portal, name = ClaimsWeb, component_type = outsystems_module, runtime_name = ClaimsWeb application = Claims Portal, name = Login, component_type = screen_or_flow application = Mobile API, name = auth-worker, component_type = worker, asset_id = prd-worker-02 Rule: use assets for where it runs; use application_components for what part of the app it is. telemetry_sources Meaning: a configured source/feed of telemetry for one application. Fits here: OutSystems log export for Claims Portal, OTLP receiver for Mobile API, Prometheus scrape source for one app, Loki label selector config for one app. Examples: application = Claims Portal, source_type = outsystems, ingest_mode = loki_query, config = {\"log_labels\":{\"app\":\"Claims\"}} application = Mobile API, source_type = otel, ingest_mode = otlp, config = {\"service_name\":\"mobile-api\"} Rule: no direct environment_id on telemetry_sources in MVP. The environment is derived through telemetry_sources.application_id -\u003e applications.environment_id. This avoids duplicate ownership paths while still supporting multiple apps per environment, each w/ different sources. outsystems_sources MVP decision: do not create this as a physical table initially. Reasoning: OutSystems is an application platform/source type, not a separate domain object parallel to applications. Creating outsystems_sources too early risks duplicating application identity. MVP placement: OutSystems-specific source settings live in telemetry_sources.config, with applications.platform and application_components.component_type carrying the app/component model. Future-only reason to add it: if OutSystems needs many queryable/validated fields, constraints, migrations, or UI workflows that become awkward inside telemetry_sources.config. Future examples if promoted later: environment_key, application_key, tenant_key, log_format_version, module_mapping_strategy. normalization_rules Meaning: versioned parsing/mapping rules that convert source-specific telemetry fields into Heartbeat's normalized labels and fields. Fits here: mapping OutSystems SessionId -\u003e session.id, UserId -\u003e enduser.id, module/action fields -\u003e normalized component/action fields, IP extraction logic. Examples: source_type = outsystems, version = default-log-v1, rule_config = {\"session_field\":\"SessionId\",\"user_field\":\"UserId\"} source_type = otel, version = semantic-conventions-v1, rule_config = {\"service_field\":\"service.name\"} Rule: rules are source-type/version scoped, not environment-owned. Session identity mappings MVP decision: do not store session/user correlation mappings in PostgreSQL. Reasoning: sessions are telemetry-derived, high-volume, potentially sensitive, and better reconstructed from Loki logs/metrics during investigation. Use PostgreSQL only for the investigation request/result metadata; use Loki/Prometheus for session evidence and correlation. Data ownership boundary PostgreSQL: product metadata, application inventory, investigation requests/results, report metadata, alert policy metadata, and alert event history Prometheus: raw/derived metrics, recording rules, and live metric alert evaluation Loki: raw/normalized logs and LogQL investigation queries Alertmanager: alert grouping, deduplication, routing, silencing, and notification delivery Redis: queues, short-lived caches, idempotency keys, worker locks, and transient processing state YAML config files: deployment-owned integration endpoints, dashboard URL templates, and runtime collector desired state Append-only log files: audit events in JSONL format for MVP Object storage / filesystem: report artifacts when persistence is needed Rules: PostgreSQL must not store raw OutSystems metrics. PostgreSQL must not store raw logs. PostgreSQL must not perform live alert evaluation. PostgreSQL must not store report binaries. Heartbeat can store alert definitions in PostgreSQL, but executable rules are rendered to Prometheus/Alertmanager configuration or APIs. Identity and access users id, email, display_name, status, last_login_at roles id, name, description user_roles user_id, role_id Environment and inventory environments id, name, slug, type, description, status, labels jsonb applications id, environment_id, name, platform, owner_team, criticality, status application_components id, application_id, name, component_type, runtime_name, labels jsonb telemetry_sources id, application_id, source_type, ingest_mode, status, config jsonb normalization_rules id, source_type, version, enabled, rule_config jsonb, created_by Deferred until SQL Server/host topology is in scope: assets: id, environment_id, name, asset_type, platform, status, labels jsonb asset_relationships: id, source_asset_id, target_asset_id, relationship_type applications.primary_asset_id application_components.asset_id Database observability / SQL Server second priority database_targets id, environment_id, asset_id nullable, engine, name, host, port, database_name nullable, status, credential_ref, labels jsonb probe_definitions id, engine, name, version, category, default_interval_seconds, timeout_ms, query_template, enabled probe_assignments id, database_target_id, probe_definition_id, interval_seconds, enabled, config jsonb Not stored in PostgreSQL for MVP: collector desired state, collector groups, integration endpoints, and runtime add/remove config live in YAML/Kubernetes collector runtime state should be exposed through /metrics, /healthz, /readyz, and logs first; add PostgreSQL runtime tables only if the UI needs historical fleet inventory Investigations and evidence investigations id, environment_id, created_by, status, subject_type, subject_value, time_start, time_end, query jsonb investigation_jobs id, investigation_id, status, queued_at, started_at, finished_at, error_message nullable investigation_results id, investigation_id, summary, severity, result jsonb, created_at evidence_links id, investigation_id, source_type, title, url, time_start, time_end, metadata jsonb Alerting alert_policies id, application_id, name, signal_type, severity, enabled, condition jsonb, labels jsonb, rendered_rule_ref nullable adaptive_baselines id, application_id, signal_key, window, method, parameters jsonb, last_computed_at notification_routes id, application_id, name, channel_type, target_ref, routing_rules jsonb, enabled, alertmanager_route_ref nullable alert_events id, application_id, alert_policy_id nullable, status, severity, fingerprint, started_at, resolved_at nullable, labels jsonb Reporting report_templates id, name, description, template_type, template_config jsonb, enabled report_schedules id, application_id, report_template_id, cron_expression, timezone, recipients jsonb, enabled report_runs id, application_id, report_schedule_id nullable, requested_by nullable, status, time_start, time_end, artifact_uri nullable, started_at, finished_at Integrations and audit No PostgreSQL tables for integrations/audit in MVP. Integration endpoints and Grafana URL templates live in versioned YAML config files. Audit events are written as append-only JSONL files. Recommended indexes environments(slug) unique applications(environment_id, name) telemetry_sources(application_id, source_type, status) database_targets(environment_id, engine, status) investigations(environment_id, created_at desc) investigations(environment_id, subject_type, subject_value, time_start, time_end) alert_events(application_id, fingerprint, started_at desc) Deferred w/ asset tables: assets(environment_id, asset_type) PostgreSQL ER diagram This is the authoritative PostgreSQL model for the current plan. It intentionally excludes integration config, Grafana link templates, audit streams, raw telemetry, raw logs, raw sessions, and report binaries. Deferred topology extension, only when SQL Server/host topology is in scope: Exact ER relationships This is the authoritative MVP relationship model. Keep it simple; avoid redundant joins unless a real workflow requires them. Identity and access users.id 1 -\u003e N user_roles.user_id FK: user_roles.user_id -\u003e users.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict user deletion if audit/history exists; prefer users.status = disabled roles.id 1 -\u003e N user_roles.role_id FK: user_roles.role_id -\u003e roles.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict Unique constraints: users.email unique roles.name unique user_roles(user_id, role_id) unique Environment and inventory environments.id 1 -\u003e N applications.environment_id FK: applications.environment_id -\u003e environments.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict if applications exist applications.id 1 -\u003e N application_components.application_id FK: application_components.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade only on hard-delete; prefer soft-delete/status changes Deferred asset/topology relationships for SQL Server/host observability slice: environments.id 1 -\u003e N assets.environment_id assets.id 1 -\u003e N applications.primary_asset_id assets.id 1 -\u003e N application_components.asset_id assets.id N -\u003e N assets.id through asset_relationships Unique constraints: environments.slug unique applications(environment_id, name) unique application_components(application_id, name, component_type) unique deferred w/ asset tables: assets(environment_id, name) unique deferred w/ asset tables: asset_relationships(source_asset_id, target_asset_id, relationship_type) unique Explicit non-relationships: no environment -\u003e environment relation in MVP no environment_assets join table no direct application -\u003e database_target relation in MVP Telemetry source and OutSystems applications.id 1 -\u003e N telemetry_sources.application_id FK: telemetry_sources.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict if active; cascade only through intentional application hard-delete cleanup users.id 1 -\u003e N normalization_rules.created_by FK: normalization_rules.created_by -\u003e users.id Required: no for system-created rules Delete behavior: set null Unique constraints: telemetry_sources(application_id, source_type, ingest_mode) partial/conditional as needed normalization_rules(source_type, version) unique Notes: OutSystems Traditional and Reactive should be represented through applications.platform, application_components.component_type, and telemetry_sources.config only where necessary. Do not create outsystems_sources in the MVP unless OutSystems-specific config becomes large, queryable, or constraint-heavy enough to justify a source-specific extension table. Keep default OutSystems log field compatibility before adding improved mappings. SQL Server collector environments.id 1 -\u003e N database_targets.environment_id FK: database_targets.environment_id -\u003e environments.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict if probe assignments/history exist assets.id 1 -\u003e N database_targets.asset_id FK: database_targets.asset_id -\u003e assets.id Required: no Delete behavior: set null Deferred: only active when asset/topology extension is enabled database_targets.id 1 -\u003e N probe_assignments.database_target_id FK: probe_assignments.database_target_id -\u003e database_targets.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade assignment rows only probe_definitions.id 1 -\u003e N probe_assignments.probe_definition_id FK: probe_assignments.probe_definition_id -\u003e probe_definitions.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict; disable/version probes instead of deleting Unique constraints: database_targets(environment_id, engine, host, port, database_name) unique probe_definitions(engine, name, version) unique probe_assignments(database_target_id, probe_definition_id) unique Notes: Collector desired state, grouping, scaling, and runtime add/remove are controlled by YAML/Kubernetes, not PostgreSQL assignment tables. If the UI later needs collector fleet history, add observed runtime tables separately; do not mix desired runtime config into target/probe metadata. Safety invariant: SQL Server probe definitions must be reviewed for non-blocking behavior before assignment to production targets. Investigations and evidence environments.id 1 -\u003e N investigations.environment_id FK: investigations.environment_id -\u003e environments.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict; retain investigation history according to policy users.id 1 -\u003e N investigations.created_by FK: investigations.created_by -\u003e users.id Required: yes for user-created investigations; system user can be used for automation Delete behavior: restrict or set to retained system/deleted user marker investigations.id 1 -\u003e N investigation_jobs.investigation_id FK: investigation_jobs.investigation_id -\u003e investigations.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade only if investigation is hard-deleted by retention job investigations.id 1 -\u003e N investigation_results.investigation_id FK: investigation_results.investigation_id -\u003e investigations.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade only by retention job investigations.id 1 -\u003e N evidence_links.investigation_id FK: evidence_links.investigation_id -\u003e investigations.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade only by retention job Unique constraints: optional investigation_jobs(investigation_id, status) partial unique for one active job per investigation Notes: investigations.subject_type should start with user, ip, session, request. evidence_links points to Grafana/Loki/Prometheus views; it does not store raw evidence payloads. Alerting applications.id 1 -\u003e N alert_policies.application_id FK: alert_policies.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict; disable policies instead of deleting applications.id 1 -\u003e N adaptive_baselines.application_id FK: adaptive_baselines.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: cascade only through retention/config cleanup applications.id 1 -\u003e N notification_routes.application_id FK: notification_routes.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict if referenced by active policies/config applications.id 1 -\u003e N alert_events.application_id FK: alert_events.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: retention-based cleanup alert_policies.id 1 -\u003e N alert_events.alert_policy_id FK: alert_events.alert_policy_id -\u003e alert_policies.id Required: no; external/unmapped Alertmanager events may exist Delete behavior: set null or restrict; prefer policy disablement Unique constraints: alert_policies(application_id, name) unique adaptive_baselines(application_id, signal_key, window, method) unique notification_routes(application_id, name) unique alert_events(application_id, fingerprint, started_at) unique Notes: Alerting is application-owned in MVP. Environment context is derived through applications.environment_id. PostgreSQL stores policy intent and alert event metadata only. Prometheus evaluates; Alertmanager routes/dedupes/delivers. Reporting report_templates.id 1 -\u003e N report_schedules.report_template_id FK: report_schedules.report_template_id -\u003e report_templates.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict if schedules exist; disable templates instead applications.id 1 -\u003e N report_schedules.application_id FK: report_schedules.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict applications.id 1 -\u003e N report_runs.application_id FK: report_runs.application_id -\u003e applications.id Required: yes Delete behavior: restrict while report history is retained report_schedules.id 1 -\u003e N report_runs.report_schedule_id FK: report_runs.report_schedule_id -\u003e report_schedules.id Required: no; on-demand report runs may not have a schedule Delete behavior: set null or restrict based on retention policy users.id 1 -\u003e N report_runs.requested_by FK: report_runs.requested_by -\u003e users.id Required: no; scheduled/system reports may not have a requester Delete behavior: set null Unique constraints: report_templates(name) unique report_schedules(application_id, report_template_id, cron_expression, timezone) unique Notes: Reporting is application-owned in MVP. Environment-level summaries can be generated later by aggregating application reports. report_runs.artifact_uri is a pointer only. Report artifacts may be deleted after confirmed delivery; keep only run metadata if no retention is required. Integrations, runtime collectors, and audit No PostgreSQL ER relationships for integration config in MVP. config/integrations.yaml owns integration endpoint metadata, credential references, dashboard URL templates, and collector definitions. Runtime add/remove is supported by hot-reloading the YAML file, not by storing integration config in PostgreSQL. Services load config through a shared config manager that validates the YAML, computes a config version/hash, and atomically swaps the active collector set. Collector lifecycle is derived from config diff: new collector in YAML -\u003e validate -\u003e start/register collector removed collector from YAML -\u003e graceful drain -\u003e unregister/stop collector changed collector config -\u003e validate -\u003e restart or live-update that collector depending on connector support Reload triggers: filesystem watch for local/dev explicit SIGHUP or /admin/config/reload endpoint for production Kubernetes ConfigMap/Secret projected-volume update or rollout-compatible reload sidecar if deployed on K8s Keep collector runtime state in /metrics, /healthz, /readyz, and logs for MVP; add PostgreSQL fleet tables only if later UI/history workflows need them. Audit events are append-only JSONL files under a configured audit directory. Future PostgreSQL audit/config tables are only justified if the product needs UI-driven integration CRUD, relational audit search, or DB-backed compliance exports. PostgreSQL topic review Identity and access Purpose: control the minimal wallboard/admin access needed for MVP. Review: Keep auth intentionally simple for phase 1: one shared SRE/admin-style user is acceptable. users, roles, and user_roles are enough for MVP. Do not add permissions, SSO-specific tables, or environment-scoped RBAC yet. Keep the schema easy to extend later if named users or SSO become necessary. Risk: Do not let a temporary shared-user model leak into long-term enterprise design. Treat it as an MVP shortcut. Environment and inventory Purpose: define the clean ownership tree for everything Heartbeat monitors. Review: environment is the top boundary. Everything observable belongs to exactly one environment. application belongs to one environment. application_component belongs to one application. asset is deferred unless SQL Server/host topology is part of the active MVP slice. If enabled, assets represent concrete runtime/infrastructure/database nodes, not telemetry. asset_relationships is deferred w/ assets and is only for asset-to-asset dependency links such as app server -\u003e database, gateway -\u003e backend, worker -\u003e queue. Canonical MVP relationships: environment 1 -\u003e N applications application 1 -\u003e N application_components Deferred topology relationships: environment 1 -\u003e N assets application_component N -\u003e 1 asset optional asset N -\u003e N asset through asset_relationships MVP decision: Keep assets only if SQL Server/host topology work is in scope for the same MVP slice. If the first slice is strictly OutSystems/application observability, defer assets, asset_relationships, applications.primary_asset_id, and application_components.asset_id until DB/host topology is needed. The argument for keeping assets in PostgreSQL is that they are stable inventory metadata used for ownership and collector targeting, not raw telemetry. Rules to avoid redundancy: no environment -\u003e environment relationship table for MVP no direct environment_assets join table; assets.environment_id is enough no direct application -\u003e database_target relation unless a real workflow requires it later no duplicate ownership paths; ownership should be inferable from foreign keys Risk: Asset modeling can become a CMDB. Keep only what Heartbeat needs for observability and investigation. Application observability / OutSystems-first Purpose: make OutSystems the first-class phase-1 platform without making it a separate domain object parallel to applications. Review: applications and application_components represent business apps, modules, services, screens/actions, or runtime components. telemetry_sources is generic and application-owned so future platforms fit without schema churn. Do not create outsystems_sources in MVP. Put OutSystems-specific source configuration in telemetry_sources.config unless it grows into queryable/validated relational data. OutSystems ingestion should follow the default OutSystems log pattern first so existing SRE troubleshooting queries remain reusable. The model must support both Traditional and Reactive OutSystems instances from the start. normalization_rules lets parsers evolve by source type/version, but phase 1 should begin w/ faithful normalization rather than aggressive reinterpretation. Session/user correlation is reconstructed from Loki/Prometheus during investigations; do not persist session identity mappings in PostgreSQL for MVP. Risk: Session correlation depends on consistent source fields and normalization. Validate the default OutSystems log fields early. Avoid storing PII unnecessarily; prefer hashed/normalized values in logs where possible. If we identify improvements over the default OutSystems pattern, confirm before implementing them so we do not break current operational workflows. Database observability / SQL Server second priority Purpose: prepare the DB collector domain without making it phase-1 blocker. Review: database_targets stores DB endpoint metadata and secret references. Runtime collector groups/add-remove config live in config/integrations.yaml and Kubernetes, not PostgreSQL. Collector target selection should read database_targets/probe_assignments from API/PostgreSQL when SQL Server observability is enabled. probe_definitions versions safe SQL probes. probe_assignments allows per-target enablement and intervals. All production extraction access must use non-blocking transactions and query patterns that do not interfere with workload execution. This matters especially for extraction queues and recurring production reads. Risk: Never store raw DB credentials. Probe definitions need review/versioning because unsafe SQL can hurt production DBs. Blocking reads on production are unacceptable; collector queries must be explicitly designed and reviewed for non-blocking behavior. Investigations and evidence Purpose: persist investigation lifecycle and durable summaries while raw data remains in Prometheus/Loki. Review: investigations stores the query intent: subject, environment, time range, and filters. investigation_jobs tracks async execution through Redis-backed workers. investigation_results stores durable summary output, not raw logs/metrics. evidence_links points to Grafana/Loki/Prometheus views and specific time windows. Risk: Do not duplicate raw telemetry into PostgreSQL. Keep result JSON bounded; large evidence payloads belong in object storage or generated views. Alerting Purpose: store application-owned alert configuration and alert history while Prometheus/Alertmanager remain the live alerting plane. Review: alert_policies stores UI-managed policy intent and metadata for one application. These policies are rendered into Prometheus-compatible rules; PostgreSQL does not evaluate them live. adaptive_baselines stores per-application computed threshold parameters that rule generation or analysis jobs can use. notification_routes stores per-application route intent and references to Alertmanager routes; Alertmanager performs grouping, dedupe, silence, and delivery. alert_events records application-scoped lifecycle/fingerprint from Alertmanager/webhook events for audit, dedupe, reporting, and investigation context. Environment context is derived from application.environment_id; do not duplicate environment_id in alert tables for MVP. Risk: Avoid splitting alert truth across too many systems. Prometheus evaluates alerts, Alertmanager routes alerts, PostgreSQL tracks product-owned intent/history. Do not build a second alert engine in PostgreSQL. Reporting Purpose: schedule, run, and audit generated operational reports for one application. Review: report_templates defines report structure. report_schedules defines cadence, timezone, recipients, and application. report_runs records executions, application, requester, and artifact references. PostgreSQL should store report metadata and delivery state only. Report artifacts may be deleted after confirmed delivery if retention is not required. Environment-level reporting can be added later as aggregation across applications in the same environment. Risk: Report artifacts should not live as large blobs or binaries in PostgreSQL. Store URI/reference only. Integrations, runtime collectors, and audit Purpose: keep deployment-owned integrations simple while still allowing collector add/remove at runtime through validated YAML hot-reload. Review: Use YAML config files for integrations in MVP. Primary file: config/integrations.yaml Contents: Grafana base URL, Loki endpoint, Alertmanager endpoint, SMTP/webhook provider, credential references, Grafana URL templates, and collector definitions. Store secret references only; raw secrets stay in the secret manager or environment-specific secret files. Runtime add/remove works by reloading YAML and diffing desired collector config vs currently running collectors. Add: new YAML collector entry -\u003e validate -\u003e instantiate/start/register collector. Remove: deleted YAML collector entry -\u003e graceful drain -\u003e unregister/stop collector. Change: modified YAML collector entry -\u003e validate -\u003e live-update if supported, otherwise restart that collector only. Implement a shared config manager. Parse and schema-validate YAML before applying anything. Compute config_version / hash. Apply changes atomically: invalid config keeps the previous active config. Expose current config version and reload status via health/admin endpoint. Kubernetes/Go reload strategy: Treat YAML as desired state delivered by Kubernetes ConfigMap plus secret references, not raw secrets. Mount config as a projected volume, but do not rely on watching the file itself; Kubernetes updates ConfigMap volumes through symlink swaps, so watch the parent directory and debounce events. Support explicit SIGHUP and authenticated /admin/config/reload as deterministic production reload triggers. Prefer an off-the-shelf reload sidecar/controller for production rollout signaling if needed, e.g. checksum-annotation rollout or reloader sidecar that calls /admin/config/reload; do not build a fragile custom Kubernetes watcher first. In Go, implement a small config manager around typed structs, YAML decoding, schema/business validation, config hashing, and atomic.Value/copy-on-write snapshots. Never mutate active config in place. Build a candidate config, validate all dependencies, compute the diff, then swap active config only after successful collector reconciliation. Collector manager owns lifecycle: Start(ctx, cfg), Update(ctx, cfg) when safe, Drain(ctx), Stop(ctx). Expose /healthz, /readyz, /admin/config, and /metrics with current config_version, last reload timestamp, last reload result, and per-collector state. Do not create a grafana_links PostgreSQL table in MVP. Prefer deterministic Grafana URLs from templates and variables in YAML. Add a table only if operators need editable per-app/per-panel dashboard mappings from the UI. Use append-only audit log files in MVP, preferably JSONL. Example file pattern: audit/heartbeat-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl Each line: timestamp, actor, action, entity_type, entity_id, before/after metadata, request_id, config_version. Log config reload success/failure and collector add/remove/change decisions. PostgreSQL audit/config tables become useful later if we need UI CRUD, relational audit search, compliance exports, or multi-tenant admin workflows. Example config/integrations.yaml shape: Risk: Hot reload must be conservative: invalid YAML, missing secrets, duplicate collector IDs, or unsafe collector changes must fail closed and keep the previous active config. Kubernetes ConfigMap updates are eventually reflected in mounted volumes and use symlink replacement semantics. File watchers must watch the directory and debounce, or production should use explicit reload signaling. Runtime config files can drift from Git if edited manually on servers. Prefer GitOps or controlled deployment path even though the running service can hot-reload. Reloads must be observable. Alert on repeated reload failures, stale config versions, collector crash loops, and collectors stuck draining. File audit logs need rotation, retention, and shipping; otherwise they become hard to query. Audit should store metadata diffs and secret references only; never raw credentials/tokens. Next implementation steps: Convert the PostgreSQL ER diagram into explicit migrations and constraints. Create migration files for core tables: users/roles, environments/applications/components, telemetry sources, normalization rules, investigations/evidence, alerting, reporting. Keep assets/topology, integrations, Grafana links, sessions, and audit streams out of PostgreSQL for MVP unless separately approved. Add database-level safety constraints. Unique indexes listed above. FK delete behavior matching the ER relationship section. Check constraints for status/severity/type enums where practical. JSONB shape validation only where stable enough; avoid over-constraining early config blobs. Add schema tests/verification. Migration up/down smoke test. FK/unique constraint tests. Query-plan checks for first investigation, alert, and reporting lookups. Define config/integrations.yaml schema and sample fixtures. Create Go config package, likely internal/config, with typed structs, YAML decoder, validation, hashing, and redacted rendering. Create Go collector lifecycle interface, likely internal/collectors, with Start, Update, Drain, Stop, Status. Create config reconciliation loop that diffs desired collectors by stable id and applies add/remove/change operations w/ rollback/fail-closed behavior. Add reload triggers: SIGHUP, authenticated POST /admin/config/reload, and optional fsnotify parent-directory watcher for dev/local. Add Kubernetes manifests: ConfigMap for YAML, Secrets or external-secret refs for credentials, projected volume mount, probes, metrics scrape config, and optional reloader sidecar/controller. Add config/reload tests: valid/invalid YAML, duplicate IDs, missing secret refs, config hash stability, atomic swap behavior, collector add/remove/change diffing, failed reload keeps previous active config, K8s-style symlink swap watcher behavior if fsnotify is enabled. Indexing strategy Purpose: keep the first workflows fast. Review: Index environment-scoped lookups first. Prioritize investigation queries by subject + time range. Prioritize audit lookups by entity + timestamp. Add more indexes from query plans, not guesses. Risk: Too many early indexes slow writes and migrations. Start with the listed indexes only. Redis data classes job queues: report:*, investigation:*, alert:* short TTL caches for expensive investigation results distributed locks for singleton schedulers/workers idempotency keys for retried jobs rate-limit counters for expensive operations Observability backends Prometheus: metrics and recording rules Loki: logs and derived log queries Grafana: dashboards, exploration, and alert entry links 3.8 Architectural guardrails Do not let the API service query SQL Server directly -\u003e all DB-specific collection stays in db-collector Do not make Redis the system of record -\u003e durable truth stays in PostgreSQL Store durable job/request state in PostgreSQL and use Redis for dispatch, locks, retry coordination, and short-lived caches only Do not over-label Loki streams -\u003e keep label cardinality controlled; place high-cardinality fields in log payload/body Do not put adaptive alert math inside Grafana dashboards -\u003e compute/version baselines in services and render executable rules to Prometheus Do not let custom parsers bypass the shared telemetry contract -\u003e normalization must be centralized Do not duplicate ownership paths: app-owned children derive environment through applications.environment_id Do not put runtime collector desired state in PostgreSQL for MVP; YAML/Kubernetes is the desired-state source, metrics/logs expose runtime state 3.9 Pre-implementation review findings and refinements Concerns found and resolved before implementation: Environment vs application ownership drift Earlier wording mixed environment-scoped session searches, telemetry sources, alerts, and reports. Refined rule: application-owned workflows use application_id; environment is derived from the application. Environment remains useful for grouping, database targets, and coarse filtering. Assets were leaking back into the MVP Earlier service/UI wording implied first-class asset management even though assets are deferred. Refined rule: phase-1 UI/API focuses on environments, applications, components, telemetry sources, investigations, alerts, and reports. Assets/topology are introduced only when SQL Server/host topology needs them. Collector runtime state conflicted with YAML hot-reload Earlier ER included collector_instances and collector_assignments, which conflicts with YAML/Kubernetes as desired runtime state. Refined rule: PostgreSQL stores database targets and probe policies; YAML/Kubernetes stores collector runtime groups/add-remove config. Collector health/status is exposed via /metrics, /healthz, /readyz, and logs first. Add DB fleet tables only if product UI needs historical collector inventory. Go project layout should follow Go conventions Avoid Node-style src/modules as the real backend layout. Use cmd/\u003cservice\u003e/main.go, internal/\u003cdomain\u003e/, and pkg/ only for intentionally shared libraries. Existing task paths that say src/modules are conceptual; implementation should use idiomatic Go packages. OTel gateway should not become a second OTel Collector Use stock OpenTelemetry Collector config and processors first. Custom Go code is justified only for OutSystems-specific parsing/normalization that cannot be cleanly expressed in collector config. Any custom parser must output the shared Heartbeat telemetry contract. Redis durability boundary needed tightening Redis is acceptable for queues, locks, and caches. Durable job state belongs in PostgreSQL tables like investigation_jobs and report_runs; workers should be idempotent and recover from Redis/job retries. Kubernetes config reload needs explicit production semantics ConfigMap projected volumes use symlink swap behavior and eventually-consistent update timing. Production should support explicit SIGHUP or authenticated /admin/config/reload; fsnotify is optional/dev-friendly and must watch the parent directory with debounce. 4. Recommended repo layout Create the project as a monorepo so shared schemas and deployment assets stay aligned. Go backend layout rule Backend services are Go services. Use idiomatic Go layout: cmd/\u003cservice\u003e/main.go for entrypoints internal/\u003cdomain\u003e/ for service-private packages pkg/ only for intentionally shared public libraries Avoid Node-style src/modules in implementation. Any later task path mentioning src/modules should be translated to the matching Go internal/\u003cdomain\u003e package. Keep shared schemas/contracts generated or versioned under packages/ only if multiple services consume them; otherwise keep code local to the service. 5. Domain model to design early Primary PostgreSQL entities Environment Application ApplicationComponent TelemetrySource NormalizationRule DatabaseTarget ProbeDefinition ProbeAssignment SessionInvestigation InvestigationJob InvestigationResult EvidenceLink AlertPolicy AdaptiveBaseline NotificationRoute AlertEvent ReportTemplate ReportSchedule ReportRun User Role Non-PostgreSQL MVP config/state IntegrationConnection equivalent lives in config/integrations.yaml. Grafana dashboard/deep-link mappings live in YAML/templates and Grafana provisioning. Audit events are append-only JSONL and should be shipped to Loki/SIEM. Runtime collector desired state lives in YAML/Kubernetes. Runtime collector health/state lives in metrics/logs/health endpoints first. Asset and AssetRelationship are deferred until SQL Server/host topology needs explicit inventory modeling. Key relationships An Environment owns many Applications. An Application owns many ApplicationComponents, TelemetrySources, alert policies, notification routes, baselines, and report schedules. A DatabaseTarget belongs to an Environment and has probe assignments; collector runtime groups select/read targets but are not modeled as PostgreSQL assignments in MVP. A SessionInvestigation is scoped by environment plus query payload and should normally be created from an application-centric UI flow. AlertPolicy is application-owned and rendered to Prometheus/Alertmanager. ReportSchedule is application-owned; environment-level reporting is a later aggregation feature. 6. MVP feature breakdown 6.1 Application observability / OutSystems-first Start with OutSystems as the first application platform. Required OutSystems signals application/module health request latency and error rate user/session/request correlation identifiers environment and tenant labels where available slow actions/screens/API calls exception fingerprints and recurring error patterns deployment/version context where available host/node/runtime metadata compatibility with default OutSystems log fields and query patterns used by current SRE workflows support for both Traditional and Reactive OutSystems instances Output expectations normalized Heartbeat event envelope for OutSystems telemetry faithful mapping of default OutSystems logs first, so existing troubleshooting queries can be reused Loki logs with controlled labels: environment, application, component, severity, event type Prometheus metrics for latency, throughput, errors, availability, and saturation where available Grafana dashboards for OutSystems application health and investigation entry points 6.2 Database full-coverage observability SQL Server is the second delivery priority after OutSystems application observability. Required SQL Server signals Instance availability CPU pressure indicators Memory pressure indicators Buffer/cache hit behavior Wait statistics Blocking chains and locks Active sessions and connection saturation Query latency and throughput Transaction rate / rollbacks Physical/logical reads/writes TempDB pressure Database size / free space / growth trends Error events where available Output expectations Stable normalized metric names Labels for environment, cluster, host, database, instance, application, user where safe Dashboards for overview, waits/locks, sessions, storage, performance regressions 6.3 End-to-end telemetry and logs OTLP metrics/log ingestion endpoint OutSystems log/event normalization first MuleSoft and generic JSON/plaintext inputs later Source normalization into a common event envelope Mapping to environment/service/component labels 6.4 Session analysis Inputs: application, user or IP, time range Outputs: correlated metrics and logs affected services/assets anomaly windows likely bottlenecks and evidence links Must support drilling from session view -\u003e logs -\u003e dashboards 6.5 Grafana / Loki integration Provision Grafana datasources and dashboards Provide Loki log views and saved LogQL queries Deep-link from Heartbeat UI -\u003e Grafana panel / Loki explore view 6.6 Alerting Static threshold rules for hard limits Adaptive thresholds for noisy metrics Alert grouping/dedupe/routing Alert evidence links to sessions, dashboards, logs 6.7 Reporting Scheduled daily/weekly health reports Top regressions recurring wait/lock hotspots alert noise summary application/service health summary 7. Phased implementation plan Phase 0: Requirements and architecture baseline Task 0.1: Write product requirements document Objective: Convert the feature summary into a concrete MVP spec. Files: Create: docs/product/requirements.md Create: docs/product/phased-roadmap.md Deliverables: MVP feature list non-goals personas: SRE, DBA, support engineer acceptance criteria per feature phase 2+ future items Validation: Review doc covers every user-provided feature Review doc separates MVP vs future clearly Task 0.2: Write architecture overview Objective: Freeze the initial system boundaries before implementation starts. Files: Create: docs/architecture/overview.md Create: docs/architecture/database-observability.md Create: docs/architecture/session-analysis.md Create: docs/architecture/alerting.md Validation: Diagrams or structured sections cover ingest, store, analyze, present Each subsystem has responsibilities and interfaces Task 0.3: Select MVP platform choices Objective: Avoid stalling later on infra debates. Files: Modify: docs/architecture/overview.md Modify: docs/product/requirements.md Decisions to make: API stack Web stack Metrics backend for MVP Log backend for MVP queue/job mechanism local dev topology Open recommendation: API: Go Web: React + TypeScript Jobs/cache: Redis-backed workers Metadata DB: PostgreSQL Metrics/logs: Prometheus + Loki Phase 1: Repository and platform bootstrap Task 1.1: Create monorepo scaffolding Objective: Establish a clean workspace layout. Files: Create: apps/api/ Create: apps/web/ Create: services/db-collector/ Create: services/otel-gateway/ Create: services/session-analyzer/ Create: services/reporting/ Create: packages/config-schema/ Create: packages/telemetry-contracts/ Create: tests/integration/ Create: tests/e2e/ Create: tests/performance/ Validation: Fresh clone boots local workspace successfully Task 1.2: Add local infrastructure stack Objective: Make the system runnable in dev before app logic grows. Files: Create: infra/docker-compose.yml Create: infra/grafana/ Create: infra/prometheus/ Create: infra/loki/ Create: infra/otel-collector/ Create: infra/alertmanager/ Validation: Local stack starts w/ metadata DB, metrics store, log store, Grafana, Alertmanager Health checks are documented in docs/runbooks/local-dev.md Task 1.3: Define shared schemas/contracts Objective: Prevent drift between services. Files: Create: packages/config-schema/ Create: packages/telemetry-contracts/ Schema subjects: normalized metric naming environment/asset labels session event envelope alert evidence payload report payloads Phase 2: Application observability MVP — OutSystems first Task 2.1: Implement OutSystems telemetry source management Objective: Support onboarding OutSystems applications/environments before database collectors. Files likely: Create: apps/api/internal/applications/ Create: apps/api/internal/telemetrysources/ Create: apps/web/src/pages/applications/ Create: apps/web/src/pages/telemetry-sources/ Test: tests/integration/outsystems-sources/ Acceptance criteria: Add/edit/disable an OutSystems telemetry source Associate source to application; derive environment from the application Validate required labels and ingest configuration Store only secret references, not raw credentials Task 2.2: Implement OutSystems normalization contract Objective: Convert OutSystems logs/events into Heartbeat's canonical event model. Normalization rule: phase 1 must follow the default OutSystems log pattern first so current SRE monitoring and troubleshooting queries remain reusable if we discover improvements to the pattern or query model, document them and ask before changing the default mapping support both Traditional and Reactive OutSystems instances Fields to normalize first: environment application/module component/action/screen/API operation user/session/request identifiers client IP where available severity error fingerprint duration/latency host/node/runtime metadata Files likely: Create: packages/telemetry-contracts/src/application_event.* Create: services/otel-gateway/internal/parsers/outsystems/ Create: services/otel-gateway/internal/normalization/outsystems/ Validation: Parsed events contain consistent environment/application/session labels High-cardinality fields are kept out of Loki labels where possible Task 2.3: Wire OutSystems logs/metrics into Loki and Prometheus Objective: Make application telemetry visible through Grafana stack. Files likely: Create: infra/otel-collector/collector.yaml Create: infra/loki/ Create: infra/prometheus/prometheus.yml Create: infra/prometheus/rules/outsystems-recording-rules.yml Validation: Sample OutSystems telemetry appears in Loki Derived/application metrics appear in Prometheus Grafana can query both Task 2.4: Build OutSystems dashboards and investigation entry points Objective: Provide immediate application-level operator value. Files: Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/outsystems-overview.json Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/outsystems-errors.json Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/outsystems-latency.json Create: infra/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/heartbeat.yaml Create: infra/grafana/provisioning/datasources/*.yaml Validation: Dashboards load without manual setup Drill-down from application overview -\u003e errors/latency -\u003e Loki logs works Phase 3: Database observability MVP — SQL Server second Task 3.1: Implement SQL Server connection management Objective: Support secure target onboarding after application telemetry is in place. Files likely: Create: services/db-collector/internal/connectors/sqlserver/ Create: apps/api/internal/databasetargets/ Create: apps/web/src/pages/database-targets/ Test: tests/integration/sqlserver-targets/ Acceptance criteria: Add/edit/disable a SQL Server target Validate credentials/network reachability safely Store secrets via secret provider reference, not plaintext where possible Task 3.2: Implement core SQL Server metric probes Objective: Capture high-value DB signals after the application path is working. Safety rule: all production extraction queries must use non-blocking transaction patterns especially for queue-reading/extraction workflows, collector access must never block production processing query approach must be reviewed for lock behavior before rollout Metric groups: waits locks/blocking sessions/connections memory pressure disk/file usage throughput/latency Files likely: Create: services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/waits/ Create: services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/locks/ Create: services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/sessions/ Create: services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/memory/ Create: services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/storage/ Validation: Probe outputs conform to shared metric schema Polling budgets and timeout guards exist Task 3.3: Export normalized SQL Server metrics Objective: Make SQL Server metrics usable by Grafana/Alertmanager. Files likely: Create: services/db-collector/internal/export/ Create: infra/prometheus/prometheus.yml Create: infra/prometheus/rules/sqlserver-recording-rules.yml Validation: Prometheus scrapes collector Grafana can graph normalized metrics Task 3.4: Build SQL Server dashboards Objective: Provide database deep-dive value after OutSystems visibility exists. Files: Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/sqlserver-overview.json Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/sqlserver-waits-and-locks.json Create: infra/grafana/dashboards/sqlserver-sessions.json Create: infra/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/heartbeat.yaml Create: infra/grafana/provisioning/datasources/*.yaml Validation: Dashboards load without manual setup Drill-down from overview -\u003e waits/locks -\u003e sessions works Phase 4: Generic OTLP and additional log sources Task 4.1: Harden OTLP gateway Objective: Accept metrics/logs from external systems using standard protocols after OutSystems path is proven. Files likely: Modify: services/otel-gateway/ Modify: infra/otel-collector/ Validation: Test emitter can send OTLP metrics/logs Events are normalized and routed correctly Task 4.2: Add next source normalization Objective: Extend normalization beyond OutSystems only when MVP value is proven. Inputs to support next: generic OTLP MuleSoft log format generic JSON/plaintext inputs Files likely: Create: services/otel-gateway/internal/parsers/mulesoft/ Create: services/otel-gateway/internal/normalization/generic/ Validation: Parsed events contain consistent environment/service/session labels Task 4.3: Wire Loki log views/search Objective: Support operational investigation workflows through Grafana/Loki. Files: Create: infra/loki/ Create: apps/api/internal/logsearch/ Validation: Search by time range, environment, service, user, IP where fields are present Saved query/deep-link support exists Phase 5: Session analysis Task 5.1: Define session correlation model Objective: Turn user/IP/time-range requests into deterministic queries. Persistence rule: Do not create a PostgreSQL session table or session identity mapping table. Session correlation is computed from Loki/Prometheus at investigation time. Files: Create: docs/architecture/session-analysis.md Create: packages/telemetry-contracts/src/session.* Correlation dimensions: environment user id IP request/session ids host/service/component time windows Task 5.2: Build investigation API Objective: Expose the session analysis workflow. Files likely: Create: apps/api/internal/investigations/ Test: tests/integration/investigations/ Key endpoints: create investigation query fetch correlated logs/metrics/events fetch anomaly summary fetch evidence links Task 5.3: Build session investigation UI Objective: Let SREs discover issues quickly. Files likely: Create: apps/web/src/pages/investigations/ Create: apps/web/src/components/session-analysis/ Test: tests/e2e/session-analysis/ Required UX: filter by application + user/IP + time range render event timeline highlight anomaly windows link out to Grafana/Loki Phase 6: Alerting and adaptive telemetry Task 6.1: Implement static alert policy provisioning Objective: Store Heartbeat alert policy intent and render executable rules to Prometheus/Alertmanager instead of evaluating alerts in PostgreSQL. Files likely: Create: apps/api/internal/alerts/ Create: apps/api/internal/rulerendering/ Create: infra/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml Create: infra/prometheus/rules/*.yml Validation: Heartbeat policy is persisted in PostgreSQL executable rule is rendered/provisioned into Prometheus Alertmanager handles grouping/routing/delivery Hard-limit rules trigger, route, dedupe, resolve correctly Task 6.2: Implement adaptive baseline calculation v1 Objective: Reduce alert noise for variable workloads while keeping live evaluation in Prometheus-compatible rules. Approach: start simple: rolling median + MAD or EWMA bands compute per metric/application/period class store baseline parameters/version metadata in PostgreSQL render executable thresholds/rules to Prometheus-compatible rule definitions never replace hard guardrails Files likely: Create: services/session-analyzer/internal/baselines/ Create: apps/api/internal/adaptivealerting/ Test: tests/integration/adaptive-alerting/ Validation: Baselines are explainable Outlier events are reproducible and auditable Alert suppression rules do not hide critical issues Task 6.3: Implement adaptive telemetry controls Objective: Increase sampling/detail only when signals justify it. Design hint: telemetry profiles by asset/environment escalation on anomalies or incident mode cooldown back to normal collection rates Risk: extra collection must not amplify DB load during incidents Phase 7: Reporting and integrations Task 7.1: Implement report templates and schedules Objective: Deliver recurring operational summaries. Files likely: Create: services/reporting/internal/templates/ Create: services/reporting/internal/jobs/ Create: apps/api/internal/reports/ Create: apps/web/src/pages/reports/ Report sections: environment health summary top alerts / noisy alerts wait-event regressions lock hotspots SLA/SLO deltas if available Task 7.2: Deliver reports and artifacts Objective: Make reports usable outside the app. Delivery options: email downloadable HTML/PDF/CSV object storage retention Task 7.3: Harden Grafana and Loki integrations Objective: Turn integrations into productized features. Features: integration config validation from YAML datasource validation deep links permission-aware sharing Phase 8: Security, reliability, and operability Task 8.1: Add authn/authz and audit logging Objective: Secure operator access and trace changes. Files likely: apps/api/internal/auth/ apps/api/internal/audit/ apps/web/src/pages/admin/ Task 8.2: Add rate limits and query budgets Objective: Protect monitored databases and shared infra. Rules: max polling concurrency per target timeout caps query allowlists/versioning backoff on failures Task 8.3: Add service health and runbooks Objective: Make Heartbeat operable by SRE teams. Files: Create: docs/runbooks/local-dev.md Create: docs/runbooks/sqlserver-onboarding.md Create: docs/runbooks/alert-tuning.md 8. Files likely to change in implementation Product/docs README.md docs/product/requirements.md docs/product/phased-roadmap.md docs/architecture/overview.md docs/architecture/database-observability.md docs/architecture/session-analysis.md docs/architecture/alerting.md docs/runbooks/*.md API/UI apps/api/internal/** apps/web/src/pages/** apps/web/src/components/** Services services/db-collector/internal/** services/otel-gateway/internal/** services/session-analyzer/internal/** services/reporting/internal/** Shared packages packages/config-schema/src/** packages/telemetry-contracts/src/** Infra infra/docker-compose.yml infra/k8s/** infra/prometheus/** infra/grafana/** infra/loki/** infra/otel-collector/** infra/alertmanager/** Tests tests/integration/** tests/e2e/** tests/performance/** 9. Test and validation strategy 9.1 Unit tests SQL normalization logic metric labeling/schema validation adaptive threshold calculations session correlation rules report rendering logic 9.2 Integration tests collector -\u003e metrics backend OTLP ingest -\u003e normalized store API -\u003e metadata DB alert rules -\u003e Alertmanager routing investigation query -\u003e logs + metrics evidence assembly 9.3 End-to-end tests onboard OutSystems telemetry source ingest sample OutSystems logs/events see application health in Grafana investigate by user/IP/time range onboard SQL Server target as second capability trigger alert generate/download report 9.4 Performance / resilience tests high-cardinality telemetry labels slow or unreachable SQL Server targets burst log ingestion alert storm conditions storage retention pressure 9.5 MVP exit criteria One OutSystems application/environment can be onboarded end-to-end SRE can see application health, errors, latency, and correlated Loki logs in Grafana Session analysis returns useful correlated evidence for a real OutSystems incident sample SQL Server collector is implemented as second capability or at least fully designed and ready for implementation Static + adaptive alerting both function and are auditable Weekly report runs automatically and exports successfully 10. Risks, tradeoffs, open questions Risks OutSystems telemetry quality may vary by deployment/version and available export paths Session analysis can become fuzzy if source systems lack stable correlation ids Loki requires careful label design; high-cardinality user/session/IP labels can damage performance Database-specific observability can sprawl fast -\u003e keep SQL Server second and focused Adaptive alerting can degrade trust if it is opaque Building both a UI and deep integrations can spread the team thin Tradeoffs Grafana/Loki reuse =\u003e faster delivery, less custom UI/search freedom OutSystems-first priority =\u003e faster application value, but delays DB observability proof SQL polling =\u003e strong DB insights, but query safety/load management is critical OTel-first =\u003e interoperability, but upstream data quality may vary a lot Open questions Which OutSystems version/deployment model is phase 1 targeting? What OutSystems telemetry export paths are available today? Which user/session/request identifiers are reliably present in OutSystems logs? Do we need traces in MVP or only metrics/logs? Do reports need email delivery in MVP or is in-app download enough? What auth model is required: local RBAC, SSO/OIDC, or both? What scale should MVP target: number of environments, apps, daily log volume, retention? Is SQL Server definitely the only phase-2 DB engine? 11. Recommended delivery order Requirements + architecture docs Repo bootstrap + local infra PostgreSQL schema + migrations for environments, applications, telemetry sources, investigations, alerts, reports OutSystems telemetry source onboarding OutSystems normalization + Loki/Prometheus ingestion Grafana dashboards for OutSystems application health SQL Server collector + normalized metrics SQL Server Grafana dashboards Session investigation API/UI Static alert policy provisioning to Prometheus/Alertmanager Adaptive baseline calculation v1 Reporting Security/operability hardening Generic OTLP/MuleSoft/additional sources after the OutSystems + SQL Server paths are proven This order gets operator value early through application observability, then adds SQL Server coverage as the second priority, then expands correlation, alerting, reporting, and additional sources. 12. Suggested first milestone Milestone: OutSystems Application Observability Slice onboard one OutSystems application/environment ingest representative logs/events/metrics normalize user/session/request identifiers store metadata in PostgreSQL route logs to Loki and derived metrics to Prometheus visualize health/errors/latency in Grafana run one user/IP/time-range investigation document onboarding + Loki label rules If this slice is weak, the application-first product thesis is weak. If it is strong, SQL Server observability becomes a powerful second layer instead of the only initial value proposition."},{"t":"Archive","u":"archive/index.html","h":null,"x":"Archive Superseded planning documents, kept for history. Do not treat them as current. Their lasting content has moved into the live docs. Document What it was Where its content lives now original-plan.md The first vision: a database monitoring tool with Grafana, a backoffice and adaptive alerting Scope: product overview. SQL Server metrics list: signal coverage. Open questions: product overview. Collector HA and Alloy steps: database observability. 2026-05-30-mvp-implementation-plan.md The detailed MVP implementation plan the repository was bootstrapped from (previously .hermes/plans/) Goals, non-goals, exit criteria, risks: product overview. Technology choices: ADR 0004. Service responsibilities and data flows: operator workflows. PostgreSQL model and ER diagram: data model and ADR 0001. Config reload design: ADR 0002. Guardrails: architecture overview. Phases and tasks: roadmap and TODO.md. Known differences from the live design: Both plans put OutSystems before SQL Server; the SQL Server collector was built first. The MVP plan had the collector read targets from the API/PostgreSQL; the current design reads them from YAML (ADR 0002). The original plan's MVP slice used PostgreSQL and MySQL connectors and a collector-service/ / backoffice-api/ layout; neither was adopted. The migrated schema adds investigations.application_id, which the MVP plan's ER diagram lacks."},{"t":"Heartbeat Plan: Database Monitoring Tool with Grafana","u":"archive/original-plan.html","a":true,"h":[{"t":"Goal","id":"goal"},{"t":"Current context and assumptions","id":"current-context-and-assumptions"},{"t":"Proposed architecture","id":"proposed-architecture"},{"t":"Step-by-step implementation plan","id":"step-by-step-implementation-plan"},{"t":"Files/components likely to change (implementation phase)","id":"filescomponents-likely-to-change-implementation-phase"},{"t":"Test and verification plan","id":"test-and-verification-plan"},{"t":"Risks and tradeoffs","id":"risks-and-tradeoffs"},{"t":"Open questions","id":"open-questions"},{"t":"Suggested MVP slice (4–6 weeks)","id":"suggested-mvp-slice-46-weeks"}],"x":"Heartbeat Plan: Database Monitoring Tool with Grafana Archived. Superseded planning document kept for history; see docs/archive/README.md for where its content lives now. Goal Design and implement a database monitoring platform that visualizes metrics in Grafana, supports adaptive alerting and reporting, and provides a backoffice for operational management (new DB connections, query changes, etc.), while ensuring collectors are not installed on the database server itself. Current context and assumptions Primary visualization layer: Grafana. Non-invasive collection requirement: no agent/collector installed on DB hosts. Multiple database engines may need support over time (e.g., PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, MongoDB). Prometheus and/or Mimir are acceptable for metrics storage/querying. Backoffice must allow runtime configuration updates without redeploying the whole stack. Security and network access to DBs will be managed from a separate monitoring environment. File-based ingestion may be supported later as a last-resort fallback when direct database connectivity is not feasible; it is explicitly lower priority than live collection from SQL Server or other database instances. Proposed architecture Collection layer (off-DB hosts): Dedicated collector services running in a monitoring VPC/cluster. DB metric collection via read-only SQL queries and/or exporter endpoints from a remote host. Pluggable “connector” model per DB type. Metrics pipeline: Prometheus for scraping and rule evaluation. Long-term, horizontally scalable metrics storage with Mimir. Alertmanager for routing alerts. Visualization and analysis: Grafana dashboards for DB health, performance, and SLOs. Grafana Alerting for dashboard-driven rules where appropriate. Adaptive alerting engine: Baseline/anomaly logic using rolling windows (hour/day/week seasonality). Dynamic thresholds (e.g., median + k*MAD, quantile bands, EWMA deviations). Suppression/correlation to reduce alert noise. Reporting: Scheduled daily/weekly reports (PDF/HTML/CSV) with KPI trends, incidents, and top regressions. Backoffice: Web app + API for CRUD on database connections, query templates, alert policies, notification routing, report schedules, and RBAC. Audit logs for all admin actions. Step-by-step implementation plan Define scope and SLOs Decide initial DB engines and mandatory metrics per engine. SQL Server Metrics: CPU, Memory, I/O, IOPs, User Transactions, Wait Events, Locks, Cache Hit Ratio, Connections, Errors, Latency, Availability, Rollbacks, Active Sessions, Sessions, Physical Reads/sec, Physical Writes/sec, Logical Reads/sec, Logical Writes/sec, Free Space, Database Size Define alerting objectives (MTTA, MTTR, false-positive tolerance). Define report consumers and cadence. Weekly summary report generation and email delivery. Define target deployment model Choose runtime: Kubernetes for MVP. Split components: collector-service, Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, backoffice API/UI, Mimir (or consider alternatives like Thanos or VictoriaMetrics). Build metrics collection service (remote) Implement connection manager for DB targets with encrypted secrets. Implement query scheduler and execution workers per DB connector. Export metrics in Prometheus exposition format. Add health metrics for the collector itself (query latency, failures, staleness). Defer file-ingestion adapters until after the primary live-collection path is stable; position them as fallback-only. Implement configuration/backoffice domain model Entities: DatabaseConnection, MetricQuery, QueryTemplate, AlertPolicy, NotificationChannel, ReportSchedule, User/Role, AuditEvent. CRUD APIs with validation and versioning for query changes. UI screens: connection onboarding, query editor/tester, policy editor, schedule management. Integrate Prometheus/Mimir Configure Prometheus scrape jobs for collector instances. Define recording rules for normalized metrics and aggregations. Optionally wire remote_write to Mimir for retention \u003e30 days and HA scale. Build Grafana dashboards Provision dashboards as code (JSON/Terraform) for reproducibility. Create overview + deep-dive dashboards per DB type. Include panels for availability, throughput, errors, latency, locks, replication, storage, connection saturation. Implement adaptive alerting Start with hybrid strategy: Static guardrails for critical hard limits. Dynamic anomaly bands for variable workloads. Build baseline jobs to compute rolling statistics. Add debounce, cooldown, and multi-signal correlation. Route alerts via Alertmanager/Grafana channels (Slack/Email/PagerDuty). Implement reporting subsystem Scheduled report generation using templated queries. Include trend deltas, anomalies, SLO burn, top-N regressions, muted alerts summary. Deliver via email and store report artifacts for backoffice download. Security, compliance, and reliability hardening Secrets management (Vault/KMS/SSM), per-tenant encryption. RBAC, SSO/OIDC for backoffice and Grafana. Audit trails and immutable action logs. Rate-limiting and query sandboxing to protect DB targets. Implement collector failure isolation, bounded retries, freshness signals, safe reloads, and deployment recovery before adding replicas. Define target ownership/takeover and downstream resilience against explicit recovery-time and data-gap objectives; validate failure scenarios before claiming HA. Follow the planned collector HA design. Testing and validation Unit tests for connectors, query parser/validator, anomaly calculations. Integration tests with containerized DB fixtures. Load tests for metric ingestion and alert throughput. Chaos tests for DB unavailability, network partitions, slow queries. Rollout strategy MVP in staging with 1–2 DB engines and a few production-like targets. Observe false-positive/false-negative rates; tune adaptive alerting. Progressive production rollout by team or environment. Late-roadmap custom collector / Alloy comparison Once the core workflow and reliability baseline are validated, run a parallel comparison on a dedicated branch using equivalent non-production SQL Server workloads and isolated telemetry. Compare coverage, custom probes/evidence, safety, database load, recovery, configuration integration, and operational cost; assess future Oracle support separately. Keep both implementations available as an option if distinct needs justify it, with explicit per-target ownership and a shared telemetry contract. No replacement or production adoption is decided yet. Follow the comparison design and decision criteria. Files/components likely to change (implementation phase) infra/ docker-compose.yml or k8s/*.yaml prometheus/prometheus.yml prometheus/rules/*.yml alertmanager/alertmanager.yml grafana/provisioning/datasources/*.yaml grafana/provisioning/dashboards/*.yaml grafana/dashboards/*.json collector-service/ connectors/* scheduler/* metrics_exporter/* config/* backoffice-api/ models/* routes/* services/* migrations/* backoffice-ui/ pages/connections/* pages/queries/* pages/alerts/* pages/reports/* reporting-service/ jobs/* templates/* delivery/* docs/ architecture.md runbooks/*.md Test and verification plan Functional Verify a new DB connection can be created and validated from backoffice. Verify query changes are versioned, approved (if needed), and applied safely. Verify dashboards populate within expected scrape intervals. Alerting Simulate threshold breach and anomaly patterns. Validate deduplication/suppression and routing behavior. Reporting Verify schedules trigger correctly and reports include expected sections/data. Non-functional Confirm no software deployment on DB servers. Measure end-to-end latency from metric generation to dashboard/alert. Validate security controls (RBAC, audit, secret handling). Risks and tradeoffs Adaptive alerting complexity can increase operational overhead; start simple and iterate. Remote SQL polling may add DB load if query design is poor; enforce query budgets and sampling limits. Multi-DB support increases connector maintenance; prioritize by business impact. File-based ingestion reduces dependency on direct connectivity but increases freshness, validation, and operator workflow risks; keep it as a last-resort path. Mimir improves scale/retention but adds infra complexity; defer for MVP unless required. Open questions Which DB engines and versions are in phase 1? Expected scale: number of DB instances, scrape interval, retention period? Preferred notification channels and on-call tooling? Is multi-tenancy required in backoffice/Grafana from day one? Compliance constraints (PII handling, retention, audit requirements)? Suggested MVP slice (4–6 weeks) PostgreSQL + MySQL connectors. Prometheus + Grafana + Alertmanager (Mimir optional later). Backoffice: connection CRUD, query CRUD, basic alert policy CRUD. Adaptive alerting v1: static + simple rolling baseline. Weekly summary report generation and email delivery."},{"t":"Onboarding SQL Server Targets","u":"guides/database-targets.html","h":[{"t":"1. Get a login from the DBAs","id":"1-get-a-login-from-the-dbas"},{"t":"Collector login permissions","id":"collector-login-permissions"},{"t":"Certificates","id":"certificates"},{"t":"OutSystems logging database","id":"outsystems-logging-database"},{"t":"What the collector runs on the server","id":"what-the-collector-runs-on-the-server"},{"t":"2. Add the target","id":"2-add-the-target"},{"t":"Through the API","id":"through-the-api"},{"t":"By hand","id":"by-hand"},{"t":"3. Provide the credential","id":"3-provide-the-credential"},{"t":"4. Apply the change","id":"4-apply-the-change"},{"t":"5. Verify","id":"5-verify"}],"x":"Onboarding SQL Server Targets How to add, change or remove a monitored SQL Server database. Monitored targets are configured in config/integrations.yaml, not in PostgreSQL or a UI. Onboarding a database is a config change (ADR 0002). All keys are described in the configuration reference. SQL Server is the only supported engine. Adding PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle or MongoDB targets requires a new collector kind and connector in code, not just config. 1. Get a login from the DBAs Production targets use a dedicated observability login, never sa or any other sysadmin login. Collector login permissions The built-in probes need SQL Server 2012 or later (the newest feature they use is CONCAT); CI runs them against SQL Server 2022 on Linux. They are read-only and need: Permission Needed for VIEW SERVER STATE sys.dm_os_wait_stats, sys.dm_exec_requests, sys.dm_exec_sessions, sys.dm_os_performance_counters, sys.dm_os_ring_buffers, sys.dm_os_sys_info, sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats. Every built-in probe was verified as a login holding only VIEW SERVER STATE and VIEW ANY DEFINITION. SQL Server 2022's narrower VIEW SERVER PERFORMANCE STATE has not been verified on its own, so grant VIEW SERVER STATE. VIEW ANY DEFINITION sys.master_files for the storage and file_io probes. Without it, file sizes and file I/O are silently missing. VIEW ANY DATABASE (granted to public by default) Database names (DB_NAME()) in the storage and file_io database_name label. If it was revoked, files of databases the login cannot see are labelled database_id:\u003cid\u003e instead. Access to the target's database_name Used as the initial database No write, db_owner or sysadmin rights are required. make test-sqlserver and make kind-e2e run every built-in probe as a login holding only these grants. A query_template override runs with the same login; review it against the probe review checklist, and with the DBAs first if it needs more than these permissions. The collector rejects an override that is not one read-only SELECT (rules), but that text check is only a safety net: these read-only grants are what stop a probe from changing anything, so never give the collector's login write rights. For the DBAs, the T-SQL for such a login (SQL Server authentication; pick your own name and a strong password): scripts/sqlserver-login.sh creates the same login in the local sandbox, and refuses one that ends up sysadmin-equivalent. If the login is sysadmin-equivalent anyway (a member of sysadmin, or holding CONTROL SERVER), the collector logs a warning (with the target and credential_ref) and exports heartbeat_collector_target_login_sysadmin as 1 (metrics reference). It checks when it first connects to the target and about every 10 minutes after, so a change to the login's rights shows up within that time. If SQL Server cannot tell, the collector logs that once per check and exports no value. The HeartbeatCollectorLoginElevated alert fires when the metric has been 1 for 15 minutes. Certificates Connections use encrypt=true and verify the server's certificate. It must be issued for the target's host and chain to a public CA in the collector's image (Debian's ca-certificates). Otherwise every probe fails: x509: certificate is not valid for any names, but wanted to match \u003chost\u003e for SQL Server's default self-signed certificate, or x509: certificate signed by unknown authority. Trusting a private CA is not supported yet: the chart has no setting to add one to the collector. The chart value dbCollector.sqlserver.trustServerCertificate: true (the variable HEARTBEAT_DB_COLLECTOR_SQLSERVER_TRUST_SERVER_CERTIFICATE) turns verification off. It is for the local dev container only: it applies to every target, and the collector logs a warning at startup and lists it in GET /admin/config. OutSystems logging database An outsystems_logs collector reads the OutSystems 11 logging database with its own login, which needs SELECT on the oslog_* views its log-type queries read (oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e and oslog_\u003cTYPE\u003e_Previous; the views and their cycle tables share an owner, so no grant on the tables), access to that database, and nothing else: no server permission. make test-sqlserver runs every log-type query as such a login. It runs the same login check and session settings as below. Settings are in the configuration reference. What the collector runs on the server At most 2 sessions per target, with the application name HeartbeatDBCollector. When it first connects to a target, and about every 10 minutes after, one SELECT IS_SRVROLEMEMBER('sysadmin'), HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME(NULL, NULL,\n'CONTROL SERVER'). It needs no extra permission. Each probe query, in one batch prefixed with SET LOCK_TIMEOUT 1000; SET DEADLOCK_PRIORITY LOW;. A probe waits at most 1 second for a lock, then fails with error 1222 for that cycle (logged, the target is reported failed and retried on the next cycle). In a deadlock with an application session at the default (NORMAL) or a higher priority, SQL Server picks the collector as the victim; against another LOW session it picks the one cheaper to roll back, which for a read-only probe is usually the collector. query_template overrides get the same prefix, so a template must be a query, not a bare stored procedure name (see the probe review checklist). 1 second is well below the default probe timeout (half the scrape interval, at most 10 seconds), so a lock wait fails fast with a specific error instead of holding a place in the lock queue, ahead of application requests, until the probe deadline. 2. Add the target There are two ways: through the API, or by hand in the values. Through the API Register the target at /api/v1/database-targets, assign probes to it at /api/v1/probe-assignments, and have an admin approve each assignment (ADR 0014). The API renders the approved ones into the chart's managedCollectors, one collector per target; all of a target's assignments run at one interval. On kind, make kind-assignments deploys the render, and later make kind-deploy runs keep it. In production the pull request to heartbeat-deploy that carries it waits for that repository [18]; until then, add production targets by hand. Provide the credential (step 3) before the deploy; an assignment shows applied once the collector runs it. Do not add a target to both: the collector refuses a target that two enabled collectors scrape in one environment, and keeps its last good config (step 5 shows how to tell). By hand Add an entry under collectors[].config.targets[] of the environment's integrations values (the chart renders them into integrations.yaml; see config by environment). Lists replace rather than merge across values files, so restate the whole collectors list: To run only some targets of a collector, list them in config.target_names. Collector ids that start with api- are reserved for the collectors the API renders. To change a target, edit its entry: host/port for a new server, database_name for another database on the same server, credential_ref for a new login. To remove one, delete the entry; the collector stops exporting its series after the reload. 3. Provide the credential credential_ref: env/finance-prod is read from the key HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_ENV_FINANCE_PROD, with the value username:password (resolution rules). Keep values out of version control. Put the key in a Secret and name it in dbCollector.credentials.existingSecret. The chart mounts that Secret into the collector container as files, which the collector reads on every connection: adding a key for a new target, or changing a password, needs no restart. The kubelet updates the files within about a minute. Where the collector runs How the Secret is created kind kubectl -n heartbeat create secret generic \u003cname\u003e --from-literal=HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_ENV_FINANCE_PROD=\u003cuser\u003e:\u003cpassword\u003e kind SQL Server sandbox Already wired: make sqlserver-dev-up creates it from the ignored .env.sqlserver-dev Production An External Secrets Operator ExternalSecret from AWS Secrets Manager (ADR 0005) 4. Apply the change Where Steps kind Put the values in a file and run EXTRA_VALUES=\u003cfile\u003e make kind-deploy; for approved probe assignments, make kind-assignments. Installed from a release Put the values in a file and run helm upgrade with it and all your other values files (installing a release). Production Commit the values to the heartbeat-deploy repository; Argo CD syncs them. Helm rejects values that fail the integrations schema before anything changes. A config-only change does not restart the collector: the ConfigMap reaches the pod within about a minute (kubelet sync) and the collector picks it up through file polling (5s). To apply it sooner, POST /admin/config/reload with the admin token. A change to the credentials Secret needs no restart either: the files reach the pod the same way, and the next connection uses them. An invalid file is rejected and the previous config keeps running, so the change has not taken effect: step 5 shows how to tell. 5. Verify First check that the collector took the change. Helm accepting the values does not mean the collector did: it also checks host names, credential references, intervals and probe names. heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_successful should be 1, and heartbeat_collector_config_info should show a new version. At 0 the change was rejected and the last good config still runs, and after 2 minutes Prometheus fires HeartbeatCollectorConfigReloadFailed, so a rejection shows in alerting even when nobody runs this check (config reload metrics). The reason is last_reload_err in GET /admin/config and in the pod log; fix the values and apply them again. Then check the target: heartbeat_collector_target_up should be 1, and heartbeat_sqlserver_* series should appear with target=\"finance-prod\". In Grafana, open the SQL Server overview dashboard and select the target. If the target stays down, the target's error above shows the driver's message (login, host or certificate); the collector's JSON logs have one line per probe failure, with collector, target and probe. Repeated failures back off exponentially up to 5 minutes, so a fix can take that long to show up unless you reload."},{"t":"Installing a Release","u":"guides/install-from-release.html","h":[{"t":"What you need","id":"what-you-need"},{"t":"1. Create the namespace and Secrets","id":"1-create-the-namespace-and-secrets"},{"t":"2. Pin the release","id":"2-pin-the-release"},{"t":"3. Install","id":"3-install"},{"t":"4. Check that it works","id":"4-check-that-it-works"},{"t":"5. Add a SQL Server target","id":"5-add-a-sql-server-target"},{"t":"Upgrade","id":"upgrade"},{"t":"Uninstall","id":"uninstall"}],"x":"Installing a Release How to install, upgrade and remove Heartbeat from a published GitHub release, with Helm and no source checkout. Each release publishes: Artifact Where Helm chart oci://ghcr.io/gdpais/charts/heartbeat, version X.Y.Z DB collector image ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/db-collector:X.Y.Z, for linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 API image ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/api:X.Y.Z, for linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 All are public: no registry login is needed. The release notes list each artifact's digest. The chart also installs pinned Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, Loki, Tempo and OpenTelemetry Collector from their own registries, and the API's PostgreSQL and Valkey from their official images (what the chart deploys). Those two are for trying Heartbeat out: one replica each and no backups. Production points the API at managed services instead (production example, ADR 0015). To work on Heartbeat itself, use local development instead. Production deploys the same chart from ECR through Argo CD (ADR 0005). What you need A Kubernetes cluster. CI tests Kubernetes 1.36 on kind. Helm 4.2 and kubectl. The chart also renders with Helm 3, but installs are tested with Helm 4. A default StorageClass. The release asks for seven volumes, 23.25 GiB in all (listed under Uninstall). Memory: the pods request about 1.0 GiB, and their limits add up to about 4.2 GiB. A CNI that enforces NetworkPolicy (kindnet, Calico, Cilium, Amazon VPC CNI with network policy on). Without one the chart's policies are accepted and drop nothing. To limit where the collector connects, set dbCollector.networkPolicy.egress with your databases' subnets, and keep its Service ClusterIP (network access). The commands below use the namespace heartbeat and the release name heartbeat. Any names work, but install one release per namespace: the object names are fixed. 1. Create the namespace and Secrets The chart never creates Secrets (ADR 0005), so create these first. Without them the collector, API, PostgreSQL, Valkey and Grafana pods wait in CreateContainerConfigError or ContainerCreating. PostgreSQL reads its password once, when it creates the database on its volume; changing it later takes an ALTER ROLE as well as both Secrets. The values reach kubectl through a pipe, not its command line. To read one back: kubectl -n heartbeat get secret grafana-admin -o jsonpath='{.data.admin-password}' | base64 -d. Kubernetes delivery lists every Secret the chart reads and where kind and production get them. 2. Pin the release Put your values in a file and keep it: every helm upgrade passes it again. Start with the image digest from the release notes' Published artifacts table: Without them the chart runs ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/db-collector:X.Y.Z and ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/api:X.Y.Z, the images of its own version. A digest names exactly the build the release lists; a digest wins over a tag. 3. Install Helm prints Pulled: ghcr.io/gdpais/charts/heartbeat:X.Y.Z and a Digest: sha256:...; check it against the chart row of the release notes. It then prints the chart's notes: the Secrets it needs, how to check the install and how to reach Grafana. helm show readme oci://ghcr.io/gdpais/charts/heartbeat --version \"$VERSION\" prints the chart's README. If a value was wrong, fix it in heartbeat.yaml and upgrade with it: A collector pod that never became Ready is replaced by that upgrade; there is no pod to delete by hand. 4. Check that it works The admin call prints version, the SHA-256 of the active config, the readiness status, and warnings, empty unless an insecure setting is on: Use free local ports. If something on your machine already holds 127.0.0.1:8082 or :3000, kubectl port-forward listens on [::1] only, without an error, and localhost may reach the other program. Then forward another port, for example 18082:8082, and use it in the URLs. Then open http://localhost:3000 and log in with the grafana-admin Secret's user and password. The Heartbeat folder holds the dashboards. They show data once a SQL Server target is added (next step). The API has no default user. Create the first admin in an API pod, with the password on standard input, so it never reaches a command line or the shell history (api users create-admin): It logs the user's ID and its audit record's ID. The record reaches Loki through the OTel Collector within seconds; in Grafana's Explore: {service_name=\"heartbeat-api\"} | event_name=\"heartbeat.audit\". /readyz says whether the collector itself works, not the databases: it stays ready while every SQL Server target fails (endpoints). Symptom Cause and fix CreateContainerConfigError on db-collector-0, valkey-0 or the Grafana pod A Secret from step 1 is missing or lacks a key; kubectl -n heartbeat describe pod \u003cpod\u003e names it. Create it; the pod then starts by itself The API pod in Init:Error or Init:CrashLoopBackOff Its migrate init container could not apply the migrations. On a first install it fails a few times, about a minute in all, until PostgreSQL accepts connections, then succeeds by itself. Otherwise kubectl -n heartbeat logs deployment/api -c migrate says why, for example a password that does not match heartbeat-postgresql The API pod Running but not ready kubectl -n heartbeat logs deployment/api -c api has a readiness changed line naming the check that fails: PostgreSQL, the schema version, or Redis (Valkey) db-collector-0 stays ContainerCreating with a FailedMount event The Secret named in dbCollector.credentials.existingSecret is missing. Create it (step 5); the pod then starts by itself postgresql-0 stays ContainerCreating, or the API pod Init:0/1, with a FailedMount event heartbeat-postgresql or heartbeat-api-credentials from step 1 is missing: both are mounted as files. Create it; the pod then starts by itself ImagePullBackOff or ErrImagePull A digest in heartbeat.yaml is wrong, or the cluster cannot reach ghcr.io (or Docker Hub, quay.io for the engines) Pods Pending with an unbound claim No default StorageClass. Make one the default, or name a class for each claim in the values values don't meet the specifications of the schema A value is misspelt or out of range; the message gives its path. Unknown keys are rejected too, at the top level and inside dbCollector, the security contexts and the chart's other own components 5. Add a SQL Server target Onboarding SQL Server targets is the reference: the login and its grants, with the T-SQL to create it (step 1), the target entry (step 2) and the credential (step 3). With a release install: In a second values file, targets.yaml, name that Secret and write the whole integrations.collectors list as in step 2, with the target under targets. Lists replace rather than merge, so the list must be complete. Apply it with every values file, in the same order each time: Naming the Secret for the first time restarts the collector to mount it, which ends the step 4 port-forward (error: lost connection to pod): start it again. Later changes need no restart: the target list hot-reloads, and a key added to the Secret, or a changed password, reaches the collector within about a minute, because it reads the mounted files on every connection. Then check the target as in step 5. The collector verifies the server's TLS certificate. SQL Server's default self-signed certificate fails with x509: certificate is not valid for any\nnames, and a certificate from a private CA cannot be trusted yet (certificates). Upgrade Read the release notes first, and set the new digest in heartbeat.yaml. Check that your values pass the new chart's schema, which touches nothing in the cluster, then upgrade: Pass every values file you have, in the same order each time (targets.yaml exists once you have done step 5), rather than --reuse-values, so the new chart's defaults apply to everything you did not set. --rollback-on-failure (--atomic in Helm 3) rolls a failed upgrade back to the last revision that worked: Helm applies that revision's objects again and deletes the ones the failed upgrade added. Without it, a failed upgrade is left half done. Helm applies every object it can, keeps the objects the new version removes, and marks the revision failed. Fix the cause and upgrade again, or go back with helm rollback heartbeat. Grafana keeps a provisioned dashboard after a release removes its file, as a safeguard. The releases since 0.1.0 replace Heartbeat Overview with Heartbeat Service; delete the old one by hand if you had it. From 0.1.0. Do these once, before the first upgrade, in this order: Update your values files. The release notes list the values renamed or removed since 0.1.0. Remove otelGateway: the OTel gateway is gone (ADR 0009), and unknown top-level values now fail. The helm template check above must pass before the next step, so that the upgrade cannot fail on the values after it. If you repoint otlp_http/logs at another platform, repoint otlp_http/self-logs too (example), or the services' own logs go to a Loki that is not there. Delete the collector's StatefulSet, leaving its pod running: It now uses podManagementPolicy: Parallel, so it can replace a pod that never became Ready, and Kubernetes cannot change that field on an existing StatefulSet. Run the upgrade above. It creates the StatefulSet again; the new StatefulSet adopts the running pod and replaces it with the new version. If it fails and rolls back, the rollback brings back the 0.1.0 StatefulSet: repeat step 2 before the next attempt. An upgrade attempted without step 2 fails with StatefulSet.apps \"db-collector\" is invalid: spec: Forbidden. With --rollback-on-failure, Helm then returns to 0.1.0; do steps 2 and 3. Without it, Helm has applied everything else and removed nothing: the ConfigMaps are the new ones, integrations.yaml included; Grafana and Loki run the new version, and their pods restarted; the new NetworkPolicies, the db-collector-state and otel-collector-queue claims and the new OTel Collector StatefulSet exist, beside the old OTel Collector Deployment and the OTel gateway, which still run. The 0.1.0 collector rejects the new config (invalid candidate config in its log) and keeps collecting with its old one. Do not restart it: it reads the new file at startup and would exit. Do steps 2 and 3. The second upgrade completes the change and removes the old objects. Uninstall This deletes every object the release created, but not the claims Kubernetes created for the StatefulSets' volume templates, nor what you created yourself: Left behind Size Holds PersistentVolumeClaim/storage-loki-0 10 GiB The logs in Loki PersistentVolumeClaim/storage-tempo-0 2 GiB The traces in Tempo PersistentVolumeClaim/storage-alertmanager-0 1 GiB Alertmanager's silences and notification log The step 1 Secrets and your credentials Secret  Step 1 and step 5 Namespace/heartbeat   The other claims are deleted with the release, and their data with them: prometheus (8 GiB, the metric history), postgresql-data (1 GiB, the API's database), db-collector-state (256 MiB, the outsystems_logs watermarks) and otel-collector-queue (1 GiB). A new install in the same namespace reuses the three claims left behind, so Loki, Tempo and Alertmanager start with their old data. To remove everything: With the usual Delete reclaim policy, deleting a claim also deletes its volume and data."},{"t":"Walkthrough: Run and Test Heartbeat on kind","u":"guides/kind-walkthrough.html","h":[{"t":"1. Prepare your machine","id":"1-prepare-your-machine"},{"t":"2. Start the platform","id":"2-start-the-platform"},{"t":"3. Look around","id":"3-look-around"},{"t":"4. Add a SQL Server target","id":"4-add-a-sql-server-target"},{"t":"5. Change the config without a restart","id":"5-change-the-config-without-a-restart"},{"t":"6. Break the database, not the collector","id":"6-break-the-database-not-the-collector"},{"t":"7. Ship a code change","id":"7-ship-a-code-change"},{"t":"8. Try the small profile","id":"8-try-the-small-profile"},{"t":"9. Run the automated acceptance checks","id":"9-run-the-automated-acceptance-checks"},{"t":"10. Clean up","id":"10-clean-up"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","id":"troubleshooting"}],"x":"Walkthrough: Run and Test Heartbeat on kind A guided first run of the full stack on your machine, about 30 minutes. Each step says what to run and what you should see. The local development guide is the reference for the same commands; Kubernetes delivery explains what the chart deploys. 1. Prepare your machine Need Version macOS (Homebrew) Docker Desktop (or Docker Engine on Linux) any current release; cgroup v2 brew install --cask docker kind v0.33.x brew install kind kubectl 1.35–1.37 brew install kubectl Helm 4.2 or later brew install helm Go 1.27+ brew install go jq, openssl, curl any brew install jq (openssl and curl ship with macOS) Docker settings: Give Docker at least 4 CPUs and 6 GB of memory. The full profile uses about 2 GB, SQL Server about 1 GB, and image builds need headroom. On Apple silicon, enable Use Rosetta for x86_64/amd64 emulation. SQL Server only ships an AMD64 image. These ports must be free on 127.0.0.1: 3001, 4317, 4318, 8082, 9090, 9093 and 11433. Then, from the repository root: Every line should start with ok. Any FAIL line says what to install or change. 2. Start the platform The first make kind-up takes several minutes while Docker pulls the node and engine images. It ends by listing pods and services; all six pods should be 1/1 Running: Check the endpoints: Each check prints a body; the command fails on the first one that does not answer. 3. Look around Open What you should see http://localhost:3001 (Grafana, admin/admin) Dashboards → Heartbeat folder with Heartbeat Service, SQL Server Overview and the four OutSystems dashboards (empty until an outsystems_logs collector runs, for example with PatientZero); Connections → Data sources lists Prometheus and Loki http://localhost:9090/targets (Prometheus) Six jobs, all UP: alertmanager, db-collector, loki, otel-collector, prometheus, tempo http://localhost:9090/alerts Watchdog firing. It always fires; that is how a dead-man's switch knows alerting works http://localhost:9093 (Alertmanager) The Watchdog alert, received from Prometheus http://localhost:8082/readyz {\"status\":\"ready\"}; the details need the admin token (next section) So far the collector monitors nothing: the default config has no SQL Server targets. 4. Add a SQL Server target The first run pulls the SQL Server image (about 1.5 GB). The container, heartbeat-sqlserver-dev, runs next to the cluster, not in it, and the collector reaches it by name. Within a minute: shows the target sqlserver-dev with \"state\": \"ok\". Then: In Prometheus, query heartbeat_collector_target_up: value 1 for target=\"sqlserver-dev\". In Grafana, open SQL Server Overview and pick sqlserver-dev. The panels fill in as samples arrive (15s scrape interval). 5. Change the config without a restart The collector hot-reloads integrations.yaml. Note the current version, then deploy a change (here, a 30s scrape interval): Run the first command again until the version changes. It usually takes 10 to 60 seconds: kubelet updates the mounted ConfigMap, then the collector polls it every 5s. Confirm the pod was not replaced: its AGE keeps counting. To apply a config change immediately instead of waiting: make kind-deploy without EXTRA_VALUES puts the 15s interval back. 6. Break the database, not the collector Within about 20 seconds GET /admin/config shows the target as \"failed\", with the driver's error, but the collector itself stays ready (/readyz answers 200), and Prometheus shows heartbeat_collector_target_up at 0. The collector is not restarted: a database outage must never take down monitoring. The target returns to \"ok\" within about 30 seconds of SQL Server accepting connections. 7. Ship a code change Change something in services/db-collector (even a log message), then: The image tag (heartbeat/db-collector:dev-\u003cid\u003e) changes with the binary, and the collector is replaced by a new pod. The old pod stops before the new one starts, so the database is never polled twice. 8. Try the small profile 9. Run the automated acceptance checks This creates a second, temporary cluster (heartbeat-e2e-\u003cid\u003e) with its own SQL Server, runs every check above and more (invalid config, rollouts, persistence after pod replacement, an OTel Collector restart during two log backlogs of 45 minutes and three hours), prints measurements, and deletes what it created. Your heartbeat cluster is untouched. It takes about 10 minutes and ends with All kind e2e checks passed. CI runs the same script on every pull request. 10. Clean up Before you clean up, the live demo goes deeper on the same cluster: the collector's exact SQL, raw rows against exported samples, blocking, a frozen server and the parts that are not implemented yet. Troubleshooting See troubleshooting in the local development guide."},{"t":"Kubernetes Delivery (Helm on kind)","u":"guides/kubernetes-local.html","h":[{"t":"Layout","id":"layout"},{"t":"What the chart deploys","id":"what-the-chart-deploys"},{"t":"Network access","id":"network-access"},{"t":"Secrets","id":"secrets"},{"t":"Images","id":"images"},{"t":"The cluster","id":"the-cluster"},{"t":"Working with it","id":"working-with-it"},{"t":"Production","id":"production"}],"x":"Kubernetes Delivery (Helm on kind) Heartbeat is packaged as one Helm chart, used unchanged for kind, CI and production with environment-specific values (ADR 0003, ADR 0005). To just run it, see local development. This page explains what is deployed and how to work with it. Layout Path Contents infra/helm/heartbeat/ The chart: Heartbeat templates, values.yaml defaults, values.schema.json, Chart.yaml/Chart.lock infra/helm/heartbeat/files/ Prometheus rules (and their promtool tests) and Grafana dashboards, shipped as ConfigMaps infra/helm/values/ Environment values: kind.yaml, minimal.yaml, sqlserver-dev.yaml, production.example.yaml, and external-platform.example.yaml (no bundled engines; the OTel Collector sends everything as OTLP to another platform, ADR 0011) infra/kind/cluster.yaml kind cluster: one node, host port mappings on 127.0.0.1 infra/kind/e2e.yaml The temporary cluster of make kind-e2e: no host ports, and a 10 s kubelet sync so changed ConfigMaps and Secrets arrive sooner scripts/kind.sh Cluster, image and deploy workflow behind the make kind-* targets scripts/kind-e2e.sh Acceptance checks on an isolated, temporary cluster (make kind-e2e) What the chart deploys Heartbeat's own objects (fixed names; one release per namespace): Object Notes StatefulSet/db-collector Always one replica: a replacement pod starts only after the old one is gone, so SQL targets are never polled twice. podManagementPolicy: Parallel, so an upgrade replaces a pod that never became Ready (kubernetes/kubernetes#67250); the field cannot change in place, so an install from chart 0.1.0 deletes the StatefulSet once. Liveness /healthz (process only), readiness /readyz. No config checksum: ConfigMap changes hot-reload without a restart Service/db-collector-headless Headless, publishNotReadyAddresses; Prometheus discovers the collector through it even while it is unready Service/db-collector Operator access (health, admin endpoints); ready pods only. kind: NodePort with externalTrafficPolicy: Local, so the NetworkPolicy sees the host's address NetworkPolicy/db-collector Ingress to port 8082 only from the bundled Prometheus, the API (its GET /admin/config, for whether telemetry sources are observed), and the dbCollector.networkPolicy.scrapers (a shared Prometheus or another platform's scraper) and .operators peers; no peers means deny all. kind adds every source outside the pod subnet 10.244.0.0/16 (the host through the loopback NodePort, and containers on the Docker kind network); the production example adds a VPN range. Kubelet probes and kubectl port-forward are not affected. Needs a CNI that enforces NetworkPolicy (kind's kindnet does) Deployment/api The API (ADR 0013), api.replicas pods. Its init container migrate runs api migrate up from the same image before the API starts, so a new image applies its migrations first; golang-migrate's advisory lock lets replicas start together. No Helm or Argo CD hook. Liveness /healthz (process only), readiness /readyz: PostgreSQL at the newest migration the image embeds, and Redis. Logins from the api.credentials Secret, mounted as files. A 30s grace period covers its shutdown: 5s serving unready while the Service drops it, a 10s drain, the audit drainer's last delete and its last logs Service/api-headless, Service/api As the collector's: the headless one for Prometheus, ready or not; api (8081, ClusterIP) for clients; an ingress in front of it serves the site's origin, api.siteOrigin, which logins must come from (http://localhost:8081 on kind, for kubectl port-forward) StatefulSet/postgresql, Service/postgresql, PersistentVolumeClaim/postgresql-data The API's PostgreSQL on the postgres image (ADR 0015): one replica, data on a 1 GiB claim that a pod restart keeps, readiness pg_isready, user 999, read-only root filesystem. For kind and CI: no backups, replicas or major-version upgrades; production turns it off (postgresql.enabled) for Amazon RDS StatefulSet/valkey, Service/valkey Redis, run as Valkey on valkey/valkey: one replica, an emptyDir (valkey.persistence puts an append-only file on a claim), noeviction below its memory limit, readiness valkey-cli ping. Its password reaches it on standard input, never a command line. Production turns it off for Amazon ElastiCache NetworkPolicy/api, /postgresql, /valkey The API's 8081 from Prometheus and api.networkPolicy.peers; PostgreSQL's 5432 and Valkey's 6379 from the API and their extraPeers only NetworkPolicy/otel-collector, /prometheus, /alertmanager, /loki, /tempo Ingress to each bundled engine only from the components that use it, on their ports; see network access. Rendered only for an engine that is on ConfigMap/heartbeat-integrations integrations.yaml, rendered from the integrations values, with the managedCollectors list appended to its collectors (collectors rendered by the API) ConfigMap/heartbeat-prometheus-rules Rule files; Prometheus mounts them at /etc/prometheus-rules and its config reloader reloads on change. prometheusRules.configReloadAlert.enabled: false leaves out the config reload alert (rules) ConfigMap/heartbeat-dashboards Dashboards; label grafana_dashboard: \"1\" for shared Grafanas with a dashboard sidecar PersistentVolumeClaim/otel-collector-queue The OTel Collector's logs export queue (otelCollectorQueue.persistence, 1 GiB): rows the Collector accepted survive its restart. Only with the Collector, so not in the minimal profile. kind uses its default standard (local-path) class, a directory on the node that lives as long as the cluster. Without persistence the queue is an emptyDir, which survives a container restart but not a new pod Third-party engines are pinned chart dependencies, each turned off with \u003cname\u003e.enabled=false so an environment can use shared services instead: Dependency Version Engine Notes prometheus (prometheus-community) 29.35.0 Prometheus v3.15.0, Alertmanager v0.34.1 Static and DNS discovery only, no cluster RBAC. Sends alerts to Alertmanager grafana (grafana-community) 13.2.7 Grafana 13.2.3 Stateless; datasources and dashboards from source loki (grafana-community) 18.13.7 Loki 3.7.8 Monolithic, filesystem storage locally opentelemetry-collector (alias otelCollector) 0.175.0 otelcol-contrib 0.161.0 One-replica StatefulSet (otel-collector-0), so only one pod at a time opens the export queue. Metrics to Prometheus, logs to Loki through a queue on disk Rules the chart keeps (checked by make chart-check): only namespaced objects, no CRDs; deterministic rendering (no random values, lookups or hooks), because Argo CD renders the chart itself; Secrets created outside the chart and referenced by name; unknown values rejected by the schema, at the top level and inside the chart's own components; renders with Helm 3 and Helm 4. Network access Every Heartbeat component and bundled engine except Grafana has an ingress NetworkPolicy, so a pod in the namespace (or in another one) reaches only what the chart lists. Loki holds raw user IDs, session IDs and client IPs, so who reaches it is part of their protection (ADR 0012). The engine policies are backendNetworkPolicies in the values; the collector's is dbCollector.networkPolicy, the API's api.networkPolicy, and PostgreSQL's and Valkey's their own networkPolicy. Policy Port Admitted from Why db-collector 8082 Prometheus Scrape db-collector 8082 API GET /admin/config with the collector's admin token, for telemetry source observation api 8081 Prometheus, api.networkPolicy.peers Scrape; the API's clients when they exist (the Web UI [5]) postgresql 5432 API Its database; the workers join when they exist valkey 6379 API Sessions, jobs, locks; the workers join when they exist otel-collector 4318 db-collector, API OTLP/HTTP: logs from outsystems_logs collectors, the API's audit records (ADR 0016), and both services' spans otel-collector 4319 db-collector, API, while telemetry.enabled OTLP/HTTP: the services' own logs (ADR 0018)  8889 Prometheus Scrape of the Collector's Prometheus exporter (metrics pushed as OTLP) prometheus 9090 Grafana Prometheus datasource alertmanager 9093 Prometheus Alerts, and the alertmanager scrape job tempo 4318 OTel Collector OTLP/HTTP traces  3200 Grafana, Prometheus Tempo datasource; scrape loki 3100 OTel Collector OTLP log pushes  3100 Grafana Loki datasource  3100 Prometheus The loki scrape job  9095, 7946 Loki Its own gRPC (compactor address) and memberlist ring, through its Services A row exists only while both ends are on: minimal renders no Loki or Collector policy and admits neither to anything, a disabled scrape job admits no Prometheus, and the external-platform example renders only the Collector's policy, for the db-collector. An engine nothing uses denies all ingress (ingress: []); an empty from, which would admit every source, is never rendered. Peers match by the pods' app.kubernetes.io/name and instance labels in the release namespace, so whoever may create pods there can claim them: RBAC on the namespace is part of the access control. Not covered, by design: Grafana has no policy. Whatever serves its UI (an ingress, a load balancer, the operator) must reach port 3000, and its login is the control. Anonymous viewing is on only in the kind values, so on kind any pod, and any container on the Docker kind network, can query Loki through Grafana's datasource; that is why kind holds only synthetic data. Grafana has no Alertmanager datasource and Prometheus does not scrape Grafana, so neither flow is admitted anywhere. Kubelet probes come from the pod's node, which NetworkPolicy does not filter: the collector's :8082, the API's :8081, the Collector's :13133, Prometheus's :9090 and its reloader's :8080, Alertmanager's :9093 and its reloader's :8080, Loki's :3100, Tempo's :3200 and Grafana's :3000. kubectl port-forward connects from inside the pod, so NetworkPolicy never sees it. It is how operators reach Loki, and the others outside kind: kubectl -n heartbeat port-forward svc/loki 3100. kubectl exec into postgresql-0 (psql -U heartbeat) is the operator's way into the database, unfiltered for the same reason. PatientZero sends nothing to the engines: it writes its logs to SQL Server, and the db-collector reads them. The collector's egress. The policies above limit what reaches each pod. dbCollector.networkPolicy.egress also limits where the collector connects, off by default and on in kind.yaml and the production example: Destination Ports Why egress.dns (kube-dns in kube-system by default) UDP and TCP 53 Name resolution The bundled OTel Collector, while it is on its OTLP/HTTP container ports, 4318 and, while telemetry.enabled, 4319 by default OTLP/HTTP logs: OutSystems rows, and the service's own egress.targets each port of the enabled collectors' targets (1433 while there are none) The monitored SQL Servers and logging databases egress.extraRules as given An OTel Collector outside the chart, a proxy NetworkPolicy matches addresses, not host names, so egress.targets are peers, usually ipBlock CIDRs of the databases' subnets; with egress on and a target configured, an install without them fails rather than cutting the collector off its databases. On kind they are everything outside the pod subnet, since the sandbox SQL Server is a container on the Docker kind network: the collector can reach it, DNS and the OTel Collector, and times out on any other pod or Service, which make kind-e2e checks. egress.dns takes peers and allows port 53 only. Where cluster DNS is something else, set dns: [] and allow it in egress.extraRules: with NodeLocal DNSCache, ipBlocks for its address (169.254.20.10/32 by default) and for the kube-dns Service IP; on OpenShift, the openshift-dns pods on port 5353. Keep the collector's Service ClusterIP in production. /metrics and /healthz are unauthenticated and name the collectors, targets and environments. A NodePort or LoadBalancer Service makes port 8082 reachable from anything that reaches a node, and with externalTrafficPolicy: Cluster the NetworkPolicy then sees the node's address instead of the client's, so its peers no longer tell sources apart. kind.yaml uses a NodePort only because kind binds it to 127.0.0.1. backendNetworkPolicies.\u003cengine\u003e.extraPeers admits more NetworkPolicyPeers to an engine's ports (the Collector: 4317, 4318, 4319, and 8889 while its exporter is on; Prometheus 9090; Alertmanager 9093; Loki 3100; Tempo 3200 and 4318), such as a shared Grafana or Prometheus in another namespace (a namespaceSelector and a podSelector in one peer) or an operator range. kind.yaml adds every source outside the pod subnet to Prometheus, Alertmanager and the Collector, as for the collector, so the loopback NodePorts and make health keep working; Loki has no NodePort and stays limited to its three clients. backendNetworkPolicies.enabled: false removes the four engine policies. On kind, the API server's service and pod proxy (which make kind-e2e uses) also comes from the node, so it is not filtered either; in a cluster whose control plane runs elsewhere it is. kind's kindnet enforces NetworkPolicy, and make kind-e2e checks it: pods the policies do not list, in the namespace and in another one with Grafana's labels, time out on the collector, the API, PostgreSQL, Valkey, Loki, Tempo, Prometheus, Alertmanager and the Collector, while the flows above keep working. A cluster whose CNI does not enforce NetworkPolicy accepts the objects and drops nothing. Secrets The chart never creates Secrets. scripts/kind.sh creates kind-only values; an install from a release creates its own (installing a release): Secret Keys kind value Production heartbeat-runtime admin-token local-admin-token External Secrets Operator heartbeat-postgresql password Random, generated once per cluster and then kept: PostgreSQL reads it only when it creates its database Not used: RDS heartbeat-valkey password Random, generated once per cluster and then kept Not used: ElastiCache heartbeat-api-credentials HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_POSTGRES, HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_REDIS heartbeat: and default: with the two passwords above, rewritten on every deploy External Secrets Operator: the RDS login and the ElastiCache user grafana-admin admin-user, admin-password admin / admin Generated, External Secrets Operator heartbeat-sqlserver-dev-credentials HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_ENV_SQLSERVER_DEV From .env.sqlserver-dev Per-environment credential Secret Images The chart's default images are the release's, ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/db-collector and ghcr.io/gdpais/heartbeat/api at the chart's appVersion. On kind, kind.yaml points them at the local heartbeat/db-collector and heartbeat/api instead: make kind-images builds each for the node's CPU architecture (it refuses a mismatch), tags it dev-\u003cfirst 12 hex of the image ID\u003e and loads it with kind load. The API's image embeds db/migrations, so its own apps/api/Dockerfile.dockerignore keeps them in the build context that the root .dockerignore leaves them out of. Pods use imagePullPolicy: Never, so the cluster can only run what was loaded. The tag changes whenever the binary changes, so a code change always produces a new pod. Production references ECR images by digest (image.digest). The tags are kept in .tmp/kind/\u003ccluster\u003e/images.env. make kind-deploy, and everything that deploys through it (make sqlserver-dev-up, PatientZero), rebuilds first when a file under services/, apps/, db/ or internal/, go.mod or go.sum is newer than that file, or when it has no tag for an image, so a pull or a branch switch never leaves the current chart running an image built from older code. Unchanged code builds from cache to the same tag, and nothing rolls out. The cluster make kind-up creates heartbeat from the node image pinned in the Makefile (KIND_NODE_IMAGE, the newest Kubernetes minor EKS supports) and infra/kind/cluster.yaml. NodePorts map to the Compose-era host ports on 127.0.0.1 only. Every kubectl/helm call in the scripts names the kind-heartbeat context, so they never act on another cluster. SQL Server is not in the cluster: make sqlserver-dev-up runs it as a Docker container on the kind network, and pods reach it by container name. Working with it Change the deployed config through values and make kind-deploy. Helm 4 upgrades with server-side apply, so a field you edit by hand with kubectl (for example the integrations ConfigMap) becomes owned by kubectl, and the next upgrade fails with a conflict. Re-apply the chart's version with --server-side --field-manager=helm --force-conflicts, or delete the object and redeploy. In production Argo CD reverts such drift. Production Production values live in the heartbeat-deploy config repository and Argo CD renders the chart from them (ADR 0005). production.example.yaml shows the shape (ECR digests, External Secrets, gp3 volumes, S3 for Loki, the API on Amazon RDS and ElastiCache over TLS with the RDS CA bundle mounted, internal ALB for Grafana, Slack/Teams/WhatsApp and healthchecks.io receivers) and is rendered in CI so it stays valid. kind does not test cloud storage, networking, identity, ingress or TLS; those need testing in the target environment."},{"t":"Live Demo: Every Implemented Feature, Stage by Stage","u":"guides/live-demo.html","h":[{"t":"0. Load the helpers","id":"0-load-the-helpers"},{"t":"1. Extract: what the collector asks SQL Server","id":"1-extract-what-the-collector-asks-sql-server"},{"t":"2. Transform: rows into samples","id":"2-transform-rows-into-samples"},{"t":"3. Load: from /metrics to Grafana and Alertmanager","id":"3-load-from-metrics-to-grafana-and-alertmanager"},{"t":"4. Make the database do something","id":"4-make-the-database-do-something"},{"t":"5. Break things","id":"5-break-things"},{"t":"SQL Server stops","id":"sql-server-stops"},{"t":"SQL Server freezes","id":"sql-server-freezes"},{"t":"A config that must not load","id":"a-config-that-must-not-load"},{"t":"One probe fails, only that probe","id":"one-probe-fails-only-that-probe"},{"t":"The collector login gains sysadmin","id":"the-collector-login-gains-sysadmin"},{"t":"Admin endpoints","id":"admin-endpoints"},{"t":"An application fails, on purpose","id":"an-application-fails-on-purpose"},{"t":"6. Alert delivery","id":"6-alert-delivery"},{"t":"7. What is deployed but not used yet","id":"7-what-is-deployed-but-not-used-yet"},{"t":"What to judge","id":"what-to-judge"},{"t":"Clean up","id":"clean-up"}],"x":"Live Demo: Every Implemented Feature, Stage by Stage A hands-on tour of what Heartbeat does today, for reviewing the project's state before starting the next phase. It follows one SQL Server sample from the query the collector sends (extract), through the probe catalog's descriptors (transform), to /metrics, Prometheus, rules, Grafana and Alertmanager (load). Then it breaks things on purpose and shows the parts that are only scaffolds. About 60 minutes. This page does not start anything: it inspects a stack started as in local development. New to the stack? Do the walkthrough first. Every step names the decision it exercises, so a surprise points at the ADR or TODO item to revisit. What to judge collects them at the end. 0. Load the helpers Start the stack and the SQL Server sandbox as in Run everything; if a step fails, see troubleshooting. If Grafana runs on another port there, use that port wherever this page says 3001. In every terminal you use for the demo, from the repository root: scripts/demo-helpers.sh defines short functions and changes nothing by itself. hb_sql and hb_probe run SQL as the collector's own login, so you see exactly what the collector sees. Helper Does hb_sql / hb_sql_sa Run SQL from stdin as the collector login / as sa (setup and workload only) hb_probe NAME Run probe NAME as the collector sends it, as its login hb_metrics REGEX Collector /metrics samples matching REGEX hb_prom 'PROMQL' Prometheus instant query, one line per series hb_admin [JQ ARGS] GET /admin/config through jq; default: target states hb_reload POST /admin/config/reload hb_logs [N] Last N collector log lines hb_k ARGS kubectl in the kind cluster's heartbeat namespace You should see \"status\": \"ready\" and the target sqlserver-dev in state ok. 1. Extract: what the collector asks SQL Server The probe catalog (catalog.go) is the extractor: each probe is one SQL query plus metric descriptors. List it: Print one probe's batch as it goes over the wire, then run it as the collector: Every batch starts with the session settings: a probe gives up on a lock after 1s and is always the deadlock victim. Run the other eight (waits blocking memory_pressure storage throughput cpu buffer_cache\nfile_io) the same way. blocking returns no rows while nothing is blocked, and cpu returns NULL for other_process_percent on Linux. Both matter in the next step. Least privilege (database targets). The login sees server state but no application data: The first shows heartbeat_collector|0. Step 4 creates a database and shows the login cannot read it. 2. Transform: rows into samples The runner decodes each row with the probe's descriptors: ValueColumn becomes the value, multiplied by Scale; LabelColumns become labels, plus environment and target; a NULL or missing value makes no sample. Compare the raw rows with what the collector exports: 544 pages × 8192 = 4,456,448 bytes. Check the other conversions the same way: Raw (hb_probe) Exported (hb_metrics) Rule memory_pressure: total_server_memory_kb heartbeat_sqlserver_total_server_memory_bytes × 1024 waits: wait_time_ms per wait_type heartbeat_sqlserver_wait_seconds_total × 0.001, counter cpu: sql_process_percent, other_process_percent = NULL only heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_sql_process_ratio × 0.01; NULL → no series file_io: num_of_reads for master/master heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_reads_total{database_name=\"master\",file_name=\"master\"} as is, counter throughput: batch_requests, transactions (one row) two counters one row feeds two descriptors blocking: no rows no heartbeat_sqlserver_blocked_requests series absent, not 0 Gauges move between two reads; counters only go up. Compare values from the same moment, because the collector reads every 15s. 3. Load: from /metrics to Grafana and Alertmanager Recording rules and alerts: Only Watchdog should be firing. Right after the collector pod is replaced, HeartbeatServiceDown can show pending for the old pod's IP for about a minute, until Prometheus's DNS discovery drops it, or, while no pod runs, for the missing db-collector job. A replacement is back well within its 2 minutes, so it never reaches firing. Grafana (admin/admin): open http://localhost:3001/d/sqlserver-overview?var-target=sqlserver-dev. All 14 panels should have data except Blocked Requests, which reads 0. The same data through Grafana's API: 4. Make the database do something Create a small application database as sa. The collector never writes, so this is the only way to create load: Within 15s, hb_metrics 'database_name=\"heartbeat_demo\"' shows the new files (size and six I/O counters each). The Throughput, File I/O and Database File Size panels move. Blocking. One session holds a lock for 90s while three others wait for it: You should see a series per blocking session, blocking_session_id as a label, and a total of 3 in the recording rule and in the Blocked Requests panel. While it runs, look at the collector from the server side: lock_timeout 1000 and deadlock_priority -5 confirm the session settings from step 1. When the blocker rolls back (wait for the jobs), the series disappear and the rule falls back to 0. The lock waits show up in heartbeat_sqlserver_wait_seconds_total{wait_type=\"LCK_M_S\"} only then: SQL Server adds a wait to sys.dm_os_wait_stats when it ends. The collector produced evidence for the blocking and sessions probes in every cycle, and the default sink discarded it: nothing records which session blocked whom or with what statement. That is by design until TODO §12.4. 5. Break things Each scenario ends with the collector still ready (curl -s\nlocalhost:8082/readyz) and never restarted (hb_k get pod db-collector-0 keeps its age and restart count). SQL Server stops The target becomes failed with the driver's error (lookup\nheartbeat-sqlserver-dev ... no such host, since a stopped container leaves the kind network's DNS). Errors count under reason=\"error\" and the probe series are cleared, not frozen. It returns to ok about 30s after the start. SQL Server freezes A paused container accepts nothing and answers nothing, the case the probe hard deadline exists for. The first probe times out after 7.5s (half the 15s interval) and is abandoned 2s later. The rest of the target's probes are not_started while that call is still pending, and the target backs off (17s, 33s, 71s, ..., capped at 5m). Within about a minute the log shows abandoned probe returned;\nresult discarded, ended by the driver's 30s socket timeout (i/o timeout). After unpause, the target recovers at its next attempt. A config that must not load Helm's values schema rejects a probe timeout above 25s before anything is deployed: The collector's own loader is the second line of defence. Write a broken config straight into the ConfigMap, bypassing Helm: last_reload_err says platform \"unsupported\" is not supported (supported:\ngrafana, generic); the version, the readiness and the target are unchanged. Monitoring sees the rejection too: Restore it as Helm's field manager, so the next make kind-deploy does not conflict: A valid change reloads without a restart: see the walkthrough. One probe fails, only that probe A query_template override that divides by zero breaks only blocking: The target is degraded: heartbeat_collector_target_up stays 1 and the eight working probes keep collecting, so exported follows live in every cycle. Only blocking fails: heartbeat_collector_probe_up{probe=\"blocking\"} is 0, its series are gone, and /admin/config lists it under the target's probes, failed on the cycles it runs and backoff while it waits out its own backoff, with its consecutive failures growing. heartbeat:sqlserver_blocked_requests:sum and the Blocked Requests panel go empty (unknown), not 0. Restore with make kind-deploy and hb_reload. The collector login gains sysadmin Without the restart, the check repeats about every 10 minutes. Admin endpoints An application fails, on purpose With PatientZero running (make patientzero-up), a scenario breaks the application instead of the database and checks that Heartbeat sees it: Half of the checkout's PlaceOrder calls fail for three minutes, each with an error row in PatientZero's logging database. The command prints each check as it settles: the PlaceOrder error ratio from the collector's derived metrics rises above 0.3, the injected errors appear in Loki, and the ratio falls back once the fault ends. Watch it in Grafana's OutSystems Errors dashboard meanwhile. The run's journal, what PatientZero injected and served every 15 seconds, is in .tmp/patientzero-scenarios/ (format). Two longer scenarios page in Alertmanager, through the chart's SLO burn-rate alerts as shipped: bad-deploy publishes a broken version of the checkout module, and every PlaceOrder fails with a new exception until it is rolled back: OutSystemsErrorBudgetBurnFast fires within about 5 minutes and resolves after the rollback, and OutSystemsErrorBudgetBurnSlow fires later. In slow-sql the order list's query runs about 3 s longer in SQL Server: slow SQL in the OutSystems Overview dashboard, more running sessions in SQL Server Overview, and the latency alerts. Open Alertmanager on http://localhost:9093 meanwhile. Each waits for a slow burn that resolves only 40 to 50 minutes after its fault started, so give a second run of the same scenario that long (SLO scenarios). 6. Alert delivery Alertmanager routes Watchdog to deadmans-switch and every other alert to default. Neither has an integration locally (ADR 0009), so alerts are visible in Alertmanager and Grafana but notify no one. Send a synthetic alert and find it: 7. What is deployed but not used yet These checks come back empty or trivial today, and that is expected: Check Result Planned in hb_k get --raw /api/v1/namespaces/heartbeat/services/loki:3100/proxy/loki/api/v1/labels (a pod of your own would time out: Loki admits only its clients) {\"status\":\"success\"} with no labels: nothing writes to Loki Phase 2 Evidence from blocking and sessions discarded by the default sink Phase 7 (TODO §12.4) hb_k exec postgresql-0 -- psql -U heartbeat -Atc 'SELECT version FROM schema_migrations' the newest migration's number; the tables are empty: the API has no endpoint that writes yet Phase 3 (TODO §4.2–4.5) Probe assignments from the API config comes only from YAML Phase 3 (TODO §2.5) What to judge You saw Decision it exercises Revisit if Config changes applied with no restart; bad config rejected twice (Helm schema, then the loader) ADR 0002: runtime config in YAML, hot reload Reload latency (30 to 75s here, mostly kubelet sync) is too slow for operators One chart, same commands as CI ADR 0003 The local loop is too heavy for your machine Raw rows match exported samples; units and counter types as documented Probe catalog and descriptors (TODO §2.3, §2.4) A signal you need cannot be expressed as one query plus descriptors lock_timeout 1000, deadlock_priority -5, no access to application data, sysadmin alert Least privilege and query safety (TODO §2.2) The grants are not acceptable to DBAs Frozen server: collector stays ready, bounded stuck call Hard deadline (TODO §2.6) Recovery takes too long One broken probe fails the target; its healthy probes' series are cleared during backoff Per-target failure handling (TODO §2.6; per-probe isolation is a follow-up) Losing every probe of a target for up to 5 minutes because one probe is broken is not acceptable blocking_session_id as a label Probe label choice Session IDs churn and create new series; the detail may belong in evidence instead Evidence, Loki and PostgreSQL unused Phases 2, 3 and 7 Something you need now depends on them Clean up"},{"t":"Local Development","u":"guides/local-development.html","h":[{"t":"Run everything","id":"run-everything"},{"t":"Start the platform","id":"start-the-platform"},{"t":"Daily loop","id":"daily-loop"},{"t":"SQL Server sandbox","id":"sql-server-sandbox"},{"t":"PatientZero","id":"patientzero"},{"t":"Run tests","id":"run-tests"},{"t":"How integration tests stay isolated","id":"how-integration-tests-stay-isolated"},{"t":"Build images","id":"build-images"},{"t":"Change collector config without restarting","id":"change-collector-config-without-restarting"},{"t":"Run the API","id":"run-the-api"},{"t":"Outside the cluster","id":"outside-the-cluster"},{"t":"Change the API contract","id":"change-the-api-contract"},{"t":"Change a query or a migration","id":"change-a-query-or-a-migration"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","id":"troubleshooting"}],"x":"Local Development How to run Heartbeat: the Heartbeat stack on a local kind cluster, the DB collector pointed at a throwaway SQL Server, and the tests. This is the only page you need to run the app. Two optional guides build on it: Guide For Walkthrough Your first run: installs the tools and shows what each step should look like Live demo Reviewing what each feature does on a running stack, stage by stage Prerequisites: Docker, Go 1.27+, and kind, kubectl and Helm at the pinned versions (kind v0.33, kubectl within one minor of Kubernetes 1.36, Helm 4.2+). make tools-check verifies all of them and says how to fix any mismatch. Run make help for the full target list. Run everything From the repository root, the whole stack collecting from a local SQL Server: Grafana is then at http://localhost:3001 (admin/admin). The sections below explain each step; Troubleshooting covers the usual failures. To stop: make sqlserver-dev-down and make kind-down. Start the platform make kind-up deploys the same Helm chart CI and production use (ADR 0003), with kind.yaml values. Two profiles: Profile Deploys full (default) DB collector, the API with its PostgreSQL and Valkey (Redis), Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, Loki, Tempo, OpenTelemetry Collector minimal DB collector, the API with its PostgreSQL and Valkey, Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana Choose one with PROFILE=minimal make kind-up (or make kind-deploy). The API migrates its database before it starts; see run the API. Services keep the ports Compose used, on 127.0.0.1 only: Grafana http://localhost:3001 (admin/admin), Prometheus :9090, Alertmanager :9093, collector :8082, OTLP :4317/:4318. The metrics and endpoints reference lists them. The default config has no SQL Server targets, so the collector runs but collects nothing. Use the sandbox below, or configure a real target. How the chart, images and cluster fit together, and how to debug them: Kubernetes delivery. Daily loop You changed Run Go code in a service, or a migration make kind-images kind-deploy; the API's pod applies new migrations before it starts Chart templates or values make kind-deploy Prometheus rules or dashboards (infra/helm/heartbeat/files/) make kind-deploy (rules reload in place; run make rules-check first) The API's contract (apps/api/openapi.yaml) make generate, and commit the regenerated code with it (below) An API query (apps/api/internal/store/queries/) or a migration (db/migrations/) make generate, and commit the regenerated code with it (below) Images are tagged by content (dev-\u003cimage id\u003e), so a code change always rolls out a new pod and a stale image cannot hide behind a reused tag. Every deploy rebuilds first when the Go sources changed since the last build, for example after a pull (Images). The loop has a memory budget: the full profile holds at most 2.5 GiB of RAM in the kind node. To check it, watch docker stats heartbeat-control-plane (\u003ccluster\u003e-control-plane for another KIND_CLUSTER), take the peak over 30 minutes of make patientzero-load, and subtract PatientZero and its k6 Job, which run in the same node (docker exec heartbeat-control-plane crictl\nstats --label io.kubernetes.pod.namespace=patientzero). A second cluster, such as make kind-e2e, fits next to a sandbox that has run for hours only while the profile stays near today's 1.85 GiB (2026-11-02, with Tempo (what the telemetry costs) and the API, PostgreSQL and Valkey); at the budget it does not fit in a 7.6 GiB Docker host. Run it in CI, or docker restart heartbeat-sqlserver-dev first: that frees the sandbox's memory and keeps its databases. A change that would exceed the budget is trimmed first, or asks for a new one (ADR 0003). SQL Server sandbox A throwaway SQL Server runs as a Docker container on the kind network, outside the cluster, and the collector is pointed at it. Piece File Tracked Env template .env.sqlserver-dev.example yes Env file (random SA and collector passwords, mode 600) .env.sqlserver-dev no Script that creates the collector login scripts/sqlserver-login.sh yes Values pointing the collector at the container infra/helm/values/sqlserver-dev.yaml yes The container is named heartbeat-sqlserver-dev, uses MSSQL_PID=Developer, publishes 1433 only on 127.0.0.1:11433, runs the AMD64 image (emulated on ARM hosts) and must pass a SQL health check before deploying. Its memory is capped at 2048 MB (SQLSERVER_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB, at least 2048, SQL Server's minimum); uncapped, SQL Server sizes itself to 80% of the Docker VM, and on an 8 GB Docker host a PatientZero load flood or a second kind cluster got it killed. A container keeps the cap it was created with: make sqlserver-dev-up warns when it differs, and docker rm -f heartbeat-sqlserver-dev followed by make sqlserver-dev-up recreates it with the new one, keeping its databases, which are on the heartbeat-sqlserver-dev-data volume. make test-sqlserver caps its disposable container the same way. Once started, later make kind-deploy runs keep the target until make sqlserver-dev-down. The collector logs in as heartbeat_collector, which make sqlserver-dev-up creates with only the documented grants; sa is used only to set the container up. An .env.sqlserver-dev from before this login existed has an sa collector credential, which make sqlserver-dev-up refuses: run make sqlserver-dev-init (it adds the login and keeps the SA password) and then make sqlserver-dev-up again. The collector trusts the container's self-signed certificate (dbCollector.sqlserver.trustServerCertificate), which is acceptable only because the container is throwaway; production targets need a trusted certificate. PatientZero PatientZero, the synthetic OutSystems application Heartbeat is tested against, runs next to the chart and writes into its own two databases on the sandbox; the collector reads its logging database. Run tests Command What it runs Needs make test First make generate-check: fails when the API's generated code differs from what its OpenAPI spec and its SQL queries generate. Then all Go unit and repository contract tests, including the docs generator's and PatientZero's modules Go, a C compiler (sqlc's parser), network access the first time (the generators are fetched by go run) make test-race The same suite with the race detector Go make vet Go static checks Go make vulncheck govulncheck on all three Go modules; fails on a known vulnerability the code can reach, standard library included Go, network access make rules-check promtool rule validation and unit tests Docker make test-integration PostgreSQL migration and index tests; then the API against PostgreSQL and Valkey: golang-migrate applies db/migrations (again, concurrently, over a newer schema), the readiness check reads the schema version, credentials rotate, and /readyz follows each dependency going away and coming back (-tags=integration) Docker (postgres:18, valkey/valkey) make test-sqlserver Every built-in probe against a disposable SQL Server, as a login with only the documented grants; fails on duplicate series or padded label values; checks the session settings and the sysadmin-equivalence flag for that login, sa and a CONTROL SERVER login. Every OutSystems log query against its fixture tables, views and rows, as a login with only SELECT on them, returning every row of the current and the previous week; and the extraction of oslog_Error to a fake OTel Collector across cycles, a restart and a weekly log table rotation, each row exactly once (-tags=sqlserver). Then PatientZero's live tests (TestLive*) as sa: its entry points write oslog_* rows that the collector's own queries read back, and a small production-shaped log (make patientzero-scale's generator) loads, matches its known answers and is measured Docker (SQL Server 2022) make chart-check helm lint, then renders every values profile and checks it (below); then lints and renders PatientZero's chart and checks that its NetworkPolicies admit port 8080 only from the load Job and its Service stays ClusterIP Helm, make chart-deps make kind-e2e Acceptance checks on a temporary kind cluster (below) Docker, kind, kubectl, Helm, jq CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) runs all of them, and repeats chart-check with Helm 3; on a pull request the slower ones run only when the change can affect them (CONTRIBUTING.md). make chart-check renders the chart for each profile (defaults, kind, minimal, SQL Server sandbox, production example) and fails if rendering is not deterministic, an object is cluster-scoped or a Helm hook, the rendered integrations.yaml fails the Go loader, the collector is not a single replica with a writable state volume at HEARTBEAT_STATE_DIR and a read-only root filesystem, a one-replica StatefulSet is not Parallel, a misspelt top-level value or a misspelt key inside a component (dbCollector.enabeld, podSecurityContext.runAsNonroot) renders, the notes helm install prints miss a required Secret, or Prometheus, Alertmanager or Grafana points at a Service the profile does not deploy. make kind-e2e creates its own cluster (heartbeat-e2e-\u003cid\u003e, private kubeconfig, no host ports) and SQL Server container, so it runs next to your dev cluster without touching it, and removes only what it created. It checks the ADR 0003 acceptance criteria: every workload ready; the running collector is the image just built; the SQL target's metrics are fresh in Prometheus and visible through Grafana, collected by a login that is not sysadmin; a failed target is isolated; the Watchdog alert reaches Alertmanager; an outsystems_logs collector, added by a hot reload, extracts a fixture oslog_Error table to Loki and counts the rows in Prometheus; a valid ConfigMap change hot-reloads without a restart; an invalid one (a misspelled probe) is rejected while collection continues, shows in the reload metric and alert, and clears with the next valid one; a SQL Server outage shows as a failed target without restarting the collector; a collector update never runs two collectors (it prints the rollout time, collection gap and SQL Server session count); a deploy replaces a collector pod that never became Ready, and moves a StatefulSet created with OrderedReady to Parallel without restarting a pod whose template it does not change; and Prometheus data and Alertmanager silences survive pod replacement. It takes about 10 minutes. How integration tests stay isolated Integration tests never touch your developer stack: Each test in tests/ uses infra/docker-compose.test.yml (a disposable PostgreSQL fixture, not the platform) under a random heartbeat-test-\u003cid\u003e project, with no published ports and tmpfs storage. Only db/migrations is bind-mounted, read-only. COMPOSE_* overrides and dotenv files are ignored; your selected Docker daemon is kept. The API's tests (apps/api) connect from the test process, so each starts its own containers with docker run instead: a random heartbeat-api-test-\u003cid\u003e name, a random password passed through the environment, tmpfs storage, and one port published on 127.0.0.1 at a random port. PostgreSQL is the fixture's image, so both kinds of test run the same major. Cleanup is registered before startup and removes only that test's project or containers. Tests run concurrently, and separate test processes are isolated too. If a test process is killed, find the leftover project with docker compose ls and remove only that heartbeat-test-\u003cid\u003e project using the test Compose file; find leftover API test containers with docker ps -a --filter name=heartbeat-api-test- and remove them with docker rm -fv. Build images make kind-images builds the collector image for the kind node's own architecture and loads it. To build by hand: the Dockerfile compiles for Docker's selected target platform; the build stage runs natively and cross-compiles Go, so no CPU emulation is needed. BuildKit supplies TARGETOS and TARGETARCH; do not override them separately from --platform. .dockerignore keeps everything but Go sources out of the build context. Released images are built by CI, not by hand: see releases. Change collector config without restarting Edit the integrations values and run make kind-deploy; for probe assignments approved through the API, run make kind-assignments, which renders them in the API's pod into .tmp/kind/heartbeat/managed-collectors.yaml and deploys with it (later make kind-deploy runs keep it; delete the file to drop them). The collector is not restarted: the ConfigMap reaches the pod within about a minute and the collector polls the file every 5s. To apply it sooner: local-admin-token is the kind-only value of the heartbeat-runtime Secret. Credentials need no restart either: the collector reads the mounted credentials Secret on every connection, and the kubelet updates it within about a minute. See the configuration reference. Run the API make kind-up deploys it with the chart's PostgreSQL and Valkey and their Secrets, which scripts/kind.sh generates once per cluster. Its pods run api migrate up in an init container first. It has no NodePort: reach it through kubectl port-forward, which its NetworkPolicy does not filter, and the database through kubectl exec: Log in as that admin through the same port-forward, from the origin the kind values set (http://localhost:8081), as logging in and sessions shows. On a new cluster the migrate init container fails a few times, about a minute in all, until PostgreSQL accepts connections; Kubernetes retries it. To start again from an empty database, delete the postgresql-data claim and postgresql-0, then make kind-deploy; the same goes for a new PostgreSQL major, which the chart does not upgrade (ADR 0015). Outside the cluster For a faster loop with go run, run it on your machine against a PostgreSQL and a Valkey container of your own. Host ports 55432 and 56379, on 127.0.0.1 only, stay clear of a local PostgreSQL or Redis: Without HEARTBEAT_API_AUDIT_ENDPOINT the audit records stay in the outbox, where docker exec heartbeat-api-dev-postgres psql -U heartbeat -c 'select record from audit_outbox' shows them. To see them delivered, point it at an OTLP/HTTP logs receiver, such as kubectl --context kind-heartbeat -n heartbeat port-forward svc/otel-collector 4318 on kind, with HEARTBEAT_API_AUDIT_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318. The defaults expect TLS on both (verify-full and HEARTBEAT_API_REDIS_TLS=true), which local containers don't have, hence the two overrides. The API starts without either dependency: /readyz answers 503 and the log's readiness changed line says which check fails and why, for example schema not migrated before migrate up. Every setting is in the configuration reference; the endpoints are in the endpoints reference. Remove the containers with docker rm -fv heartbeat-api-dev-postgres heartbeat-api-dev-valkey. Change the API contract The API's contract is apps/api/openapi.yaml, written by hand (ADR 0013). After editing it, regenerate the server types and commit them with the spec: make test and CI, through make generate-check, fail when the committed code differs from what the spec generates. The generator, oapi-codegen, is pinned in the Makefile (OAPI_CODEGEN_VERSION) and fetched by go run, so there is nothing to install. The handlers in apps/api/internal/httpapi implement the generated StrictServerInterface: a new operation does not compile until it has one, and a handler can only return the responses the spec declares. Change a query or a migration The API's SQL is in apps/api/internal/store/queries/, one -- name: \u003cMethod\u003e :one|:many|:exec comment per query (ADR 0013). sqlc reads the schema from db/migrations (the .up.sql files) and writes typed Go for pgx into apps/api/internal/store/*.gen.go. Settings are in apps/api/sqlc.yaml. After editing a query, or adding a migration that a query reads: As with the contract, make test fails while the committed code is stale, and a query that no longer matches the schema fails make generate, not the API at run time. sqlc is pinned in the Makefile (SQLC_VERSION) and built by go run; its PostgreSQL parser is C, so it needs a C compiler (the Xcode Command Line Tools on macOS) and takes about half a minute the first time. A migration is a NNNN_name.up.sql and NNNN_name.down.sql pair in db/migrations, numbered next, four digits, and written expand then contract (CONTRIBUTING). The API embeds the files and api migrate up applies the new ones with golang-migrate, recording the version in schema_migrations; in a cluster an init container will run it before the API starts [4.1]. Until the database has the newest version the API embeds, its readiness check fails, so a replica of a new release never serves an old schema. An older release started against a newer schema (a rollback) migrates nothing and is ready. golang-migrate marks a version dirty while its migration runs. If the migration fails, the version stays dirty, migrate up refuses to run, and the readiness of a release that needs that version says so; an older release, whose schema is complete, stays ready. PostgreSQL ran the file as one implicit transaction, so none of it applied: fix the file, set the version back with update schema_migrations set version = \u003cprevious\u003e, dirty = false (or delete from schema_migrations when it was the first), and run migrate up again. api migrate version prints the version, with (dirty) when it is. Troubleshooting Symptom Cause and fix make tools-check reports a wrong kubectl Another kubectl (often Docker Desktop's) is earlier in PATH; put Homebrew's first make kind-up fails with port is already allocated Something else holds one of the ports in the port list (often an old Compose stack: docker compose ls; find the holder with lsof -nP -iTCP:\u003cport\u003e -sTCP:LISTEN). Grafana uses 3001, so a Grafana on 3000 does not clash. Stop the holder and rerun, or move that port: delete the half-created cluster (make kind-down), then, for example, sed 's/hostPort: 3001,/hostPort: 3002,/' infra/kind/cluster.yaml \u003e .tmp/kind-cluster.yaml and KIND_CONFIG=.tmp/kind-cluster.yaml make kind-up. make health still checks 3001, so check a moved Grafana with curl localhost:3002/api/health kind node fails with a cgroup v1 error kubelet 1.36 needs cgroup v2. Docker Desktop has it; on Linux, use a distribution with cgroup v2 Pods stay ErrImageNeverPull The images were not loaded into this cluster (for example after recreating it by hand). Run make kind-images kind-deploy make health or the browser get \"connection reset\" or Empty reply from server right after Docker or the laptop restarts, or right after make sqlserver-dev-up kube-proxy inside the node is still starting, or still routing the host port to the collector pod that was just replaced. Wait a few seconds (up to a minute after a restart) and rerun Image pulls fail with 429 Too Many Requests Docker Hub's anonymous rate limit. docker login, wait, and rerun SQL Server never becomes healthy on Apple silicon Rosetta emulation is off in Docker Desktop, or Docker has too little memory make sqlserver-dev-up says the credential must be a dedicated login, not sa .env.sqlserver-dev predates the least-privilege login. Run make sqlserver-dev-init (it adds the login and keeps the SA password), then make sqlserver-dev-up again helm upgrade fails with resource StatefulSet/heartbeat/db-collector not ready ... 1 pods have failed The collector is crash-looping on the image or config just deployed; kubectl --context kind-heartbeat -n heartbeat logs db-collector-0 says why. Fix it and rerun make kind-deploy: the StatefulSet replaces the broken pod (podManagementPolicy: Parallel) make kind-deploy prints recreating StatefulSet db-collector with podManagementPolicy Parallel Once, on a cluster deployed before the chart moved to Parallel, which cannot change in place: it deletes the StatefulSet with --cascade=orphan and the deploy creates it again. The pod keeps running until the new StatefulSet replaces it, which it does only if the deploy changes the pod template (after a kubectl rollout restart it does) helm upgrade fails with a field-manager conflict Something was edited by hand with kubectl. See Kubernetes delivery"},{"t":"How We Work","u":"product/operating-model.html","h":[{"t":"Outcomes over features","id":"outcomes-over-features"},{"t":"Alignment and autonomy","id":"alignment-and-autonomy"},{"t":"Scope","id":"scope"},{"t":"Test data","id":"test-data"},{"t":"Reversals","id":"reversals"},{"t":"Measures","id":"measures"}],"x":"How We Work How Heartbeat is planned and delivered: what everyone agrees on, what the team (people and agents) decides alone, how scope and reversals are handled, and what the board scorecard watches. Branching, commits, checks and releases are in CONTRIBUTING.md; delivery order and status are in TODO.md. Outcomes over features Each roadmap phase is a user outcome: who can now do what, and the evidence that shows it. The outcomes are in the roadmap. A phase is done when its outcome is shown, not when its checklist is ticked. Every checklist item serves the current phase's outcome, or a later phase's. If it serves neither, it does not go in. Each phase ends with a demo of the outcome on real-shaped data (test data): the user, the question they ask, and what Heartbeat answers. The demo is the phase's exit check, ahead of the release. Build a component in the same slice as its first caller. A part with no user and no caller waits. When two items compete, the one that moves the current outcome wins over hardening, tooling or docs for a later phase. Alignment and autonomy Scope grows because needs are found while building. That is expected; the answer is faster delivery, not a frozen plan. Delivery is fast when the team can act alone inside clear limits, and the owner answers what only the owner can answer, quickly. The owner sets (alignment): the phase outcomes and their order (roadmap) the guardrails: architecture decisions, CONTRIBUTING.md and the agents' delivery policy (local to the owner's machine, not in git: what agents may commit, push and open without asking) what needs the owner: merging, releases, deploys, spending, credentials and access, compliance, and any change to an outcome or a guardrail The team decides alone (autonomy), inside those limits: how to slice, build and test a phase's outcome adding, splitting or reordering items that serve the current outcome moving an item to a later phase, or to section 17, when it does not recording a decision as an ADR when it follows from the guardrails Owner actions are marked and answered fast. An item only the owner can do starts with Owner in TODO.md, with the date it became needed, or what it waits for until then. The aim is an answer within two working days. Status updates list open owner actions first, oldest first. Everything else continues around them. Decisions come before the work that depends on them. A platform, compliance or organization question is answered, or explicitly deferred with the cost of waiting written down, before code builds on one answer. Scope New items are welcome; they are sorted when they arrive, not when they are built: A new need… Goes to blocks the current phase's outcome, or is a defect the current phase serves a later phase's outcome that phase's section serves no planned outcome section 17, until an outcome needs it Review findings follow the same rule. The scorecard shows items added against items finished each week; the aim is to finish more than is added over a month. Test data Two kinds of data, for different questions:  PatientZero (ADR 0010) Replica of a real logging database Answers Does Heartbeat produce the right signal when this fault happens? Is what Heartbeat shows useful on a real application's data? Data Synthetic, shaped by the replica's survey; the answer is known Real: one week of a real installation's screens, integrations, slow SQL and users Live Yes: load, faults, rotation, restarts No: the one surveyed was a stale extract (oslog_all) with the native log tables empty, deleted after the survey Use for Fault scenarios, regressions, nightly CI Today: its schema and traffic profile only (ADR 0010). Outcome demos on its rows need a decision first A replica holds personal data (ADR 0012): it is used only on the owner's machine and deleted once its numbers are taken, and only DDL and aggregates reach git. Reversals A reversal is merged work or an accepted decision that is later undone or replaced: a component removed, a delivery path retired, an ADR superseded, a configuration contract broken. Plans reordered before anything is built are not reversals, and neither is ordinary fixing. Reversals are part of learning; the aim is to find them sooner and cheaper. Each one is logged here when its pull request merges, with its cause: Late information: a fact that existed but was not asked for in time Built ahead of need: no user or caller when it was built Wrong assumption: tested and found wrong Changed need: the need itself changed Reversed What Built or decided Cause Cost Lesson 2026-10-03 Docker Compose platform and Kustomize bundle retired for one Helm chart on kind (ADR 0003) 2026-06-04 Late information ~1,500 lines; a second delivery path kept for four months Local, CI and production share one delivery path from the first slice 2026-10-05 Phase branch db-collectors retired for trunk-based development (ADR 0006) 2026-06-04 Wrong assumption A ruleset and a CI trigger Short-lived branches on master by default 2026-10-10 Per-job required checks replaced by ci-ok (ADR 0007) 2026-10-05 Wrong assumption One ruleset change Try a CI rule on a scenario pull request before requiring it 2026-10-18 OTel gateway service removed (ADR 0009) 2026-06-13 Built ahead of need ~770 lines; planned as the next stage on 2026-10-04 Build a component with its first caller 2026-10-19 Integrations config renamed to be platform-neutral; push endpoints dropped (ADR 0011) 2026-06-13 Late information A breaking config change Ask which platforms the organization uses before naming one in a contract 2026-11-02 Traces sampled at 10% by default replaced by keeping every trace (ADR 0018) 2026-11-02 Wrong assumption One default, the ADR and its docs Set a sampling or retention limit from a measured volume, not a convention The board scorecard counts reversals per month by cause. Measures The board scorecard reads these from the repository and GitHub: Measure Why Phase outcomes shown The product measure: what users can now do Items finished and added per week Delivery speed against growing scope Open owner actions and their age Where delivery waits on the owner Reversals per month, by cause How often merged work is undone Time from a question to its decision Late decisions are the main cause of reversals Pull request time to merge, build pass rate, nightly scenario results Flow and quality of each change"},{"t":"Heartbeat Product Overview","u":"product/overview.html","h":[{"t":"Who it is for","id":"who-it-is-for"},{"t":"Principles","id":"principles"},{"t":"MVP scope","id":"mvp-scope"},{"t":"MVP exit criteria","id":"mvp-exit-criteria"},{"t":"Non-goals for the MVP","id":"non-goals-for-the-mvp"},{"t":"Current status","id":"current-status"},{"t":"Risks","id":"risks"},{"t":"Open questions","id":"open-questions"}],"x":"Heartbeat Product Overview Heartbeat is an SRE-focused monitoring platform. It covers deep database observability, application telemetry, session-centric investigation, adaptive alerting and operational reporting, on top of Grafana, Prometheus and Loki. It complements commercial APMs such as Dynatrace and Datadog rather than replacing them. The focus is on gaps those tools leave: Deep database observability beyond generic APM plugins: waits, locks and blocking, memory pressure, session and connection saturation, storage pressure. Cross-system telemetry that brings application and platform logs and metrics into one place. Session-centric troubleshooting: \"what happened to this user (or IP, session, request) in this application between 10:00 and 10:15?\" Flexible, standards-based integrations (OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, Loki, Grafana) instead of vendor lock-in. Who it is for Audience Uses Heartbeat to SREs and on-call engineers Investigate incidents across application logs, metrics and database evidence; receive low-noise alerts Platform and DBA teams Watch SQL Server health without installing agents on database hosts Application owners Read application health, error and latency trends; receive scheduled reports Heartbeat operators Manage environments, applications, monitored databases, alert policies and report schedules Principles No agents on monitored databases. Collection runs from a separate monitoring environment using remote, read-only, non-blocking queries with least-privilege logins and query budgets. Alert quality over alert count. Static guardrails plus explainable adaptive thresholds; grouping and deduplication to reduce false positives. Reuse Grafana and Loki for dashboards and log exploration; no custom dashboard or search engine. Application-first. Investigations, alerts and reports belong to an application; the environment is derived from it. Fail closed. Invalid configuration never replaces a working one. MVP scope Capability MVP outcome Application observability OutSystems first (Traditional and Reactive): normalized logs in Loki, derived metrics in Prometheus, health/errors/latency dashboards, compatibility with default OutSystems log fields Database observability SQL Server: waits, blocking, sessions, memory, storage, throughput, CPU, buffer cache and file I/O metrics, collector self-observability, dashboards (signal coverage) Telemetry ingestion OTLP metrics, logs and traces through the OpenTelemetry Collector; traces kept in a trace store, Grafana Tempo expected (TODO 3.6) Session investigation Query by application + user/IP/session/request + time range; timeline, anomaly windows and evidence links into Grafana/Loki Alerting Static rules plus adaptive baselines, rendered to Prometheus and routed by Alertmanager Reporting Scheduled (weekly first) and on-demand reports, delivered by email Operator backoffice API and web UI to manage inventory, targets, policies and schedules, with audit logging MVP exit criteria One OutSystems application/environment can be onboarded end to end. An SRE can see application health, errors, latency and correlated Loki logs in Grafana. Session analysis returns useful correlated evidence for a real incident sample. The SQL Server collector runs against real targets with safe probes. Static and adaptive alerting both work and are auditable. A weekly report runs automatically and is delivered. Non-goals for the MVP Parity across database engines. SQL Server first, Oracle next; PostgreSQL is Heartbeat's metadata store, not a monitored engine. A code-level APM tracing agent ecosystem. Autonomous root-cause analysis. A custom dashboard engine. File-based ingestion, except later as a last-resort fallback when direct database connectivity is impossible. Current status Heartbeat is in early MVP construction. See the roadmap for phase-by-phase status and TODO.md for the task list. Area Status Shared contracts, PostgreSQL schema Done Kubernetes delivery: one Helm chart for kind, CI and production; kind workflow and acceptance tests Done (production values and Argo CD pending) SQL Server DB collector (probes, hot reload, failure isolation, readiness) Done for phase 1: secured endpoints, least-privilege login, counters and self-metrics, CPU, buffer cache and file I/O signals OTel Collector pipeline, Prometheus/Loki/Grafana/Alertmanager provisioning Configured; alerts reach Alertmanager (with a Watchdog), chat receivers only in the production example OutSystems 11 log extraction (outsystems_logs collector kind) In progress: extraction to Loki, derived metrics and dashboards built; real-database survey done; query review pending API Started: service bootstrap with health, readiness, metrics and the OpenAPI spec; no database or deployment yet Web UI, session analyzer, reporting Not started Risks OutSystems telemetry quality and export paths vary by version and deployment. Session correlation is only as good as the identifiers present in source logs. High-cardinality labels (user, session, IP) can damage Loki; they stay out of labels. Poorly designed remote SQL polling can load production databases; every probe is reviewed and budgeted. Opaque adaptive alerting erodes trust, so baselines must be explainable. Building both a UI and deep integrations can spread the team thin. Open questions Which user, session and request identifiers are reliably present in each OutSystems log type? (The first release targets OutSystems 11 self-managed, read from its logging database: ADR 0008.) Is in-app report download enough, or is email delivery required from day one? Auth model: local RBAC, SSO/OIDC, or both? Is multi-tenancy needed? Expected scale: environments, applications, database instances, log volume, scrape interval and retention? Preferred notification channels and on-call tooling? Compliance constraints: PII handling, retention and audit? (Identifiers in Loki are kept raw pending sign-off before production: ADR 0012.)"},{"t":"Heartbeat Roadmap","u":"product/roadmap.html","h":[{"t":"Phase outcomes","id":"phase-outcomes"},{"t":"Test application: PatientZero","id":"test-application-patientzero"},{"t":"Phase 0 — Foundations (done)","id":"phase-0--foundations-done"},{"t":"Phase 1 — DB collector (done)","id":"phase-1--db-collector-done"},{"t":"Phase 2 — OutSystems ingest","id":"phase-2--outsystems-ingest"},{"t":"Phase 3 — API / control plane","id":"phase-3--api--control-plane"},{"t":"Phase 4 — Web UI","id":"phase-4--web-ui"},{"t":"Phase 5 — Production delivery (EKS + Argo CD)","id":"phase-5--production-delivery-eks--argo-cd"},{"t":"Phase 6 — Complete application and database product workflows","id":"phase-6--complete-application-and-database-product-workflows"},{"t":"Phase 7 — Investigations, alerting and reporting","id":"phase-7--investigations-alerting-and-reporting"},{"t":"Phase 8 — Advanced reliability, HA and security","id":"phase-8--advanced-reliability-ha-and-security"},{"t":"Phase 9 — Fallback file ingestion (deferred)","id":"phase-9--fallback-file-ingestion-deferred"},{"t":"Phase 10 — Oracle collector (late roadmap)","id":"phase-10--oracle-collector-late-roadmap"},{"t":"Phase 11 — Custom collector vs Grafana Alloy comparison (last)","id":"phase-11--custom-collector-vs-grafana-alloy-comparison-last"},{"t":"Only if scope expands","id":"only-if-scope-expands"}],"x":"Heartbeat Roadmap The single phase plan for Heartbeat. It summarizes TODO.md, which is the source of truth for delivery order and task status (TODO section numbers in brackets). The core order is DB collector → OutSystems ingest → API/control plane → Web UI; each phase is tested as it is delivered, using the shared validation inventory [16] throughout rather than as a final stage. Phase Theme Status 0 Foundations, including kind + Helm delivery Done 1 DB collector (SQL Server) Done 2 OutSystems ingest (OutSystems 11 log extraction) At its exit: everything it builds is done (ADR 0008), and the stage validation passed on 2026-11-02: the nightly run of every scenario has been green since 2026-10-28, and the 48 hour backfill matched its known answer in that workflow on 2026-11-02. Left: the outcome demo, and the release, which waits for the owner to create the release app [0.5] 3 API / control plane In progress: the service bootstrap is done [4.1]: the chart deploys the API with its PostgreSQL and Valkey, migrating in an init container, its logs, metrics and traces wired like the collector's; audit records reach Loki through an outbox [4.10]; login, sessions, users and roles [4.2]; the inventory of environments, applications and components [4.3]; telemetry sources, with whether the collector observes them [4.4]; database targets, probe definitions and probe assignments, approved into managedCollectors and applied on kind [4.5]; next Grafana deep links [4.9], then the stage's validation. Planned into pull-request-sized slices [4]; decisions recorded in ADRs 0013–0017; runs without waiting for phase 2's release 4 Web UI Planned: built and served as ADR 0020 decides, a Vite app embedded in the API; next the scaffold [5.1] 5 Production delivery (EKS + Argo CD) Not started; decisions recorded 6 Complete application and database product workflows Not started 7 Investigations, alerting and reporting Not started 8 Advanced reliability, HA and security Not started 9 Fallback file ingestion Deferred 10 Oracle collector, gated by an Alloy coverage check Late roadmap 11 Custom collector vs Grafana Alloy comparison Last planned phase Phase outcomes What each phase lets someone do. A phase is done when its outcome is shown in a demo on real-shaped data, not when its checklist is ticked (how we work). Phase Who Can now 0 Developer Run the whole stack locally and in CI with one chart and one command sequence 1 DBA, SRE See a SQL Server's waits, blocking, sessions, memory and storage in Grafana, with no agent on the database host 2 SRE See an OutSystems application's health, errors and latency, with its logs, in Grafana, and be alerted when its objective burns 3 Operator Onboard an application, a SQL Server target and its probes through the API, without editing YAML, with every change audited 4 Operator Do the same in a browser 5 SRE, on-call Run Heartbeat on EKS against production databases, with alerts reaching on-call 6 Application owner Read an application's and its databases' health together, end to end 7 SRE Ask what happened to a user, session or request in a time window and get correlated evidence; get low-noise alerts and a weekly report 8 SRE Lose a collector or a node without a data gap beyond the agreed objective 9 Operator Load exported logs where Heartbeat cannot reach the database 10 DBA See Oracle as they see SQL Server 11 Owner Decide, on evidence, whether to keep the custom collector or adopt Alloy Test application: PatientZero Heartbeat only reads, so the phases are tested against PatientZero, a synthetic application that behaves like an OutSystems 11 application (ADR 0010) [16.5]. Each request runs real SQL against its business database on the sandbox SQL Server and writes the log rows the platform would into its own logging database, so database load and application logs share a cause. Its logging schema and traffic profile come from a survey of a real OutSystems 11 logging database, which is only read. k6 load with virtual users of stable identity, in steady, peak, soak and day profiles Named fault scenarios, each with the signals it must produce, run by make patientzero-scenario: weekly rotation, log backlog and growth, late commits, restarts and outages, a bad deploy and slow SQL now; blocking, a slow partner, late timers and a bad front end with phase 7 A compressed calendar for the extractor: rotation on demand, backfill, late commits and time zones, without touching the collector's clock A journal of every run: the known answer for investigations and reports It grows with the phases: logs and derived metrics in phase 2, onboarding in phases 3 and 4, investigations, alerts and reports in phase 7. Phase 0 — Foundations (done) Monorepo scaffolding, local platform, CI [0.2] Shared JSON-schema contracts for config, telemetry, investigations, alerts and reports [0.3] PostgreSQL schema migrations and tests (data model) [1] Prometheus, Loki, Grafana, Alertmanager and OTel Collector provisioning; Prometheus sends alerts to Alertmanager with a Watchdog route [9] Shared config manager with validation, versioning and hot reload; reload results exported as metrics and a rejected reload alerted on [8] One Helm chart for kind, CI and production; acceptance checks run on kind in CI; platform Compose and the Kustomize bundle retired (ADR 0003) [0.4] Trunk-based development and tagged releases: v0.1.0 is public on GHCR and installs from the install guide (ADR 0006) [0.5] Open housekeeping: the release GitHub App and the tag-creation ruleset wait for the end of phase 2 (ADR 0006) [0.5]. Phase 1 — DB collector (done) Runtime collection is configured in YAML and needs no API or UI. Probe catalog: waits, blocking, sessions, memory, storage, throughput, CPU, page life expectancy and buffer cache hit ratio, file I/O; per-target connection pooling and timeouts [2.1–2.3] Endpoint security: admin token on diagnostics and reload (constant-time check), status-only /readyz, full redaction and credential-free URLs, NetworkPolicy, TrustServerCertificate warning [2.7] Least privilege and query safety: every probe runs with LOCK_TIMEOUT and low deadlock priority; a sysadmin-equivalent login is logged, exported and alerted on; tests and the sandbox run as a login with only the documented grants; probe review checklist [2.2]; a query_template must be one read-only SELECT, checked when the configuration loads [2.7] Metrics: cumulative values as counters in base units, per-probe durations and error counters, Go runtime and process metrics [2.4, 2.6] Reliability: failure isolation, bounded backoff, freshness metrics, stale-series cleanup, readiness that reflects collector state, reload rollback, a hard deadline so a probe the driver cannot cancel never stalls the collector, and a row cap per probe (max_rows) [2.6] SQL Server overview dashboard and local SQL Server sandbox; every probe runs against a real SQL Server in CI, and make kind-e2e covers live SQL Server → collector → Prometheus → Grafana on kind, reloads and target outages with a least-privilege login Not in this phase: API-driven probe assignments [2.5] join in phase 3, probe versioning with them [4.5], and evidence publication in phase 7 [12.4]. A failing probe fails only itself since 2026-10-27: its target stays up and keeps collecting while the probe backs off on its own [2.6]. Phase 2 — OutSystems ingest OutSystems 11 self-managed first. Its logs are read from the logging database by a new db-collector kind (ADR 0008; design in application observability). PatientZero's first slice: the logging-database survey, its schema and traffic profile, the business and logging databases on the sandbox, k6 load, and the scenario runner with the ingest and SLO scenarios (rotation, backlog, late commits, log growth, restarts and outages, a bad deploy, slow SQL), which are part of this phase's exit checks; each ingest scenario compares Loki with the rows PatientZero committed. The other scenarios come with phase 7 [16.5] A persistent, ordered export queue in the OTel Collector: a Collector restart or a Loki outage during a backlog loses and reorders nothing; the db-collector waits for the Collector without failing its target [3.3] One reviewed .sql query per log type (error, screen, integration, ...), watermarked incremental reads, OTLP to the OTel Collector and Loki with OutSystems field names kept [3.3] User, session and request IDs and client IP stay searchable as Loki structured metadata, never labels [9.2] Derived SRE, infrastructure and business metrics on the collector's /metrics; recording rules and SLO burn-rate alerts [3.3] OutSystems overview, errors, latency and request-timeline dashboards [10.4, 3.5] Use configured application/environment identities until API inventory exists Later: request traces, Tempo with synthesized spans from Request_Key, built together once something consumes them: the first OTLP trace source or phase 7's investigation timeline [3.5, 3.6]; MuleSoft and generic sources [3.4]; OutSystems Cloud and ODC through their OTLP streams, straight into the OTel Collector (the OTel gateway was removed: ADR 0009) Exit: every query tested against a SQL Server fixture with the oslog_* tables; logging database → collector → Loki and Prometheus → Grafana on kind, including restarts and outages without gaps, shown by the PatientZero scenarios in a scheduled CI run; the SLO scenarios fire the burn-rate alerts; a 48-hour backfill matches its known answer in Loki. The phase ends with its release: the release GitHub App is created then, and release-please cuts the release from the commits since v0.1.0 [0.5]. Done for the exit: the signal gaps the outage scenarios found (an alert when the log export is blocked, HeartbeatServiceDown for a missing db-collector or Loki, a late-row counter for rows committed past the lag, and the log delay metric described as extraction freshness) [3.3, 9.1], and a CI workflow that runs every scenario on kind nightly, by hand and on the release pull request; the scenarios take over two hours, so they stay out of the per-PR make kind-e2e [16.5]. Validated on 2026-11-02, with the nightly runs and master's CI as evidence (TODO 3): every log-type query against SQL Server, extraction → Collector → Loki and derived metrics → Prometheus on kind, restarts and outages without gaps, and the SLO scenarios firing the burn-rate alerts. The first green nightly run was on 2026-10-28. The 48 hour backfill matched its known answer in Loki on the current export path in the scenarios workflow on 2026-11-02 (run: all 48 hours in full, users, sessions and error fingerprints exact), and runs after the scenarios from now on [16.5]. Left before the release, in order: The outcome demo: an SRE sees a broken deploy's errors, latency and logs in Grafana and the burn-rate alert page. The nightly run checks signals in Prometheus, Loki and Alertmanager, not what Grafana shows, and the live demo runs the scenarios without walking through the dashboards or the logs [16.5] The release. Owner: create the release GitHub App, needed since 2026-10-05 [0.5] Not in the exit, and done alongside phase 3 before the first production deploy: a series budget [2.6], collector egress and exposure [2.7], unknown keys in component values [0.4], Alertmanager's rollout policy [9.4] and PatientZero's hardening [16.5]. Phase 3 — API / control plane Decisions: API design and the frozen subsystem boundaries (ADR 0013), approved probe assignments as pull requests to the config repository (ADR 0014), PostgreSQL and Redis delivery (ADR 0015), audit records through an outbox to Loki (ADR 0016), OutSystems sources as application identity mappings (ADR 0017). Its prerequisites are done, so it starts while phase 2's release waits for the release app. In order, one pull request each: Bootstrap, PostgreSQL and Redis connectivity, migrations, and the API, PostgreSQL and Valkey in the chart [4.1] (done) Audit output, first, so every later change is audited [4.10] (done) Local auth with minimal roles: login, sessions, users and roles [4.2] (done) Environments, applications, components, telemetry sources, database targets, probe definitions and assignments; approved assignments become YAML/Kubernetes runtime config through the managedCollectors chart key, applied on kind by make kind-assignments; the collector reads credentials from mounted Secret files, so a new target needs no restart [4.3–4.5, 2.5] (done) Grafana deep links from the integrations templates [4.9] The pull requests to heartbeat-deploy (ADR 0014) wait for that repository in phase 5. Investigation, alerting and reporting APIs and their job contracts move to phase 7 and are built with their workers [4.6–4.8]: nothing consumes them before, and their shape depends on the correlation model and the evidence design. Exit: auth/ownership, CRUD persistence, secret refs and audit redaction; PatientZero registered through the API as an application with its telemetry source and database target, whose approved probes the collector runs on kind. Phase 4 — Web UI Bootstrap, auth, and inventory/onboarding screens on the phase-3 APIs [5], a Vite app embedded in the API and served from its origin (ADR 0020) Investigation, alert and report screens come in phase 7, with their APIs and workers Exit: API-backed login, roles, onboarding and Grafana/Loki deep links; PatientZero onboarded from the UI. Phase 5 — Production delivery (EKS + Argo CD) The chart already runs on kind and in CI; this phase takes it to production with the defaults in ADR 0005 [18]. It comes after the Web UI so the first production deploy carries a usable product; pull it forward if a production SQL Server must be monitored sooner. The observability platform is chosen: the Grafana stack stays, and other applications receive Heartbeat's telemetry as OTLP from the Collector (ADR 0019) [18] heartbeat-deploy repository with Argo CD Applications and per-environment values; optional Argo CD rehearsal on kind CI publishes multi-arch images and the chart to ECR through GitHub OIDC EKS infrastructure as code; External Secrets; healthchecks.io dead-man's switch; Discord, then Slack, Teams and WhatsApp receivers Where the monitored SQL Servers sit relative to the EKS VPC Exit: production networking, identity, TLS, storage, retention and restore validated in the target environment. Phase 6 — Complete application and database product workflows OutSystems and SQL Server onboarding, the remaining signal coverage, dashboards and drill-down [10, 11]; CPU, cache and I/O arrive in phase 1 (SQL Server signal coverage) Exit: onboarding in the UI → ingest/collection → Loki/Prometheus → Grafana, with field/metric semantics, cardinality and database load checked. Phase 7 — Investigations, alerting and reporting Job/result contracts on Redis Streams, versioned before the first producer and consumer (ADR 0013) [4] Collector evidence: content, destination and publication, retrievable before the analyzer uses it [12.4] Session analyzer, the investigations API and the investigation UI [4.6, 6, 12] Alerting API, alert policy rendering to Prometheus and Alertmanager; adaptive baselines [4.7, 13] Reporting API, report templates, schedules, generation and email delivery [4.8, 7, 14] The rest of PatientZero: timers, extension calls, a fake partner, and the blocking, slow-partner, timer-backlog, bad front-end and daylight saving scenarios [16.5]; request traces if they haven't come earlier [3.5, 3.6] See operator workflows Exit: deterministic correlation/baseline/report tests; alert trigger/route/dedupe/resolve and scheduled/on-demand reports end to end; investigations and reports on PatientZero runs match their journals. Phase 8 — Advanced reliability, HA and security Collector replica ownership and takeover with fencing; recovery-time and data-gap objectives; outage testing [19] (collector HA design) Authn/authz hardening, query budgets, rate limits, worker idempotency and backoff [15.2] Audit rotation and shipping; operational runbooks [15.3] Query safety and collector endpoint security are phase 1 work, not deferred here. Phase 9 — Fallback file ingestion (deferred) Ingest uploaded/exported files only when direct database connectivity is impossible, with validation, lineage and freshness warnings [20] Always lower priority than live collection Phase 10 — Oracle collector (late roadmap) Gate first: a time-boxed check of what Grafana Alloy and established Oracle exporters cover, recorded as an ADR. It decides whether to build a custom Oracle collector, adopt Alloy for Oracle, or run the phase-11 comparison first, so a custom collector is not built only to be replaced [21] Then: connectivity, probes, evidence, config, API/Web onboarding, dashboards, rules, investigation/report integration, an isolated test target and a runbook Exit: supported versions, least privilege, metric semantics and query load validated, plus failure/reload/ownership tests. Phase 11 — Custom collector vs Grafana Alloy comparison (last) A parallel, evidence-based comparison on a dedicated branch, for SQL Server and Oracle separately; the outcome is open [22] See the comparison design Only if scope expands Asset and topology inventory, collector fleet history in PostgreSQL, OIDC/SSO, Mimir scale-out, engines beyond SQL Server and Oracle, DB-backed integration CRUD and audit search, custom dashboards, autonomous RCA [17]."},{"t":"Configuration Reference","u":"reference/configuration.html","h":[{"t":"Config files by environment","id":"config-files-by-environment"},{"t":"Collectors rendered by the API","id":"collectors-rendered-by-the-api"},{"t":"integrations.yaml","id":"integrationsyaml"},{"t":"Endpoints","id":"endpoints"},{"t":"Notification channels","id":"notification-channels"},{"t":"Credential references","id":"credential-references"},{"t":"Collectors","id":"collectors"},{"t":"outsystems_logs collectors","id":"outsystems_logs-collectors"},{"t":"Validation and reload behavior","id":"validation-and-reload-behavior"},{"t":"Credential resolution","id":"credential-resolution"},{"t":"Environment variables","id":"environment-variables"},{"t":"DB collector","id":"db-collector"},{"t":"API","id":"api"},{"t":"Local development files","id":"local-development-files"}],"x":"Configuration Reference Heartbeat services are configured by one integrations file plus environment variables. Why config lives in YAML rather than PostgreSQL is explained in ADR 0002. Sources of truth: Loader and validation: internal/config/config.go JSON schema: packages/config-schema/src/integrations.schema.json Deployed config: the integrations key of the Helm chart's values (defaults, no SQL Server targets), overridden per environment by files in infra/helm/values/ Binary default for running a service outside Kubernetes: config/integrations.yaml Config files by environment The chart renders integrations into the heartbeat-integrations ConfigMap, mounted at /app/config/integrations.yaml. Helm merges maps across values files but replaces lists, so an environment that changes collectors restates the whole list. Environment Where integrations comes from kind Chart defaults (values.yaml) plus kind.yaml kind with the SQL Server sandbox Adds sqlserver-dev.yaml Production Values in the heartbeat-deploy config repository; production.example.yaml shows the shape Service run directly (go run) config/integrations.yaml, or HEARTBEAT_INTEGRATIONS_PATH Helm validates integrations against the integrations schema (embedded in the chart's values.schema.json) before anything is applied, and make chart-check loads each profile's rendered file with the Go loader. Collectors rendered by the API Approved probe assignments reach the collector as collectors of their own, which the API renders into a generated values file it owns (ADR 0014). That file sets the chart key managedCollectors, not integrations, and the chart appends that list to integrations.collectors when it renders integrations.yaml. Because lists replace, the separate key keeps the two apart: the environment's values hold the hand-written collectors, the API's file holds its own, and neither overwrites the other. The rendered file has the same format either way; the collector loads it like any other and does not know which collectors came from the API. The API renders the file from approved assignments (probe assignments): on kind, make kind-assignments deploys it; in production a pull request to heartbeat-deploy will carry it [18]. Empty or absent, the chart renders exactly what integrations alone renders. Managed targets count like hand-written ones: the collector's egress NetworkPolicy opens their ports, and the install notes count them. Helm checks these rules whenever it renders the chart (helm template, install and upgrade, and Argo CD's render), before anything is applied: Rule Checked by Helm's error Each managedCollectors entry is a collector of kind sqlserver; outsystems_logs collectors stay hand-written (ADR 0017) values schema at '/managedCollectors/0/kind': value must be 'sqlserver' A managedCollectors id starts with api-; the API writes api-\u003ctarget id\u003e-\u003cinterval\u003es values schema at '/managedCollectors/0/id': 'finance' does not match pattern '^api-' A hand-written id, in integrations.collectors, does not start with api-, leading spaces included values schema at '/integrations/collectors/0/id': 'not' failed An id appears once across both lists, compared without surrounding spaces, as the collector compares them chart templates collector id \"sql\" is listed twice, at integrations.collectors[0] and integrations.collectors[1] The last rule covers the hand-written list on its own too: a duplicate id there used to render, and the collector then rejected the file at its next reload, or failed to start. integrations.yaml Endpoints Heartbeat never writes to the observability platform itself: logs go to the OpenTelemetry Collector, and metrics are scraped from /metrics (ADR 0011). These keys say where the platform's UI and query APIs are, for links and, later, the API's queries. Key Required Notes platform no grafana (default: Grafana, Prometheus, Loki, Alertmanager) or generic (any other platform; dashboards, logs and alerting must then be empty). Anything else is rejected dashboards.base_url no The platform's UI (Grafana), as seen from the operator's browser dashboards.dashboard_templates no Map of name → dashboard path, e.g. sqlserver-overview: /d/sqlserver-overview dashboards.deep_link_templates no Map of name → path with ${variables}, e.g. /d/sqlserver-overview?var-target=${target} logs.base_url no The platform's log query API (Loki) alerting.base_url no The platform's alert API (Alertmanager) opentelemetry.endpoint for outsystems_logs Absolute base URL of the OTel Collector's OTLP/HTTP receiver, e.g. http://otel-collector:4318; logs go to \u003cendpoint\u003e/v1/logs. The chart's NetworkPolicy admits the collector to the bundled Collector's port 4318 only (network access) No URL may embed credentials (https://user:password@host): the loader rejects userinfo in every base_url, endpoint, dashboard and deep-link template, and channel target_ref, for all four sections. Put secrets in a credential_ref instead; URLs end up in logs, diagnostics and browser links. The check also catches templates with ${variables} in the host and the forms browsers accept (http:\\\\user@host, http:user@host, tabs or newlines inside the URL). A target_ref is also rejected when it is a scheme-less user:password@host, which tools like curl read as credentials; plain addresses (ops@example.com) and mailto: are fine. Notification channels Credential references credential_refs is an optional map of name → secret reference. Every credential reference in the file (credential_refs.*, collector, target and channel credential_ref) must start with env/, kv/ or secret/. Values are never stored in the file. Collectors In the chart's values, collector ids that start with api- are reserved for the collectors the API renders. host is a DNS name or IPv4 address (letters, digits, ., -, _) or a bare IPv6 address such as 2001:db8::5, without brackets. A port (db:1433), credentials, a path, an instance name (db\\INST) or whitespace is rejected: the collector builds the connection URL from it, and an unparsable URL would put the password into the driver's error. Built-in probe names: waits, blocking, sessions, memory_pressure, storage, throughput, cpu, buffer_cache, file_io. See the metrics reference. Any other name, in an enabled collector or not, is rejected when the file is loaded or reloaded, and the error lists the valid ones. A query_template replaces a built-in probe's query; it cannot add a probe. Probe timeout defaults to min(scrape_interval / 2, 10s); timeout_ms overrides it and is capped at the interval. timeout_ms may not exceed 25000 (25s): a larger value is rejected when the configuration is loaded or reloaded. A probe that has not stopped 2s after its timeout is abandoned and fails as a timeout. Every database read also gives up after 30s, the 25s maximum plus a margin for the cancel round trip, so the driver never cuts off a probe that is still within its timeout; only an already-dead connection reaches it. A probe reads at most max_rows rows (default 10000, at most 100000). When its query returns more, the collector stops reading at the first row past the limit, cancels the query and fails the probe for that cycle, as for a query error: the error is logged (returned more rows than max_rows), the target is reported failed, and heartbeat_collector_probe_errors_total{reason=\"error\"} grows (metrics reference). The built-in probes return at most a few hundred rows; the limit stops an override, or a server with unusually many databases or files, from filling the collector's memory and the metric series. max_rows applies to sqlserver collectors only and is rejected on other kinds. A query_template override runs with the same login and the same session settings. It must be exactly one read-only SELECT, optionally led by WITH (CTEs); the configuration is rejected when it is loaded or reloaded otherwise, with an error that names the collector, target and probe and the reason, without quoting the query. Rejected: more than one statement: anything after a ; other than comments or more ;, or a second top-level SELECT that does not follow UNION, EXCEPT or INTERSECT (T-SQL needs no ; between statements); a first word other than SELECT or WITH, so a bare procedure name too; DML and DDL (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, TRUNCATE, CREATE, ALTER, DROP, ADD), permissions (GRANT, DENY, REVOKE), EXEC and EXECUTE, SELECT … INTO, OPENROWSET, OPENQUERY, OPENDATASOURCE, WAITFOR, SHUTDOWN, DBCC, BACKUP, RESTORE, USE, and the other T-SQL statement keywords: SET, DECLARE, transactions (BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVE), KILL, RECONFIGURE and the like (the full list is in query.go). The query hints MERGE JOIN, MERGE UNION, USE HINT (…) and USE PLAN are allowed; a name starting with sp_ or xp_ (system and extended procedures), and an END without a matching CASE; unbalanced parentheses, an unterminated string, comment or quoted identifier, or a control or Unicode line-separator character other than tab, CR and LF. Keywords inside string literals ('…', N'…'), comments (--, nested /* */) and quoted identifiers ([…], \"…\") are ignored, so a column named [Backup] is fine, while t.Backup is rejected (a reserved word must be quoted there too). A statement after a comment, or glued to a number as in SELECT 1DROP …, SELECT 1.DROP … or SELECT 1eDROP …, is still caught. The built-in probe and log-type queries pass the same check. It is a safety net, not the control: a text check cannot prove a query has no side effects. What keeps probes read-only is the collector's login, which holds only read grants (login permissions). Review every override against the probe review checklist and with the DBAs. outsystems_logs collectors An outsystems_logs collector extracts OutSystems 11 logs from the logging database and sends them to the OTel Collector as OTLP logs (ADR 0008). It uses the same document as sqlserver: environment, credential_ref, target_names, targets (the logging databases) and probes. Each probe is a log type, named after its query file in queries/ (error.sql is error). query_template replaces that query for one collector, under the same contract, and must be one read-only SELECT as for SQL Server probes; timeout_ms works as for SQL Server probes. max_rows does not apply: batch_limit bounds every read. scrape_interval is the poll interval. Setting Default Notes lag 60s Go duration, \u003e 0. Rows the log service commits later than this after their Instant are missed probes[].lag the collector's lag Go duration, \u003e 0. Replaces lag for one log type. A screen or cyclic_job row has the operation's start as Instant and is written when it ends, so set it above the longest screen request or timer (the timer timeout, 20 minutes by default) batch_limit 5000 1–50000. A full batch is followed at once by the next, up to 10 per cycle. If every row of a full batch has the same Instant, the cycle fails and heartbeat_outsystems_extract_stuck_batches_total grows: raise batch_limit. A batch also ends early at about 8 MiB of row data (below) initial_lookback 15m Go duration, \u003e 0. Also used when the stored watermark is unreadable or belongs to another database log_timezone UTC IANA name such as Europe/Lisbon; Local is rejected. With a zone that has daylight saving time, rows logged in the hour that repeats when clocks go back can be missed: Instant itself is ambiguous then recount_window 10m 0s (off), or whole minutes from 2m to 1h. Once a minute, each log type counts again the rows in this window behind its watermark, to find rows committed past the lag (heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total) or read twice (recount). A wider window finds later commits and counts more index rows each minute max_derived_series 50000 1–1000000. The most series the derived metrics hold for this collector, counted as Prometheus stores them: one per counter, and a histogram's buckets, +Inf, sum and count (13 for the latency and timer histograms, 11 for screen state). Past it, a row that needs a new series is not counted in that metric, heartbeat_outsystems_derived_series_overflow_total grows and OutSystemsDerivedSeriesOverflow fires; series that exist keep counting. Each series takes about 690 bytes of the collector's memory, so 50000 is about 33 MiB. The default is a proposal, not a measured need: 29 times the 1,716 series of PatientZero's production-sized profile (6 applications, 14 modules, 8 screens each, 20 timers, 4 front-ends); size it from heartbeat_outsystems_derived_series on the real installation. Changing it restarts the collector, which deletes its derived series and starts counting again Rules: opentelemetry.endpoint is required while the collector is enabled. Changing it restarts every outsystems_logs collector. A log type with no query file is rejected when the file is loaded or reloaded, and so is a log type listed twice for one target (each has one watermark). A log type of one logging database (same host, port and database name, compared case-insensitively) can be extracted by one enabled collector only; two would send every row twice. lag, batch_limit, initial_lookback, log_timezone, recount_window, max_derived_series and probes[].lag are rejected on other kinds, and probes[].max_rows on this one. With the recount on, a query_template must be one a derived table holds: no WITH, top-level OPTION, or FOR XML, JSON or BROWSE, an ORDER BY only with TOP (@limit) or OFFSET, and every column named. All but unnamed columns are rejected when the file is loaded; otherwise set recount_window: 0s. A query reads at most batch_limit rows: the collector stops at the first row past it and fails the cycle without sending anything, so a query_template that ignores @limit cannot fill the collector's memory. A batch stops reading once it holds about 8 MiB of row data, at the next row with a new Instant, and is then handled as a full batch: rows that share an Instant are never split. This bounds the collector's memory when a backlog of error rows with long stacks builds up, which batch_limit alone does not; a smaller batch is followed at once by the next. The body and every text attribute of a record are cut at 16 KiB, on a character boundary and ending in …[truncated], and the record gets outsystems.truncated = true. Stack and EnvironmentInformation are the usual ones; Loki keeps attributes as structured metadata, 64 KB per line by default. error.fingerprint is computed from the whole Message and Stack, so it does not change. The watermark of each collector, target and log type is a file under HEARTBEAT_STATE_DIR (outsystems_logs/\u003ccollector\u003e.\u003ctarget\u003e.\u003clog type\u003e.json). It moves only after the OTel Collector accepted the batch, so delivery is at least once. The chart's Collector keeps the batch it accepted on disk (the otelCollectorQueue volume), so a Collector restart does not lose it, and accepts the next one only once Loki has taken it (application observability). A batch the Collector has not accepted by the end of the probe execution is not a failure: its export carries on in the background, retried until the Collector accepts it, and the log type reads nothing more meanwhile. heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked is 1 and the target stays up (metrics). At most 64 MiB of row data is held in such batches across all log types; near that, a batch reads only what is left, and with less than 1 MiB left the other log types wait too. In each cycle the calls of one target wait for the Collector at most half the poll interval in all, so its other log types still run. The error in GET /admin/config names the last failed attempt, and an attempt that failed for another reason than waiting (a wrong opentelemetry.endpoint, a 5xx) is also logged as a warning. Removing a collector leaves its files; a collector re-added with the same id resumes from them. The login needs SELECT on the oslog_* views the queries read (the current and the previous week's) and nothing else; see database targets. Validation and reload behavior The file is validated on startup and on every reload. Values that fail the schema never render; a ConfigMap edited by hand can still be invalid, and is then rejected as below. An invalid candidate is rejected and the previous config keeps running. POST /admin/config/reload returns 400 for invalid config and 500 for a failed apply. At startup there is no previous config: the collector exits, so a file left invalid stops the next pod from starting. A rejected reload, or a failed one that was rolled back, does not make the pod unready. Monitoring shows it: heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_successful is 0 until the next successful reload, and HeartbeatCollectorConfigReloadFailed fires after 2 minutes (config reload metrics). Collectors are diffed by id; only changed, added or removed collectors are restarted. A failed apply is rolled back. If the rollback also fails, runtime_diverged is reported and /readyz returns 503. Reload triggers: SIGHUP, authenticated POST /admin/config/reload, and file polling when HEARTBEAT_CONFIG_WATCH_INTERVAL is set. GET /admin/config shows the active config redacted: credential values and every notification channel config value are masked, and userinfo is stripped from URLs. Credential resolution The DB collector looks up a credential_ref, and the API its PostgreSQL and Redis credential references, under one name: \u003cREF\u003e is the full reference upper-cased, with /, - and . replaced by _. The value must be username:password; the password is everything after the first :. credential_ref Name env/sqlserver-b HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_ENV_SQLSERVER_B kv/sqlserver-default HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_KV_SQLSERVER_DEFAULT It is looked up, in order: A file of that name in HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIALS_DIR, when that is set. A trailing line break is ignored. An environment variable of that name. Both read the file every time they open a connection, never cache it, so a new or changed credential needs no restart. A file that exists but cannot be read, or holds no :, fails that connection (a collector target, or the API's readiness); it never falls back to the environment variable. The lookup is internal/credentials. In Kubernetes the chart mounts the Secret named by dbCollector.credentials.existingSecret as that directory (/var/run/secrets/heartbeat/credentials), one file per Secret key. After you add or change a key, the kubelet updates the files within about a minute, and the next collection cycle uses them; the collector is not restarted. A target whose credential is missing fails on its own, and the others keep running. The Secret is not loaded into the environment, so a HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_* variable reaches the container only through dbCollector.extraEnv. The chart mounts the API's Secret, api.credentials.existingSecret, the same way, in the API's container and in its migrate init container; its keys are HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_POSTGRES and HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_REDIS (API). Outside Kubernetes (go run, tests), leave HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIALS_DIR unset and export the variables; exporting them in your host shell does not pass them into a pod. Environment variables DB collector Variable Default Purpose HEARTBEAT_DB_COLLECTOR_LISTEN_ADDR :8082 HTTP listen address HEARTBEAT_INTEGRATIONS_PATH config/integrations.yaml Integrations file path HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN unset Bearer token for GET /admin/config and POST /admin/config/reload; both answer 401 without it, and startup logs a warning. Surrounding whitespace is ignored HEARTBEAT_CONFIG_WATCH_INTERVAL unset (off) Go duration; poll the config file for changes (the chart sets 5s, dbCollector.configWatchInterval) HEARTBEAT_STATE_DIR /var/lib/heartbeat Writable directory for state that must survive a restart: the outsystems_logs watermarks. Created on the first save; the sqlserver kind never uses it. The chart mounts the dbCollector.persistence volume there HEARTBEAT_DB_COLLECTOR_SQLSERVER_TRUST_SERVER_CERTIFICATE false Skip TLS certificate verification. Local dev container only; applies to every target. When set, startup logs a warning and GET /admin/config lists it under warnings HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIALS_DIR unset Directory with one HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e file per credential, read on every connection; references without a file come from the environment. The chart sets it to the mounted dbCollector.credentials.existingSecret. See credential resolution HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e — Target credentials, see credential resolution GOMEMLIMIT unset Go's soft memory limit, such as 200MiB. The chart sets 80% of dbCollector.resources.limits.memory (204MiB for the default 256Mi), so the garbage collector works harder before the pod is OOM-killed; dbCollector.goMemLimit overrides it, off disables it OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT unset (stderr only) Where the service sends its own logs, besides stderr: the OTel Collector's otlp/self receiver, such as http://otel-collector:4319/v1/logs. The chart sets it while telemetry.enabled and otelCollector.enabled are on. Not read from OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, whose port is the OutSystems delivery path (ADR 0018) OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT unset (no spans) Where the service sends its spans, such as http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces. The chart sets it while telemetry.traces.enabled and otelCollector.enabled are on. The OpenTelemetry SDK reads the other standard variables itself OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER, OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG always on The chart sets parentbased_traceidratio and telemetry.traces.sampleRatio: every cycle and request by default OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES unset deployment.environment.name is read from it: the environment Heartbeat itself runs in. The chart sets it from telemetry.environment OTEL_SDK_DISABLED unset true turns off the OTLP export; stderr is unaffected HEARTBEAT_PPROF_ADDR unset (off) Loopback address for the runtime profiles at /debug/pprof/, such as 127.0.0.1:6060; any other address is refused with an error in the log. Set it with dbCollector.extraEnv, then kubectl port-forward pod/db-collector-0 6060 API The chart sets these from the api values: api.postgres and api.redis, or, when those are empty, the bundled postgresql and valkey without TLS (disable and false); any other server gets verify-full and TLS unless the values turn them off. HEARTBEAT_API_SITE_ORIGIN comes from api.siteOrigin; the other session settings take their defaults, or api.extraEnv. The telemetry variables are set as the collector's. A value that cannot work stops startup with an error naming the variable, instead of falling back to the default. Variable Default Purpose HEARTBEAT_API_LISTEN_ADDR :8081 HTTP listen address, host:port HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_HOST required PostgreSQL server: a host name or IP address, without a port HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_PORT 5432 PostgreSQL port HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_DATABASE heartbeat Database name HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_SSLMODE verify-full disable, require, verify-ca or verify-full, with libpq's meaning. allow and prefer are rejected: they fall back to plaintext without saying so. disable is for a server without TLS on a trusted network: kind, a local container HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_SSLROOTCERT unset (the system's CAs) PEM file of the CA certificates verify-ca and verify-full trust. Amazon RDS needs its CA bundle here HEARTBEAT_API_POSTGRES_CREDENTIAL_REF secret/api-postgres Credential reference of the login, username:password (credential resolution): by default HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_POSTGRES. api migrate up uses it too; give that command another reference to migrate as a different role, such as the schema's owner HEARTBEAT_API_REDIS_ADDR required Redis (Valkey) server, host:port HEARTBEAT_API_REDIS_TLS true TLS, verified against the system's CAs and the host name, as ElastiCache's in-transit encryption needs. false for a server without TLS on a trusted network: kind, a local container HEARTBEAT_API_REDIS_CREDENTIAL_REF secret/api-redis Credential reference of the login, username:password: by default HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_SECRET_API_REDIS. For Valkey's requirepass or an ElastiCache AUTH token, the username is default, or empty to send the password alone HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIALS_DIR unset As for the DB collector: a directory of HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e files, read on every new connection HEARTBEAT_API_AUDIT_ENDPOINT unset (records wait) OTLP/HTTP base URL audit records are sent to: the OTel Collector's logs receiver with the persistent queue, the one the OutSystems logs use, such as http://otel-collector:4318; a trailing /v1/logs is dropped. The chart sets it while otelCollector.enabled is on. Unset, the API logs audit delivery off at startup and the records wait in the outbox, which HeartbeatAuditDeliveryStalled reports (audit delivery). Not OTEL_SDK_DISABLED's to turn off: audit records are not the API's own telemetry, and never go to the 4319 receiver of its own logs HEARTBEAT_API_SITE_ORIGIN unset (unsafe requests refused) The origin browsers reach the API at, such as https://heartbeat.example.com: scheme and host, a port only when it is not the scheme's default, no path. POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE need one Origin header equal to it, or are refused with 403 before they run, logging in included (sessions). Written as a browser writes the header: lower case, without :443 or :80 or a trailing slash. Unset, the API logs a warning at startup and nobody can log in HEARTBEAT_API_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT 8h A session ends after this long without a request. A Go duration, at least 1m, at most the max age. Requests move the deadline at most once a minute HEARTBEAT_API_SESSION_MAX_AGE 168h (7 days) A session ends this long after login, however active. At least 1m. The cookie's Max-Age too HEARTBEAT_API_TRUSTED_PROXIES unset (none) Comma-separated addresses or CIDRs, such as 10.0.0.0/8, of proxies (an ingress controller) whose X-Forwarded-For names the client. The API reads the header from its end, only as far as these proxies wrote it, so a client cannot choose its own address. The client address is what failed logins are counted by and what audit records show; unset, it is the TCP peer's HEARTBEAT_API_COLLECTOR_URL unset (observations unknown) The DB collector's base URL, such as http://db-collector:8082, without a path. The API reads its redacted GET /admin/config, at most every 15s, to say whether a telemetry source is observed (telemetry sources). The chart sets it to the db-collector Service while dbCollector.enabled is on, and the collector's NetworkPolicy admits the API HEARTBEAT_API_COLLECTOR_ADMIN_TOKEN unset The collector's admin token (its HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN), sent as a bearer token. The chart takes it from the collector's Secret, dbCollector.adminToken; it is never logged. Without it the collector answers 401, and observations are unknown. Both services read the token at startup, so rotating it means restarting the collector and the API; until both run with the new one, observations are unknown (refused the API's admin token) HEARTBEAT_API_SHUTDOWN_DELAY 5s How long the API keeps serving after SIGTERM, with GET /readyz answering 503, before it stops accepting connections: time for its Service to stop sending it requests. 0s to 1m. The chart's api.terminationGracePeriodSeconds (30) covers 5s plus the 10s drain and the rest of the shutdown; raise it with the delay OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT, OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT, OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER, OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG, OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES, OTEL_SDK_DISABLED, HEARTBEAT_PPROF_ADDR unset As for the DB collector. The api migrate and api users commands send no telemetry GOMEMLIMIT unset As for the DB collector, from api.resources.limits.memory and api.goMemLimit HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e — A credential without a file, see credential resolution The settings above win over libpq's PG* environment variables, which the PostgreSQL driver also reads. Each replica keeps at most 8 PostgreSQL connections (application_name heartbeat-api); a connection attempt gives up after 5s. The API connects lazily: it starts while PostgreSQL or Redis is down or its credential is missing, and GET /readyz answers 503 until both work (endpoints). The same binary applies the migrations and creates admins (ADR 0013): the chart runs api migrate up in each API pod's migrate init container, before the API starts. These commands read only the PostgreSQL settings and the credential lookup, then exit: Command Does api migrate up Applies, in order, each migration of db/migrations newer than the database's version, and records it in schema_migrations. Replicas may run it at once: a PostgreSQL advisory lock makes the others wait and each migration runs once. Each wait for the lock ends after 5 minutes, or on SIGTERM, and fails the command. A database already past the newest migration it embeds (after rolling the API back) is left alone, and the command succeeds. A dirty version fails it (repair) api migrate version Prints the schema version, 0 when never migrated, with  (dirty) while a migration runs or after one failed partway. It only reads schema_migrations: it creates nothing and does not wait for a migration in progress api users create-admin --email ADDRESS [--name NAME] Creates a user with the admin role and the password read from standard input, and its audit record, in one transaction. There is no default password: this is how the first admin is made, and how anyone who can run a command in an API container gets back in. The email is stored in lower case and must not belong to a user already, in any case; the name defaults to the email's local part. The password is the first line of standard input, its line end dropped, from 15 characters (NIST SP 800-63B-4's minimum for a single factor) to 1,024 bytes, stored only as its argon2id hash (RFC 9106's second recommended setting: 64 MiB, t=3, p=4). A terminal is refused, since it echoes what is typed: pipe the password in. Fails, writing nothing, on a schema older than the API's (run api migrate up). Logs the user's ID and the audit record's ID; the record is sent by the API's drainer, as api users create-admin on the pod's host name Creating the first admin, where the password never reaches the command line or the shell history; on a cluster, the same through kubectl exec -i into an API pod (the chart adds the API [4.1]): Local development files File Tracked Purpose .env.sqlserver-dev.example yes Template for the SQL Server sandbox .env.sqlserver-dev no (mode 600) Random per-machine SA password and least-privilege collector credential (heartbeat_collector:\u003cpassword\u003e), generated by make sqlserver-dev-init; make sqlserver-dev-up creates that login with sa and copies only the collector credential into the heartbeat-sqlserver-dev-credentials Secret infra/helm/values/sqlserver-dev.yaml yes Values that point the collector at the sandbox container"},{"t":"Metrics and Endpoints Reference","u":"reference/metrics-and-endpoints.html","h":[{"t":"Local ports (kind)","id":"local-ports-kind"},{"t":"DB collector (:8082)","id":"db-collector-8082"},{"t":"API (:8081)","id":"api-8081"},{"t":"Logging in and sessions","id":"logging-in-and-sessions"},{"t":"Inventory","id":"inventory"},{"t":"Telemetry sources","id":"telemetry-sources"},{"t":"Database targets","id":"database-targets"},{"t":"Probe definitions","id":"probe-definitions"},{"t":"Probe assignments","id":"probe-assignments"},{"t":"Users and roles","id":"users-and-roles"},{"t":"Metrics","id":"metrics"},{"t":"SQL Server probe metrics","id":"sql-server-probe-metrics"},{"t":"Collector self-observability","id":"collector-self-observability"},{"t":"Config reloads","id":"config-reloads"},{"t":"OutSystems log extraction","id":"outsystems-log-extraction"},{"t":"Derived OutSystems metrics","id":"derived-outsystems-metrics"},{"t":"API requests","id":"api-requests"},{"t":"Logins","id":"logins"},{"t":"Audit delivery","id":"audit-delivery"},{"t":"Heartbeat's own telemetry","id":"heartbeats-own-telemetry"},{"t":"What the telemetry costs","id":"what-the-telemetry-costs"},{"t":"Prometheus scrape jobs and rules","id":"prometheus-scrape-jobs-and-rules"},{"t":"Known gaps","id":"known-gaps"}],"x":"Metrics and Endpoints Reference Ports, HTTP endpoints and Prometheus metrics exposed by the local stack and Heartbeat services. For how the pieces connect, see the architecture overview. Local ports (kind) The kind cluster (infra/kind/cluster.yaml) maps fixed NodePorts to these ports on 127.0.0.1 only. Service Host port(s) Notes DB collector 8082 Heartbeat service Prometheus 9090 15s scrape interval Grafana 3001 Local login admin/admin, anonymous viewer enabled. Local only. Alertmanager 9093  OpenTelemetry Collector 4317 (OTLP gRPC), 4318 (OTLP HTTP) full profile. Heartbeat's own logs use a third port, 4319, in-cluster only Loki none In-cluster only (loki:3100); query it through Grafana, or kubectl port-forward svc/loki 3100 Tempo none In-cluster only (tempo:3200, OTLP on tempo:4318); query it through Grafana, or kubectl port-forward svc/tempo 3200. full profile SQL Server (dev target only) 127.0.0.1:11433 Loopback-bound Docker container; see the local development guide Inside the cluster every component keeps its Compose-era name and port (prometheus:9090, alertmanager:9093, loki:3100, otel-collector:4318, db-collector:8082), beside the API (api:8081), its PostgreSQL (postgresql:5432) and Valkey (valkey:6379). NetworkPolicies admit only the components that use each one (network access): a pod of your own times out on them, so reach them through the host ports above, Grafana or kubectl port-forward; the API has no host port. DB collector (:8082) Endpoint Auth Behavior GET /metrics none Prometheus exposition: probe metrics and self-observability GET /healthz (alias /healthcheck) none Liveness only; 200 while the process serves HTTP GET /readyz none 503 until every collector finishes its first cycle, when a collector is failed or crash-looping, when a collector has not completed a cycle within 2× its interval + 10s, or when the runtime diverged after a failed rollback. A monitored database being down does not make it unready, even when every target fails: readiness says whether the collector itself works, so a database outage never takes it out of its Service or fails a helm upgrade --wait. Target health is heartbeat_collector_target_up and the target states below. The body is only {\"status\":\"ready\"} or {\"status\":\"not_ready\"}; the reasons are in GET /admin/config. GET /admin/config Authorization: Bearer $HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN Diagnostics, see below POST /admin/config/reload Authorization: Bearer $HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN 400 invalid config, 500 apply failure, 503 before the first reconcile or while another reload holds the lock. The body includes the error text and the readiness report Reload can also be triggered with SIGHUP or file polling. GET /admin/config returns: Field Content version, loaded_at, last_reload_at Active config version (SHA-256 of the file) and timestamps last_reload_err, runtime_diverged, rollback_err Why the last reload was rejected or failed; set until the next successful reload. Monitoring sees it as heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_successful 0 warnings Insecure startup settings, e.g. sqlserver_trust_server_certificate readiness What /readyz decided and why: status, reasons, and per collector its phase, cycle age, restarts and targets. Each target has its state (ok, failed, backoff, blocked for an outsystems_logs target waiting for the OTel Collector, or degraded for a target with some probes failing), consecutive failures, last success and the raw driver error of its last failed cycle, kept while it backs off, which can name the host, port and login but never contains the password. A blocked target's error says which log type waits, with how many rows, and for how long. A degraded target lists its failing probes, each with its state (failed, or backoff while it waits out its own backoff), consecutive failures, next attempt and error config The active config, redacted (configuration reference) Admin authentication. Both admin endpoints go through one check: exactly one Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e header (scheme case-insensitive), whose SHA-256 digest is compared in constant time with the digest of HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN computed at startup, so neither the token nor its length leaks through timing. A missing or wrong token gets 401 with a WWW-Authenticate: Bearer challenge before the method is checked (an authenticated wrong method gets 405). Admin responses are Cache-Control:\nno-store. With no token configured, both endpoints always answer 401. Network access. In Kubernetes the db-collector NetworkPolicy admits only Prometheus and the configured operator peers to port 8082 (what the chart deploys). Operators reach the admin endpoints with kubectl -n heartbeat port-forward pod/db-collector-0 8082, which NetworkPolicy does not apply to, or from a listed peer such as a VPN range. API (:8081) The control plane (ADR 0013). The chart deploys it as Deployment/api behind Service/api (ClusterIP); reach it with kubectl -n heartbeat port-forward svc/api 8081. Its contract is apps/api/openapi.yaml; /metrics is served beside it. Endpoint Auth Behavior GET /metrics none Prometheus exposition: request metrics, logins, audit delivery, Go runtime and process metrics GET /healthz none Liveness only; 200 ok while the process serves HTTP. It checks no dependency, so a PostgreSQL or Redis outage never restarts the pod GET /readyz none 200 when every readiness check passes, 503 otherwise. The checks run concurrently on each call, 2s at most in all; a check without an answer by then fails. postgresql: a query reaches the database and its schema version is at least the newest migration the API embeds, and that migration is not dirty (a newer version passes, dirty or not, so the previous release keeps serving while the next one migrates). redis: a PING reaches Redis (Valkey) and authenticates. Each passes again on its own once its dependency is back. The body is only {\"status\":\"ready\"} or {\"status\":\"not_ready\"}; the failing checks and their errors (schema not migrated, a refused connection, a failed login, never a password) are logged when the status changes POST /api/v1/session none; Origin Log in: 201 with the session and its cookie (below) GET /api/v1/session session, viewer 200 with the current session and its user DELETE /api/v1/session session, viewer; Origin Log out: 204, and the cookie is cleared GET /api/v1/environments, /applications, /components and GET …/{id} session, viewer The inventory (below) POST, PATCH /{id} and DELETE /{id} on /api/v1/environments, /applications, /components session, operator; Origin Create, change and delete inventory, each audited (below) GET /api/v1/telemetry-sources and GET …/{id} session, viewer Telemetry sources, each with its observation (below) POST, PATCH /{id} and DELETE /{id} on /api/v1/telemetry-sources session, operator; Origin Register, change and delete telemetry sources, each audited (below) GET /api/v1/database-targets and GET /api/v1/database-targets/{id} session, viewer Monitored databases (below) POST, PATCH /{id} and DELETE /{id} on /api/v1/database-targets session, operator; Origin Create, change and delete them GET /api/v1/probe-definitions session, viewer 200 with the latest version of every probe definition (below) POST /api/v1/probe-definitions session, admin; Origin Create a custom definition: 201 with version 1 GET /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id} session, viewer 200 with that version, the latest or an older one PATCH /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id} session, admin; Origin {\"enabled\": false} disables a custom definition, every version of it; true enables it again GET /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id}/versions session, viewer 200 with every version of the definition, the newest first POST /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id}/versions session, admin; Origin Change a custom definition: 201 with the next version PUT /api/v1/account/password session, viewer; Origin Change your own password: 204, every session of yours ends and the cookie is cleared (below) GET /api/v1/users, GET /api/v1/users/{id} session, admin The users, by email (?status=active or disabled), or one POST /api/v1/users, PATCH /api/v1/users/{id} session, admin; Origin Create a user, or change their email, name or status PUT /api/v1/users/{id}/password session, admin; Origin Set a user's password: 204, and every session of theirs ends PUT, DELETE /api/v1/users/{id}/roles/{role} session, admin; Origin Grant or revoke a role On SIGTERM or SIGINT the API answers GET /readyz with 503 and keeps serving for HEARTBEAT_API_SHUTDOWN_DELAY (5s), while its Service stops sending it requests; then it stops accepting connections and its audit drainer, waits up to 10s for the requests in flight, closes the rest, then closes its PostgreSQL and Redis connections. An audit export in flight is abandoned, and its records are sent again by another replica or the next one; records the Collector already accepted are still deleted, within 5s. Logging in and sessions Local users log in with their email and password and get a cookie session (ADR 0013 §7–8). The first admin comes from api users create-admin; admins make the other users and give them roles through the API (users and roles). Who may call what. Every operation in openapi.yaml declares it with security: [] for one anyone may call, or the session scheme with the least role it needs. Roles rank viewer (reads; wallboards) \u003c operator (changes inventory, sources, targets and probes) \u003c admin (everything, users and roles included); a user may call what any of their roles allows. The API reads each operation's rule from the spec at startup and refuses to start if one declares none, so no operation is served on a default. Before each operation, in this order: the response gets an X-Request-Id (a UUID, the request_id of any audit record the request writes); a body over 1 MiB is refused; POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE need one Origin header equal to HEARTBEAT_API_SITE_ORIGIN (403 otherwise; browsers send it with every such request, so a page on another site cannot make one with the user's cookie); then, unless the operation is public, the session cookie must name a live session (401, and the cookie is cleared) whose user is active and has the role (403). The user and their roles are read on every request: disabling a user or changing their roles applies to their next request. Only then are the path and query parameters read (400 for a malformed one), so a request without a session gets 401 whatever its parameters, and then the body: a JSON object whose members are all listed by the operation's schema, spelt exactly, or 400 naming the first that is not. A member that cannot be set is refused, never ignored. Logging in. POST /api/v1/session with {\"email\": \"...\", \"password\": \"...\"}: Status When 201 Logged in. The body is the session (id, user with id, email, display_name, roles, created_at, expires_at); Set-Cookie holds the session 400 Not JSON, an email that is not an address, or a password that is empty, over 1,024 bytes or not UTF-8. Not counted as a failure 401 Wrong password, unknown email, disabled user, a user without a password: the same wrong email or password for all, after the same work (an unknown email is checked against a decoy hash), so neither the answer nor its time tells which accounts exist 403 Origin missing or not the site 429 Too many failures: 10 for the account, or 50 from the client address (an IPv6 address counts as its /64), within 15 minutes of the first. Refused without checking the password, even the right one, until the window ends; Retry-After says in how many seconds. A login counts before its password is checked, so concurrent attempts cannot pass the limits. A success clears the account's count, not the address's A hash made with older argon2id parameters is replaced with one made with the current ones at a successful login. The cookie is __Host-heartbeat_session: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, Path=/, no Domain, kept until the session's absolute end. Its value is 256 random bits, base64url; Redis keeps only its SHA-256, as the key of the session (user, client address, created and last-seen times), which expires by itself at the earlier of 8 hours without a request and 7 days after login (HEARTBEAT_API_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT, _MAX_AGE). The session's id in the body and in audit records is another random UUID: it identifies the session but cannot log in. A browser keeps a Secure cookie only from HTTPS, or from localhost. Audit. A login, a logout and a failed login each write an audit record of entity type session (audit delivery): login with the session in after and the user as actor, logout with it in before, login_failed with the email tried, the user's ID when it exists and the reason (wrong_password, unknown_email, disabled, no_password, unreadable_hash, changed_meanwhile) in after, and the client's address as actor (client). The client is never told the reason. No record holds a password, a hash, the token or its SHA-256. A login is written in one transaction with its record and the user's last_login_at; the session is created in Redis before it commits and deleted again if the commit fails. A logout whose session cannot be deleted fails and records nothing. On kind, through a port-forward, with the admin made by api users create-admin; the password comes from a file, never the command line: Inventory Environments, their applications, and the applications' components (data model), under /api/v1. Viewers read; operators create, change and delete. The fields and their limits are in openapi.yaml. Path Operations Notes /api/v1/environments GET (?status=), POST Listed whole, by name: an organisation has few /api/v1/environments/{id} GET, PATCH, DELETE slug cannot change /api/v1/applications GET (?environment_id=, ?status=, paged), POST environment_id in the body /api/v1/applications/{id} GET, PATCH, DELETE environment_id cannot change /api/v1/components GET (?application_id=, paged), POST application_id in the body /api/v1/components/{id} GET, PATCH, DELETE application_id cannot change One ownership path. An application belongs to the environment it was created in, and a component to its application, for good: the same system in another environment is another application. A component's environment_id is read through its application; it is not stored and cannot be set. A PATCH or POST naming an owner it cannot set is 400. Environment slugs are what the collectors call the environment: each collector's environment, the environment label of the probe metrics and the logs' deployment.environment.name (ADR 0017 §2). Lower-case letters, digits and hyphens, 1 to 63, starting and ending with a letter or a digit (prod, local-dev); unique, and fixed once created. Pages. Lists of applications and components hold limit items (1 to 200, 50 by default), by name then ID, and a next_cursor while more follow; pass it as cursor for the next page. The cursor marks a position, not an offset, so an item added or removed meanwhile neither repeats nor skips another. Changes. POST answers 201 with the entity and its Location. PATCH changes the members the body holds and keeps the others; labels are replaced whole. A PATCH that changes nothing answers 200 with the entity as it is, keeps updated_at and writes no audit record. Disable an environment or an application (status: disabled) to retire it; disabling an environment leaves its applications' status as it is. Status When 400 A field out of its limits or format, an unknown member, a malformed ID, limit or cursor 404 No entity has the ID 409 Another environment has the slug, the environment has an application of that name, or the application a component of that name and type; or a DELETE while other rows refer to the entity: the detail names them (an environment's applications, database targets or investigations; an application's components, telemetry sources, investigations, alert policies, notification routes, alert events or reports), and nothing is deleted. A deleted application's adaptive baselines go with it 422 The body's environment_id or application_id names nothing Audit. Each change writes one record in its transaction: entity type environment, application or application_component, action create, update or delete, the entity as the API shows it in before and after, the user as actor. A refused change records nothing. Telemetry sources Which telemetry is an application's own (ADR 0017), under /api/v1/telemetry-sources: GET (?application_id=, ?environment_id=, ?source_type=, ?status=, paged by application_name) and POST; GET, PATCH and DELETE on /{id}. Viewers read; operators register, change and delete. Each change is audited as entity type telemetry_source. Owner. A source belongs to the application it was registered for, for good; its environment_id is the application's, read through it. Neither the application nor the source type can change. config is checked against its type's JSON Schema, packages/config-schema/src/telemetry-sources. For outsystems, application_name is the OutSystems Application_Name, the service.name of its logs, matched exactly. The schemas are closed and hold no credential: a schema names one only as a reference (env/, kv/ or secret/, format credential-ref), and a test fails on a schema that breaks this. ingest_mode is agentless (OutSystems 11, read from the logging database) or otlp (OutSystems Cloud and ODC). One name, one application. Within an environment, an application_name belongs to one application (409 otherwise), and an application has one source per type, ingest mode and name. Observation. Each source says whether the DB collector observes it. The API reads the collector's redacted GET /admin/config with the collector's admin token, at most every 15 seconds, within 2 seconds. observation.status When observed An enabled outsystems_logs collector extracts a target whose environment is the source's environment's slug (the collector's environment unless the target sets its own; only the targets target_names selects). collectors lists them not_observed The collector answered, and none does. A source may be registered before its extraction is set up; nothing is refused unknown The collector could not be asked: no URL set, no answer, a refused token. detail says which; the request itself still succeeds not_applicable An otlp source, which sends its telemetry itself The observation is not stored or audited: it is read with the source. Database targets The databases the collector monitors [4.5], under /api/v1/database-targets (GET with ?environment_id=, ?status= and pages like applications'; POST; GET, PATCH and DELETE on /{id}). Viewers read; operators create, change and delete. A target belongs to the environment it was created in, for good, as an application does. Field Rule name The target label of its metrics, beside the environment's: a slug like an environment's (finance-prod), unique in its environment, fixed once created. The collector refuses two targets of one environment with one name, since they would write the same series engine sqlserver, the default; fixed host Checked as the collector checks it: a host name, an IPv4 address or a bare IPv6 address, with no port, brackets, credentials or path; at most 253 characters; stored in lower case, and an IPv6 address in its canonical form port 1 to 65535; 1433 when left out database_name Empty for the login's default database; at most 128 characters; compared without regard to case, as SQL Server does by default credential_ref kv/, secret/ or env/ and a name of lower-case letters, digits and -, at most 200. The collector reads username:password from the key HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e of its credentials Secret (credential resolution): env/finance-prod is HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_ENV_FINANCE_PROD. Stricter than the collector's own rule, which the API runs too, so the API stores no reference the collector refuses, and each key has one spelling: env/Finance.Prod, which the collector would read from the same key, is refused rather than passing for another reference. A value shaped like a credential is refused, and the problem does not repeat it status, labels As for applications The environment, engine and host, port and database together are unique: a second target for the same database in one environment is 409. Credentials and reachability. The API stores the reference only and never connects to a target (ADR 0013 §1): it has no credential to connect with. The credential reaches the collector on its own path, a Secret key (database targets guide). Whether a target is reachable is the collector's to report, once a probe assignment renders it into a collector: its heartbeat_collector_target_up, and its state and driver error in GET /admin/config (readiness.collectors[].targets[]). A change to a target's host, port, database or credential reference sends its approved assignments back to draft, each with an audit record of its own: an approval covers the target as it was, so the collector never logs in to a server, or with a credential, that nobody approved. A change of status or labels keeps them: a disabled target is not rendered, and enabled again it runs as approved. Deletes. A target's probe assignments would be deleted with it, so a delete is refused (409) while it has any: disable it instead (status: disabled). Audit. Each change writes one record in its transaction: entity type database_target, action create, update or delete, the target as the API shows it, credential reference included, in before and after. Probe definitions A probe definition is what a probe assignment runs against a database target [4.5]. It is versioned: each version is immutable once stored, except whether its definition is enabled, so an assignment that pins a version keeps what was approved, and nothing is ever deleted. Built-in definitions are the db-collector's SQL Server catalog, one per probe, version 1, seeded by migration 0004 with no query: the collector runs its own. They are read-only (409) and change only with the collector; a test fails when the seed and the catalog differ. The OutSystems log types are not definitions: their collectors stay in YAML (ADR 0017). Custom definitions replace the query of the built-in they overrides, as a query_template does: that built-in's metric descriptors decode the rows, so the query returns the same columns in the same units, and the definition takes its category. Only admins create and change them, since their SQL runs on monitored databases; review each one with the probe review checklist. POST /api/v1/probe-definitions takes engine (sqlserver), name (custom_ and then lower-case snake_case, at most 63 characters: no built-in's name starts with custom_, so a probe the collector adds later never clashes with one), overrides, query_template and, optionally, description (at most 1,000 characters), default_interval_seconds (5 to 3,600; 30, the collector's default, when left out) and timeout_ms (1 to 25,000; left out, the collector's default, half the interval and at most 10 s). engine, name and overrides are set once: every version overrides the same built-in. POST /api/v1/probe-definitions/{id}/versions takes the rest: a version is the whole content, so a field left out takes its default, not the previous version's value. The query must be one read-only SELECT, checked by the same function the db-collector runs when it loads integrations.yaml (config.ValidateReadOnlySelect), so the API stores no query the collector would refuse; the problem's detail says why, without quoting the query. As in the collector, the check is a safety net and the collector's read-only login is the control. It does not check which columns the query returns. Status When 201 Created, or a version added; Location is the new version's URL 200 From POST .../versions: the content equals the version the path names, so nothing was added 400 A field out of bounds or an unknown member, an explicit 0 for default_interval_seconds or timeout_ms, an overrides that names no enabled built-in of the engine, a query that is not one read-only SELECT, or a malformed ID 404 No version has the ID 409 The name is taken; the definition is built-in, or disabled (enable it before adding a version); or the version the path names is no longer the latest, because someone added one since it was read: read the latest and change that, so no change is lost Changes to one definition take turns: each locks the definition until it commits. Audit. Every change writes an audit record of entity type probe_definition in the same transaction (audit delivery), with the admin as actor and the versions in full, query included: create with version 1 in after; update for a new version, whose ID is the record's entity_id, with the version it follows in before; disable and enable with the version the request named. Probe assignments A probe assignment runs one probe definition version against one database target, at an interval [4.5] (ADR 0014), under /api/v1/probe-assignments (GET with ?database_target_id=, ?state= and pages; POST; GET, PATCH and DELETE on /{id}; POST /{id}/approve and POST /{id}/reject). Viewers read; operators create, change and delete; admins approve and reject. The API never writes cluster state. It renders every approved, enabled assignment of an active target and an enabled definition into one values file, the chart's managedCollectors: one sqlserver collector per target, with the ID api-\u003ctarget id\u003e-\u003cinterval\u003es, which the assignment shows as collector_id. A built-in definition is listed by name; a custom one by the name of the probe it overrides, with its query in full, for review. The file is deterministic (sorted, no timestamps), and checked with the integrations schema and the collector's loader before it leaves the API. GET /api/v1/probe-assignments/rendered serves it to operators as application/yaml; on kind, make kind-assignments deploys it. The pull request to heartbeat-deploy that carries it in production waits for that repository [18]. State Meaning draft Created or changed, not yet approved; not rendered approved Approved by an admin; rendered applied The collector runs it as rendered: its collector is in GET /admin/config, enabled at the interval, with the target as rendered and the probe with the same query, timeout and row limit. The API checks every 30 s and moves approved to applied only rejected Refused by an admin, with the reason in state_detail; not rendered. Change it, or approve it after all, to try again proposed, failed With the pull request output: in an open pull request, or refused by the config repository's checks or by the collector Field Rule database_target_id Set at creation, for good probe_definition_id A version of an enabled definition for the target's engine, pinned: a new version changes nothing until the assignment is changed to it. A change may name another version of the same definition only interval_seconds 5 to 3,600; the definition's default_interval_seconds when left out. A target runs at one interval, its collector's scrape_interval: an approval is refused while the target's other approved, enabled assignments run at another timeout_ms 0 or left out for the definition's timeout, or else the collector's (half the interval, at most 10 s); at most 25,000 and at most the interval, the definition's counted when the assignment sets none max_rows 0 or left out for the collector's 10,000; at most 100,000. A run that returns more fails enabled true when left out. A disabled assignment is not rendered A target runs each probe once: a second assignment of a definition on one target, or of another definition that runs the same probe (a custom definition runs the built-in it overrides), is 409. Any change, to the assignment or to its target's connection, puts the assignment back to draft, unapproved, so the next render leaves it out until an admin approves it again: what runs is always what was approved. A body that changes nothing changes nothing. Changes to one target's assignments take turns, on the target's row lock. Status When 201 Created, as a draft 400 A field out of bounds or an unknown member, a reason with control characters, or a malformed ID 404 No assignment has the ID 409 The probe is assigned on the target already; the definition is disabled, for another engine, or (on a change) another definition; an approval of an assignment that is not a draft or rejected, whose definition was disabled since, at another interval than the target's approved ones, or whose render the collector would refuse; a rejection of one that is not a draft 422 The target or the definition version does not exist Audit. Every change writes an audit record of entity type probe_assignment in its transaction, with the assignment as the API shows it in before and after: create, update, delete, and approve and reject by the admin. apply is written by the API itself, actor type system with the command probe assignment applied check. Users and roles What each role may do. Each user has one or more roles; a role allows what the roles below it do, and a user may call what any of their roles allows. Role May For viewer Read everything the API serves: inventory, telemetry sources, database targets, probes and their assignments. Show their own session, log out, change their own password. Nothing else changes Wallboards and people who only look. A wallboard logs in as a user of its own with only viewer, so its session, left on a shared screen, can change nothing operator What a viewer may, and create, change and delete inventory, telemetry sources, database targets and probe assignments The SREs who onboard applications and databases admin What an operator may, and manage custom probe definitions, whose SQL runs on monitored databases (below), and users and roles Whoever runs Heartbeat. The first comes from api users create-admin Each operation's least role is its security in openapi.yaml, and the roles are rows in roles (migrations 0002 and 0003). Managing users takes admin. Users are never deleted: disable them. Create: POST /api/v1/users with {\"email\", \"display_name\", \"password\", \"roles\": [...]}. display_name defaults to the email's local part, and at least one role is needed. The email is stored in lower case and must not belong to another user in any case (409). The password is 15 characters to 1,024 bytes and is stored only as its argon2id hash. It appears in no response, log or audit record. Change: PATCH /api/v1/users/{id} with any of email, display_name and status. Disabling a user ends every session they have at once, and refuses their logins until they are active again. A body that changes nothing changes and audits nothing. Set a password: PUT /api/v1/users/{id}/password with {\"password\"}, as for a user who has forgotten theirs. Every session of theirs ends. Roles: PUT and DELETE /api/v1/users/{id}/roles/{role} grant and revoke one. Granting a role the user has, or revoking one they lack, changes nothing. A user keeps at least one role: revoking their only one is refused (409); disable them instead. The user's next request has the new roles; their sessions go on. The last active admin can be neither disabled nor stripped of admin (409). The check and the change hold a lock on the admin role, so two admins cannot disable each other at the same moment and leave none. A password change and its sessions' end are one step. The change locks the user's row and ends their sessions in Redis before it commits; if the sessions cannot end, nothing changes. A login records itself only while the user is active and still has the hash it checked. So a login that checked the old password just before the change gets 401 instead of a session, and its audit record gives the reason changed_meanwhile. Your own password: PUT /api/v1/account/password with {\"current_password\", \"new_password\"} takes any role. Status When 204 Changed. Every session of yours ends, this one included, and the cookie is cleared: log in again 400 A new password that is too short or too long, or a current one that is empty or over 1,024 bytes 403 The current password is wrong, or Origin is missing or not the site. A wrong current password counts against the login limits as a failed login does, so a stolen session cannot be used to guess the password 429 Past those limits; refused without checking Audit. Each change writes a record of entity type user, with the admin, or the user themselves, as actor: Action Change Record create a user created the user in after update a user changed the user in before and after: email, display_name, status, roles grant_role, revoke_role a role granted or revoked the user in before and after set_password a password set, by an admin or the user after is the user's id, their email and sessions_ended: true set_password_failed a refused change of your own password the reason wrong_password No record holds a password, a hash or a session token: the secrets test makes each of these changes and checks. Metrics SQL Server probe metrics Every probe metric carries environment and target labels, plus the probe's own label columns. Names follow Prometheus conventions: base units (seconds, bytes) and a _total suffix on counters only. Probe Source view Metric Type Extra labels Series per target waits sys.dm_os_wait_stats heartbeat_sqlserver_wait_seconds_total counter wait_type one per wait type with non-zero wait time, benign idle waits excluded: usually tens to low hundreds, at most the ~1,000 wait types SQL Server defines blocking sys.dm_exec_requests heartbeat_sqlserver_blocked_requests gauge blocking_session_id one per blocking session; none while nothing is blocked sessions sys.dm_exec_sessions heartbeat_sqlserver_sessions gauge status one per session status (about 5) memory_pressure sys.dm_os_performance_counters heartbeat_sqlserver_total_server_memory_bytes gauge none 1 storage sys.master_files heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_size_bytes gauge database_name, file_name, file_type one per database file; tempdb files report their configured startup size, not their current size throughput sys.dm_os_performance_counters heartbeat_sqlserver_batch_requests_total counter none 1 throughput sys.dm_os_performance_counters heartbeat_sqlserver_transactions_total counter none 1 (server-wide _Total) cpu sys.dm_os_ring_buffers heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_sql_process_ratio gauge none 1 cpu sys.dm_os_ring_buffers heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_other_process_ratio gauge none 1 on Windows, none on Linux buffer_cache sys.dm_os_performance_counters heartbeat_sqlserver_page_life_expectancy_seconds gauge none 1 buffer_cache sys.dm_os_performance_counters heartbeat_sqlserver_buffer_cache_hit_ratio gauge none 1 file_io sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats, sys.master_files heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_reads_total, heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_writes_total counter database_name, file_name, file_type one per database file each file_io sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats, sys.master_files heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_read_bytes_total, heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_written_bytes_total counter database_name, file_name, file_type one per database file each file_io sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats, sys.master_files heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_read_stall_seconds_total, heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_write_stall_seconds_total counter database_name, file_name, file_type one per database file each Counters. Waits, throughput and file I/O are cumulative in SQL Server (the Batch Requests/sec and Transactions/sec performance counters are totals despite their names), so they are exported as counters with the value SQL Server reports. Query them with rate() or increase(), never raw. A SQL Server restart, a failover, or DBCC SQLPERF('sys.dm_os_wait_stats', CLEAR) lowers the value; rate() and increase() treat that drop as a counter reset, so rates stay correct (the increase between the last scrape before the reset and the reset itself is lost). A failed probe, a target backing off or a collector restart leaves a gap but no reset: the counter resumes at SQL Server's value. A wait type appears when its wait time first becomes non-zero, so its first increase after SQL Server starts is not counted. The waits probe leaves out benign idle waits (system tasks sleeping, queues waiting for work, timers such as SLEEP_TASK, LAZYWRITER_SLEEP or XE_TIMER_EVENT), which grow by about a second per second on an idle server and would dominate top-N panels. The list follows Paul Randal's widely used wait statistics query and lives in catalog.go. CPU. SQL Server's scheduler monitor writes one record a minute with the host's CPU split over that minute: the SQL Server process, other processes, and idle. The cpu probe reads only the newest record, so both ratios (0–1, of all the CPUs SQL Server sees) change once a minute whatever the scrape interval, and lag the real load by up to a minute; idle is 1 - their sum. SQL Server on Linux reports no idle time (always 0, measured on 2019 and 2022), so heartbeat_sqlserver_cpu_other_process_ratio is exported on Windows only (the probe reads the platform from @@VERSION). Nothing is exported in the first minute after SQL Server starts, nor while the newest record is 3 minutes old or more, so a scheduler monitor that stops writing shows as a gap rather than a frozen value. Buffer cache. Page life expectancy (seconds) and the buffer cache hit ratio (0–1) come from the server-wide Buffer Manager performance counters, on default and named instances alike. There is no fixed good page life expectancy; compare a target with its own baseline. The hit ratio stays close to 1 on most servers even under memory pressure, so read it together with page life expectancy and file reads. Both are server-wide: on a server with several NUMA nodes, a page life expectancy drop on one node can hide in the Buffer Manager value. Per-node values (Buffer Node counters) are planned (TODO §11.2). File I/O. file_io exports six counters per database file, labelled like the storage probe's file size: read and write operations, bytes read and written, and I/O stall seconds (the time SQL Server waited for reads or writes to complete). Average latency per operation is the stall rate over the operation rate, e.g. rate(heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_read_stall_seconds_total[5m]) / (rate(heartbeat_sqlserver_database_file_reads_total[5m]) \u003e 0), where \u003e 0 leaves no value instead of dividing by zero for an idle file. The counters restart at 0 when SQL Server restarts or a database comes back online, which rate() treats as a reset. A stock instance has about 10 files (60 series); budget 6 series per file, so a server with 100 databases and two files each exports about 1,200. storage and file_io label a database whose name SQL Server does not return (the login lacks VIEW ANY DATABASE, or the database was dropped during the query) database_id:\u003cid\u003e. A target, identified by environment and name, may be enabled in only one sqlserver collector: configuration that lists it twice is rejected, because both collectors would write the same series and double the load on the database. The exporter also keeps each counter series to a single writer and logs any other write instead of applying it. Each catalog metric descriptor sets the type and the unit scale applied to the query column (milliseconds to seconds, KB or 8 KB pages to bytes). A collector that overrides a probe's query_template must return the same columns in the same units. A failed probe clears its series instead of exporting stale values, and a removed collector's series are deleted. A failing probe fails only itself. For example, storage and file_io read sys.master_files, which a long RESTORE or schema change can lock until the probe times out. While the target's other probes succeed, the target is degraded, stays up (heartbeat_collector_target_up 1) and keeps collecting, and the failing probe (heartbeat_collector_probe_up 0) backs off on its own, with the same schedule as a target (the next cycle, then doubling up to 5m, with jitter) and its series cleared until it succeeds again. A target fails, and backs off as a whole, only when every probe that ran failed, as when its database is unreachable or its login is refused; the probes' own backoffs are then forgotten. When every probe of a target is backing off on its own, they all run. While a target backs off none of its probes run and all its probe series are cleared, so they leave a gap instead of a frozen value; only the heartbeat_collector_target_* and heartbeat_collector_probe_up series stay. A probe that returns no rows exports no series: heartbeat_sqlserver_blocked_requests is absent, not 0, while nothing is blocked. Queries that need a zero fall back to targets whose blocking probe succeeded (heartbeat_collector_probe_up{probe=\"blocking\"} == 1), as the dashboard's Blocked Requests panel does, so neither an unreachable target nor a failing blocking probe reads as 0. The catalog lives in catalog.go. Collector self-observability Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_collector_target_up gauge collector, environment, target 1 if the last cycle for the target succeeded, including an outsystems_logs target that waits for the OTel Collector (blocked) and one with some probes failing (degraded) heartbeat_collector_probe_up gauge collector, environment, target, probe 1 if the probe's last execution succeeded, waiting for the OTel Collector included; 0 if it failed, backs off on its own, or its target failed or backs off heartbeat_collector_target_consecutive_failures gauge collector, environment, target Failed cycles in a row; a degraded cycle is not one heartbeat_collector_target_last_success_timestamp_seconds gauge collector, environment, target Unix time of the last cycle that did not fail the target (ok, blocked or degraded); use it for freshness alerts, and heartbeat_collector_probe_up for a single probe heartbeat_collector_cycle_duration_seconds gauge collector Duration of the last collection cycle heartbeat_collector_cycle_seconds histogram collector Duration of every collection cycle; buckets 10ms, 50ms, 100ms, 500ms, 1s, 5s, 10s, 30s, 60s heartbeat_collector_cycles_total counter collector, outcome Cycles by outcome: on_time (finished within its interval), late (used the whole interval: the deadline ended what was still running, and the next cycle starts late) or panic. Created at 0. A cycle ended by the collector stopping is not counted heartbeat_collector_cycle_start_delay_seconds histogram collector How much later than the interval after the previous start a cycle started; buckets 10ms, 100ms, 500ms, 1s, 5s, 15s, 60s heartbeat_collector_db_connections gauge environment, target, state Connections to a monitored database, in_use or idle. Read at each scrape, so a target's series go with its last connection pool. Targets on one server and database with one credential share a pool, reported under the first of them, in name order, used in the last 15 minutes; a renamed target takes its series over within 15 minutes heartbeat_collector_db_connection_waits_total, heartbeat_collector_db_connection_wait_seconds_total counter environment, target Waits for a free connection, and the time spent in them, including those of the target's pools closed since, as after a credential rotation heartbeat_collector_http_requests_total counter method, route, code Requests to the collector's own endpoints, as the API's heartbeat_collector_http_request_duration_seconds histogram method, route Time to serve them heartbeat_collector_target_login_sysadmin gauge collector, environment, target 1 if the collector's login for the target is sysadmin-equivalent: a member of sysadmin, or holding CONTROL SERVER (it should be 0; see login permissions). Checked when the collector first connects to the target and about every 10 minutes after; absent until the first check, and while SQL Server cannot tell heartbeat_collector_probe_duration_seconds histogram collector, environment, target, probe Probe execution time, failed and timed-out executions included (an abandoned probe is observed at the time it was abandoned); buckets 5ms, 10ms, 50ms, 100ms, 500ms, 1s, 5s, 10s, 30s, 60s heartbeat_collector_probe_errors_total counter collector, environment, target, probe, reason Failed probe executions. reason is one of timeout (probe timeout or cycle deadline reached while running, including a probe abandoned because it did not stop within 2s, or a database read or write that timed out), error (connection, login, query or decoding error, or more rows than the probe's max_rows), not_started (cycle deadline passed before the probe could start, or an abandoned probe of the target is still running), panic. Probes interrupted because the collector is stopping (shutdown or reload) are not counted go_*, process_*  none Go runtime (goroutines, GC, memory) and process (CPU, resident memory, open file descriptors, start time) metrics of the collector itself The probe error counters are created at 0 for every scheduled probe and reason when a collector starts, so rate() and increase() see the first error. Targets skipped during backoff run no probes, so they add neither errors nor durations; heartbeat_collector_target_up and heartbeat_collector_target_consecutive_failures cover them. Error text is logged, never used as a label. The probe series of a collector are deleted when it stops and recreated when it starts, so a reload that changes a collector resets its error counters to 0 (a counter reset for rate()) and drops the series of removed targets and probes. Per target, the self-observability series are 4 target gauges plus, per scheduled probe, 13 histogram series (10 buckets, +Inf, sum, count) and 4 error counters: 157 series for a target with the 9 built-in probes. Probe metric series are listed in the table above. Config reloads The outcome of every load of integrations.yaml: at startup, and on each reload, whatever triggered it (file polling, SIGHUP or POST /admin/config/reload). 7 series in all. Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_successful gauge none 1 if the last load or reload succeeded; 0 if it was rejected (invalid file) or could not be applied, until the next successful one. The last good config keeps running meanwhile, unless the rollback of a failed apply failed too (runtime_diverged, and /readyz returns 503). It is 0 exactly while GET /admin/config shows a last_reload_err heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_success_timestamp_seconds gauge none Unix time of the last successful load or reload: when the active config was loaded heartbeat_collector_config_reloads_total counter result Loads and reloads by result, the result of POST /admin/config/reload: ok, invalid_candidate (unreadable or invalid file, nothing applied), apply_failed (valid file, collectors not reconciled; rolled back or diverged), busy (gave up waiting for another reload; nothing read, the gauges unchanged). Created at 0 for every result heartbeat_collector_config_info gauge version Always 1. version is the active config's SHA-256, as in GET /admin/config. One series, which changes only when a reload activates a different file The startup load counts as one ok: a file that fails validation at startup stops the collector instead (it exits, and Kubernetes restarts it), so HeartbeatServiceDown reports that case. A rejected reload therefore matters twice: the change has not taken effect, and if the file in the ConfigMap stays invalid, the next pod restart fails. HeartbeatCollectorConfigReloadFailed (below) fires on it. OutSystems log extraction outsystems_logs collectors (configuration) report these on the same /metrics, labelled collector, environment, target and log_type. Their targets also get the self-observability series above, with the log type as the probe label: one probe execution reads, delivers and acknowledges up to 10 batches. A batch the OTel Collector has not accepted by the end of the probe execution is backpressure, not a failure. Its export carries on in the background and the log type reads nothing more until the Collector takes it (the chart's Collector takes the next batch only once Loki has the one before; see application observability). Meanwhile heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked is 1, the target stays up (heartbeat_collector_target_up 1, no backoff, no probe error), and GET /admin/config shows it as blocked, counted in targets_blocked, with what it waits for. A database the collector cannot read still fails the target as before. Metric Type Meaning heartbeat_outsystems_extract_rows_total counter Rows extracted and accepted by the OTel Collector heartbeat_outsystems_extract_lag_seconds gauge How far the watermark trails now, in real time: about the lag (the collector's, or the log type's own) plus the poll interval when extraction keeps up; it grows while extraction fails or falls behind heartbeat_outsystems_extract_lag_setting_seconds gauge The lag the log type is configured with, its own or the collector's: heartbeat_outsystems_extract_lag_seconds never goes below it. Set when the collector starts heartbeat_outsystems_export_failures_total counter Batches the OTel Collector rejected for good (a 4xx status other than 429). Network errors, timeouts, 429 and 5xx are retried with backoff (up to 5 s apart, 10 s per attempt) until the Collector accepts the batch. The watermark stays, so the rows are read again heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked gauge 1 while the log type waits for the OTel Collector to accept a batch, past the probe execution that read it: its extraction is paused, but its target is up. 0 otherwise. OutSystemsLogExportBlocked fires once it has been 1 for 2 minutes; meanwhile the lag grows, and OutSystemsLogExtractionBehind fires once it has been above 30 minutes for 10 heartbeat_outsystems_extract_stuck_batches_total counter Cycles that failed because every row of a full batch had the same Instant. The log type does not move until batch_limit is raised heartbeat_outsystems_log_delay_seconds gauge Now minus the newest Instant extracted, set when rows arrive: about the lag plus the poll interval while extraction keeps up. A row is read only once it is older than the lag, so this cannot show OutSystems' log service falling behind; heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total does. Absent until the first row heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total counter Rows the recount found in the logging database behind the watermark that were never read: committed later than the lag after their Instant. They are not in Loki and are not sent (ADR 0008). OutSystemsLateLogRows fires on any heartbeat_outsystems_duplicate_rows_total counter Rows the recount found delivered more often than the logging database holds them: read twice by the extraction, a defect, or deleted from the database after they were sent. It does not count a batch sent again after the OTel Collector had taken it (its answer lost, or a restart before the watermark was saved), which delivery at least once allows (ADR 0008), so 0 does not mean Loki holds no row twice. OutSystemsDuplicateLogRows fires on any heartbeat_outsystems_recounts_total counter Recounts run, failed ones included: one in each minute that has a poll heartbeat_outsystems_recount_failures_total counter Recounts that failed (query error, more than 1,000,000 rows in the window). Late and duplicate rows are not counted meanwhile; extraction goes on Two more have other labels. heartbeat_outsystems_stage_duration_seconds (histogram; collector, log_type, stage) is the time of one step of a batch: read (the query), build (rows to OTLP records), export (until the OTel Collector accepted it, waiting for Loki included), derive (the derived metrics) and watermark (the save to disk); buckets 10ms to 300s, at most 440 series per collector. heartbeat_outsystems_held_bytes (gauge, no labels) is the row data held in memory now, read, being exported or pending, against the 64 MiB allowed. The recount. Once a minute (in each minute that has a poll), each log type counts again, per minute of Instant, the rows its query returns in the recount_window (default 10 minutes, configuration) behind its watermark, and compares them with the rows it delivered in each minute. Each minute's difference is counted once, however many recounts cover it. The count wraps the log type's own query, so it reads what extraction reads, from the Instant index. The delivered counts are in memory and start again after the current watermark after a restart, and when a reload changes recount_window or the query: only the minutes after it are compared. A row committed more than about the window after its Instant goes uncounted. The counters and heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked are created at 0 when a collector starts and, like the probe series, deleted when it stops. Error text (contract violations, login and query errors) is logged and reported per target in GET /admin/config. Derived OutSystems metrics Computed from the rows each batch delivers, labelled collector, environment and application plus the labels shown. Design, label caps and the entry-point log types: derived metrics. Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_outsystems_operations_total counter module, log_type, operation, outcome Entry points: screens, Reactive server calls, timers and exposed APIs heartbeat_outsystems_operation_duration_seconds histogram module, log_type, outcome Entry-point duration; buckets 0.1 to 60 s heartbeat_outsystems_operation_seconds_total counter module, log_type, operation Entry-point seconds, for the mean per operation heartbeat_outsystems_front_end_operations_total counter front_end, outcome Entry points per front-end server heartbeat_outsystems_integration_duration_seconds histogram direction, type, outcome Integration calls, consumed and exposed heartbeat_outsystems_errors_total counter module Error log rows heartbeat_outsystems_slow_events_total counter module, kind Slow SQL and slow extension rows of the general log heartbeat_outsystems_timer_delay_seconds histogram operation Timer start minus its schedule; buckets 1 s to 1 h heartbeat_outsystems_screen_state_bytes histogram module, kind Traditional web screen session and viewstate size; buckets 1 KiB to 16 MiB They are deleted with their collector. Per application, each operation adds 3 counter series (two outcomes and its seconds) and each module, log type and outcome 13 histogram series (11 buckets, sum, count). The label caps bound each label, but they multiply: 100 modules and 100 operations per application and log type, up to 1,000 applications. So each collector holds at most max_derived_series of these series (configuration), counted as Prometheus stores them. Past it, a row that needs a new series is not counted in that metric; series that exist keep counting, so the metrics of applications, modules and operations seen first stay right and later ones are missing. Each metric is admitted on its own: as the budget fills, a row can be counted in some metrics and not in others. The budget is freed with the series when the collector stops or a reload changes it. Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_outsystems_derived_series gauge collector Derived series the collector holds, as the budget counts them; at most its max_derived_series heartbeat_outsystems_derived_series_overflow_total counter collector, metric Rows a derived metric (metric, its name) did not count because they needed a new series past max_derived_series: one per row and metric. OutSystemsDerivedSeriesOverflow fires on any Both are created, the counter at 0 for each of the nine metrics, when a collector starts, and deleted when it stops. API requests The API records every request it serves, /metrics and the health endpoints included. Prometheus scrapes it as job api. Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_api_http_requests_total counter method, route, code Requests served, by status code heartbeat_api_http_request_duration_seconds histogram method, route Time from receiving a request to its handler returning; the Prometheus client's default buckets, 5ms to 10s heartbeat_api_db_connections gauge state PostgreSQL pool connections: in_use, idle or constructing heartbeat_api_db_connections_max gauge none The most connections the pool opens heartbeat_api_db_connection_waits_total, heartbeat_api_db_connection_wait_seconds_total counter none Acquires that found no idle connection, and the time they waited heartbeat_api_redis_connections gauge state Redis pool connections: in_use or idle heartbeat_api_redis_connection_timeouts_total counter none Commands that gave up waiting for a connection from the pool route is the pattern that served the request, without the method (/readyz, /api/v1/applications/{id}, never the ID itself), or unmatched when none did: a 404, a 405 or a redirect. method is the request's method, or other outside GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE and OPTIONS. A scan of made-up paths and methods therefore lands in the unmatched and other series instead of adding new ones. Logins Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_api_login_attempts_total counter outcome Logins this replica answered: succeeded; failed (wrong credentials, an unknown email, a disabled user, each also audited); rate_limited (refused unchecked, past the limits). Requests it could not check (400) are not counted. Each outcome starts at 0. A rise in failed or rate_limited is someone guessing; the audit records say which accounts and from where Audit delivery Every admin or operator change writes an audit record to the audit_outbox table in its own transaction; a drainer in each API replica sends the oldest records to the OTel Collector and deletes them once it accepts them (ADR 0016). So far the audited changes are api users create-admin (configuration), logins, logouts and failed logins (sessions), inventory changes (inventory), telemetry sources (telemetry sources), and users and their roles (users and roles). A test makes each audited change and fails if a record holds a password, a hash or a session token. Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_api_audit_outbox_rows gauge none Records waiting in the outbox, read at each scrape. Every replica reports the same table. Absent from a scrape while PostgreSQL does not answer within 2s; the rest of the scrape still works heartbeat_api_audit_outbox_oldest_age_seconds gauge none Age of the oldest waiting record, 0 when none waits; normally a few seconds at most. HeartbeatAuditDeliveryStalled fires above 2 minutes for 2 minutes heartbeat_api_audit_records_delivered_total counter none Records this replica delivered: the Collector accepted them and they left the outbox. A record can be delivered twice (an export the Collector took after the API gave up on it); its id tells the copies apart The drainer claims up to 100 of the oldest rows at a time with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, so replicas never send the same rows at once, and sends them with the OTLP client the db-collector uses (client=\"audit\" in heartbeat_otlp_export_attempts_total): one export, retried for up to 30s, holds a round's rows. It goes on at once while it finds full batches, polls every 2s once the outbox is empty, and backs off from 1s to 30s while the Collector fails, logging audit: delivery failed once, then every minute, and audit: delivery resumed. A request the Collector accepts while refusing some of its records (OTLP partial success) is not sent again: those records are counted in heartbeat_otlp_export_rejected_records_total{client=\"audit\"} and HeartbeatOTLPRecordsRejected fires. Without HEARTBEAT_API_AUDIT_ENDPOINT, nothing is sent and records wait; the chart sets it to the bundled OTel Collector's 4318 while the Collector is on, so in the minimal profile records wait. A round that finds records is traced: audit.drain → audit.export and the db.query of the delete. Each record is an OTLP log record: severity INFO, the record's time, the record as compact JSON in the body (audit_record.schema.json), attributes event.name = heartbeat.audit and log.record.uid = the record's id, and the resource service.name = heartbeat-api with the replica's service.version, service.instance.id and deployment.environment.name. There is no service.namespace, so audit records stay out of Heartbeat's own logs (service_namespace=\"heartbeat\"). The record's fields are id, time, request_id, actor (a user's user_id and email, a cli command and the host it ran on, or the address of a client that is not logged in), action, entity_type, entity_id, before, after and, for runtime config, config_version. In Grafana's Explore, on Loki: Heartbeat's own telemetry Both services expose these (ADR 0018). Metric Type Labels Meaning heartbeat_service_info gauge service, version, revision, environment Always 1: what is running. environment is telemetry.environment, where the service itself runs; the environment of a heartbeat_collector_* series is the monitored target's heartbeat_telemetry_log_records_total counter outcome The service's own log records sent over OTLP: exported, dropped_queue_full (the queue holds 1,024 records and 8 MiB, while the Collector is slow or down) or dropped_export_failed (two attempts within 5 s failed, or the service stopped first). All are created at 0, and a service with no endpoint stays at 0. Dropped records are still on stderr heartbeat_telemetry_trace_errors_total counter none Errors the tracing SDK reported, mostly batches of spans it could not send; it keeps at most 2,048 spans waiting and drops the rest heartbeat_otlp_export_attempts_total counter client, outcome OTLP/HTTP attempts: accepted, retryable (network error, timeout, 429, 5xx) or rejected (any other status). client is self-logs, outsystems on the collector, or audit on the API; self-logs and audit exist only while the service has their endpoint. Created at 0. An attempt cancelled because the service is stopping is not counted heartbeat_otlp_export_attempt_duration_seconds histogram client Time of one attempt, whatever its outcome; buckets 5ms to 30s. Attempts near 10 s mean the receiver waits: for outsystems, the Collector waiting for Loki heartbeat_otlp_export_bytes_total counter client Compressed bytes of accepted requests heartbeat_otlp_export_rejected_records_total counter client Records the receiver refused inside a request it accepted (OTLP partial success). Such a request is not sent again, so the records are lost; HeartbeatOTLPRecordsRejected fires on any. Created at 0 The logs themselves are in Loki with the labels service_namespace=\"heartbeat\", service_name (db-collector, api) and deployment_environment_name: Traces are in Tempo. A collection cycle is one trace, collector.cycle → collector.target → collector.probe → db.query or the outsystems.read, outsystems.build, outsystems.export, outsystems.derive and outsystems.watermark steps of a batch. An API request is \u003cmethod\u003e \u003croute\u003e → db.query and redis.\u003ccommand\u003e. /metrics and the health endpoints make none. A client's traceparent is linked, not continued. Spans name what ran, never query values, command arguments or error text, which can name a host and a login. A log line in a sampled span has its trace_id, and Grafana links the two ways; a line in a span that was not sampled has none, since Tempo never gets that trace. What the telemetry costs Measured on kind on 2026-11-02 under make patientzero-load LOAD=steady, the same way as the memory budget, against the build before it (2844ec1), each on a freshly restarted node:  Before With it Budget Full profile in the kind node, peak less PatientZero 1.44 GiB 1.79 GiB (Tempo about 210 MiB of it) 2.5 GiB db-collector resident memory, peak 24.6 MiB 32.9 MiB 20 MiB more at most db-collector CPU 2.7 millicores 2.9 millicores within run-to-run noise (1.8 to 2.7) Collector /metrics series; scrape time, mean and max 1,015; 7.5 and 12.5 ms 1,697; 7.8 and 10.7 ms under 100 ms Probe p95 35 ms 30 ms no regression Rows extracted a second; extraction lag, max 9.5; 61 s 9.7; 60 s no regression Tempo kept every trace (telemetry.traces.sampleRatio: 1, now the default), about 5.6 spans a second, and its memory moved between 140 and 210 MiB without growing. A later 4.7-hour run on the same load took 4.8 spans and 655 bytes a second, stored about 190 bytes a span (some 80 MB a day, 550 MB over the 7-day retention, a quarter of its 2 GiB volume), and peaked at 249 MiB, so Tempo's limit went from 256 to 384 MiB. A log call costs about 1.4 µs and 18 allocations with the OTLP export on, 0.45 µs and none with it off; a request that is not sampled about 1.7 µs in the tracing middleware. Prometheus scrape jobs and rules Scrape jobs (prometheus.scrapeConfigs in the chart's values.yaml): prometheus, otel-collector, db-collector, api, alertmanager, loki, tempo; the minimal profile drops otel-collector, loki and tempo, which it does not deploy. db-collector and api use DNS discovery on the headless db-collector-headless and api-headless Services, which list the pods even while they are unready, so their failure metrics are still scraped. With api.enabled: false, turn the api job off too. Prometheus sends alerts to alertmanager:9093. Rules (heartbeat.rules.yml, heartbeat-config-reload.rules.yml for the config reload alert, and heartbeat-collector-missing.rules.yml for a db-collector with no pod, heartbeat-api-missing.rules.yml for an API with no pod, and heartbeat-self.rules.yml for Heartbeat's own objectives): Rule Type heartbeat:up:count, heartbeat:service_up:ratio Recording heartbeat:sqlserver_wait_seconds:rate5m Recording: wait seconds per second by wait_type, rate() of heartbeat_sqlserver_wait_seconds_total heartbeat:sqlserver_blocked_requests:sum Recording: blocked requests per target; 0 when the target's blocking probe succeeded and nothing is blocked heartbeat:sqlserver_sessions:sum Recording: sessions per target, all statuses heartbeat:outsystems_operations:rate5m, heartbeat:outsystems_errors:rate5m Recording: OutSystems entry points per second by log type and outcome; error log rows per second by module heartbeat:outsystems_request_duration_seconds:p95_5m, heartbeat:outsystems_integration_duration_seconds:p95_5m Recording: p95 request and integration latency heartbeat:outsystems_request_errors:ratio_rate{5m,30m,1h,6h}, heartbeat:outsystems_request_slow:ratio_rate{5m,30m,1h,6h}, heartbeat:outsystems_requests:rate{1h,6h} Recording: availability and latency (2 s) SLIs per application over screens, Reactive server calls and exposed APIs, and their traffic OutSystemsErrorBudgetBurnFast, OutSystemsLatencyBudgetBurnFast Alert (critical): 14.4× burn of a 99.9% availability or 99%-within-2-s objective over 1h and 5m, for applications with at least five (availability) or one (latency) requests a minute OutSystemsErrorBudgetBurnSlow, OutSystemsLatencyBudgetBurnSlow Alert (warning): 6× burn over 6h and 30m OutSystemsLogExtractionBehind Alert (warning): a log type's extraction lag above 30 minutes for 10m; the OutSystems metrics and SLO alerts of that target are stale OutSystemsLogExportBlocked Alert (warning): heartbeat_outsystems_export_blocked 1 for 2m, one alert per target: the OTel Collector has not taken a batch, so its log types read nothing more, usually because Loki is down or slow. Nothing is lost while the rows are still in the logging database's views OutSystemsLateLogRows Alert (warning): heartbeat_outsystems_late_rows_total grew in the last 15 minutes, per log type: rows committed later than the lag, not in Loki. Raise the log type's lag OutSystemsDuplicateLogRows Alert (warning): heartbeat_outsystems_duplicate_rows_total grew in the last 15 minutes: rows the extraction read twice, a defect, or rows deleted from the logging database after they were sent OutSystemsDerivedSeriesOverflow Alert (warning): heartbeat_outsystems_derived_series_overflow_total grew in the last 15 minutes, per collector: its derived series reached max_derived_series, so rows that need a new series (a new application, module, operation or front-end) are missing from the derived metrics, dashboards and SLO alerts. Raise max_derived_series if the collector's memory allows OutSystemsLogRecountFailing Alert (warning): more than half of a log type's recounts failed in the last 15 minutes, of three or more, whatever the poll interval; the reason is in the collector's log HeartbeatServiceDown Alert (warning): up == 0 for 2m for the DB collector, the API, the OTel Collector, Loki or Tempo; and no up series at all for the DB collector or the API, whose DNS discovery finds no target while they have no pod (absent(up{job=\"db-collector\"}), absent(up{job=\"api\"})). Where a name on Prometheus' DNS search path fails instead of answering NXDOMAIN, discovery keeps the collector's last target, and the up == 0 rule fires instead LokiDiscardingLogs Alert (warning): Loki discarded log rows in the last 10 minutes for a reason other than its rate limit (loki_discarded_samples_total); those rows are lost HeartbeatCollectorLoginElevated Alert (warning): heartbeat_collector_target_login_sysadmin == 1 for 15m, the collector's login for a target is sysadmin-equivalent HeartbeatCollectorConfigReloadFailed Alert (warning): heartbeat_collector_config_last_reload_successful == 0 for 2m, the collector rejected or could not apply its last config change and still runs the last good config. The reason is last_reload_err in GET /admin/config and the config reload rejected or config reload failed line in the pod log. Clears on the next successful reload. On by default; prometheusRules.configReloadAlert.enabled: false leaves it out heartbeat:collector_cycles_late:ratio_rate{5m,30m,1h,6h}, heartbeat:outsystems_extract_fresh:ratio_avg{5m,30m,1h,6h}, heartbeat:api_request_errors:ratio_rate{5m,30m,1h,6h}, heartbeat:api_request_slow:ratio_rate{5m,1h} Recording: Heartbeat's own SLIs: late cycles per collector, the share of time a log type's extraction lag stayed within 5 minutes of its configured lag, with a target that is down counted as behind, and the API's failed and slower-than-250-ms requests, without /metrics and the health endpoints HeartbeatCollectorCyclesLateFast, HeartbeatCollectorCyclesLate Alert (warning): 14.4× over 1h and 5m, and 6× over 6h and 30m, burn of the objective of 99.5% of cycles on time, for collectors with at least 30 cycles an hour. A slow monitored database can cause it as well as Heartbeat, so it stays a warning HeartbeatExtractionFreshnessBurn, HeartbeatExtractionFreshnessBurnSlow Alert (warning): the same burn rates against an extraction lag within 5 minutes of its configured lag 99% of the time HeartbeatAPIErrorBudgetBurnFast (critical), HeartbeatAPIErrorBudgetBurnSlow (warning), HeartbeatAPISlow (warning) Alert: 99.9% of the API's requests succeed and 95% answer within 250 ms, with at least five (errors) or one (latency) requests a minute HeartbeatServiceRestarting Alert (warning): more than 3 process starts in an hour, from process_start_time_seconds HeartbeatServiceMemoryHigh Alert (warning): resident memory above GOMEMLIMIT, 80% of the container's limit under the chart, for 15m HeartbeatGoroutinesGrowing Alert (warning): over 200 goroutines and growing by more than 20 an hour over 6 hours, for 1h HeartbeatOwnLogsDropped Alert (warning): heartbeat_telemetry_log_records_total counted drops in the last 15 minutes HeartbeatOTLPExportRejected Alert (warning): the OTel Collector rejected a request of a client for good in the last 15 minutes HeartbeatOTLPRecordsRejected Alert (warning): the OTel Collector accepted a request but refused some of its records (OTLP partial success) in the last 15 minutes, per client; those records are lost HeartbeatAuditDeliveryStalled Alert (warning): the oldest audit record in the API's outbox above 2 minutes old for 2 minutes, max() over the replicas, which report the same table: the Collector does not take audit records, or HEARTBEAT_API_AUDIT_ENDPOINT is unset. Changes still commit and no record is lost (audit delivery) Watchdog Alert: always firing. Alertmanager routes it to the deadmans-switch receiver (healthchecks.io in production, ADR 0005), which notifies when it stops arriving files/prometheus/rules/generated/ (in the chart) is loaded but empty; it is reserved for rules rendered from alert policies. The chart ships all rule files in the heartbeat-prometheus-rules ConfigMap. An alert that can be turned off has a rule file of its own, which the chart leaves out of the ConfigMap when its value is false, so operators do not edit rule files: prometheusRules.configReloadAlert.enabled for heartbeat-config-reload.rules.yml. heartbeat-collector-missing.rules.yml is in it only when the release has the db-collector and the bundled Prometheus scrapes it as job db-collector (not disabled, no job_name override): absent() fires wherever nothing matches. A shared Prometheus never gets it, even one that loads this ConfigMap and scrapes the collector as db-collector; it needs its own copy of the rule, for its own job name. prometheusRules.enabled: false leaves out every rule. make rules-check validates the rules and runs their promtool unit tests (files/prometheus/rules/tests/). A Go test fails if a rule, rule test, dashboard, script or metrics doc names a SQL Server metric the probe catalog does not emit, an OutSystems metric or a collector self-metric (heartbeat_collector_*) the collector does not export, or if a rule or dashboard reads a counter without rate() or increase(). Known gaps Alerts notify no one by default. Alerts reach Alertmanager, but the chart's default receiver has no integrations (ADR 0009). Chat and WhatsApp receivers exist only in production.example.yaml. Admin token attempts are not rate limited. The NetworkPolicy limits who can try. Tracked in TODO §15.2."},{"t":"Heartbeat DB Collector","u":"services/db-collector/index.html","h":[{"t":"Runtime Flow","id":"runtime-flow"},{"t":"Collector Model","id":"collector-model"},{"t":"Failure Isolation And Readiness","id":"failure-isolation-and-readiness"},{"t":"Probe / Extractor Model","id":"probe--extractor-model"},{"t":"How Rows Become Metrics","id":"how-rows-become-metrics"},{"t":"Evidence Path","id":"evidence-path"},{"t":"Connection Handling","id":"connection-handling"},{"t":"OutSystems Log Extraction","id":"outsystems-log-extraction"},{"t":"Configuration and Endpoints","id":"configuration-and-endpoints"},{"t":"Extending The Service","id":"extending-the-service"},{"t":"Related Code","id":"related-code"}],"x":"Heartbeat DB Collector db-collector is the runtime service that connects to SQL Server targets, runs configured probes on an interval, and exports the results as Prometheus metrics. Its outsystems_logs collectors also extract OutSystems 11 logs from the logging database and send them to the OTel Collector as OTLP logs (see OutSystems log extraction). The current implementation is intentionally narrow: SQL Server is the only supported engine. Collector desired state comes from integrations.yaml (the Helm chart's integrations values when deployed; config/integrations.yaml when run directly). Probe definitions are implemented as a built-in SQL Server catalog. Metrics are exported to Prometheus. Structured evidence is produced for blocking/session-style probes, but the default wiring currently discards that evidence instead of persisting it. Runtime Flow The implemented path is: services/db-collector/cmd/db-collector/main.go starts the app. internal/app/app.go loads integration config and creates the shared Prometheus registry (with Go runtime, process and per-probe metrics), SQL executor, exporter, and collector lifecycle. Each enabled collector becomes a background Poller. The Poller repeatedly calls Runner.RunOnce on the configured scrape interval. Runner expands the collector into scheduled probes and executes them one by one. SQLExecutor opens a SQL Server connection, runs the probe query, decodes rows into samples, and optionally emits evidence. PrometheusExporter records the samples as gauges or counters, as the probe's metric descriptors say, exposed on /metrics. Collector Model A collector is the unit of runtime configuration defined in internal/config/config.go. Each collector contains: an ID and kind an enabled/disabled flag a scrape interval a collector-level credential reference one or more targets optional target filtering through target_names The collector lifecycle in internal/app/lifecycle.go supports: Start for new collectors Update for changed collectors Drain for graceful shutdown preparation Stop for full removal Collectors are reconciled by stable ID, so config reloads can restart only the collectors that changed. Reloads are serialized and bounded (30s). Invalid candidates keep the previous configuration running. A valid candidate is diffed against the collectors that are actually running, and collectors that are not running (crashed or failed) are restarted even if their config is unchanged. If applying fails part-way, the applied steps are rolled back in reverse order; if the rollback also fails, runtime_diverged is set and /readyz returns 503 until a later reload succeeds. Unknown SQL Server probe names and OutSystems log types are invalid too: internal/app registers both catalogs' Validate with the config manager, and the runtime check stays as a backstop. Every load and reload is exported as heartbeat_collector_config_* metrics, and a rejected or failed one fires HeartbeatCollectorConfigReloadFailed (config reload metrics). Failure Isolation And Readiness Targets run concurrently (up to 8 per collector); probes within a target run one at a time. Each cycle is bounded by the scrape interval, and each probe by min(interval/2, 10s) unless timeout_ms overrides it (capped at the interval; at most 25000, a larger value is rejected when the config is loaded). Every probe also has a hard deadline: if it has not returned 2s after its timeout, the cycle deadline or a stop, the collector abandons it, reports a timeout, and moves on, so a query the SQL Server driver cannot cancel (it waits for the server to acknowledge the cancel, which a frozen or vanished server never does) never stalls the cycle, other targets, or shutdown. The abandoned call finishes in the background and its result is discarded; until it returns, the target's probes in that collector are not started (not_started) and the target counts as failed. The bound is one stuck call per collector and target name, not per database: the same database reached under another target name (another environment or collector), or a target renamed by a reload, can hold one more stuck call each. Stuck calls keep their connections, and the pool for one address, database and login holds at most 2, so later probes of that database can wait for a connection until their own timeout. The driver's 30s socket timeout (connection timeout, the 25s maximum probe timeout plus a 5s margin, so it never cuts a running probe short) bounds that wait: a dead connection fails and leaves the pool within about a minute. A probe that fails on that socket timeout counts as timeout. A failing probe fails only itself. While other probes of its target succeed, the target is degraded and stays up, and the probe backs off on its own: the first failure retries on the next cycle, repeated failures back off exponentially (capped at 5m, with jitter). A target fails, and backs off the same way as a whole, only when every probe that ran failed. Every probe failure is logged as structured JSON with collector, target, and probe. A failed probe clears its series instead of exporting stale values, for as long as it backs off. While a target backs off none of its probes run, so all its probe series are cleared; only its heartbeat_collector_target_* and heartbeat_collector_probe_up series stay. A removed collector's series are deleted. Self-observability series: heartbeat_collector_target_up, heartbeat_collector_probe_up, heartbeat_collector_target_consecutive_failures, heartbeat_collector_target_last_success_timestamp_seconds, heartbeat_collector_target_login_sysadmin, heartbeat_collector_cycle_duration_seconds, the per-probe heartbeat_collector_probe_duration_seconds histogram and heartbeat_collector_probe_errors_total counter (by reason: timeout, error, not_started, panic; created at 0 when the collector starts), and Go runtime and process metrics. A stopped collector's probe series are deleted with its other series. A crashed poller is restarted with backoff (1s doubling to 1m). /healthz is liveness only. /readyz returns 503 before every collector has completed its first cycle, when a collector is failed or crash-looping, when a collector has not completed a cycle within 2x its interval + 10s, or when the runtime diverged after a failed rollback. A monitored database being down does not make the pod unready; it shows as a failed target in metrics and in the authenticated GET /admin/config, which also carries the raw driver error of the target's last failed cycle, kept while it backs off. The /readyz body is only the status. The admin endpoints (GET /admin/config, POST /admin/config/reload) need HEARTBEAT_ADMIN_TOKEN as a bearer token, compared in constant time, and are disabled without it. Startup warns when certificate verification is off (HEARTBEAT_DB_COLLECTOR_SQLSERVER_TRUST_SERVER_CERTIFICATE). Details: endpoints. SQL Server connections are pooled per target (max 2 open, 10m lifetime); idle pools are closed after 15m. Shutdown is bounded: HTTP drain 10s, poller stop 20s, then pooled connections are closed. Probe / Extractor Model In this codebase, the extractor logic lives in the SQL Server probe catalog: internal/probes/sqlserver/catalog.go. Each probe definition includes: a name a category a default SQL query template a list of metric descriptors Each metric descriptor tells the runtime how to extract one Prometheus sample from the query result: ValueColumn becomes the numeric value; a NULL produces no sample Scale converts it to the metric's base unit (for example 0.001 for milliseconds to seconds, 1024 for KB to bytes); zero means 1 Type is gauge (the zero value) or counter; counters are values SQL Server accumulates since startup, exported as reported and read with rate() LabelColumns become Prometheus labels; pick columns with a bounded set of values Name and Help define the exported metric One row can feed several descriptors: throughput pivots its two performance counters into one row with a column per metric. Ratios are computed in the query and exported as gauges. The built-in probes, their source views and the metrics they emit are listed in the metrics reference. If a collector overrides query_template for a probe, the runtime uses that SQL instead of the catalog default. The configuration loader accepts only one read-only SELECT (ValidateReadOnlySelect in internal/config/query.go), and the catalog tests run every built-in query through the same check; the rules say what it rejects and why the read-only login, not the check, is the control. go run ./services/db-collector/cmd/probe-catalog lists every probe's metrics, and -sql \u003cprobe\u003e prints the batch the collector sends, session settings included; the live demo runs it as the collector's login. How Rows Become Metrics internal/collectors/runner.go contains the row decoding path: query rows are read into map[string]any, at most the probe's max_rows (default 10000); a row past the limit fails the probe with ErrTooManyRows without being scanned []byte values are normalized to strings numeric values are converted with toFloat64 and multiplied by the descriptor's Scale label keys are taken from the metric descriptor, not from arbitrary columns any row that does not contain the configured value column, or holds NULL in it, is skipped internal/export then records each probe's samples under its scope. Recording a known series does not allocate, and scrapes copy the current values under a short read lock, so a scrape never holds up collection. Samples whose label set or type differs from the metric's first registration, negative counter values, and label values that are not valid UTF-8 are rejected and logged. This means the extractor is schema-driven rather than dynamically inferred. Only the columns named in the probe catalog contribute to exported metrics. Evidence Path The runtime also produces structured evidence for some probe categories. Today, evidence is emitted for probes in the blocking and sessions categories when at least one row is returned. Evidence includes: Kind a human-readable Title metadata such as row count The default service wiring uses LoggingEvidenceSink, which is a no-op sink. That keeps the collector metrics-only for now while preserving the evidence API for future alerting or investigation workflows. Connection Handling internal/connectors/sqlserver/connector.go owns database access. Current behavior: credentials are resolved through a CredentialResolver on every Open: FileCredentialResolver reads a HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIAL_\u003cREF\u003e file from HEARTBEAT_CREDENTIALS_DIR (the mounted credentials Secret), so a rotated credential is used by the next connection without a restart, and falls back to an environment variable of the same name (resolution rules). The resolvers live in internal/credentials, which the API uses too. A changed credential gets a new pool; the old one is closed once idle the DSN enables TLS by default each pool is verified when it is created with one query that also checks whether the login is sysadmin-equivalent (IS_SRVROLEMEMBER('sysadmin') or HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME(NULL, NULL, 'CONTROL SERVER')); the check is repeated about every 10 minutes by the one Open that claims it, without holding the pool lock. An elevated or undeterminable login is logged as a warning and reported through Manager.Sysadmin, which SQLExecutor exposes to the runner (SysadminReporter) for the heartbeat_collector_target_login_sysadmin health series connection errors are scrubbed of the login and password before they are wrapped, because go-mssqldb's DSN parse errors quote the whole DSN every batch starts with connector.SessionSettings (SET LOCK_TIMEOUT 1000; SET DEADLOCK_PRIORITY LOW;) in the same round trip; why and how operators see it is in the onboarding guide probe execution uses a per-probe timeout OutSystems Log Extraction The outsystems_logs kind (ADR 0008) runs on the same Runner, through collectors.EngineExecutor, which picks the executor of each collector's kind. Its probes are log types, so it gets the same per-target isolation, backoff, hard deadline, readiness and probe self-metrics as SQL Server probes. internal/probes/outsystems embeds the log-type queries, queries/*.sql, one per log type, and rejects configs that name a log type without one. internal/collectors/outsystems is the executor. Each call reads Instant in (watermark, now − lag] through SQLServerSource (the same connector, pools and session settings), checks the query contract, turns the rows into OTLP records, sends them, and only then saves the watermark (Store, one file per collector, target and log type under HEARTBEAT_STATE_DIR). A full batch leaves its last Instant for the next batch, read at once up to 10 times per call. internal/otlp posts the records to \u003copentelemetry.endpoint\u003e/v1/logs (protobuf, gzip, retries). Settings: configuration reference. Metrics: OutSystems log extraction. go run ./services/db-collector/cmd/probe-catalog -sql error prints a log type's batch with its parameters declared. Configuration and Endpoints integrations.yaml keys, credential resolution and environment variables: configuration reference HTTP endpoints and metrics: metrics and endpoints reference Adding a monitored database: onboarding guide Extending The Service To add a new SQL Server probe: Add a Probe entry in internal/probes/sqlserver/catalog.go. Define the SQL query template. Add one or more Metric descriptors with the value column and label columns. Add or update tests in internal/probes/sqlserver/catalog_test.go and internal/collectors/runner_test.go. Check the probe against the probe review checklist and run make test-sqlserver. To add an OutSystems log type, add internal/probes/outsystems/queries/\u003clog\ntype\u003e.sql under the query contract, the CREATE TABLE and CREATE VIEW statements of the objects it reads to internal/collectors/outsystems/testdata/oslog/\u003clog type\u003e.sql and representative rows to \u003clog type\u003e.rows.sql next to it, then run make test-sqlserver. To add a new collector: Declare it in the environment's integrations values (or config/integrations.yaml when running the binary directly). Set the collector kind: sqlserver, or outsystems_logs for OutSystems logs. Provide target credentials through the configured credential reference. Confirm the collector appears under readiness.collectors in GET /admin/config and exports metrics on /metrics. Related Code services/db-collector/internal/app/app.go services/db-collector/internal/app/lifecycle.go services/db-collector/internal/collectors/runner.go services/db-collector/internal/collectors/outsystems/extractor.go services/db-collector/internal/connectors/sqlserver/connector.go services/db-collector/internal/export/exporter.go services/db-collector/internal/metadata/types.go services/db-collector/internal/probes/sqlserver/catalog.go services/db-collector/internal/probes/outsystems/catalog.go"}];
